6. A derivation habit
When an identity is unfamiliar, rebuild it in this order:

Name the candidate set and the desired subset.
State what one term, or one coefficient, counts.
Explain why choices multiply, or why cases add.
Test at \(n = 0, 1, 2\) or on a board you can enumerate by hand.
Only then choose a DP, a convolution, or a generating function.

The errors that cost contests are conceptual, not syntactic:

confusing \(k!\left\{ {n \atop k} \right\}\) (named groups) with \(\left\{ {n \atop k} \right\}\) (unnamed groups);
dividing by \(k!\) when it is zero in the working characteristic;
using IE when the intersections are harder than the original condition;
applying the rook product rule to board pieces that share a row or column;
counting rotations or reflections as distinct when the problem treats them as identical — that is Chapter 05.

23.2 Prefix-Constrained Bipartite Matching (\(i \le j\)) & WQS / Aliens Convex Penalty Elimination (CF 802 O — April Fools' Problem (hard))
In CF 865 D, we were allowed to make any number of profitable transactions, and a single node \(j\) could simultaneously cancel a sale and buy a fresh share. Now consider a strictly harder and ubiquitous archetype: cardinality-constrained prefix matching where left and right endpoints are distinct resources.

Problem Statement (CF 802 O — April Fools' Problem (hard))
Over \(N \le 5 \times 10^5\) days, on day \(i\) you can prepare at most \(1\) problem at cost \(a_i\), and on day \(j\) you can print at most \(1\) problem at cost \(b_j\). A problem prepared on day \(i\) can be printed on day \(j\) if and only if \(i \le j\). You must prepare and print at least \(K\) (equivalently, exactly \(K\) since \(a_i, b_j \ge 0\)) problems. Find the minimum total cost.


Why Naive \(K\)-Step Online Regret Fails (FM-23 / H-24)
Suppose we try to avoid binary search and simply run a left-to-right sweep \(j = 1 \dots N\), maintaining \(K\) best pairs online. Why does that fail?
Because the constraint \(i \le j\) is directional! In an online sweep without a global slope threshold \(\lambda\), you only know whether a pair \((i, j)\) is “worth taking” if you know the marginal cost of the \(K\)-th augmentation across the entire array \([1, N]\). Worse still, in an explicit MCMF network:

Source \(S \to u_i\) has capacity \(1\), cost \(a_i\).
Spine \(u_i \to u_{i+1}\) has capacity \(\infty\), cost \(0\).
Sink \(u_j \to T\) has capacity \(1\), cost \(b_j\).

When an augmenting path uses a reverse spine arc \(u_{t+1} \to u_t\) (meaning positive flow was already moving rightward across \(u_t \to u_{t+1}\)), it splits one long interval \([i_1, j_1]\) paired with an internal point into two valid intervals \([i_1, j_2]\) and \([i_2, j_1]\). A single left-to-right pass at a fixed Lagrangian slope \(\lambda\) automatically simulates all such interval-splitting augmentations without ever tracking interval endpoints!


Step 1: WQS / Aliens Convex Penalty Elimination
By Theorem 17.3 (Convexity of the Min-Cost Flow Function) from Chapter 17, let \(C(k)\) be the minimum cost to send \(k\) units of flow from \(S\) to \(T\) in our prefix network (\(0 \le k \le N\)). Because each SSP step chooses the shortest augmenting path, the marginal costs \(\Delta_k = C(k) - C(k-1)\) are non-decreasing:
\[
\Delta_1 \le \Delta_2 \le \dots \le \Delta_N \implies C(k) \text{ is convex on } \{0, 1, \dots, N\}.
\]
Instead of constraining the flow to equal \(K\), we apply the WQS / Aliens Lagrangian relaxation: subtract a reward \(\lambda\) from every unit of flow reaching \(T\) (equivalently, change every print cost from \(b_j\) to \(b_j - \lambda\)). For a fixed \(\lambda\), we solve the unconstrained minimum-cost circulation problem:
\[
g(\lambda) = \min_{0 \le k \le N} \big(C(k) - k \cdot \lambda\big),
\]
which simply pushes flow along every augmenting path whose penalized cost \(a_i + b_j - \lambda\) is negative!


Step 2: The Two-Candidate Regret Heap at Fixed \(\lambda\)
Once \(\lambda\) is fixed, we sweep \(j = 1, 2, \dots, N\) from left to right. Every matched pair has the form \(a_i + (b_j - \lambda) &lt; 0\) for some \(i \le j\). What are the available options in the residual graph on day \(j\)?

Candidate Type A (Unmatched Preparation \(i \le j\)): At the start of day \(j\), preparation \(u_j\) becomes available, so we push (key = a_j, type = A) into our min-heap \(Q\). If day \(j\)’s penalized print option \(b_j - \lambda\) matches with a Type-A entry key = a_i (because \(a_i + b_j - \lambda &lt; 0\)), we:

Add \(a_i + b_j - \lambda\) to total penalized cost,
Increment the matched pair count cnt by \(+1\) (since we sent \(1\) new unit of flow from \(S\) to \(T\)),
Pop \(a_i\) from \(Q\), and
Push a Type-B Regret Token (key = -(b_j - \lambda), type = REGRET_B) into \(Q\)!

Candidate Type B (Stealing a Previous Print \(j_0 &lt; j\)): Why did we push key = -(b_{j_0} - \lambda) with type = REGRET_B when \(i\) matched with \(j_0\)? Suppose a later day \(j &gt; j_0\) has a cheaper print cost \(b_j &lt; b_{j_0}\). If day \(j\) pops the regret token key = -(b_{j_0} - \lambda) from \(Q\) and pairs with it, the incremental cost added is: \[
\text{key} + (b_j - \lambda) = -(b_{j_0} - \lambda) + (b_j - \lambda) = b_j - b_{j_0} &lt; 0.
\] Adding this to the earlier cost \(a_i + b_{j_0} - \lambda\) gives: \[
(a_i + b_{j_0} - \lambda) + (b_j - b_{j_0}) = a_i + b_j - \lambda,
\] which redirects the preparation at \(i\) from print \(j_0\) to print \(j\)! Crucially:

Because this transition only redirects an existing unit of flow from \(j_0 \to T\) to \(j \to T\), the total flow count cnt changes by \(0\) (cnt += 0)!
And because day \(j\) is now matched to \(T\), we push a new regret token (key = -(b_j - \lambda), type = REGRET_B) into \(Q\) so a still later day \(j' &gt; j\) can steal from \(j\)!








WarningCritical Invariant: Collinear Slope Tie-Breaking in WQS (FM-23)



When the convex function \(C(k)\) has a flat segment of slope \(\lambda^*\) (so multiple values of \(k\) tie for the minimum of \(C(k) - k \lambda^*\)), our inner check solve(lambda) must break ties consistently—for instance, always returning the minimum (or maximum) possible pair count cnt among all optimal solutions for \(\lambda\).
To return the minimum optimal pair count cnt (and binary search for the smallest \(\lambda\) where cnt &gt;= K, or largest \(\lambda\) where cnt &lt;= K):

Only take an augmenting path if its penalized cost is strictly negative (key + b[j] - lambda &lt; 0).
Inside the min-heap \(Q\) of (key, type), if two entries have the exact same key, order type = REGRET_B (which adds \(0\) to cnt) before type = A (which adds \(+1\) to cnt)! Conversely, if you order type = A before type = REGRET_B and take paths with &lt;= 0, you consistently maximize cnt. Either convention works cleanly as long as the binary search condition matches the tie-breaker!





Complete Reference Implementation (CF 802 O Engine)
#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

struct Node {
    long long key;
    int delta_cnt; // 1 for fresh Type-A prep, 0 for Type-B regret token
    // Min-heap comparator: smaller key first; on tie, prefer delta_cnt = 0 (minimum pair count)
    bool operator&gt;(const Node& other) const {
        if (key != other.key) return key &gt; other.key;
        return delta_cnt &gt; other.delta_cnt;
    }
};

pair&lt;long long, int&gt; evaluate_slope(int n, const vector&lt;long long&gt;& a,
                                    const vector&lt;long long&gt;& b, long long lambda) {
    priority_queue&lt;Node, vector&lt;Node&gt;, greater&lt;Node&gt;&gt; pq;
    long long penalized_cost = 0;
    int cnt = 0;
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; ++j) {
        // 1. Day j's preparation option becomes available
        pq.push({a[j], 1});
        // 2. Check if day j's print option (b[j] - lambda) can profitably match
        long long print_val = b[j] - lambda;
        if (pq.top().key + print_val &lt; 0) {
            Node best = pq.top();
            pq.pop();
            penalized_cost += best.key + print_val;
            cnt += best.delta_cnt;
            // Push regret token: cancelling print j later refunds -(b[j] - lambda) with 0 net count change
            pq.push({-print_val, 0});
        }
    }
    return {penalized_cost, cnt};
}

long long solve_cf802o(int n, int k, const vector&lt;long long&gt;& a, const vector&lt;long long&gt;& b) {
    long long lo = 0, hi = 2000000005LL, best_lambda = 0;
    while (lo &lt;= hi) {
        long long mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        auto [cost, cnt] = evaluate_slope(n, a, b, mid);
        if (cnt &lt;= k) {
            best_lambda = mid;
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            hi = mid - 1;
        }
    }
    auto [cost, cnt] = evaluate_slope(n, a, b, best_lambda);
    // Reconstruct exact unpenalized cost C(K) from the supporting line of slope best_lambda
    return cost + 1LL * k * best_lambda;
}

23.6 Worked Examples & Full 8-Problem Difficulty Ladder (#177–#184, Part 1: #177–#180)
In this section and the next, we work through all 8 canonical slots #177–#184 of Chapter 23. Because Simulated MCMF Regret Heaps and Slope-Trick Isotonic Regression are LP duals of each other (Section 23.1), for slots where the curriculum and problem archive pair a Simulated-MCMF flagship with its Slope-Trick / Isotonic dual, we present both complete derivations and reference solutions so you can master both sides of the duality.


#177 (Stepping Stone 1) — [CF 865D] Buy Low Sell High & Its Slope-Trick Dual [CF 713C] Sonya and Problem Wihtout a Legend

Canonical Slot: Ch 23 — Stepping Stone 1 (SS-1) | Wave Provenance: 4B-P06 / W6-01 & W6-09
Judge URLs:

Primary (Simulated MCMF): https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/865/D
Dual Companion (Slope Trick \(\ell_1\) Isotonic Regression): https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/713/C



Constraints & Problem Statements

CF 865D (Primal Simulated Flow): Given stock prices \(p_1, \dots, p_N\) over \(N \le 3 \times 10^5\) days (\(1 \le p_i \le 10^6\)), each day you may buy \(1\) share, sell \(1\) share, or do nothing. Maximize total profit.
CF 713C (Dual \(\ell_1\) Isotonic Regression): Given array \(a_1, \dots, a_N\) (\(N \le 3 \times 10^5\), \(1 \le a_i \le 10^9\)), increment or decrement elements at unit cost to make the final integer array strictly increasing (\(b_1 &lt; b_2 &lt; \dots &lt; b_N\)). Minimize \(\sum_{i=1}^N |b_i - a_i|\).



4-Step Compilation & Duality Synthesis

Step 1 (Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal): In CF 865D, naive greedy matching \((p_i, p_j)\) blocks better future sales \(p_k &gt; p_j\). In CF 713C, after shifting \(a'_i = a_i - i\) and \(b'_i = b_i - i\) to convert strict \(b_1 &lt; \dots &lt; b_N\) into non-decreasing \(b'_1 \le \dots \le b'_N\), standard DP \(f_i(x) = \min_{y \le x} f_{i-1}(y) + |x - a'_i|\) takes \(O(N^2)\) time. Both problems ask for \(O(N \log N)\) maintenance of a 1D spine residual/slope structure where each vertex has capacity \(2\)!
Step 2 (Algebraic Representation):

For CF 865D: Matching buy \(i\) with sell \(j\) earns \(p_j - p_i\). Re-routing \(j \to k\) earns \((p_j - p_i) + (p_k - p_j) = p_k - p_i\), leaving day \(j\) with \(0\) net action so a second copy of \(p_j\) can still buy a fresh share.
For CF 713C: By LP duality, \(\min_{b'_1 \le \dots \le b'_N} \sum_{i=1}^N |b'_i - a'_i|\) is dual to sending flow \(f_i \in [-1, +1]\) along the spine with node values \(a'_i\). In Slope Trick, adding \(|x - a'_i|\) shifts slopes \(&lt; a'_i\) by \(-1\) and slopes \(&gt; a'_i\) by \(+1\) (inserting two copies of breakpoint \(a'_i\)), and taking the prefix minimum \(\min_{y \le x}\) pops the single largest breakpoint from the max-heap \(H\)!

Step 3 (Heap Invariant):

CF 865D (Min-Heap \(H\)): On day \(j\), push \(p_j\) twice and pop \(\min(H)\), adding \(p_j - \min(H)\) to ans! (Notice: if \(\min(H) == p_j\), popping \(\min(H)\) and pushing \(p_j\) twice leaves one \(p_j\) in \(H\) and adds \(0\) to ans—identical to our if form in Section 23.1!).
CF 713C (Max-Heap \(H\)): At index \(i\), let \(x = a_i - i\). Push \(x\) twice into \(H\), add \(\max(H) - x\) to ans, and pop \(\max(H)\)!

Step 4 (Failure-Mode Guard):

In CF 865D, never push \(p_j\) only once when \(p_j &gt; \min(H)\), or middle relay days cannot buy their own share.
In CF 713C, always subtract the index \(a'_i = a_i - i\) first when strict inequality \(b_1 &lt; b_2 &lt; \dots &lt; b_N\) is required!








Note3-Tier Progressive Hints (#177)






Hint 1: In CF 865D, if you bought at \(p_i\) and sold at \(p_j\), how much extra profit do you get if you change your mind and sell at \(p_k\) instead? In CF 713C, how do you transform \(b_1 &lt; b_2 &lt; \dots &lt; b_N\) on integers into \(b'_1 \le b'_2 \le \dots \le b'_N\)?
Hint 2: In CF 865D, selling at \(p_j\) creates two residual possibilities at day \(j\) of price \(p_j\): cancelling the sale (\(-1 \to 0\)) and buying a new share (\(0 \to +1\)). In CF 713C, adding \(|x - a'_i|\) changes the derivative of \(f_i(x)\) by \(+2\) at \(x = a'_i\).
Hint 3: Both algorithms boil down to an unconditional 4-statement loop body per element: push \(x\) twice into the heap, add \(|H.\text{top}() - x|\) to ans, and pop \(H.\text{top}()\)!










TipComplete C++ Solutions (CF 865D & CF 713C)





// Solution 1: CF 865D — Buy Low Sell High (Simulated MCMF Min-Heap)
// Time: O(N log N), Space: O(N)
#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n)) return 0;
    priority_queue&lt;long long, vector&lt;long long&gt;, greater&lt;long long&gt;&gt; pq;
    long long total_profit = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        long long p;
        cin &gt;&gt; p;
        // Push twice: 1 for fresh buy (0 -&gt; +1), 1 for regret token (-1 -&gt; 0) if matched
        // If p is the minimum, popping it once leaves 1 copy and adds p - p = 0!
        pq.push(p);
        pq.push(p);
        total_profit += p - pq.top();
        pq.pop();
    }
    cout &lt;&lt; total_profit &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}
// Solution 2: CF 713C — Sonya and Problem Wihtout a Legend (Slope Trick Max-Heap Dual)
// Time: O(N log N), Space: O(N)
#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n)) return 0;
    priority_queue&lt;long long&gt; pq; // Max-heap of left-slope breakpoints
    long long min_cost = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        long long a;
        cin &gt;&gt; a;
        long long x = a - i; // Transform strict b_1 &lt; ... &lt; b_n to b'_1 &lt;= ... &lt;= b'_n
        pq.push(x);
        pq.push(x);
        min_cost += pq.top() - x;
        pq.pop();
    }
    cout &lt;&lt; min_cost &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







#178 (Stepping Stone 2) — [CF 280D] k-Maximum Subsequence Sum & Archive Companion [CF 13C] Sequence

Canonical Slot: Ch 23 — Stepping Stone 2 (SS-2) | Wave Provenance: 4B-P07 & W6-10
Judge URLs:

Primary (Segment-Tree Simulated SSP): https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/280/D
Archive Companion (Non-Decreasing Isotonic Regression): https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/13/C



Constraints & Problem Statements

CF 280D: Given \(a_1, \dots, a_N\) (\(N \le 10^5\), \(|a_i| \le 500\)) and \(Q \le 10^5\) operations (0 p v point update \(a_p \gets v\); 1 L R k query max sum of \(\le k\) disjoint subarrays in \([L, R]\) with \(k \le 20\)).
CF 13C: Given \(a_1, \dots, a_N\) (\(N \le 5000\)), minimize \(\sum_{i=1}^N |b_i - a_i|\) subject to \(b_1 \le b_2 \le \dots \le b_N\) (solvable in \(O(N \log N)\) time and \(O(N)\) memory via #177 without the \(-i\) shift).



4-Step Compilation (CF 280D)

Step 1 (Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal): \(k \le 20\) per query screams Successive Shortest Path (SSP) simulated on a Segment Tree: each of the \(k\) steps extracts the single maximum subarray \([l^*, r^*] \subseteq [L, R]\) and negates its elements to represent reverse residual arcs!
Step 2 (Intermediate Residual Representation): Saturating \(u_i \to v_i\) (weight \(+a_i\)) for \(i \in [l^*, r^*]\) replaces those arcs in \(G_f\) with \(v_i \to u_i\) (weight \(-a_i\)).
Step 3 (Segment Tree Merging & Lazy Flip Contract): Define a struct Sub { int sum, l, r; } ordered by sum. Each tree node stores tot (range sum), mx, lmx, rmx (best subarray, prefix, suffix for maximum), mn, lmn, rmn (best subarray, prefix, suffix for minimum), and bool rev. Flipping a node negates tot and swaps (mx, lmx, rmx) with negated (mn, lmn, rmn).
Step 4 (Failure-Mode Guard): Always push all flipped intervals \([l^*, r^*]\) onto a vector and flip them back in reverse order at the end of the query, even if the loop breaks early because mx.sum &lt;= 0!







Note3-Tier Progressive Hints (#178)






Hint 1: Why does negating the elements inside the chosen maximum subarray \([l_1, r_1]\) allow the second maximum-subarray query to either pick a disjoint subarray outside \([l_1, r_1]\) OR split \([l_1, r_1]\) into two subarrays?
Hint 2: When a Segment Tree node’s elements are negated (\(a_i \gets -a_i\)), its new maximum subarray is the negation of its old minimum subarray!
Hint 3: Maintain both max_subarray and min_subarray (along with prefix/suffix bests and their [l, r] endpoints) in every Segment Tree node. Run up to \(k\) queries+flips, then undo all flips!










TipComplete C++ Solution (CF 280D — k-Maximum Subsequence Sum)





// CF 280D — k-Maximum Subsequence Sum (Segment-Tree Simulated SSP)
// Time: O((N + Q * k) log N), Space: O(N)
#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

struct Seg {
    int sum, l, r;
    Seg operator+(const Seg& o) const { return {sum + o.sum, l, o.r}; }
    bool operator&lt;(const Seg& o) const { return sum &lt; o.sum; }
};

struct Node {
    Seg tot, mx, lmx, rmx, mn, lmn, rmn;
    bool rev = false;

    void init(int idx, int val) {
        tot = mx = lmx = rmx = mn = lmn = rmn = {val, idx, idx};
        rev = false;
    }

    void apply_flip() {
        tot.sum = -tot.sum;
        swap(mx, mn); mx.sum = -mx.sum; mn.sum = -mn.sum;
        swap(lmx, lmn); lmx.sum = -lmx.sum; lmn.sum = -lmn.sum;
        swap(rmx, rmn); rmx.sum = -rmx.sum; rmn.sum = -rmn.sum;
        rev ^= 1;
    }
};

Node merge_nodes(const Node& A, const Node& B) {
    Node C;
    C.tot = A.tot + B.tot;
    C.lmx = max(A.lmx, A.tot + B.lmx);
    C.rmx = max(B.rmx, A.rmx + B.tot);
    C.mx = max({A.mx, B.mx, A.rmx + B.lmx});
    C.lmn = min(A.lmn, A.tot + B.lmn);
    C.rmn = min(B.rmn, A.rmn + B.tot);
    C.mn = min({A.mn, B.mn, A.rmn + B.lmn});
    C.rev = false;
    return C;
}

int n, q, arr[100005];
Node tree[400005];

void push_down(int u) {
    if (tree[u].rev) {
        tree[u &lt;&lt; 1].apply_flip();
        tree[u &lt;&lt; 1 | 1].apply_flip();
        tree[u].rev = false;
    }
}

void build(int u, int l, int r) {
    if (l == r) { tree[u].init(l, arr[l]); return; }
    int mid = (l + r) &gt;&gt; 1;
    build(u &lt;&lt; 1, l, mid);
    build(u &lt;&lt; 1 | 1, mid + 1, r);
    tree[u] = merge_nodes(tree[u &lt;&lt; 1], tree[u &lt;&lt; 1 | 1]);
}

void update_point(int u, int l, int r, int p, int val) {
    if (l == r) { tree[u].init(l, val); return; }
    push_down(u);
    int mid = (l + r) &gt;&gt; 1;
    if (p &lt;= mid) update_point(u &lt;&lt; 1, l, mid, p, val);
    else update_point(u &lt;&lt; 1 | 1, mid + 1, r, p, val);
    tree[u] = merge_nodes(tree[u &lt;&lt; 1], tree[u &lt;&lt; 1 | 1]);
}

void update_flip(int u, int l, int r, int ql, int qr) {
    if (ql &lt;= l && r &lt;= qr) { tree[u].apply_flip(); return; }
    push_down(u);
    int mid = (l + r) &gt;&gt; 1;
    if (ql &lt;= mid) update_flip(u &lt;&lt; 1, l, mid, ql, qr);
    if (qr &gt; mid) update_flip(u &lt;&lt; 1 | 1, mid + 1, r, ql, qr);
    tree[u] = merge_nodes(tree[u &lt;&lt; 1], tree[u &lt;&lt; 1 | 1]);
}

Node query_range(int u, int l, int r, int ql, int qr) {
    if (ql &lt;= l && r &lt;= qr) return tree[u];
    push_down(u);
    int mid = (l + r) &gt;&gt; 1;
    if (qr &lt;= mid) return query_range(u &lt;&lt; 1, l, mid, ql, qr);
    if (ql &gt; mid) return query_range(u &lt;&lt; 1 | 1, mid + 1, r, ql, qr);
    return merge_nodes(query_range(u &lt;&lt; 1, l, mid, ql, qr),
                       query_range(u &lt;&lt; 1 | 1, mid + 1, r, ql, qr));
}

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n)) return 0;
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; arr[i];
    build(1, 1, n);
    cin &gt;&gt; q;
    while (q--) {
        int type;
        cin &gt;&gt; type;
        if (type == 0) {
            int p, v;
            cin &gt;&gt; p &gt;&gt; v;
            update_point(1, 1, n, p, v);
        } else {
            int l, r, k;
            cin &gt;&gt; l &gt;&gt; r &gt;&gt; k;
            vector&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt; flipped;
            int ans = 0;
            for (int step = 0; step &lt; k; ++step) {
                Node res = query_range(1, 1, n, l, r);
                if (res.mx.sum &lt;= 0) break;
                ans += res.mx.sum;
                flipped.push_back({res.mx.l, res.mx.r});
                update_flip(1, 1, n, res.mx.l, res.mx.r);
            }
            for (auto [fl, fr] : flipped) update_flip(1, 1, n, fl, fr);
            cout &lt;&lt; ans &lt;&lt; "\n";
        }
    }
    return 0;
}







#179 (Intermediate 1) — [CF 802O] April Fools' Problem (hard) & Archive Companion [Luogu P4331 / BalticOI 2004] Sequence

Canonical Slot: Ch 23 — Intermediate 1 (Int-1) | Wave Provenance: 4B-P08 & W6-11
Judge URLs:

Primary (WQS + Prefix Regret Heap): https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/802/O
Archive Companion (Isotonic Sequence Reconstruction): https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P4331



Constraints & Problem Statements

CF 802O: Given \(a_1 \dots a_N\) and \(b_1 \dots b_N\) (\(N \le 5 \times 10^5\), \(1 \le a_i, b_j \le 10^9\)), choose \(K\) disjoint pairs \((i_r, j_r)\) with \(i_r \le j_r\) minimizing \(\sum_{r=1}^K (a_{i_r} + b_{j_r})\).
Luogu P4331 [BalticOI 2004] Sequence: Given \(a_1 \dots a_N\) (\(N \le 10^6\)), find a strictly increasing integer sequence \(b_1 &lt; b_2 &lt; \dots &lt; b_N\) minimizing \(\sum_{i=1}^N |b_i - a_i|\), and print both the minimum cost and the optimal sequence \(b_1 \dots b_N\)!



4-Step Synthesis

For CF 802O: As derived in Section 23.2, WQS binary search on \(\lambda \in [0, 2 \times 10^9]\) eliminates the global cardinality constraint \(K\), and the 2-type min-heap (delta_cnt = 1 for \(a_i\), delta_cnt = 0 for regret key \(-(b_{j_0} - \lambda)\)) simulates all augmenting paths in \(O(N \log N)\) per binary search step.
For Luogu P4331 (How to Reconstruct \(b_1 \dots b_N\) from the Heap in 2 Lines!): Traditionally, Chinese tutorials solve P4331 with a 150-line Leftist Heap implementing the Pool-Adjacent-Violators (PAV) median-merging algorithm. You do not need a Leftist Heap! In the standard std::priority_queue Slope Trick loop from #177, after step \(i\) (where we pushed \(a'_i = a_i - i\) twice and popped \(\max(H)\) once), the rightmost breakpoint \(v_i = \max(H)\) is the exact point where the prefix DP function \(f_i(x)\) transitions from slope \(0\) to flattened prefix-min! Therefore:

At \(i = N\), \(b'_N = v_N\) minimizes \(f_N(x)\).
Moving backward \(i = N-1, N-2, \dots, 1\), since \(f_{i}(x) = g_{i-1}(\min(x, v_{i-1})) + |x - a'_i|\), once \(b'_{i+1}\) is fixed, the optimal predecessor subject to \(b'_i \le b'_{i+1}\) is simply: \[
b'_i = \min(b'_{i+1},\; v_i)!
\] Finally, add back the index shift \(b_i = b'_i + i\). Total code: 20 lines!








Note3-Tier Progressive Hints (#179)






Hint 1: In CF 802O, why does matching day \(j\)’s print option with a regret token -(b[j0] - lambda) leave the total number of matched pairs unchanged (delta_cnt = 0)?
Hint 2: In P4331, after processing \(a'_i = a_i - i\) at step \(i\) with the Slope Trick max-heap, what is the geometric meaning of v[i] = pq.top()?
Hint 3: v[i] is the optimal unconstrained endpoint for prefix \(1 \dots i\). A single backward sweep b[i] = min(b[i+1], v[i]) for \(i = N-1 \dots 1\) reconstructs the exact optimal non-decreasing sequence!










TipComplete C++ Solutions (CF 802O & Luogu P4331)





// Solution 1: CF 802O — April Fools' Problem (hard)
// Time: O(N log N log(max_val)), Space: O(N)
#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

struct Item {
    long long key;
    int dcnt;
    bool operator&gt;(const Item& o) const {
        if (key != o.key) return key &gt; o.key;
        return dcnt &gt; o.dcnt; // tie-break toward smaller pair count (dcnt = 0)
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, k;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; k)) return 0;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; a(n), b(n);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; a[i];
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; b[i];

    auto check = [&](long long lambda) -&gt; pair&lt;long long, int&gt; {
        priority_queue&lt;Item, vector&lt;Item&gt;, greater&lt;Item&gt;&gt; pq;
        long long cost = 0;
        int cnt = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
            pq.push({a[i], 1});
            long long c = b[i] - lambda;
            if (pq.top().key + c &lt; 0) {
                auto top = pq.top();
                pq.pop();
                cost += top.key + c;
                cnt += top.dcnt;
                pq.push({-c, 0});
            }
        }
        return {cost, cnt};
    };

    long long lo = 0, hi = 2000000005LL, best_lam = 0;
    while (lo &lt;= hi) {
        long long mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (check(mid).second &lt;= k) {
            best_lam = mid;
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            hi = mid - 1;
        }
    }
    auto [pen_cost, _] = check(best_lam);
    cout &lt;&lt; pen_cost + 1LL * k * best_lam &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}
// Solution 2: Luogu P4331 — [BalticOI 2004] Sequence (Slope Trick + 2-Line Reconstruction)
// Time: O(N log N), Space: O(N)
