6.1 Problem #145 (SS-1): [Luogu P3358] 最长k可重区间集问题 (\(K\)-Overlapping Intervals)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P3358
Difficulty Tier: Stepping Stone (SS-1)
Constraints: \(1 \le n \le 500\) open intervals \(I_j = (L_j, R_j)\), \(1 \le K \le 3\), \(0 \le L_j &lt; R_j \le 10^5\). Weight of interval \(j\) is its length \(w_j = R_j - L_j\). Maximize the total length of selected intervals such that no real point \(x \in \mathbb{R}\) is covered by more than \(K\) selected intervals.


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal

Open intervals \((L_j, R_j)\) with weight \(w_j = R_j - L_j\) and point overlap cap \(K\). Since \(K &gt; 1\), greedy fails, and since \(L_j, R_j \le 10^5\) with \(n \le 500\), coordinate compression reduces the active coordinates to \(V \le 2n \le 1000\) vertices.



Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation

Compress the distinct endpoints \(\{L_j, R_j\}\) into \(1 &lt; 2 &lt; \dots &lt; M\) (\(M \le 2n\)).
For each elementary half-open segment \([i, i+1)\) (\(i = 1, \dots, M-1\)) and unused capacity slack \(y_i \ge 0\):
\[
E_i : \quad \sum_{j \,:\, l_j \le i &lt; r_j} x_j + y_i = K \qquad (i = 1, \dots, M-1), \qquad \min \sum_{j=1}^n (-w_j) x_j.
\]



Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Compilation)

Taking \(\Delta E_i = E_i - E_{i-1}\) (\(i = 1, \dots, M\) with \(E_0 = E_M = 0\)):

Source \(S \xrightarrow{\text{cap } K, \text{cost } 0} 1\) (since \(b_1 = +K\)) and \(M \xrightarrow{\text{cap } K, \text{cost } 0} T\) (since \(b_M = -K\)).
For each slack \(y_i\) (\(1 \le i &lt; M\)): forward backbone edge \(i \xrightarrow{\text{cap } \infty, \text{cost } 0} i + 1\).
For each interval \(j\) (\(1 \le j \le n\)): forward bypass edge \(l_j \xrightarrow{\text{cap } 1, \text{cost } -w_j} r_j\).

Because all initial edges go from smaller index to larger index (\(u &lt; v\)), we initialize \(\pi_0\) in \(O(M + n)\) time via topological DAG DP and run \(K\) steps of Dijkstra SSP.



Step 4: Toy Trace & Boundary Verification

Suppose \(K = 1\) and two intervals are \((1, 3)\) (weight \(2\)) and \((3, 5)\) (weight \(2\)). Compressed coordinates: \(1 \mapsto 1, 3 \mapsto 2, 5 \mapsto 3\). Arcs: \(1 \to 2\) (cost \(-2\)) and \(2 \to 3\) (cost \(-2\)). Because the intervals are open, they touch at vertex \(2\) (\(x = 3\)) and a single unit of flow traverses \(1 \to 2 \to 3\) for total weight \(4\).







NoteProgressive Hints for Luogu P3358 (#145)






Hint 1 (Coordinate Compression & Touching Endpoints): Because the intervals are open \((L_j, R_j)\), two intervals \((1, 3)\) and \((3, 5)\) can both be chosen by the same track of flow. Coordinate-compress all \(L_j\) and \(R_j\) values directly without shifting \(R_j\).
Hint 2 (Algebraic-to-Graph Compilation): Write \(E_i : \sum_{l_j \le i &lt; r_j} x_j + y_i = K\) for each elementary segment \([i, i+1)\). Subtracting \(E_i - E_{i-1}\) gives \(l_j \to r_j\) (cap \(1\), cost \(-(R_j - L_j)\)) and backbone \(i \to i+1\) (cap \(K\), cost \(0\)).
Hint 3 (Anti-SPFA Potential Initialization): Since every edge satisfies \(u &lt; v\), compute \(\pi_0[v] = \min(\pi_0[v-1], \min_{r_j = v} (\pi_0[l_j] - w_j))\) in a single loop for \(v = 1 \dots M\), then run \(K\) steps of Dijkstra.










NoteComplete C++ Solution — Luogu P3358 (#145)





// Luogu P3358 — 最长k可重区间集问题 (#145)
// Method: Equation Differencing + O(N+M) DAG DP Potential Initialization + K-step Dijkstra MCMF
#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 1e18;

struct Edge {
    int to, rev, cap;
    long long cost;
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, K;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; K)) return 0;

    vector&lt;long long&gt; L(n), R(n), W(n), coords;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        cin &gt;&gt; L[i] &gt;&gt; R[i];
        if (L[i] &gt; R[i]) swap(L[i], R[i]);
        W[i] = R[i] - L[i];
        coords.push_back(L[i]);
        coords.push_back(R[i]);
    }

    sort(coords.begin(), coords.end());
    coords.erase(unique(coords.begin(), coords.end()), coords.end());
    int M = (int)coords.size();

    // Vertices: 0 = S, 1..M = compressed coordinates, M+1 = T
    int S = 0, T = M + 1, V = M + 2;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; adj(V);
    auto add_edge = [&](int u, int v, int cap, long long cost) {
        adj[u].push_back({v, (int)adj[v].size(), cap, cost});
        adj[v].push_back({u, (int)adj[u].size() - 1, 0, -cost});
    };

    // Source and Sink supplies/demands from b_1 = +K, b_M = -K
    add_edge(S, 1, K, 0);
    add_edge(M, T, K, 0);

    // Forward slack backbone arcs y_i: i -&gt; i + 1
    for (int i = 1; i &lt; M; ++i) {
        add_edge(i, i + 1, K, 0);
    }

    // Interval bypass arcs x_j: l_j -&gt; r_j
    vector&lt;long long&gt; pi(V, INF);
    pi[S] = 0;
    pi[1] = 0;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;pair&lt;int, long long&gt;&gt;&gt; dag_edges(M + 1);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        int l = (int)(lower_bound(coords.begin(), coords.end(), L[i]) - coords.begin()) + 1;
        int r = (int)(lower_bound(coords.begin(), coords.end(), R[i]) - coords.begin()) + 1;
        add_edge(l, r, 1, -W[i]);
        dag_edges[l].push_back({r, -W[i]});
    }

    // O(N + M) Topological DAG DP to initialize Johnson potentials pi[]
    for (int u = 1; u &lt;= M; ++u) {
        if (u &lt; M) pi[u + 1] = min(pi[u + 1], pi[u]);
        for (auto [v, c] : dag_edges[u]) {
            pi[v] = min(pi[v], pi[u] + c);
        }
    }
    pi[T] = pi[M];

    // Run at most K iterations of Dijkstra SSP
    long long min_cost = 0;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist(V);
    vector&lt;int&gt; pv(V), pe(V);

    while (true) {
        fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
        priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
        dist[S] = 0;
        pq.push({0, S});

        while (!pq.empty()) {
            auto [d, u] = pq.top();
            pq.pop();
            if (d != dist[u]) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)adj[u].size(); ++i) {
                const auto& e = adj[u][i];
                if (e.cap &gt; 0) {
                    long long nd = d + e.cost + pi[u] - pi[e.to];
                    if (nd &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = nd;
                        pv[e.to] = u;
                        pe[e.to] = i;
                        pq.push({nd, e.to});
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        if (dist[T] == INF) break;
        for (int v = 0; v &lt; V; ++v) {
            if (dist[v] &lt; INF) pi[v] += dist[v];
        }
        // If shortest path has &gt;= 0 true cost, we still push because S-&gt;1-&gt;...-&gt;M-&gt;T has 0 cost slack
        int pushed = K;
        for (int v = T; v != S; v = pv[v]) {
            pushed = min(pushed, adj[pv[v]][pe[v]].cap);
        }
        for (int v = T; v != S; v = pv[v]) {
            auto& e = adj[pv[v]][pe[v]];
            e.cap -= pushed;
            adj[v][e.rev].cap += pushed;
        }
        min_cost += 1LL * pushed * pi[T];
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; -min_cost &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.2 Problem #146 (SS-2): [POJ 3680] Intervals & [CF 164C] Machine Programming

Judge URL: http://poj.org/problem?id=3680 / https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/164/C
Difficulty Tier: Stepping Stone (SS-2)
Constraints:

POJ 3680: \(T_{\text{cases}} \le 200\), \(N \le 200\) open intervals \((a_j, b_j)\) with arbitrary positive weights \(w_j \le 10^5\), \(K \le 10^5\) (\(a_j &lt; b_j \le 10^5\)). Maximize total weight of selected intervals with point overlap \(\le K\).
CF 164C: \(N \le 1000\) tasks \([s_j, s_j + t_j)\) with arbitrary profit \(c_j \le 10^9\), \(K \le 50\) machines. Output both the maximum profit and the binary selection vector \(x_j \in \{0, 1\}\).



Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal

Identical mathematical structure to #145, except the interval profits \(w_j\) are arbitrary (decoupled from interval length \(b_j - a_j\)), and in CF 164C we must also reconstruct the exact binary indicators \(x_j \in \{0, 1\}\) from the residual capacities of the bypass edges \(e_{x_j} = (l_j \to r_j)\).



Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation

After coordinate-compressing the \(2N\) endpoints into \(1 &lt; 2 &lt; \dots &lt; M\), each interval \(j\) covers elementary segments \(i \in \{l_j, \dots, r_j - 1\}\).
Introducing slack \(y_i \ge 0\) for each segment \(i \in \{1, \dots, M-1\}\) gives \(E_i : \sum_{l_j \le i &lt; r_j} x_j + y_i = K\), with objective \(\min \sum_{j=1}^N (-w_j) x_j\).



Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad

Taking \(\Delta E_i = E_i - E_{i-1}\) produces:

\(S \xrightarrow{\text{cap } K, \text{cost } 0} 1\) and \(M \xrightarrow{\text{cap } K, \text{cost } 0} T\).
Slack arcs \(i \xrightarrow{\text{cap } K, \text{cost } 0} i + 1\) for \(1 \le i &lt; M\).
Decision arcs \(l_j \xrightarrow{\text{cap } 1, \text{cost } -w_j} r_j\) for \(1 \le j \le N\).

Choice Reconstruction: After the MCMF terminates, interval \(j\) is selected (\(x_j = 1\)) if and only if the residual capacity of its forward bypass edge \(l_j \to r_j\) is \(0\) (equivalently, its reverse edge has residual capacity \(1\)).







NoteProgressive Hints for POJ 3680 / CF 164C (#146)






Hint 1: For half-open task intervals \([s_j, s_j + t_j)\) in CF 164C, a task ending at time \(s_a + t_a\) frees its machine immediately for another task starting at \(s_b = s_a + t_a\). So compress \(L_j = s_j\) and \(R_j = s_j + t_j\) directly.
Hint 2: Store the adjacency-list index of each task’s forward edge \((l_j \to r_j)\) when building the graph so you can read off \(x_j = 1 - \text{cap}(e_{x_j})\) in \(O(1)\) per task after MCMF finishes.










NoteComplete C++ Solution — POJ 3680 / CF 164C (#146)





// CF 164C (Machine Programming) & POJ 3680 (Intervals) (#146)
// Method: Equation Differencing + DAG DP Potentials + Dijkstra MCMF + Decision Reconstruction
#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 1e18;

struct Edge {
    int to, rev, cap;
    long long cost;
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, K;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; K)) return 0;

    vector&lt;long long&gt; L(n), R(n), W(n), coords;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        long long s, t, c;
        cin &gt;&gt; s &gt;&gt; t &gt;&gt; c; // For POJ 3680, read L[i] &gt;&gt; R[i] &gt;&gt; W[i] directly
        L[i] = s;
        R[i] = s + t;
        W[i] = c;
        coords.push_back(L[i]);
        coords.push_back(R[i]);
    }

    sort(coords.begin(), coords.end());
    coords.erase(unique(coords.begin(), coords.end()), coords.end());
    int M = (int)coords.size();

    int S = 0, T = M + 1, V = M + 2;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; adj(V);
    auto add_edge = [&](int u, int v, int cap, long long cost) -&gt; pair&lt;int, int&gt; {
        int idx = (int)adj[u].size();
        adj[u].push_back({v, (int)adj[v].size(), cap, cost});
        adj[v].push_back({u, idx, 0, -cost});
        return {u, idx};
    };

    add_edge(S, 1, K, 0);
    add_edge(M, T, K, 0);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt; M; ++i) {
        add_edge(i, i + 1, K, 0);
    }

    vector&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt; task_edge(n);
    vector&lt;vector&lt;pair&lt;int, long long&gt;&gt;&gt; dag_edges(M + 1);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        int l = (int)(lower_bound(coords.begin(), coords.end(), L[i]) - coords.begin()) + 1;
        int r = (int)(lower_bound(coords.begin(), coords.end(), R[i]) - coords.begin()) + 1;
        task_edge[i] = add_edge(l, r, 1, -W[i]);
        dag_edges[l].push_back({r, -W[i]});
    }

    vector&lt;long long&gt; pi(V, INF);
    pi[S] = pi[1] = 0;
    for (int u = 1; u &lt;= M; ++u) {
        if (u &lt; M) pi[u + 1] = min(pi[u + 1], pi[u]);
        for (auto [v, c] : dag_edges[u]) {
            pi[v] = min(pi[v], pi[u] + c);
        }
    }
    pi[T] = pi[M];

    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist(V);
    vector&lt;int&gt; pv(V), pe(V);
    while (true) {
        fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
        priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
        dist[S] = 0;
        pq.push({0, S});
        while (!pq.empty()) {
            auto [d, u] = pq.top();
            pq.pop();
            if (d != dist[u]) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)adj[u].size(); ++i) {
                const auto& e = adj[u][i];
                if (e.cap &gt; 0) {
                    long long nd = d + e.cost + pi[u] - pi[e.to];
                    if (nd &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = nd;
                        pv[e.to] = u;
                        pe[e.to] = i;
                        pq.push({nd, e.to});
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if (dist[T] == INF) break;
        for (int v = 0; v &lt; V; ++v) {
            if (dist[v] &lt; INF) pi[v] += dist[v];
        }
        int pushed = K;
        for (int v = T; v != S; v = pv[v]) {
            pushed = min(pushed, adj[pv[v]][pe[v]].cap);
        }
        for (int v = T; v != S; v = pv[v]) {
            auto& e = adj[pv[v]][pe[v]];
            e.cap -= pushed;
            adj[v][e.rev].cap += pushed;
        }
    }

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        auto [u, idx] = task_edge[i];
        int chosen = (adj[u][idx].cap == 0) ? 1 : 0;
        cout &lt;&lt; chosen &lt;&lt; (i + 1 == n ? '\n' : ' ');
    }
    return 0;
}







6.3 Problem #147 (Int-1): [Luogu P3357] 最长k可重线段集问题 (\(K\)-Overlapping 2D Segments)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P3357
Difficulty Tier: Intermediate (Int-1)
Constraints: \(1 \le n \le 500\) open 2D segments \(((x_{j,1}, y_{j,1}), (x_{j,2}, y_{j,2}))\), \(1 \le K \le 10\), integer coordinates \(|x|, |y| \le 10^5\). Weight of segment \(j\) is \(w_j = \lfloor \sqrt{(x_{j,1} - x_{j,2})^2 + (y_{j,1} - y_{j,2})^2} \rfloor\). No vertical line \(x = x_0\) (\(x_0 \in \mathbb{R}\)) may intersect more than \(K\) selected segments.


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal

The vertical-line intersection constraint depends only on \(x\)-coordinates, while \(y\)-coordinates only determine \(w_j\). However, vertical segments (\(x_{j,1} = x_{j,2}\)) have positive weight \(w_j = |y_{j,1} - y_{j,2}| &gt; 0\) and project to single points \([x, x]\) on the \(x\)-axis!



Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation (Coordinate Doubling \(x \mapsto (2x, 2x+1)\))

Apply the Coordinate Doubling Compiler from Section 4.2:

Replace every endpoint \(x\)-coordinate by its doubled pair \((2x, 2x+1)\).
If segment \(j\) is vertical (\(x_1 = x_2 = x\)), it covers the point-slice \([2x, 2x + 1)\).
If segment \(j\) is non-vertical (\(x_1 &lt; x_2\), open endpoints under P3357 convention), it covers the open interval \((x_1, x_2)\), which corresponds to the half-open doubled interval \([2x_1 + 1, 2x_2)\).




Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad

After transforming each segment \(j\) to its doubled half-open interval \([L'_j, R'_j)\) (where \(L'_j &lt; R'_j\) is now strictly guaranteed even for vertical segments!), coordinate-compress the \(2n\) doubled endpoints \(\{L'_j, R'_j\}\) into \(1 &lt; 2 &lt; \dots &lt; M\) and compile via Equation Differencing exactly as in #145.







NoteProgressive Hints for Luogu P3357 (#147)






Hint 1: Ensure \(x_{j,1} \le x_{j,2}\) by swapping the two endpoints if \(x_{j,1} &gt; x_{j,2}\) before inspecting verticality.
Hint 2: Map vertical segments (\(x_1 = x_2\)) to \([2x_1, 2x_1 + 1)\) and non-vertical open segments (\(x_1 &lt; x_2\)) to \([2x_1 + 1, 2x_2)\). Notice that \(L'_j &lt; R'_j\) always holds, eliminating all self-loops!










NoteComplete C++ Solution — Luogu P3357 (#147)





