6.7 Problem #151 (Adv-2): [Luogu P6967 / ICPC NEERC 2016 D] Delight for a Cat

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P6967 / https://codeforces.com/gym/101190
Difficulty Tier: Advanced (Adv-2)
Constraints: \(1 \le k \le n \le 1000\), \(0 \le m_s, m_e \le k\) with \(m_s + m_e \le k\). Arrays \(s_1, \dots, s_n\) and \(e_1, \dots, e_n\) (\(0 \le s_j, e_j \le 10^9\)). In every sliding window of length \(k\), at least \(m_s\) hours must be S (sleep) and at least \(m_e\) hours must be E (eat). Output the maximum total happiness and the length-\(n\) string of S/E choices.


Summary of the 4-Step Compilation (Derived in Full in Section 2.2)

Baseline & Decision Variable: Start with all hours sleeping (\(S_{\text{base}} = \sum_{j=1}^n s_j\)). Let \(x_j \in \{0, 1\}\) indicate switching hour \(j\) to eating (E), with cost \(c_j = s_j - e_j\).
Two-Sided Window Inequality: For each window \(i \in \{1, \dots, W\}\) (\(W = n - k + 1\)), \(L \le \sum_{j : l_j \le i \le r_j} x_j \le R\), where \(L = m_e\), \(R = k - m_s\), \(l_j = \max(1, j - k + 1)\), and \(r_j = \min(W, j)\).
Variant A (Upper-Slack Forward-DAG Compilation):

Introduce bounded slack \(y_i \in [0, R - L]\) so \(\sum_{j : l_j \le i \le r_j} x_j + y_i = R\).
Taking \(\Delta E_i = E_i - E_{i-1}\) (\(i = 1, \dots, W + 1\)) produces a pure topological DAG on vertices \(1, \dots, W + 1\):

Supply \(S \xrightarrow{\text{cap } R, \text{cost } 0} 1\) and demand \(W + 1 \xrightarrow{\text{cap } R, \text{cost } 0} T\).
Bounded slack spine \(i \xrightarrow{\text{cap } R - L, \text{cost } 0} i + 1\) for \(i = 1, \dots, W\).
Decision arc \(l_j \xrightarrow{\text{cap } 1, \text{cost } s_j - e_j} r_j + 1\) for \(j = 1, \dots, n\).


Why Variant A Beats SPFA: Because every edge \((u \to v)\) has \(u &lt; v\) and \(R = k - m_s\) is the exact required flow from \(S\) to \(T\) (every feasible schedule corresponds to a flow of value \(R\)), we initialize \(\pi_0\) in \(O(n + W)\) via DAG DP and run Primal-Dual Dijkstra until \(R\) units of flow reach \(T\)!







NoteProgressive Hints for NEERC 2016 Delight for a Cat (#151)






Hint 1: Notice that the upper-slack variable \(y_i = R - \sum_{j=i}^{i+k-1} x_j\) must satisfy \(0 \le y_i \le R - L = (k - m_s) - m_e\). If you forget the upper capacity \(R - L\) on the slack edge \(i \to i + 1\), the lower bound \(\sum x_j \ge m_e\) will be violated!
Hint 2: Unlike #145 where positive-cost paths can bypass \(T\), here we must push all \(R = k - m_s\) units of flow from \(S\) to \(T\) even if a shortest path has positive cost \(s_j - e_j &gt; 0\), because the cat is forced to eat at least \(m_e\) hours per window (and the slack arcs \(i \to i + 1\) only have capacity \(R - L\), forcing at least \(L = m_e\) units onto the \(x_j\) arcs!).










NoteComplete C++ Solution — Luogu P6967 [NEERC 2016] Delight for a Cat (#151)





// Luogu P6967 [ICPC NEERC 2016 D] — Delight for a Cat (#151)
// Method: Two-Sided Sliding Window Upper-Slack Forward-DAG + DAG DP Potentials + Primal-Dual Dijkstra
#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 4e18;

struct Edge {
    int to, rev, cap;
    long long cost;
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, k, ms, me;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; k &gt;&gt; ms &gt;&gt; me)) return 0;

    vector&lt;long long&gt; s(n + 1), e(n + 1);
    long long base_sum = 0;
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= n; ++j) {
        cin &gt;&gt; s[j];
        base_sum += s[j];
    }
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= n; ++j) {
        cin &gt;&gt; e[j];
    }

    int W = n - k + 1;
    int L_bound = me, R_bound = k - ms;
    int S = 0, T = W + 2, V = W + 3;

    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; adj(V);
    auto add_edge = [&](int u, int v, int cap, long long cost) -&gt; pair&lt;int, int&gt; {
        int idx = (int)adj[u].size();
        adj[u].push_back({v, (int)adj[v].size(), cap, cost});
        adj[v].push_back({u, idx, 0, -cost});
        return {u, idx};
    };

    // Total required flow is R_bound = k - ms
    add_edge(S, 1, R_bound, 0);
    add_edge(W + 1, T, R_bound, 0);

    // Bounded forward slack arcs y_i in [0, R_bound - L_bound]: i -&gt; i + 1
    vector&lt;vector&lt;pair&lt;int, long long&gt;&gt;&gt; dag_edges(W + 2);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= W; ++i) {
        if (R_bound - L_bound &gt; 0) {
            add_edge(i, i + 1, R_bound - L_bound, 0);
            dag_edges[i].push_back({i + 1, 0});
        }
    }

    // Hour decision arcs x_j: l_j -&gt; r_j + 1 with cap 1, cost s_j - e_j
    vector&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt; hour_edge(n + 1);
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= n; ++j) {
        int l = max(1, j - k + 1);
        int r = min(W, j);
        long long cost = s[j] - e[j];
        hour_edge[j] = add_edge(l, r + 1, 1, cost);
        dag_edges[l].push_back({r + 1, cost});
    }

    // O(n + W) Topological DAG DP for initial Johnson potentials pi[]
    vector&lt;long long&gt; pi(V, INF);
    pi[S] = pi[1] = 0;
    for (int u = 1; u &lt;= W + 1; ++u) {
        if (pi[u] == INF) continue;
        for (auto [v, c] : dag_edges[u]) {
            pi[v] = min(pi[v], pi[u] + c);
        }
    }
    pi[T] = pi[W + 1];

    long long min_cost = 0;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist(V);
    vector&lt;int&gt; cur(V);
    vector&lt;bool&gt; vis(V);

    auto dijkstra = [&]() -&gt; bool {
        fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
        priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
        dist[S] = 0;
        pq.push({0, S});
        while (!pq.empty()) {
            auto [d, u] = pq.top();
            pq.pop();
            if (d != dist[u]) continue;
            for (const auto& ed : adj[u]) {
                if (ed.cap &gt; 0) {
                    long long nd = d + ed.cost + pi[u] - pi[ed.to];
                    if (nd &lt; dist[ed.to]) {
                        dist[ed.to] = nd;
                        pq.push({nd, ed.to});
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if (dist[T] == INF) return false;
        for (int v = 0; v &lt; V; ++v) {
            if (dist[v] &lt; INF) pi[v] += dist[v];
        }
        return true;
    };

    auto dfs = [&](auto& self, int u, int pushed) -&gt; int {
        if (u == T || pushed == 0) return pushed;
        vis[u] = true;
        int total = 0;
        for (int& i = cur[u]; i &lt; (int)adj[u].size(); ++i) {
            auto& ed = adj[u][i];
            if (!vis[ed.to] && ed.cap &gt; 0 && pi[u] + ed.cost - pi[ed.to] == 0) {
                int tr = self(self, ed.to, min(pushed, ed.cap));
                if (tr &gt; 0) {
                    ed.cap -= tr;
                    adj[ed.to][ed.rev].cap += tr;
                    pushed -= tr;
                    total += tr;
                    min_cost += 1LL * tr * ed.cost;
                    if (pushed == 0) break;
                }
            }
        }
        vis[u] = false;
        return total;
    };

    while (dijkstra()) {
        fill(cur.begin(), cur.end(), 0);
        fill(vis.begin(), vis.end(), false);
        while (dfs(dfs, S, R_bound) &gt; 0) {}
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; base_sum - min_cost &lt;&lt; "\n";
    string ans(n, 'S');
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= n; ++j) {
        auto [u, idx] = hour_edge[j];
        if (adj[u][idx].cap == 0) ans[j - 1] = 'E';
    }
    cout &lt;&lt; ans &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.8 Problem #152 (Mas-1): [BZOJ 3550 / ONTAK 2010] Vacation & [LOJ #6405] Rooted Tree Equation Differencing (树上流守恒差分)
We conclude the ladder with two Mastery problems that push Equation Differencing beyond a single 1D consecutive-ones system: multi-scale coupled sliding windows ([ONTAK 2010 / BZOJ 3550] Vacation) and rooted tree ancestor-chain differencing ([LOJ #6405] 无源汇有上下界最小流 / 树上流守恒差分).

Judge URLs: https://hydro.ac/d/bzoj/p/3550 (BZOJ 3550) / https://loj.ac/p/6405 (LOJ #6405)
Difficulty Tier: Mastery (Mas-1)
Constraints:

BZOJ 3550 [ONTAK 2010] Vacation: \(3K\) days (\(K \le 200\), so \(N = 3K \le 600\)) with profits \(c_1, \dots, c_{3K}\). Maximize \(\sum_{j=1}^{3K} c_j x_j\) (\(x_j \in \{0, 1\}\)) such that:

Every contiguous window of length \(K\) has at most \(L_1\) selected days (\(\sum_{j=i}^{i+K-1} x_j \le L_1\) for \(i = 1, \dots, 2K + 1\)).
The entire \(3K\)-day horizon \([1, 3K]\) has at most \(L_2\) selected days (\(\sum_{j=1}^{3K} x_j \le L_2\), or equivalently every window of length \(2K\) / \(3K\) is bounded).

LOJ #6405: A rooted tree \(T\) with \(n \le 5000\) vertices where each tree edge \(e = (\text{parent}(u), u)\) has a lower bound \(A_u\) on flow, and \(m\) upward back-edges \(q_j \to p_j\) (\(p_j\) ancestor of \(q_j\)) have cost \(c_j\). Find the minimum-cost circulation (or minimum-cost vertical chain cover).



Part A: 4-Step Compilation of BZOJ 3550 [ONTAK 2010] Vacation

Diagnostic Signal: We are given \(N = 3K\) vacation days with profits \(c_1, \dots, c_{3K}\) and must choose \(x_j \in \{0, 1\}\) to maximize \(\sum_{j=1}^{3K} c_j x_j\) subject to the sliding-window upper bound \(\sum_{j=i}^{i+K-1} x_j \le L\) for every contiguous \(K\)-day window \(i = 1, 2, \dots, 2K + 1\).
Intermediate Algebraic System:

Let \(W = 2K + 1\) be the number of sliding windows of length \(K\). Introducing unused-capacity slack variables \(y_i \ge 0\) for each window \(i \in \{1, \dots, 2K + 1\}\) yields:
\[
E_i : \quad \sum_{j=i}^{i+K-1} x_j + y_i = L \qquad (i = 1, 2, \dots, 2K + 1), \qquad E_0 : 0 = 0, \quad E_{2K+2} : 0 = 0.
\]
Notice how the \(3K\) decision variables partition cleanly into three consecutive blocks of length \(K\):

Period 1 (\(1 \le j \le K\)): \(x_j\) appears in windows \(E_1, \dots, E_j\) (entering at \(\Delta E_1\) and exiting at \(\Delta E_{j+1}\)).
Period 2 (\(K + 1 \le j \le 2K\)): \(x_j\) appears in windows \(E_{j-K+1}, \dots, E_j\) (entering at \(\Delta E_{j-K+1}\) and exiting at \(\Delta E_{j+1}\)).
Period 3 (\(2K + 1 \le j \le 3K\)): \(x_j\) appears in windows \(E_{j-K+1}, \dots, E_{2K+1}\) (entering at \(\Delta E_{j-K+1}\) and exiting at \(\Delta E_{2K+2}\)).


Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad:

Taking \(\Delta E_i = E_i - E_{i-1}\) for \(i = 1, \dots, 2K + 2\) produces a 3-layer topological DAG on vertices \(1, \dots, 2K + 2\):

Supply \(b_1 = +L \implies S \xrightarrow{\text{cap } L, \text{cost } 0} 1\); Demand \(b_{2K+2} = -L \implies 2K + 2 \xrightarrow{\text{cap } L, \text{cost } 0} T\).
Slack spine \(y_i\) (\(1 \le i \le 2K + 1\)): \(i \xrightarrow{\text{cap } L, \text{cost } 0} i + 1\).
Day decision \(x_j\) (\(1 \le j \le 3K\)): \(\max(1, j - K + 1) \xrightarrow{\text{cap } 1, \text{cost } -c_j} \min(2K + 1, j) + 1\).





Part B: 4-Step Compilation of LOJ #6405 (Rooted Tree Equation Differencing 树上流守恒差分)

Diagnostic Signal: Each vertex \(u \in \{2, \dots, n\}\) in a rooted tree (root \(1\)) has a coverage demand \(A_u \ge 0\) on the tree edge \((\text{parent}(u), u)\), and we are given \(m\) vertical ancestor-descendant paths \(P_j = \text{path}(p_j, q_j)\) (\(p_j\) is an ancestor of \(q_j\)) with cost \(c_j\) (plus the ability to add extra flow along any root-to-vertex or downward tree edge at cost \(d_u\)).
Intermediate Algebraic Representation:

For each node \(u \in \{2, \dots, n\}\), let \(E_u : \sum_{j : u \in \text{path}(p_j, q_j)} x_j - y_u = A_u\) (\(y_u \ge 0\)), with root equation \(E_1 : 0 = 0\).

Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Applying Theorem 19.3 \(\Delta^{\text{tree}} E_u = E_u - \sum_{v \in \text{child}(u)} E_v\)):

Nodal balance at \(u \in \{1, \dots, n\}\): \(b_u = A_u - \sum_{v \in \text{child}(u)} A_v\) (\(A_1 = 0\)).
Every vertical path variable \(x_j\) covering \(u \in \text{path}(p_j, q_j) \setminus \{p_j\}\) (all tree edges on the path from \(p_j\) down to \(q_j\)) enters at \(\Delta^{\text{tree}} E_{q_j}\) with \(+1\) and exits at \(\Delta^{\text{tree}} E_{p_j}\) with \(-1\), compiling to the upward arc \(q_j \xrightarrow{\text{cap } \infty, \text{cost } c_j} p_j\)!
Every surplus slack \(y_u\) on edge \((\text{parent}(u), u)\) compiles to the downward tree arc \(\text{parent}(u) \xrightarrow{\text{cap } \infty, \text{cost } d_u} u\)!








NoteProgressive Hints for BZOJ 3550 & LOJ #6405 (#152)






Hint 1 (BZOJ 3550): Across \(N = 3K\) days with window length \(K\), there are \(W = 2K + 1\) windows. Day \(j \in [1, 3K]\) covers windows \(l_j = \max(1, j - K + 1)\) through \(r_j = \min(2K + 1, j)\), compiling to the DAG edge \(l_j \to r_j + 1\) with capacity \(1\) and cost \(-c_j\).
Hint 2 (LOJ #6405 — Rooted Tree Differencing): Do not flatten the tree with Heavy-Light Decomposition! Simply compute \(b_u = A_u - \sum_{v \in \text{child}(u)} A_v\) at every vertex \(u \in \{1, \dots, n\}\). A vertical path covering the tree edges between ancestor \(p_j\) and descendant \(q_j\) becomes a single edge \(q_j \to p_j\), and the surplus slack on edge \((\text{parent}(u), u)\) becomes a single downward edge \(\text{parent}(u) \to u\).










NoteComplete C++ Solutions — BZOJ 3550 [ONTAK 2010] Vacation & LOJ #6405 Rooted Tree Differencing (#152)





// Solution 1: BZOJ 3550 [ONTAK 2010] — Vacation (#152A)
// Method: Consecutive Equation Differencing on 3K Horizon + DAG DP Potentials + Dijkstra MCMF
#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 1e18;

struct Edge {
    int to, rev, cap;
    long long cost;
};

void solve_bzoj3550() {
    int K, L;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; K &gt;&gt; L)) return;
    int n = 3 * K;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; c(n + 1);
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= n; ++j) cin &gt;&gt; c[j];

    int W = 2 * K + 1;
    int S = 0, T = W + 2, V = W + 3;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; adj(V);
    auto add_edge = [&](int u, int v, int cap, long long cost) {
        adj[u].push_back({v, (int)adj[v].size(), cap, cost});
        adj[v].push_back({u, (int)adj[u].size() - 1, 0, -cost});
    };

    add_edge(S, 1, L, 0);
    add_edge(W + 1, T, L, 0);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= W; ++i) {
        add_edge(i, i + 1, L, 0);
    }

    vector&lt;vector&lt;pair&lt;int, long long&gt;&gt;&gt; dag_edges(W + 2);
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= n; ++j) {
        int l = max(1, j - K + 1);
        int r = min(W, j);
        add_edge(l, r + 1, 1, -c[j]);
        dag_edges[l].push_back({r + 1, -c[j]});
    }

    vector&lt;long long&gt; pi(V, INF);
    pi[S] = pi[1] = 0;
    for (int u = 1; u &lt;= W + 1; ++u) {
        if (u &lt;= W) pi[u + 1] = min(pi[u + 1], pi[u]);
        for (auto [v, cost] : dag_edges[u]) {
            pi[v] = min(pi[v], pi[u] + cost);
        }
    }
    pi[T] = pi[W + 1];

    long long min_cost = 0;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist(V);
    vector&lt;int&gt; pv(V), pe(V);
    while (true) {
        fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
        priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
        dist[S] = 0;
        pq.push({0, S});
        while (!pq.empty()) {
            auto [d, u] = pq.top();
            pq.pop();
            if (d != dist[u]) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)adj[u].size(); ++i) {
                const auto& e = adj[u][i];
                if (e.cap &gt; 0) {
                    long long nd = d + e.cost + pi[u] - pi[e.to];
                    if (nd &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = nd;
                        pv[e.to] = u;
                        pe[e.to] = i;
                        pq.push({nd, e.to});
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if (dist[T] == INF) break;
        for (int v = 0; v &lt; V; ++v) {
            if (dist[v] &lt; INF) pi[v] += dist[v];
        }
        int pushed = L;
        for (int v = T; v != S; v = pv[v]) {
            pushed = min(pushed, adj[pv[v]][pe[v]].cap);
        }
        for (int v = T; v != S; v = pv[v]) {
            auto& e = adj[pv[v]][pe[v]];
            e.cap -= pushed;
            adj[v][e.rev].cap += pushed;
        }
        min_cost += 1LL * pushed * pi[T];
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; -min_cost &lt;&lt; "\n";
}

// Solution 2: Rooted Tree Equation Differencing (\Delta^{tree} E_u = E_u - \sum_{v \in child(u)} E_v) (#152B)
// Compiles vertical ancestor-descendant path covering on a rooted tree into O(N + M) MCMF without HLD.
long long solve_tree_volunteer_recruitment(
    int n,
    const vector&lt;int&gt;& parent,          // parent[u] for u = 2..n (root is 1, parent[1] = 0)
    const vector&lt;long long&gt;& A,         // A[u] = minimum coverage required at node u (u = 1..n)
    const vector&lt;tuple&lt;int, int, long long&gt;&gt;& chains // (p_j, q_j, cost_j) covering path(p_j, q_j)
) {
    int S = n + 1, T = n + 2, V = n + 3; // Node 0 is virtual super-root parent(1)
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; adj(V);
    auto add_edge = [&](int u, int v, int cap, long long cost) {
        adj[u].push_back({v, (int)adj[v].size(), cap, cost});
        adj[v].push_back({u, (int)adj[u].size() - 1, 0, -cost});
    };

    // 1. Compute tree-differenced nodal balance b_u = A_u - \sum_{v \in child(u)} A_v
    vector&lt;long long&gt; b(n + 1, 0);
    for (int u = 1; u &lt;= n; ++u) {
        b[u] += A[u];
        b[parent[u]] -= A[u];
    }
    for (int u = 0; u &lt;= n; ++u) {
        if (b[u] &gt; 0) add_edge(S, u, (int)b[u], 0);
        else if (b[u] &lt; 0) add_edge(u, T, (int)(-b[u]), 0);
    }

    // 2. Downward surplus slack arcs y_u: parent(u) -&gt; u with cap INF, cost 0
    const int INF_CAP = 1e9;
    for (int u = 1; u &lt;= n; ++u) {
        add_edge(parent[u], u, INF_CAP, 0);
    }

    // 3. Vertical chain arcs x_j: q_j -&gt; parent(p_j) with cap INF, cost c_j
    for (auto [p, q, cost] : chains) {
        add_edge(q, parent[p], INF_CAP, cost);
    }

    // All initial costs are &gt;= 0, so pi_0 == 0 is valid for Primal-Dual Dijkstra!
    vector&lt;long long&gt; pi(V, 0), dist(V);
    vector&lt;int&gt; pv(V), pe(V);
    long long min_cost = 0;
    while (true) {
        fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
        priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
        dist[S] = 0;
        pq.push({0, S});
        while (!pq.empty()) {
            auto [d, u] = pq.top();
            pq.pop();
            if (d != dist[u]) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)adj[u].size(); ++i) {
                const auto& e = adj[u][i];
                if (e.cap &gt; 0) {
                    long long nd = d + e.cost + pi[u] - pi[e.to];
                    if (nd &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = nd;
                        pv[e.to] = u;
                        pe[e.to] = i;
                        pq.push({nd, e.to});
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if (dist[T] == INF) break;
        for (int v = 0; v &lt; V; ++v) {
            if (dist[v] &lt; INF) pi[v] += dist[v];
        }
        int pushed = INF_CAP;
        for (int v = T; v != S; v = pv[v]) {
            pushed = min(pushed, adj[pv[v]][pe[v]].cap);
        }
        for (int v = T; v != S; v = pv[v]) {
            auto& e = adj[pv[v]][pe[v]];
            e.cap -= pushed;
            adj[v][e.rev].cap += pushed;
        }
        min_cost += 1LL * pushed * pi[T];
    }
    return min_cost;
}

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    solve_bzoj3550();
    return 0;
}

4. Bounded-Demand Min-Cost Circulations (上下界最小费用可行流 / 最大流)
In Chapter 4, we mastered feasible and maximum flows where each arc \(e = (u \to v)\) has a capacity interval \([l_e, u_e]\). Extending bounded flows to Minimum-Cost Circulations and Minimum-Cost \(s\text{--}t\) Flows ([l_e, u_e], c_e) requires just one extra accumulator (base_cost) and a clean two-phase Primal–Dual protocol.

4.1 Unified Transformation for Lower Bounds \(l_e\) and Negative Costs \(c_e &lt; 0\)
Suppose we are given a network \(G = (V, E)\) where each arc \(e = (u \to v)\) has lower bound \(l_e\), upper bound \(u_e\) (\(0 \le l_e \le u_e\)), and unit cost \(c_e \in \mathbb{R}\). We want to find a feasible flow \(f: E \to \mathbb{R}\) satisfying \(l_e \le f_e \le u_e\) that minimizes \(\sum_{e \in E} c_e f_e\).
We combine Ch 04 Lower-Bound Shifting with Sec 3 Mandatory Pre-Saturation in two steps for each arc \(e = (u \to v, [l_e, u_e], c_e)\):

Case 1 (\(c_e \ge 0\) — Non-Negative Cost): Write \(f_e = l_e + \tilde{f}_e\) with \(0 \le \tilde{f}_e \le u_e - l_e\).

Add mandatory lower-bound cost to accumulator: \(\text{base\_cost} \mathrel{+}= l_e \cdot c_e\).
Update nodal balances: \(M(u) \mathrel{-}= l_e\), \(M(v) \mathrel{+}= l_e\).
Add forward residual arc \((u \to v, \text{cap} = u_e - l_e, \text{cost} = +c_e)\) to \(G^+\).

Case 2 (\(c_e &lt; 0\) — Negative Cost, Automatic Pre-Saturation!): Pre-saturate all the way to the upper bound \(u_e\) by writing \(f_e = u_e - \bar{f}_e\) with \(0 \le \bar{f}_e \le u_e - l_e\) (since \(f_e \ge l_e \iff \bar{f}_e = u_e - f_e \le u_e - l_e\)).

Add pre-saturated upper-bound cost to accumulator: \(\text{base\_cost} \mathrel{+}= u_e \cdot c_e\).
Update nodal balances: \(M(u) \mathrel{-}= u_e\), \(M(v) \mathrel{+}= u_e\).
Add reverse refund arc \((v \to u, \text{cap} = u_e - l_e, \text{cost} = -c_e &gt; 0)\) to \(G^+\).




4.2 The Three Bounded Min-Cost Flow Variants
Once all arcs are compiled into \(G^+\) (which now has zero negative edge costs, so \(\pi_0 \equiv 0\)!), we wire \(S^*\) and \(T^*\) according to \(M(v)\) (\(S^* \xrightarrow{M(v), 0} v\) if \(M(v) &gt; 0\), \(v \xrightarrow{-M(v), 0} T^*\) if \(M(v) &lt; 0\)) and solve whichever variant the problem requires:









Problem Variant
Feedback Arc \((t \to s)\)
Execution Protocol
Feasibility Check




1. Min-Cost Feasible Circulation (无源汇上下界最小费用可行流)
None
Run MCMF from \(S^*\) to \(T^*\). Total cost = base_cost + phase1_cost.
Total flow from \(S^*\) must equal \(\sum_{M(v)&gt;0} M(v)\).


2. Min-Cost Feasible \(s\text{--}t\) Flow (有源汇上下界最小费用可行流)
Add \(t \xrightarrow{[0, \infty), \text{cost}=0} s\)
Run MCMF from \(S^*\) to \(T^*\). Total cost = base_cost + phase1_cost. Flow value is \(f(t \to s)\).
Total flow from \(S^*\) must equal \(\sum_{M(v)&gt;0} M(v)\).


3. Bounded Min-Cost Max-Flow (有源汇上下界最小费用最大流)
Add \(t \xrightarrow{[0, \infty), \text{cost}=0} s\)
Phase 1: Run MCMF \(S^* \to T^*\) to achieve a cost-optimal feasible flow \(f_{\text{feas}}\) of value \(F_0 = f(t \to s)\). Delete \(S^*, T^*\), and arc \((t \to s)\).Phase 2: Run MCMF from \(s\) to \(t\) on the remaining residual graph \(G_{f_{\text{feas}}}\) to push maximum additional flow \(F_{\text{add}}\) at minimum additional cost!
Phase 1 must saturate all \(S^*\) arcs. Final flow is \(F_0 + F_{\text{add}}\).









TipLemma 17.7 (Why Deleting \(S^*, T^*, (t \to s)\) After Phase 1 Preserves Cycle-Optimality for Phase 2)



In Bounded Min-Cost Max-Flow (Variant 3), why is the feasible \(s\text{--}t\) flow \(f_{\text{feas}}\) obtained at the end of Phase 1 guaranteed to be cost-optimal for its flow value \(F_0 = f(t \to s)\), allowing Phase 2 to continue directly from \(s\) to \(t\) without re-running negative cycle cancellation?
Proof: At the end of Phase 1, the Primal–Dual MCMF from \(S^*\) to \(T^*\) terminates with a residual network \(G^{\text{aux}}_{f_{\text{feas}}}\) that contains no negative-cost directed cycles (Theorem 17.2). Deleting vertices \(S^*, T^*\) and the single feedback arc \((t \to s)\) (along with its reverse \((s \to t)\)) produces a subgraph \(G_{f_{\text{feas}}} \subseteq G^{\text{aux}}_{f_{\text{feas}}}\). Because deleting vertices and edges from a graph can only remove cycles and can never create a new cycle, \(G_{f_{\text{feas}}}\) is guaranteed to contain zero negative-cost directed cycles!
Implementation Note: When starting Phase 2 from \(s\) to \(t\), either re-initialize potentials \(\pi\) on \(G_{f_{\text{feas}}}\) or note that the final potentials \(\pi\) from Phase 1 ALREADY satisfy \(c^\pi(a) \ge 0\) for every remaining residual arc \(a \in G_{f_{\text{feas}}}\) (since \(G_{f_{\text{feas}}} \subseteq G^{\text{aux}}_{f_{\text{feas}}}\))! Thus Phase 2 can reuse the exact \(\pi\) array from the end of Phase 1 without even running an initial Bellman–Ford pass! \(\blacksquare\)




4.3 Worked Modeling Example: Codeforces 1288 F — Red-Blue Graph
Let us apply Bounded-Demand Min-Cost Circulation (Variant 2) to Codeforces 1288 F — Red-Blue Graph (\(N_1, N_2, M \le 200\)).

Problem Summary: Given a bipartite graph \(G = (L \cup R, E)\) where each vertex in \(L \cup R\) is colored Red (R), Blue (B), or Uncolored (U). You can color each edge \(e \in E\) Red (cost \(r\)), Blue (cost \(b\)), or leave it Uncolored (cost \(0\)).

For every Red vertex, it must be incident to strictly more Red edges than Blue edges (\(\deg_R(v) - \deg_B(v) \ge 1\)).
For every Blue vertex, it must be incident to strictly more Blue edges than Red edges (\(\deg_B(v) - \deg_R(v) \ge 1\)).
For every Uncolored vertex, there is no restriction. Find the minimum total cost, or output -1.

Algebraic-to-Graph Compilation (Signed Net-Color Flow): Notice that for each edge \(e = (u, v)\) with \(u \in L, v \in R\), choosing among {Red, Blue, Uncolored} is a discrete decision in \(\{+1, -1, 0\}\):

Sending \(+1\) unit of flow from \(u \in L\) to \(v \in R\) means coloring \(e\) Red at cost \(+r\): create directed arc \(u \xrightarrow{[0, 1], \text{cost} = r} v\).
Sending \(+1\) unit of flow from \(v \in R\) to \(u \in L\) means coloring \(e\) Blue at cost \(+b\): create directed arc \(v \xrightarrow{[0, 1], \text{cost} = b} u\). (Can both arcs carry flow \(1\) simultaneously? Since \(r, b &gt; 0\), their 2-cycle \(u \to v \to u\) has strictly positive cost \(r + b &gt; 0\), so an optimal min-cost flow will NEVER activate both on the same edge!) Now inspect the net outflow \(\text{out}(u) - \text{in}(u) = \deg_R(u) - \deg_B(u)\) at each vertex:
For \(u \in L\):

If \(u\) is R: \(\text{out}(u) - \text{in}(u) \ge 1 \implies\) connect source \(s \xrightarrow{[1, \infty), 0} u\).
If \(u\) is B: \(\text{in}(u) - \text{out}(u) \ge 1 \implies\) connect \(u \xrightarrow{[1, \infty), 0} t\).
If \(u\) is U: unrestricted \(\implies\) connect \(s \xrightarrow{[0, \infty), 0} u\) and \(u \xrightarrow{[0, \infty), 0} t\).

For \(v \in R\) (where Red edges enter \(v\) and Blue edges leave \(v\), so \(\deg_R(v) - \deg_B(v) = \text{in}(v) - \text{out}(v)\)):

If \(v\) is R: \(\text{in}(v) - \text{out}(v) \ge 1 \implies\) connect \(v \xrightarrow{[1, \infty), 0} t\).
If \(v\) is B: \(\text{out}(v) - \text{in}(v) \ge 1 \implies\) connect \(s \xrightarrow{[1, \infty), 0} v\).
If \(v\) is U: connect \(s \xrightarrow{[0, \infty), 0} v\) and \(v \xrightarrow{[0, \infty), 0} t\). Add feedback arc \(t \xrightarrow{[0, \infty), 0} s\) and run BoundedMCMF (Variant 2). Every edge cost is non-negative (\(\{0, r, b\}\)), so \(\pi_0 \equiv 0\)!





4.4 Reference C++ Wrapper: BoundedMCMF (Lower Bounds + Mandatory Saturation)
As promised in the textbook style rules, we assume you already have a standard PrimalDualMCMF solver (add_edge(u, v, cap, cost) and solve(S, T)). Below is the thin, self-contained modeling wrapper that compiles arbitrary \([l_e, u_e]\) bounds and negative costs \(c_e &lt; 0\) into a non-negative PrimalDualMCMF instance with \(\pi_0 \equiv 0\):
// Thin Modeling Wrapper for Bounded-Demand & Negative-Cycle MCMF
// Compiles [l, u] bounds and c &lt; 0 mandatory saturation into a non-negative MCMF instance.
struct BoundedMCMF {
    int n, S_star, T_star;
    long long base_cost = 0, total_demand = 0;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; M; // Nodal excess balance M[v] = in_mandatory - out_mandatory
    PrimalDualMCMF mcmf; // Standard Dijkstra-based MCMF initialized with pi = 0

    BoundedMCMF(int n_) : n(n_), S_star(n_ + 1), T_star(n_ + 2), M(n_ + 3, 0), mcmf(n_ + 2) {}

    // Returns the edge index in mcmf (along with whether it was reversed due to c &lt; 0)
    int add_bounded_edge(int u, int v, long long l, long long u_cap, long long cost) {
        if (cost &gt;= 0) {
            base_cost += l * cost;
            M[u] -= l;
            M[v] += l;
            return mcmf.add_edge(u, v, u_cap - l, cost);
        } else {
            // Mandatory Saturation (强制满流) up to upper bound u_cap
            base_cost += u_cap * cost;
            M[u] -= u_cap;
            M[v] += u_cap;
            return mcmf.add_edge(v, u, u_cap - l, -cost); // Reverse refund arc with -cost &gt; 0!
        }
    }

    // Variant 1 & 2: Min-Cost Feasible Circulation (or s-t flow after adding t -&gt; s [0, INF, 0])
    pair&lt;bool, long long&gt; solve_feasible(int s = -1, int t = -1, long long INF_CAP = 1e15) {
        if (s != -1 && t != -1) {
            mcmf.add_edge(t, s, INF_CAP, 0);
        }
        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
            if (M[i] &gt; 0) {
                mcmf.add_edge(S_star, i, M[i], 0);
                total_demand += M[i];
            } else if (M[i] &lt; 0) {
                mcmf.add_edge(i, T_star, -M[i], 0);
            }
        }
        auto [flow, cost] = mcmf.solve(S_star, T_star); // Runs with pi_0 = 0!
        if (flow != total_demand) return {false, -1};
        return {true, base_cost + cost};
    }
};

6. Practice Set: The 8-Problem Difficulty Ladder (#129–#136)
We now apply the theoretical spine of Sections 1–5 to eight carefully graduated problems spanning Stepping Stone (SS), Intermediate (Int), Advanced (Adv), and Mastery (Mas) tiers.


Problem #129 (SS-1): Luogu P2153 [SDOI2009] — 晨跑 (Morning Run)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P2153
Bounds: \(n \le 200\) intersections, \(m \le 2 \cdot 10^4\) directed streets with lengths \(w_e \ge 0\); time limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Statement: Elaxia runs from home (vertex \(1\)) to school (vertex \(n\)) every morning. To avoid boredom, he wants to choose the maximum possible number of days \(F\) such that every day’s route is a simple \(1 \to n\) path, no two routes share any intermediate intersection \(v \in \{2, \dots, n-1\}\) (nor use the same directed street twice), and among all maximum-day schedules, the total distance run across all \(F\) days is minimized. Output both \(F\) and the minimum total distance.







NoteHint 1 — Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal





Why does the greedy strategy “find the shortest \(1 \to n\) path via Dijkstra, delete its internal vertices, and repeat” fail? Construct a 4-vertex diamond where the single shortest path blocks both halves of two slightly longer vertex-disjoint paths. What structure allows an earlier path to be “un-routed” and split into two disjoint paths?









NoteHint 2 — Node Splitting for Internal Vertex Disjointness





How do you enforce that each intermediate vertex \(v \in \{2, \dots, n-1\}\) is visited at most once across all paths while endpoints \(1\) and \(n\) can be visited \(F\) times? Split every vertex \(v\) into \(v_{\text{in}}\) and \(v_{\text{out}}\) with a controlled internal bridge capacity and zero cost.









NoteHint 3 — Potential Regime & Suurballe Connection





Since all street lengths satisfy \(w_e \ge 0\), what is the valid initial Johnson potential \(\pi_0\)? Notice that running SSP with Johnson potentials on a non-negative graph to find \(F\) disjoint paths of minimum total length is Suurballe’s Algorithm (1974)!









TipFull 4-Step Derivation & C++ Model Builder






Step 1: Diagnostic Signal & Failure of Sequential Greedy
Sequential shortest-path deletion fails whenever the unique shortest path \(P_1\) uses a diagonal cross-link \(u \to v\) that blocks two parallel disjoint routes \(1 \to u \to n\) and \(1 \to v \to n\). In a residual flow network, pushing a second unit of flow along \(1 \to v \xrightarrow{\text{rev}} u \to n\) traverses the reverse residual arc \((v_{\text{in}} \to u_{\text{out}})\) of cost \(-w(u, v)\), automatically cancelling the diagonal cross-link and re-routing the two paths into \(1 \to u \to n\) and \(1 \to v \to n\)!


Step 2: Algebraic Representation
Assign binary flow variable \(f_e \in \{0, 1\}\) to each directed street \(e = (u \to v)\) of length \(w_e \ge 0\). We maximize \(F = \sum_{e \in \delta^+(1)} f_e\) (and secondarily minimize \(\sum_{e \in E} w_e f_e\)) subject to:
\[
\sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} f_e = \sum_{e \in \delta^+(v)} f_e \le 1 \quad \forall v \in \{2, \dots, n-1\}
\]


Step 3: Mechanical Graph Compilation

Split each vertex \(v \in \{1, \dots, n\}\) into \(v_{\text{in}} = v\) and \(v_{\text{out}} = v + n\).
Vertex Capacities:

For \(v \in \{2, \dots, n-1\}\): add internal arc \(v_{\text{in}} \xrightarrow{\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0} v_{\text{out}}\).
For endpoints \(v \in \{1, n\}\): add internal arc \(v_{\text{in}} \xrightarrow{\text{cap} = \infty, \text{cost} = 0} v_{\text{out}}\) (or start directly from \(s = 1_{\text{out}}\) and end at \(t = n_{\text{in}}\)).

Street Transitions: For each input street \((u \to v, w)\), add arc \(u_{\text{out}} \xrightarrow{\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = w} v_{\text{in}}\).
Regime & Execution: All costs \(w \ge 0 \implies\) Regime 1 (\(\pi_0 \equiv 0\)), Stopping Regime 1 (True MCMF).



Step 4: Toy Trace & Complexity
With \(|V| = 2n \le 400\), \(|E| \le 2 \cdot 10^4 + 200\), and \(F \le n = 200\), Dijkstra-based SSP executes at most \(200\) augmentations in \(O(F (V + E) \log V) \approx 1.5 \cdot 10^7\) operations (\(&lt; 30\text{ ms}\)).
void build_and_solve_p2153(int n, int m) {
    int S = 1 + n, T = n; // Start at 1_out, end at n_in
    PrimalDualMCMF mcmf(2 * n);
    for (int v = 2; v &lt;= n - 1; ++v) {
        mcmf.add_edge(v, v + n, 1, 0); // v_in -&gt; v_out: cap 1, cost 0
    }
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; m; ++i) {
        int u, v; long long w;
        cin &gt;&gt; u &gt;&gt; v &gt;&gt; w;
        mcmf.add_edge(u + n, v, 1, w); // u_out -&gt; v_in: cap 1, cost w
    }
    auto [max_days, min_dist] = mcmf.solve(S, T); // pi_0 = 0
    cout &lt;&lt; max_days &lt;&lt; " " &lt;&lt; min_dist &lt;&lt; "\n";
}







Problem #130 (SS-2): POJ 3422 / Luogu P2045 — Kaka’s Matrix Travels (方格取数加强版)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P2045
Bounds: \(N \le 50\) (\(N \times N\) grid), \(K \le 10\) monotone walkers, cell values \(w_{r,c} \in [0, 1000]\).
Problem Statement: Given an \(N \times N\) matrix of non-negative integers, Kaka starts at top-left cell \((1, 1)\) and walks to bottom-right cell \((N, N)\) exactly \(K\) times, stepping only Right \((r, c+1)\) or Down \((r+1, c)\). The first time any walker visits cell \((r, c)\), Kaka collects its reward \(w_{r,c}\) and the cell’s value becomes \(0\) for all subsequent walkers. Maximize the total reward collected across all \(K\) walks.







NoteHint 1 — Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal





For \(K = 2\), multi-dimensional DP has state \((r_1, c_1, r_2, c_2)\) of size \(O(N^3)\), which is fine. Why does DP explode at \(K = 10\)? What happens to the marginal reward of a cell \((r, c)\) as the number of visits \(f_{r,c}\) increases from \(0 \to 1 \to 2 \to \dots \to K\)?









NoteHint 2 — Parallel Harvest + Bypass Arcs





Because the marginal gain is \(-w_{r,c}\) on the \(1\)-st visit and \(0\) on visits \(2, \dots, K\), the cost function on cell \((r, c)\) is piecewise-linear and convex (\(-w_{r,c} \le 0\)). How do two parallel arcs between \((r, c)_{\text{in}}\) and \((r, c)_{\text{out}}\) encode this exact two-slope profile?









NoteHint 3 — DAG \(\pi_0\) Initialization





Since walkers only move Right or Down, the entire network is a DAG of topological length \(2N\). How do you compute the initial Johnson potentials \(\pi_0\) in \(O(N^2)\) time without calling SPFA?









TipFull 4-Step Derivation & C++ Model Builder






Step 1: Diagnostic Signal
At \(K = 10\), tracking \(K\) simultaneous walker positions in DP requires \(\binom{N+K-1}{K} \approx 10^{10}\) states. However, \(K \le 10\) is a tiny flow volume (\(F = K\)), and the diminishing return on cell \((r, c)\) (first visit collects \(w_{r,c}\), later visits collect \(0\)) is a two-piece convex cost function!


Step 2: Algebraic Representation
Let \(f_{r,c} \in \{0, 1, \dots, K\}\) be the total number of walkers passing through cell \((r, c)\). Decompose \(f_{r,c} = x_{r,c} + y_{r,c}\) where \(x_{r,c} \in \{0, 1\}\) indicates whether the cell’s treasure is harvested and \(y_{r,c} \in [0, K-1]\) counts unrewarded repeat visits. We minimize:
\[
\sum_{r=1}^N \sum_{c=1}^N (-w_{r,c}) x_{r,c} + 0 \cdot y_{r,c}
\]
Because \(-w_{r,c} \le 0\), any minimum-cost flow automatically saturates \(x_{r,c} = 1\) before routing any flow onto the bypass variable \(y_{r,c}\)!


Step 3: Mechanical Graph Compilation

Split each cell \(u = (r, c)\) into \(u_{\text{in}}\) and \(u_{\text{out}}\) and connect them with two parallel directed arcs:

Harvest arc: \(u_{\text{in}} \xrightarrow{\text{cap} = 1,\; \text{cost} = -w_{r,c}} u_{\text{out}}\).
Bypass arc: \(u_{\text{in}} \xrightarrow{\text{cap} = K - 1,\; \text{cost} = 0} u_{\text{out}}\).

Grid Transitions: From \(u_{\text{out}}\) of \((r, c)\), add arcs of \((\text{cap} = K, \text{cost} = 0)\) to \((r+1, c)_{\text{in}}\) and \((r, c+1)_{\text{in}}\).
DAG Potential Initialization (\(\pi_0\)): Because \((r, c)\) is already topologically sorted in row-major order, we compute \(\pi_0\) in a single \(O(N^2)\) loop:

\(\pi_0(u_{\text{in}}) = \min\bigl(\pi_0((r-1, c)_{\text{out}}),\, \pi_0((r, c-1)_{\text{out}})\bigr)\)
\(\pi_0(u_{\text{out}}) = \pi_0(u_{\text{in}}) - w_{r,c}\)


(Bonus Path Reconstruction Note — Luogu P3356 火星探险问题): When a problem also asks you to print the actual step-by-step moves (Right/Down) of each of the \(K\) walkers after solving the MCMF, simply run \(K\) greedy DFS walks from \((1, 1)_{\text{in}}\) to \((N, N)_{\text{out}}\) along original forward grid edges \((u_{\text{out}} \to v_{\text{in}})\) that carry positive flow (f_e = u_e - rev.cap &gt; 0), decrementing their carried flow by \(1\) at each step!
long long solve_kaka_matrix(int n, int k, const vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt;& w) {
    auto id_in  = [&](int r, int c) { return (r * n + c) * 2 + 1; };
    auto id_out = [&](int r, int c) { return (r * n + c) * 2 + 2; };
    int S = id_in(0, 0), T = id_out(n - 1, n - 1);
    PrimalDualMCMF mcmf(2 * n * n);

    for (int r = 0; r &lt; n; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; n; ++c) {
            mcmf.add_edge(id_in(r, c), id_out(r, c), 1, -w[r][c]); // Harvest arc
            if (k &gt; 1) mcmf.add_edge(id_in(r, c), id_out(r, c), k - 1, 0); // Bypass arc
            if (r + 1 &lt; n) mcmf.add_edge(id_out(r, c), id_in(r + 1, c), k, 0);
            if (c + 1 &lt; n) mcmf.add_edge(id_out(r, c), id_in(r, c + 1), k, 0);
        }
    }
    // Regime 2: O(N^2) Topological DP for pi_0 on the row-major DAG
    mcmf.pi.assign(2 * n * n + 1, 0);
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; n; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; n; ++c) {
            long long best_in = 0;
            if (r &gt; 0 || c &gt; 0) {
                best_in = 4e18;
                if (r &gt; 0) best_in = min(best_in, mcmf.pi[id_out(r - 1, c)]);
                if (c &gt; 0) best_in = min(best_in, mcmf.pi[id_out(r, c - 1)]);
            }
            mcmf.pi[id_in(r, c)] = best_in;
            mcmf.pi[id_out(r, c)] = best_in - w[r][c];
        }
    }
    auto [flow, min_cost] = mcmf.solve(S, T, k); // Push at most K units
    return -min_cost;
}







Problem #131 (Int-1): Luogu P4013 — 数字梯形问题 (Number Trapezoid)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P4013
Bounds: Top row has \(m \le 20\) numbers, trapezoid has \(n \le 20\) rows (row \(i\) has \(m + i - 1\) numbers).
Problem Statement: From each of the \(m\) numbers in the top row of a number trapezoid, start a path moving down-left \((i+1, j)\) or down-right \((i+1, j+1)\) to the bottom row (\(n\) steps total). The score of a set of \(m\) paths is the sum of all numbers along all \(m\) paths (counted with multiplicity if a cell is visited multiple times). Solve three independent subproblems maximizing the total score:

Rule 1: The \(m\) paths must be ** completely vertex-disjoint** (no shared vertices and no shared edges).
Rule 2: The \(m\) paths may share vertices, but must be edge-disjoint (no shared directed transitions, and each top-row start node initiates at most \(1\) path).
Rule 3: The \(m\) paths may share both vertices and edges freely (each top-row start node initiates \(1\) path).








NoteHint 1 — Isolating the Three Capacity Knobs





When every cell \((i, j)\) is split into \(u_{\text{in}} \to u_{\text{out}}\) with cost \(-w_{i,j}\), which arcs control vertex reuse and which arcs control transition (edge) reuse?









NoteHint 2 — The Top-Row & Bottom-Row Boundary Subtlety





In Rule 2 and Rule 3, each of the \(m\) top-row cells can still only start \(1\) path (\(s \to u_{\text{in}}\) has capacity \(1\)). What should the capacity of \((n, j)_{\text{out}} \to t\) on the bottom row be under Rule 1 vs. Rules 2 and 3?









NoteHint 3 — Parameterizing All Three Rules in One Function





Write down a 3-row table mapping Rule in {1, 2, 3} to (vertex_cap, edge_cap, bottom_sink_cap) and verify why top-down DAG DP initializes \(\pi_0\) in \(O(nm)\) time for all three rules simultaneously.









TipFull 4-Step Derivation & C++ Model Builder





Step 1–3: The 3-Rule Disjointness Compilation Table
In this problem, if a cell \((i, j)\) is visited \(f\) times (under Rules 2 and 3), its number \(w_{i,j}\) is added to the score \(f\) times (unlike POJ 3422 where subsequent visits gave \(0\)). Therefore, the internal vertex arc \(u_{\text{in}} \to u_{\text{out}}\) carries cost \(-w_{i,j}\) for all units of flow passing through it!
The three rules differ exclusively in three capacity parameters:










Rule
Disjointness Requirement
Internal Vertex Arc \(u_{\text{in}} \to u_{\text{out}}\)
Transition Arc \(u_{\text{out}} \to v_{\text{in}}\)
Bottom-Row Sink Arc \((n, j)_{\text{out}} \to t\)




Rule 1
Vertex-disjoint & Edge-disjoint
\(\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = -w_{i,j}\)
\(\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0\)
\(\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0\)


Rule 2
Vertex-sharing OK, Edge-disjoint
\(\text{cap} = \infty, \text{cost} = -w_{i,j}\)
\(\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0\)
\(\text{cap} = \infty, \text{cost} = 0\)


Rule 3
Vertex-sharing & Edge-sharing OK
\(\text{cap} = \infty, \text{cost} = -w_{i,j}\)
\(\text{cap} = \infty, \text{cost} = 0\)
\(\text{cap} = \infty, \text{cost} = 0\)



(Note: For top-row vertices \((1, j)\), \(s \to (1, j)_{\text{in}}\) ALWAYS has \(\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0\) across all three rules because each top-row vertex initiates exactly 1 path.)
long long solve_trapezoid_rule(int m, int n, const vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt;& w,
                               long long v_cap, long long e_cap, long long sink_cap) {
    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; id(n, vector&lt;int&gt;(m + n, 0));
    int V = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i)
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; m + i; ++j) id[i][j] = ++V;

    int S = 2 * V + 1, T = 2 * V + 2;
    PrimalDualMCMF mcmf(2 * V + 2);
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; m; ++j) mcmf.add_edge(S, id[0][j], 1, 0);

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; m + i; ++j) {
            int u = id[i][j];
            mcmf.add_edge(u, u + V, v_cap, -w[i][j]); // u_in -&gt; u_out
            if (i + 1 &lt; n) {
                mcmf.add_edge(u + V, id[i + 1][j], e_cap, 0);
                mcmf.add_edge(u + V, id[i + 1][j + 1], e_cap, 0);
            } else {
                mcmf.add_edge(u + V, T, sink_cap, 0);
            }
        }
    }
    // O(V) DAG DP for pi_0
    mcmf.pi.assign(2 * V + 3, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; m + i; ++j) {
            int u = id[i][j];
            if (i &gt; 0) {
                long long best = 4e18;
                if (j - 1 &gt;= 0) best = min(best, mcmf.pi[id[i - 1][j - 1] + V]);
                if (j &lt; m + i - 1) best = min(best, mcmf.pi[id[i - 1][j] + V]);
                mcmf.pi[u] = best;
            }
            mcmf.pi[u + V] = mcmf.pi[u] - w[i][j];
            if (i == n - 1) mcmf.pi[T] = min(mcmf.pi[T], mcmf.pi[u + V]);
        }
    }
    return -mcmf.solve(S, T).second;
}






Problem #132 (Int-2): Luogu P4553 — 80人环游世界 (Around the World in 80 People)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P4553
Bounds: \(n \le 100\) countries, \(m \le 80\) travelers, required visits \(V_i \in [0, m]\), flight costs \(c_{i,j} \ge 0\) for \(1 \le i &lt; j \le n\).
Problem Statement: \(m\) travelers plan a trip across \(n\) countries numbered \(1, \dots, n\). Each traveler can start at any country, fly only from lower-numbered to higher-numbered countries (\(i \to j\) with \(i &lt; j\) at ticket cost \(c_{i,j}\)), and end their trip at any country (or not travel at all). Country \(i\) must be visited by exactly \(V_i\) travelers in total. Find the minimum total flight ticket cost across all travelers.







NoteHint 1 — Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal





Every country \(i\) has an exact visit demand \(V_i\), and the total number of active travelers is capped at \(\le m\). What flow structure models an exact flow requirement \([V_i, V_i]\) through vertex \(i\)?









NoteHint 2 — Controlling the Total Traveler Count \(\le m\)





If each traveler is \(1\) unit of flow starting at \(s\) and ending at \(t\), how do we enforce that at most \(m\) units of flow leave the source in total? Introduce a throttled pre-source \(s_0 \xrightarrow{[0, m], \text{cost}=0} s\)!









NoteHint 3 — Compiling into BoundedMCMF (Sec 4)





Split country \(i\) into \(i_{\text{in}} \xrightarrow{[V_i, V_i], \text{cost}=0} i_{\text{out}}\). Since all flight costs \(c_{i,j} \ge 0\), every edge in BoundedMCMF has non-negative cost!









TipFull 4-Step Derivation & C++ Model Builder





Step 1–3: Algebraic-to-Graph Compilation
Let \(f(i_{\text{in}} \to i_{\text{out}})\) be the number of travelers visiting country \(i\). The problem requires \(f(i_{\text{in}} \to i_{\text{out}}) = V_i\), which is a tight lower-and-upper-bounded arc \([l_e, u_e] = [V_i, V_i]\) of cost \(0\).

Throttled Source: Create pre-source \(s_0\) and dispatch source \(s\) connected by \(s_0 \xrightarrow{[0, m], \text{cost} = 0} s\), plus sink \(t\).
Country Start / End / Internal Arcs: For each country \(i \in \{1, \dots, n\}\):

Any traveler can start their journey at country \(i\) for free: \(s \xrightarrow{[0, \infty), \text{cost} = 0} i_{\text{in}}\).
Exact visit constraint at country \(i\): \(i_{\text{in}} \xrightarrow{[V_i, V_i], \text{cost} = 0} i_{\text{out}}\).
Any traveler can end their journey at country \(i\) for free: \(i_{\text{out}} \xrightarrow{[0, \infty), \text{cost} = 0} t\).

Flight Transitions: For \(1 \le i &lt; j \le n\) with flight cost \(c_{i,j} \ne -1\), add \(i_{\text{out}} \xrightarrow{[0, \infty), \text{cost} = c_{i,j}} j_{\text{in}}\).
Solve via BoundedMCMF (Variant 2): Add feedback arc \(t \xrightarrow{[0, \infty), 0} s_0\) and run solve_feasible(s_0, t) with \(\pi_0 \equiv 0\).

long long solve_around_the_world(int n, int m, const vector&lt;int&gt;& V,
                                 const vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt;& flight_cost) {
    int s0 = 2 * n + 1, s = 2 * n + 2, t = 2 * n + 3;
    BoundedMCMF bmcmf(2 * n + 3);
    bmcmf.add_bounded_edge(s0, s, 0, m, 0); // At most m travelers in total
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        bmcmf.add_bounded_edge(s, i, 0, m, 0);         // Start trip at country i
        bmcmf.add_bounded_edge(i, i + n, V[i], V[i], 0); // Exact V[i] visits!
        bmcmf.add_bounded_edge(i + n, t, 0, m, 0);     // End trip at country i
        for (int j = i + 1; j &lt;= n; ++j) {
            if (flight_cost[i][j] &gt;= 0) {
                bmcmf.add_bounded_edge(i + n, j, 0, m, flight_cost[i][j]);
            }
        }
    }
    return bmcmf.solve_feasible(s0, t).second;
}






Problem #133 (Adv-1): Luogu P2469 [SDOI2010] — 星际竞速 (Star Racing)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P2469
Bounds: \(N \le 800\) planets, \(M \le 1.5 \cdot 10^4\) directed航道 (hyperlanes \(u \to v\) with \(u &lt; v\) and flight time \(w_{uv} &gt; 0\)), warp-jump time \(A_v &gt; 0\) to teleport directly to planet \(v\).
Problem Statement: You must visit every planet \(v \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) at least once — and because hyperlanes only go from lower to higher indices (\(u &lt; v\)) and visiting a planet already visited would waste time, you must visit every planet exactly once! At any point (including the start, or immediately after arriving at some planet), you can either fly along a directed hyperlane \(u \to v\) (\(u &lt; v\)) taking time \(w_{uv}\), or perform a warp-jump from anywhere directly to any unvisited planet \(v\) taking time \(A_v\). Minimize the total time required to visit all \(N\) planets.







NoteHint 1 — Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Weighted DAG Path Cover)





Notice that a warp-jump to planet \(v\) ignores where you were previously and simply starts a new path at \(v\) at cost \(A_v\)! Thus, covering all \(N\) planets with hyperlane chains and warp-jumps is a Minimum-Weight Vertex-Disjoint Path Cover on a DAG where starting a path at \(v\) costs \(A_v\) and extending a path across \(u \to v\) costs \(w_{uv}\).









NoteHint 2 — Bipartite Predecessor Assignment View





In any vertex-disjoint path cover of \(\{1, \dots, N\}\), every planet \(v \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) must acquire exactly one incoming predecessor: either it is reached by flying from some unique predecessor \(u &lt; v\) (which can itself have at most one outgoing successor!), OR it is reached via a warp-jump at cost \(A_v\).









NoteHint 3 — Bipartite MCMF Compilation





Create left nodes \(u_{\text{out}}\) (offering at most \(1\) outgoing hyperlane step from \(u\)) and right nodes \(v_{\text{in}}\) (demanding exactly \(1\) incoming arrival at \(v\), either from \(s\) via a warp-jump of cost \(A_v\) or from some \(u_{\text{out}}\) via a hyperlane of cost \(w_{uv}\)).









TipFull 4-Step Derivation & C++ Model Builder





Step 1–3: Algebraic-to-Graph Compilation (Weighted DAG Path Cover)
Let \(x_{uv} \in \{0, 1\}\) indicate whether our trajectory flies along hyperlane \(u \to v\) (\(u &lt; v\)), and let \(y_v \in \{0, 1\}\) indicate whether planet \(v\) is entered via a warp-jump. Because every planet \(v\) must be entered exactly once, and each planet \(u\) can be departed via a hyperlane at most once:
\[
y_v + \sum_{u &lt; v} x_{uv} = 1 \quad (\forall v \in \{1, \dots, N\}), \qquad \sum_{v &gt; u} x_{uv} \le 1 \quad (\forall u \in \{1, \dots, N\})
\]
Subject to these matching constraints, we minimize \(\sum_{v=1}^N A_v y_v + \sum_{(u,v) \in E} w_{uv} x_{uv}\).
This compiles directly into a clean bipartite network \(G = (S \cup L \cup R \cup T, E)\) with non-negative edge costs:

For each \(u \in \{1, \dots, N\}\), create left node \(u_{\text{out}} = u\) with \(S \xrightarrow{\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0} u_{\text{out}}\).
For each \(v \in \{1, \dots, N\}\), create right node \(v_{\text{in}} = v + N\) with \(v_{\text{in}} \xrightarrow{\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0} T\).
Warp-Jump Arrival at \(v\): Add direct arc \(S \xrightarrow{\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = A_v} v_{\text{in}}\).
Hyperlane Flight \(u \to v\) (\(u &lt; v\)): Add bridge arc \(u_{\text{out}} \xrightarrow{\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = w_{uv}} v_{\text{in}}\) (keeping only the minimum \(w_{uv}\) if parallel edges exist).
Because every \(v_{\text{in}} \to T\) has capacity \(1\) and \(S \to v_{\text{in}}\) is always available, the maximum \(S \to T\) flow is guaranteed to be \(N\), and every arc has cost \(\ge 0\) (Regime 1: \(\pi_0 \equiv 0\))!

long long solve_star_racing(int n, int m, const vector&lt;long long&gt;& A,
                            const vector&lt;tuple&lt;int, int, long long&gt;&gt;& edges) {
    int S = 2 * n + 1, T = 2 * n + 2;
    PrimalDualMCMF mcmf(2 * n + 2);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        mcmf.add_edge(S, i, 1, 0);          // u_out can initiate at most 1 flight
        mcmf.add_edge(S, i + n, 1, A[i]);   // Warp-jump directly to v_in at cost A[i]
        mcmf.add_edge(i + n, T, 1, 0);      // v_in must be visited exactly once
    }
    for (auto [u, v, w] : edges) {
        if (u &gt; v) swap(u, v);
        mcmf.add_edge(u, v + n, 1, w);      // Hyperlane u_out -&gt; v_in at cost w
    }
    return mcmf.solve(S, T).second;         // Push N units with pi_0 = 0
}






Problem #134 (Adv-2): Luogu P4043 [AHOI2014/JSOI2014] — 支线剧情 (Side Quests)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P4043
Bounds: \(N \le 300\) plot nodes, \(K_i\) outgoing storyline branches from node \(i\) (total arcs \(M \le 5000\)), play time \(T_{i,j} \ge 0\).
Problem Statement: A visual novel starts at plot node \(1\) and has a DAG of storyline transitions \(u \to v\) taking time \(T_{u,v} \ge 0\). At any plot node \(u &gt; 1\), the player can save and restart a new playthrough at node \(1\) for \(0\) cost. To unlock 100% completion, every storyline branch (directed edge \(u \to v\)) must be traversed at least once. Find the minimum total play time required to traverse every directed edge at least once.







NoteHint 1 — Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Minimum-Cost Arc Cover with Lower Bounds)





Contrast this with #132 (80人环游世界) and #133 (星际竞速): instead of covering vertices, we must cover every directed edge \(e = (u \to v)\) at least once, i.e., \(f_e \in [1, \infty)\)!









NoteHint 2 — Modeling Free Playthrough Resets





Each playthrough starts at node \(1\) and ends at some node \(u &gt; 1\), after which the player resets back to node \(1\) for \(0\) cost. How do we represent “restarting at node \(1\) from any node \(u &gt; 1\)” as a circulation arc?









NoteHint 3 — Compiling into BoundedMCMF (Sec 4)





Every storyline edge \(e = (u \to v)\) has lower bound \(l_e = 1\), upper bound \(u_e = \infty\), and cost \(T_{u,v} \ge 0\). Every node \(u \in \{2, \dots, N\}\) has a reset arc \(u \xrightarrow{[0, \infty), \text{cost} = 0} 1\). Run BoundedMCMF::solve_feasible()!









TipFull 4-Step Derivation & C++ Model Builder





Step 1–3: Algebraic-to-Graph Compilation
Let \(f_e \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 1}\) be the number of playthroughs that traverse storyline branch \(e = (u \to v)\), and let \(r_u \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}\) be the number of playthroughs that terminate at node \(u \in \{2, \dots, N\}\) and reset to node \(1\). Flow conservation at every node \(u \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) requires that the total number of times we enter \(u\) equals the total number of times we leave \(u\) (including resets \(r_u\)):

Storyline Branch \((u \to v)\): Bounded arc \(u \xrightarrow{[1, \infty),\; \text{cost} = T_{u,v}} v\).
Playthrough Reset \((u \to 1)\): Unbounded zero-cost arc \(u \xrightarrow{[0, \infty),\; \text{cost} = 0} 1\) for all \(u \in \{2, \dots, N\}\).

Because every storyline edge has \(l_e = 1, c_e = T_{u,v} \ge 0\), BoundedMCMF (Sec 4.4) pre-commits \(1\) unit on each storyline edge (base_cost += T_{u,v}, M[u] -= 1, M[v] += 1), adds residual capacity \(\infty\) at cost \(T_{u,v}\) on \((u \to v)\), and finds a minimum-cost circulation from \(S^*\) to \(T^*\) with \(\pi_0 \equiv 0\)!
long long solve_side_quests(int n, const vector&lt;tuple&lt;int, int, long long&gt;&gt;& quests) {
    BoundedMCMF bmcmf(n);
    for (auto [u, v, t_cost] : quests) {
        bmcmf.add_bounded_edge(u, v, 1, 1e9, t_cost); // Must traverse at least once!
    }
    for (int u = 2; u &lt;= n; ++u) {
        bmcmf.add_bounded_edge(u, 1, 0, 1e9, 0);      // Free reset to node 1
    }
    return bmcmf.solve_feasible().second;
}






Problem #135 (Mas-1): Codeforces 708 D — Incorrect Flow

Judge URL: https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/708/D
Bounds: \(n \le 100\) vertices (\(s = 1, t = n\)), \(m \le 100\) directed edges with capacity \(c_e \le 10^6\) and corrupted flow \(f_e \le 10^6\); time limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Statement: Given a directed network where each edge \(e = (u \to v)\) has capacity \(c_e\) and pre-assigned flow \(f_e\) (which may violate both flow conservation at internal nodes \(2, \dots, n-1\) and capacity bounds \(f_e \le c_e\)), you can pay \(1\) coin per unit to increase \(f_e\), decrease \(f_e\) (keeping \(f_e \ge 0\)), or increase \(c_e\) (capacities cannot be decreased). Find the minimum total coins needed to obtain a valid \(s\text{--}t\) flow.







NoteHint 1 — Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal





Instead of solving for the final flow \(f'_e\) from scratch (\(0 \to f'_e\)), view \(f'_e = f_e + \Delta f_e\) as a perturbation circulation around the pre-injected flow \(f_e\)! What is the initial excess balance \(M(v)\) at each vertex \(v\) induced by \(f_e\)?









NoteHint 2 — The Overflow Case \(f_e &gt; c_e\) and the Zero-Cost Refund Arc





When \(f_e &gt; c_e\), keeping flow at \(f_e\) already incurs a mandatory capacity-expansion penalty of \(f_e - c_e\). If you reduce the flow from \(f_e\) to \(f_e - 1\) (still \(\ge c_e\)), how much does the flow-change penalty \(|f'_e - f_e|\) change, and how much does the capacity-expansion penalty \(\max(0, f'_e - c_e)\) change?









NoteHint 3 — Assembling the 3-Piece Convex Gadget





Combine the \(+1\) and \(-1\) marginal changes on \([c_e, f_e]\) to see why reducing \(f'_e\) inside \([c_e, f_e]\) has net marginal cost \(0\)! Write down the exact 3 residual arcs for \(f_e \le c_e\) and the 3 residual arcs for \(f_e &gt; c_e\).









TipFull 4-Step Derivation & C++ Model Builder






Step 1–4: Complete Compilation Summary (From Section 3.4)
As derived in Section 3.4, we lock in the initial flow \(f_e\) on each edge \(e = (u \to v)\) by updating \(M(u) \mathrel{-}= f_e\) and \(M(v) \mathrel{+}= f_e\), and add the return arc \(n \xrightarrow{\infty, 0} 1\). Then for each edge \(e = (u \to v, c_e, f_e)\):

If \(f_e \le c_e\):

Backward decrease on \([0, f_e]\): \((v \to u, \text{cap} = f_e, \text{cost} = 1)\).
Forward slack increase on \([f_e, c_e]\): \((u \to v, \text{cap} = c_e - f_e, \text{cost} = 1)\).
Forward overflow increase on \([c_e, \infty)\): \((u \to v, \text{cap} = \infty, \text{cost} = 2)\).

If \(f_e &gt; c_e\): Add mandatory penalty base_cost += f_e - c_e, and add:

Backward free refund on \([c_e, f_e]\): \((v \to u, \text{cap} = f_e - c_e, \text{cost} = 0)\).
Backward decrease on \([0, c_e]\): \((v \to u, \text{cap} = c_e, \text{cost} = 1)\).
Forward overflow increase on \([f_e, \infty)\): \((u \to v, \text{cap} = \infty, \text{cost} = 2)\).




Toy Trace (\(n = 2\), single edge \(1 \to 2\) with \(c_e = 3, f_e = 7\))
Suppose a 2-node network has \(1 \to 2\) with \(c_1 = 3, f_1 = 7\) and a second edge \(2 \to 1\) with \(c_2 = 2, f_2 = 0\) (no external sink; say conservation required at both \(1\) and \(2\)). Pre-injecting \(f_1 = 7\) sets base_cost = 7 - 3 = 4, \(M(1) = -7, M(2) = +7\). The free refund arc \((2 \to 1, \text{cap} = 4, \text{cost} = 0)\) immediately routes \(4\) units back at cost \(0\) (reducing \(f'_1\) from \(7\) to \(3\)), and the remaining \(3\) units compare routing along \(2 \to 1\) at cost \(1\) vs. further refunding \(f'_1\) below \(3\) at cost \(1\), achieving total cost \(4 + 3 = 7\).
long long solve_cf708d(int n, int m, const vector&lt;tuple&lt;int, int, long long, long long&gt;&gt;& edges) {
    const long long INF = 1e15;
    int S_star = n + 1, T_star = n + 2;
    PrimalDualMCMF mcmf(n + 2);
    vector&lt;long long&gt; M(n + 1, 0);
    long long base_cost = 0;

    mcmf.add_edge(n, 1, INF, 0); // Unconstrained s-t flow value
    for (auto [u, v, c, f] : edges) {
        M[u] -= f;
        M[v] += f;
        if (f &lt;= c) {
            mcmf.add_edge(v, u, f, 1);       // Decrease flow in [0, f]
            mcmf.add_edge(u, v, c - f, 1);   // Increase flow in [f, c]
            mcmf.add_edge(u, v, INF, 2);     // Increase both flow and cap in [c, inf)
        } else {
            base_cost += (f - c);
            mcmf.add_edge(v, u, f - c, 0);   // Free refund in [c, f]!
            mcmf.add_edge(v, u, c, 1);       // Decrease flow in [0, c]
            mcmf.add_edge(u, v, INF, 2);     // Increase both flow and cap in [f, inf)
        }
    }
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        if (M[i] &gt; 0) mcmf.add_edge(S_star, i, M[i], 0);
        else if (M[i] &lt; 0) mcmf.add_edge(i, T_star, -M[i], 0);
    }
    return base_cost + mcmf.solve(S_star, T_star).second;
}







Problem #136 (Mas-2): AtCoder ABC 214 H — Collecting

Judge URL: https://atcoder.jp/contests/abc214/tasks/abc214_h
Bounds: \(N \le 2 \cdot 10^5\) vertices, \(M \le 2 \cdot 10^5\) directed edges, \(K \le 10\) walkers starting at vertex \(1\), vertex treasures \(X_v \in [1, 10^9]\); time limit \(4.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Statement: You are given a directed graph \(G = (V, E)\) (which may contain cycles!) and \(K\) tokens initially placed at vertex \(1\). You may move each token along directed edges as many steps as you like, ending at any vertex. For every vertex \(v\) visited by at least one token, you collect \(X_v\) coins (collected at most once even if multiple tokens visit \(v\)). Maximize the total coins collected.







NoteHint 1 — Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal & SCC Condensation





Whenever a token enters a Strongly Connected Component (SCC) \(C\), it can freely traverse all vertices inside \(C\) and exit from any vertex of \(C\)! Therefore, what is the first structural reduction you must apply to \(G\)?









NoteHint 2 — Why SPFA Gets Instant TLE at \(N = 2 \cdot 10^5\)





After condensing \(G\) into a DAG of SCCs with total weight \(W_C = \sum_{v \in C} X_v\), we split each SCC \(C\) into \(C_{\text{in}} \to C_{\text{out}}\) with a harvest arc (1, -W_C) and a bypass arc (K-1, 0). With \(2N \approx 4 \cdot 10^5\) vertices and negative edge costs \(-W_C\), why MUST we avoid SPFA (H-17) and how do we initialize \(\pi_0\) in \(O(N + M)\) time?









NoteHint 3 — Topological DP \(\pi_0\) + \(K\) Dijkstra Augmentations





Tarjan’s SCC algorithm naturally outputs SCCs in reverse topological order! Run an \(O(N + M)\) Topological DP from the SCC containing vertex \(1\) (scc[1]) to compute \(\pi_0\), pruning all SCCs unreachable from scc[1]. Then \(K \le 10\) Dijkstra passes take \(O(K (N + M) \log N)\) time!









TipFull 4-Step Derivation & C++ Model Builder





Step 1–4: Complete Compilation & Complexity Proof

Step 1 — Tarjan SCC Condensation: Contract each strongly connected component \(C_i\) into a single super-vertex with aggregated treasure \(W_i = \sum_{v \in C_i} X_v\). Remove self-loops and deduplicate parallel edges between distinct SCCs, yielding a Condensed DAG \(G_{\text{DAG}} = (V_{\text{SCC}}, E_{\text{DAG}})\) with \(|V_{\text{SCC}}| \le N\) and \(|E_{\text{DAG}}| \le M\).
Step 2 — Prune Unreachable SCCs & Build Harvest/Bypass Split: Only SCCs reachable from \(c_{\text{start}} = \text{scc}[1]\) can ever be visited by the \(K\) tokens. For each reachable SCC \(i\):

Split \(i\) into \(i_{\text{in}}\) and \(i_{\text{out}}\) with harvest arc \((i_{\text{in}} \to i_{\text{out}}, \text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = -W_i)\) and bypass arc \((i_{\text{in}} \to i_{\text{out}}, \text{cap} = K - 1, \text{cost} = 0)\).
Connect \(i_{\text{out}} \xrightarrow{\text{cap} = K, \text{cost} = 0} T\) (since any token can stop its walk at SCC \(i\)).
For each DAG edge \((i \to j) \in E_{\text{DAG}}\), add \(i_{\text{out}} \xrightarrow{\text{cap} = K, \text{cost} = 0} j_{\text{in}}\).

Step 3 — \(O(V + E)\) Topological DP Initialization of \(\pi_0\) (Regime 2): In topological order of reachable SCCs starting from \(c_{\text{start}}\):

\(\pi_0((c_{\text{start}})_{\text{in}}) = 0\).
For each reachable SCC \(i\): \(\pi_0(i_{\text{out}}) = \pi_0(i_{\text{in}}) - W_i\), \(\pi_0(T) = \min(\pi_0(T), \pi_0(i_{\text{out}}))\), and for each \((i \to j) \in E_{\text{DAG}}\), \(\pi_0(j_{\text{in}}) = \min(\pi_0(j_{\text{in}}), \pi_0(i_{\text{out}}))\).

Step 4 — \(K \le 10\) Primal–Dual Dijkstra Passes: Because \(\pi_0\) makes every initial reduced cost non-negative in \(O(N + M)\) time, we run at most \(K = 10\) Dijkstra calls on a graph of \(\le 4 \cdot 10^5\) vertices, finishing in \(O(K (N + M) \log N) \approx 10 \times 4 \cdot 10^5 \times 19 \approx 7.6 \cdot 10^7\) operations (\(\approx 120\text{ ms}\), well within the \(4.0\text{ s}\) limit!).

// Assumes SCC condensation has already produced DAG adjacency `dag_adj[1..C]`
// in topological order `topo` (where reachable SCCs from scc_start are marked),
// and `W[i]` is the sum of X_v in SCC i.
long long solve_abc214h(int C, int scc_start, int K, const vector&lt;long long&gt;& W,
                        const vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;& dag_adj, const vector&lt;int&gt;& topo,
                        const vector&lt;bool&gt;& reachable) {
    int S = 2 * C + 1, T = 2 * C + 2;
    PrimalDualMCMF mcmf(2 * C + 2);
    mcmf.add_edge(S, scc_start, K, 0);

    const long long INF = 4e18;
    mcmf.pi.assign(2 * C + 3, INF);
    mcmf.pi[S] = 0;
    mcmf.pi[scc_start] = 0;

    for (int u : topo) {
        if (!reachable[u]) continue;
        // Harvest arc (cap 1, cost -W[u]) + Bypass arc (cap K-1, cost 0)
        mcmf.add_edge(u, u + C, 1, -W[u]);
        if (K &gt; 1) mcmf.add_edge(u, u + C, K - 1, 0);
        mcmf.add_edge(u + C, T, K, 0);

        // O(V + E) Topological DP for Johnson potential pi_0
        mcmf.pi[u + C] = mcmf.pi[u] - W[u];
        mcmf.pi[T] = min(mcmf.pi[T], mcmf.pi[u + C]);
        for (int v : dag_adj[u]) {
            mcmf.add_edge(u + C, v, K, 0);
            mcmf.pi[v] = min(mcmf.pi[v], mcmf.pi[u + C]);
        }
    }
    return -mcmf.solve(S, T, K).second;
}

6. Worked Examples & Full 8-Problem Difficulty Ladder (#113–#120)
We now apply our planar duality toolkit across the complete 8-problem curriculum ladder from problem_archive.md (#113–#120), progressing from standard triangulated and directed grids (Stepping Stone), to 8-connected King’s graph barriers and coordinate-compressed infinite boards (Intermediate), to continuous potential Coarea collapse and Reif’s interior planar cut (Advanced), and finally to multi-terminal boundary DP and multi-level planar minimax flooding (Mastery).


6.1 [Stepping Stone #113] Luogu P4001 / BZOJ 1001 — [BJWC2006 / ICPC-Beijing 2006] 狼抓兔子 (Wolves Catching Rabbits)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P4001
Constraints: \(N, M \le 1000\), undirected edge weights \(w_e \in [1, 10^6]\). Time Limit: \(1.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
We are given an \(N \times M\) grid (\(V \le 10^6\)) with horizontal, vertical, and main-diagonal (\ top-left to bottom-right) edges, totaling \(E \approx 3 \times 10^6\) edges. We must find the minimum number of wolves (minimum edge cut capacity) needed to block all paths of rabbits from the top-left corner \(s = (1, 1)\) to the bottom-right corner \(t = (N, M)\).
Because \(V = 10^6\) and \(E = 3 \times 10^6\), running Dinic’s algorithm directly risks TLE on strict judges, whereas the graph is \((s, t)\)-planar with \(2(N-1)(M-1)\) triangular interior faces!


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Topological Representation

Degenerate Guard (\(N = 1\) or \(M = 1\)): When \(N = 1\) or \(M = 1\), there are \(0\) interior faces (\(2(N-1)(M-1) = 0\)); the primal graph is just a single path of \(\max(N, M) - 1\) edges, and the minimum cut is simply the minimum edge weight along that path!
Triangulated Dual Graph (\(N, M \ge 2\)): Each grid square \((r, c)\) (\(1 \le r &lt; N, 1 \le c &lt; M\)) is split by its diagonal edge (r, c) -- (r+1, c+1) into two triangular faces:

Lower-Left Triangle \(F_{r, c, 0}\): bounded by the left vertical edge, the bottom horizontal edge, and the diagonal edge of cell \((r, c)\).
Upper-Right Triangle \(F_{r, c, 1}\): bounded by the top horizontal edge, the right vertical edge, and the diagonal edge of cell \((r, c)\).


     (r, c) -------- top horiz -------- (r, c+1)
       |  \                                |
       |    \        Upper-Right           |
     left     \       F_{r,c,1}          right
     vert    diag                        vert
       |          \                        |
       |  F_{r,c,0} \                      |
       |  Lower-Left  \                    |
    (r+1, c) ------ bottom horiz ----- (r+1, c+1)


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)
Using Convention I (\(S^*\) on the Bottom-Left boundary, \(T^*\) on the Top-Right boundary):

Super-Source \(S^*\) (id = 0): connected to the left vertical edge of every leftmost lower-left triangle \(F_{r, 1, 0}\) (\(1 \le r &lt; N\)) and the bottom horizontal edge of every bottommost lower-left triangle \(F_{N-1, c, 0}\) (\(1 \le c &lt; M\)).
Super-Sink \(T^*\) (id = 2*(N-1)*(M-1) + 1): connected to the top horizontal edge of every topmost upper-right triangle \(F_{1, c, 1}\) (\(1 \le c &lt; M\)) and the right vertical edge of every rightmost upper-right triangle \(F_{r, M-1, 1}\) (\(1 \le r &lt; N\)).
Internal Dual Edges:

Horizontal edge (r, c) -- (r, c+1) (\(2 \le r \le N-1\)): connects \(F_{r-1, c, 0} \leftrightarrow F_{r, c, 1}\).
Vertical edge (r, c) -- (r+1, c) (\(2 \le c \le M-1\)): connects \(F_{r, c-1, 1} \leftrightarrow F_{r, c, 0}\).
Diagonal edge (r, c) -- (r+1, c+1): connects \(F_{r, c, 0} \leftrightarrow F_{r, c, 1}\).




Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

On a \(2 \times 2\) grid (\(N = 2, M = 2\)), there are \(2(1)(1) = 2\) interior triangles: \(F_{1,1,0}\) (connected to \(S^*\) via left vertical and bottom horizontal edges) and \(F_{1,1,1}\) (connected to \(T^*\) via top horizontal and right vertical edges), linked to each other by the diagonal edge of \((1, 1)\).
Any cut is a path \(S^* \leadsto T^*\): either two edges incident to \(S^*\)/\(T^*\) that bypass the diagonal, or one edge at \(S^*\) + the diagonal + one edge at \(T^*\), matching all \(4\) minimal \((s, t)\)-cuts of the \(2 \times 2\) triangulated square!







NoteProgressive Hints for #113 (Luogu P4001)






Hint 1 (Degenerate Case): What happens if \(N = 1\) or \(M = 1\)? Check the input format: when \(N = 1\), there are \(M - 1\) horizontal edges and \(0\) vertical/diagonal edges. Handle \(\min(N, M) == 1\) before building the dual graph!
Hint 2 (Face Indexing): For \(1 \le r &lt; N\) and \(1 \le c &lt; M\), map the lower-left triangle of cell \((r, c)\) to id0(r, c) = 2 * ((r - 1) * (M - 1) + (c - 1)) + 1 and the upper-right triangle to id1(r, c) = id0(r, c) + 1.
Hint 3 (Boundary Attachment): Left-perimeter vertical edges touch id0(r, 1) \(\to\) connect to \(S^*\). Bottom-perimeter horizontal edges touch id0(N - 1, c) \(\to\) connect to \(S^*\). Top-perimeter horizontal edges touch id1(1, c) \(\to\) connect to \(T^*\). Right-perimeter vertical edges touch id1(r, M - 1) \(\to\) connect to \(T^*\).










TipFull C++ Solution for #113 (Luogu P4001 — Wolves Catching Rabbits)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 1e18;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N, M;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; N &gt;&gt; M)) return 0;

    // Guard against degenerate 1D grid (N == 1 or M == 1)
    if (N == 1 || M == 1) {
        long long ans = INF, w;
        int cnt = max(N, M) - 1;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; cnt; ++i) {
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            ans = min(ans, w);
        }
        cout &lt;&lt; (cnt == 0 ? 0 : ans) &lt;&lt; "\n";
        return 0;
    }

    int R = N - 1, C = M - 1;
    int S = 0, T = 2 * R * C + 1;
    auto id0 = [&](int r, int c) { return 2 * (r * C + c) + 1; }; // Lower-Left (0-indexed r,c)
    auto id1 = [&](int r, int c) { return 2 * (r * C + c) + 2; }; // Upper-Right

    vector&lt;vector&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt;&gt; adj(T + 1);
    auto add_edge = [&](int u, int v, int w) {
        adj[u].push_back({v, w});
        adj[v].push_back({u, w});
    };

    // 1. Horizontal edges: N rows, M-1 per row
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; C; ++c) {
            int w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            if (r == 0) add_edge(id1(0, c), T, w);
            else if (r == N - 1) add_edge(S, id0(R - 1, c), w);
            else add_edge(id0(r - 1, c), id1(r, c), w);
        }
    }

    // 2. Vertical edges: N-1 rows, M per row
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; R; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
            int w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            if (c == 0) add_edge(S, id0(r, 0), w);
            else if (c == M - 1) add_edge(id1(r, C - 1), T, w);
            else add_edge(id1(r, c - 1), id0(r, c), w);
        }
    }

    // 3. Diagonal edges: N-1 rows, M-1 per row
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; R; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; C; ++c) {
            int w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            add_edge(id0(r, c), id1(r, c), w);
        }
    }

    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist(T + 1, INF);
    priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
    dist[S] = 0;
    pq.push({0, S});

    while (!pq.empty()) {
        auto [d, u] = pq.top();
        pq.pop();
        if (d &gt; dist[u]) continue;
        if (u == T) break;
        for (auto [v, w] : adj[u]) {
            if (dist[u] + w &lt; dist[v]) {
                dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
                pq.push({dist[v], v});
            }
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; dist[T] &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.2 [Stepping Stone #114] BZOJ 2007 / Luogu P2046 Prelim — [SCOI2010] 平面图 (Directed Grid Dual) & HDU 3870 (Catch the Theves)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P2046 (Directed Grid Core) / HDU 3870 (Undirected Square Grid \(N \le 400\))
Constraints: \((N+1) \times (N+1)\) vertices (\(N \le 500\)), \(4 N (N+1)\) directed edges (East, West, South, North). Time Limit: \(1.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Unlike #113 (where edges are undirected and triangulated), here we have an \((N+1) \times (N+1)\) square grid where every grid segment has two independent directed capacities (East \(w_E\) vs. West \(w_W\), and South \(w_S\) vs. North \(w_N\)). We need the minimum directed \((s, t)\)-cut from top-left \(s = (0, 0)\) to bottom-right \(t = (N, N)\).
(Note: Its undirected cousin HDU 3870 — Catch the Theves is the special case where \(w_E = w_W\) and \(w_S = w_N\) on an \(N \times N\) square grid.)


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Topological Representation
Apply Theorem 2.2 (Convention I: \(S^*\) at Bottom-Left, \(T^*\) at Top-Right, \(90^\circ\) Counter-Clockwise rotation). For each interior square cell \(F_{r, c}\) (\(1 \le r, c \le N\)), a directed primal edge \(e = (u \to v)\) compiles to a directed dual arc \(e^* = (f_L \to f_R)\) from the face on the left of \(u \to v\) to the face on the right of \(u \to v\).


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical 4-Direction Compilation)
Using the exact 4-direction table derived in Section 2.3:

East Primal Edge \((r, c-1) \to (r, c)\): Left is above (\(F_{r, c}\) or \(T^*\)), Right is below (\(F_{r+1, c}\) or \(S^*\)) \(\implies\) Dual arc South \(F_{r, c} \to F_{r+1, c}\)? Wait—let us check carefully!

Stand at \((r, c-1)\) and look East toward \((r, c)\):
Your Left hand points North (into cell \(F_{r, c}\), or \(T^*\) if \(r = 0\)).
Your Right hand points South (into cell \(F_{r+1, c}\), or \(S^*\) if \(r = N\)).
Wait: if \(u \in V_S\) and \(v \in V_T\), then \(V_S\) is at \((r, c-1)\) (Left of the cut) and \(V_T\) is at \((r, c)\) (Right of the cut). As our dual path walks from \(S^*\) (Bottom-Left) to \(T^*\) (Top-Right), \(V_S\) is on its Left and \(V_T\) is on its Right!
Therefore, when the dual path walks North from \(F_{r+1, c}\) (South) to \(F_{r, c}\) (North), its Left hand is \((r, c-1) \in V_S\) and its Right hand is \((r, c) \in V_T\)!
Thus an East primal edge \((r, c-1) \to (r, c)\) is forward-cut when the dual path steps North: \(F_{r+1, c} \to F_{r, c}\) (\(90^\circ\) CCW rotation of East is North!).

Summary of \(90^\circ\) CCW Dual Arcs (\(S^* \leadsto T^*\)):

East primal edge \((r, c-1) \to (r, c)\) \(\xrightarrow{90^\circ \text{ CCW}}\) North dual arc \(F_{r+1, c} \to F_{r, c}\) (with \(F_{N+1, c} \equiv S^*\) and \(F_{0, c} \equiv T^*\)).
South primal edge \((r-1, c) \to (r, c)\) \(\xrightarrow{90^\circ \text{ CCW}}\) East dual arc \(F_{r, c} \to F_{r, c+1}\) (with \(F_{r, 0} \equiv S^*\) and \(F_{r, N+1} \equiv T^*\)).
West primal edge \((r, c) \to (r, c-1)\) \(\xrightarrow{90^\circ \text{ CCW}}\) South dual arc \(F_{r, c} \to F_{r+1, c}\).
North primal edge \((r, c) \to (r-1, c)\) \(\xrightarrow{90^\circ \text{ CCW}}\) West dual arc \(F_{r, c+1} \to F_{r, c}\).




Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard
Look at how clean the \(90^\circ\) CCW rule is:

Every East primal edge becomes a North dual edge (\(S^* \to \dots \to T^*\)).
Every South primal edge becomes an East dual edge (\(S^* \to \dots \to T^*\)).
Since \(s = (0, 0)\) is Top-Left and \(t = (N, N)\) is Bottom-Right, all forward primal flow goes East and South, so all forward-cut dual edges go North and East—straight from \(S^*\) (Bottom-Left) to \(T^*\) (Top-Right)! If you ever see an East primal edge pointing away from \(T^*\) toward \(S^*\), your rotation is backwards (FM-15A)!







NoteProgressive Hints for #114 (Directed Grid Dual)






Hint 1: Number the \(N \times N\) interior square faces \(F_{r, c}\) for \(1 \le r, c \le N\) as id(r, c) = (r - 1) * N + c, with \(S^* = 0\) and \(T^* = N^2 + 1\).
Hint 2: Write a helper face(r, c) that automatically returns \(S^*\) whenever \(r = N + 1\) or \(c = 0\), and returns \(T^*\) whenever \(r = 0\) or \(c = N + 1\)!
Hint 3: With face(r, c) defined, the 4 edge groups require zero if/else boundary branches: East \((r, c)\) is add_arc(face(r + 1, c), face(r, c), w), South \((r, c)\) is add_arc(face(r, c), face(r, c + 1), w), West \((r, c)\) is add_arc(face(r, c), face(r + 1, c), w), and North \((r, c)\) is add_arc(face(r, c + 1), face(r, c), w).










TipFull C++ Solution for #114 (Directed Grid Dual / SCOI2010 Core)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 1e18;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; N)) return 0;

    int S = 0, T = N * N + 1;
    // Universal boundary-aware face lookup:
    // r in [0, N+1], c in [0, N+1]
    auto face = [&](int r, int c) -&gt; int {
        if (r == N + 1 || c == 0) return S; // Bottom or Left outer boundary -&gt; S*
        if (r == 0 || c == N + 1) return T; // Top or Right outer boundary -&gt; T*
        return (r - 1) * N + c;             // Interior square face (1 &lt;= r, c &lt;= N)
    };

    vector&lt;vector&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt;&gt; adj(T + 1);
    auto add_arc = [&](int u, int v, int w) {
        adj[u].push_back({v, w});
    };

    // 1. East edges: (r, c-1) -&gt; (r, c) for r in [0, N], c in [1, N]
    // 90 deg CCW of East is North: face(r + 1, c) -&gt; face(r, c)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 1; c &lt;= N; ++c) {
            int w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            add_arc(face(r + 1, c), face(r, c), w);
        }
    }

    // 2. South edges: (r-1, c) -&gt; (r, c) for r in [1, N], c in [0, N]
    // 90 deg CCW of South is East: face(r, c) -&gt; face(r, c + 1)
    for (int r = 1; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt;= N; ++c) {
            int w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            add_arc(face(r, c), face(r, c + 1), w);
        }
    }

    // 3. West edges: (r, c) -&gt; (r, c-1) for r in [0, N], c in [1, N]
    // 90 deg CCW of West is South: face(r, c) -&gt; face(r + 1, c)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 1; c &lt;= N; ++c) {
            int w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            add_arc(face(r, c), face(r + 1, c), w);
        }
    }

    // 4. North edges: (r, c) -&gt; (r-1, c) for r in [1, N], c in [0, N]
    // 90 deg CCW of North is West: face(r, c + 1) -&gt; face(r, c)
    for (int r = 1; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt;= N; ++c) {
            int w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            add_arc(face(r, c + 1), face(r, c), w);
        }
    }

    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist(T + 1, INF);
    priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
    dist[S] = 0;
    pq.push({0, S});

    while (!pq.empty()) {
        auto [d, u] = pq.top();
        pq.pop();
        if (d &gt; dist[u]) continue;
        if (u == T) break;
        for (auto [v, w] : adj[u]) {
            if (dist[u] + w &lt; dist[v]) {
                dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
                pq.push({dist[v], v});
            }
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; dist[T] &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.3 [Intermediate #115] Codeforces 1749E — Cactus Wall

Judge URL: https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1749/E
Constraints: \(\sum N \cdot M \le 2 \cdot 10^5\). Grid of . (empty) and # (existing cactus). Time Limit: \(2.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
We want to place the minimum number of new cacti # on empty cells so that no 4-connected path of . cells exists from the top row (\(r = 1\)) to the bottom row (\(r = N\)), subject to the independent-set constraint that no two cacti may share a side (no 4-adjacent # cells!).
Notice that this is a minimum vertex cut on an \(N \times M\) grid where selected cut vertices must form an independent set! Vertex-splitting + Dinic is completely impossible because the independent-set constraint between cut vertices is non-submodular on a general cut. Why does planar duality solve it in \(O(NM)\) time?


Step 2: Intermediate Topological & Algebraic Representation

4-Connected Primal Path \(\Longleftrightarrow\) 8-Connected Dual Barrier (Digital Topology Duality): By discrete Jordan curve duality on a 2D pixel grid, blocking all 4-connected (orthogonal) paths of . from row \(1\) to row \(N\) is equivalent to building an 8-connected (orthogonal + diagonal King’s move) barrier of # cells from column \(1\) (\(c = 1\)) to column \(M\) (\(c = M\))!
How the Independent-Set Rule Simplifies 8-Connectivity to Pure Diagonal Moves:

In our barrier of # cells from column \(1\) to column \(M\), no two # cells on the grid may be 4-adjacent (orthogonally adjacent).
Therefore, our 8-connected barrier cannot take orthogonal steps! Every step along the barrier from (r, c) to the next cactus must be one of the 4 diagonal moves (r ± 1, c ± 1).

The Chessboard Parity Invariant (\((r + c) \bmod 2\)): Wait: if we find a shortest purely diagonal path of valid cells (cells not orthogonally adjacent to any pre-existing #) from column \(1\) to column \(M\), could two non-consecutive vertices on this diagonal path accidentally be 4-adjacent to each other?

No! Every diagonal move \((r \pm 1, c \pm 1)\) changes both \(r\) and \(c\) by \(\pm 1\), which preserves the chessboard color parity \((r + c) \bmod 2\)!
Thus every cell on a diagonal path has the exact same parity \((r + c) \bmod 2\), whereas any two 4-adjacent cells on a grid have opposite parities!
Consequently, any simple diagonal path of valid cells from column \(1\) to column \(M\) is automatically free of internal 4-adjacencies!




Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical 0-1 BFS Compilation)

Mark a cell \((r, c)\) as valid (valid[r][c] = true) if neither \((r, c)\) nor any of its 4 orthogonal neighbors \((r \pm 1, c), (r, c \pm 1)\) contains a pre-existing # (wait: \((r, c)\) itself can contain a pre-existing #—since the input guarantees no two pre-existing # are 4-adjacent, a pre-existing # has no orthogonal # neighbors and costs \(0\) to use!).

Specifically: \((r, c)\) is forbidden if and only if at least one of its 4 orthogonal neighbors \((r \pm 1, c), (r, c \pm 1)\) already has a #.

For each non-forbidden cell \((r, c)\):

Vertex cost \(w(r, c) = 0\) if grid[r][c] == '#', else \(1\) if grid[r][c] == '.'.

Run 0-1 BFS from all non-forbidden cells in column \(1\) (\(c = 0\) in 0-indexed) using only the 4 diagonal transitions \((r \pm 1, c \pm 1)\) to find the minimum-cost path to column \(M - 1\), and reconstruct the path via parent pointers!



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard
Suppose \(N = 2, M = 3\) with # at \((0, 1)\).

Orthogonal neighbors of \((0, 1)\) are \((0, 0), (0, 2), (1, 1)\)—all three are forbidden.
Valid cells: \((1, 0)\) (cost \(1\)), \((0, 1)\) (existing #, cost \(0\)), and \((1, 2)\) (cost \(1\)).
Diagonal path from column \(0\) to column \(2\): \((1, 0) \to (0, 1) \to (1, 2)\) has total cost \(1 + 0 + 1 = 2\), and all three cells have \((r + c) \bmod 2 = 1\), so no two are 4-adjacent!







NoteProgressive Hints for #115 (CF 1749E — Cactus Wall)






Hint 1: Any path of empty cells from the top row to the bottom row uses 4-connectivity. By grid duality, a cut that blocks all 4-connected top-to-bottom paths is an 8-connected left-to-right path of cacti from column \(1\) to column \(M\).
Hint 2: Because no two cacti can be 4-adjacent, an 8-connected cactus path can only step diagonally (r ± 1, c ± 1). Why don’t we need to worry about a diagonal path curving back and touching itself orthogonally? Check \((r + c) \bmod 2\)!
Hint 3: Precompute which cells have an existing # in one of their 4 orthogonal neighbors—those cells can never be stepped on. All other cells have weight \(0\) (if #) or \(1\) (if .). Run 0-1 BFS from column \(0\) to column \(M - 1\) and print the reconstructed grid!










TipFull C++ Solution for #115 (CF 1749E — Cactus Wall)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const int INF = 1e9;
const int dr4[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
const int dc4[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};
const int drDiag[4] = {-1, -1, 1, 1};
const int dcDiag[4] = {-1, 1, -1, 1};

void solve() {
    int n, m;
    cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; m;
    vector&lt;string&gt; s(n);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; s[i];

    auto valid = [&](int r, int c) {
        if (r &lt; 0 || r &gt;= n || c &lt; 0 || c &gt;= m) return false;
        for (int d = 0; d &lt; 4; ++d) {
            int nr = r + dr4[d], nc = c + dc4[d];
            if (nr &gt;= 0 && nr &lt; n && nc &gt;= 0 && nc &lt; m && s[nr][nc] == '#')
                return false;
        }
        return true;
    };

    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; dist(n, vector&lt;int&gt;(m, INF));
    vector&lt;vector&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt;&gt; par(n, vector&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt;(m, {-1, -1}));
    deque&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt; dq;

    for (int r = 0; r &lt; n; ++r) {
        if (valid(r, 0)) {
            int w = (s[r][0] == '.' ? 1 : 0);
            dist[r][0] = w;
            if (w == 0) dq.push_front({r, 0});
            else dq.push_back({r, 0});
        }
    }

    while (!dq.empty()) {
        auto [r, c] = dq.front();
        dq.pop_front();
        for (int d = 0; d &lt; 4; ++d) {
            int nr = r + drDiag[d], nc = c + dcDiag[d];
            if (!valid(nr, nc)) continue;
            int w = (s[nr][nc] == '.' ? 1 : 0);
            if (dist[r][c] + w &lt; dist[nr][nc]) {
                dist[nr][nc] = dist[r][c] + w;
                par[nr][nc] = {r, c};
                if (w == 0) dq.push_front({nr, nc});
                else dq.push_back({nr, nc});
            }
        }
    }

    int best_r = -1;
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; n; ++r) {
        if (dist[r][m - 1] &lt; INF && (best_r == -1 || dist[r][m - 1] &lt; dist[best_r][m - 1])) {
            best_r = r;
        }
    }

    if (best_r == -1) {
        cout &lt;&lt; "NO\n";
        return;
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; "YES\n";
    int cr = best_r, cc = m - 1;
    while (cr != -1) {
        s[cr][cc] = '#';
        tie(cr, cc) = par[cr][cc];
    }
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) cout &lt;&lt; s[i] &lt;&lt; "\n";
}

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int T;
    if (cin &gt;&gt; T) {
        while (T--) solve();
    }
    return 0;
}







6.4 [Intermediate #116] Codeforces 1765I — Infinite Chess

Judge URL: https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1765/I
Constraints: Board of \(8\) rows (\(y \in [1, 8]\)) and \(10^8\) columns (\(x \in [1, 10^8]\)). \(N \le 15\) static white pieces (K, Q, R, B, N). Time Limit: \(2.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
A Black King moves (8-connected King moves, 1 step per turn) from \((x_S, y_S)\) to \((x_T, y_T)\) on an \(8 \times 10^8\) chessboard without stepping onto any square attacked by \(N \le 15\) static white pieces (the Black King may capture a white piece if that square is not defended by another white piece).
Because \(X_{\max} = 10^8\), an explicit BFS over all \(8 \times 10^8\) squares will MLE/TLE. However, from the planar dual perspective, an obstacle blocking the King from reaching \((x_T, y_T)\) is a vertical/diagonal barrier of attacked squares spanning from \(y = 1\) to \(y = 8\), and because \(N \le 15\) and the board height is only \(8\), the attack geometry only changes within a radius of \(\pm 8\) columns around each piece!


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Topological Representation
Let us analyze the board columns \(x \in [1, 10^8]\) around the \(N + 2 \le 17\) special \(x\)-coordinates (the \(N\) white pieces plus \(x_S\) and \(x_T\)):

Any diagonal attack ray from a Queen or Bishop at \((x_i, y_i)\) starts at \(y_i \in [1, 8]\) and leaves the 8-row board within \(|x - x_i| \le 7\) columns!
Outside the window \([x_i - 8, x_i + 8]\) around each special \(x\)-coordinate, only horizontal Rook/Queen rays exist, and those horizontal rays are completely invariant across the entire open column interval \((X_k, X_{k+1})\) between two consecutive retained columns!



Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Coordinate Compression + Weighted Dijkstra)

Collect all columns \(x \in [1, 10^8]\) such that \(|x - x^*| \le 8\) for at least one \(x^* \in \{x_S, x_T, x_1, \dots, x_N\}\).
Sort and deduplicate these columns into \(X_0 &lt; X_1 &lt; \dots &lt; X_{K-1}\) (where \(K \le 17 \times 17 = 289\)).
For each retained column \(X_k\) and row \(y \in [1, 8]\), compute safe(X_k, y) by simulating the attacks of the \(N\) white pieces on the original board (remembering that pieces block Queen/Rook/Bishop rays!).
Build a weighted graph on the \(8 K \le 2312\) safe states \((k, y)\):

If \(X_{k+1} = X_k + 1\) (adjacent board columns): add standard 1-step King edges of weight \(1\) between \((k, y)\) and \((k \pm 1, y + \Delta y)\) for \(\Delta y \in \{-1, 0, 1\}\).
If \(X_{k+1} &gt; X_k + 1\) (a compressed empty gap of width \(\Delta X = X_{k+1} - X_k\)): because every column inside \([X_k, X_{k+1}]\) has the exact same safe rows (no piece or diagonal ray exists in the gap!), a King at a safe row \((k, y)\) can walk straight horizontally across the gap to \((k+1, y)\) in \(\Delta X = X_{k+1} - X_k\) steps! (Within \(X_k\) and \(X_{k+1}\) themselves, the King can still move vertically \((k, y) \leftrightarrow (k, y \pm 1)\) at cost \(1\).)

Run Dijkstra from \((k_S, y_S)\) to \((k_T, y_T)\) in \(O(K \log K)\) time!



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Failure Mode: What if a Queen at \((50, 4)\) is blocked on its right by a Knight at \((55, 4)\)? When checking whether a gap column \(x \in (X_k, X_{k+1})\) is attacked horizontally by a Rook/Queen, you must check whether any white piece sits strictly between the Rook/Queen and column \(x\) on the same row \(y\)! Since our retained columns include \(x^* \pm 1\) for every piece, simply testing attacks at the real board coordinate \((X_k, y)\) automatically respects ray blocking by all \(N\) pieces!







NoteProgressive Hints for #116 (CF 1765I — Infinite Chess)






Hint 1: Since \(y \in [1, 8]\), any diagonal ray (Bishop/Queen) or Knight/King attack from a piece at \((x_i, y_i)\) can only hit the board within columns \([x_i - 8, x_i + 8]\).
Hint 2: Keep all columns within \(\pm 8\) of \(x_S, x_T\), and every white piece’s \(x_i\). Between two consecutive kept columns \(X_k\) and \(X_{k+1}\) with \(X_{k+1} &gt; X_k + 1\), no piece and no diagonal attack exists—only horizontal Rook/Queen rays, which are identical at \(X_k\) and \(X_{k+1}\)!
Hint 3: Connect adjacent kept columns (\(X_{k+1} = X_k + 1\)) with standard 8-directional King moves of cost \(1\), and connect gap columns (\(X_{k+1} &gt; X_k + 1\)) horizontally \((k, y) \leftrightarrow (k+1, y)\) with cost \(X_{k+1} - X_k\) whenever row \(y\) is safe at both \(X_k\) and \(X_{k+1}\).










TipFull C++ Solution for #116 (CF 1765I — Infinite Chess)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 1e18;

struct Piece {
    char type;
    long long x;
    int y;
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    long long xS, xT;
    int yS, yT;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; xS &gt;&gt; yS &gt;&gt; xT &gt;&gt; yT)) return 0;

    int n;
    cin &gt;&gt; n;
    vector&lt;Piece&gt; pcs(n);
    vector&lt;long long&gt; xs;
    auto add_window = [&](long long x) {
        for (long long d = -8; d &lt;= 8; ++d) {
            if (x + d &gt;= 1 && x + d &lt;= 100000000LL)
                xs.push_back(x + d);
        }
    };
    add_window(xS);
    add_window(xT);

    set&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt; occupied;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        cin &gt;&gt; pcs[i].type &gt;&gt; pcs[i].x &gt;&gt; pcs[i].y;
        add_window(pcs[i].x);
        occupied.insert({pcs[i].x, pcs[i].y});
    }

    sort(xs.begin(), xs.end());
    xs.erase(unique(xs.begin(), xs.end()), xs.end());
    int K = xs.size();

    auto is_safe = [&](long long x, int y) -&gt; bool {
        for (const auto& p : pcs) {
            if (p.x == x && p.y == y) continue; // Capturing the piece itself is allowed if undefended
            long long dx = x - p.x, dy = y - p.y;
            long long adx = abs(dx), ady = abs(dy);
            if (p.type == 'K') {
                if (max(adx, ady) &lt;= 1) return false;
            } else if (p.type == 'N') {
                if ((adx == 1 && ady == 2) || (adx == 2 && ady == 1)) return false;
            } else {
                bool ortho = (dx == 0 || dy == 0);
                bool diag = (adx == ady);
                if ((p.type == 'R' && ortho) || (p.type == 'B' && diag) || (p.type == 'Q' && (ortho || diag))) {
                    long long sx = (dx == 0 ? 0 : (dx &gt; 0 ? 1 : -1));
                    int sy = (dy == 0 ? 0 : (dy &gt; 0 ? 1 : -1));
                    bool blocked = false;
                    for (const auto& q : pcs) {
                        if (q.x == p.x && q.y == p.y) continue;
                        if (q.x == x && q.y == y) continue;
                        long long qdx = q.x - p.x, qdy = q.y - p.y;
                        // Check if q lies strictly on the ray segment from p to (x, y)
                        if (sx == 0 && qdx == 0 && qdy * sy &gt; 0 && abs(qdy) &lt; ady) blocked = true;
                        else if (sy == 0 && qdy == 0 && qdx * sx &gt; 0 && abs(qdx) &lt; adx) blocked = true;
                        else if (sx != 0 && sy != 0 && qdx * sx &gt; 0 && qdy * sy &gt; 0 && abs(qdx) == abs(qdy) && abs(qdx) &lt; adx)
                            blocked = true;
                    }
                    if (!blocked) return false;
                }
            }
        }
        return true;
    };

    vector&lt;vector&lt;bool&gt;&gt; safe(K, vector&lt;bool&gt;(9, false));
    for (int k = 0; k &lt; K; ++k)
        for (int y = 1; y &lt;= 8; ++y)
            safe[k][y] = is_safe(xs[k], y);

    auto id = [&](int k, int y) { return k * 8 + (y - 1); };
    vector&lt;vector&lt;pair&lt;int, long long&gt;&gt;&gt; adj(K * 8);

    for (int k = 0; k &lt; K; ++k) {
        for (int y = 1; y &lt;= 8; ++y) {
            if (!safe[k][y]) continue;
            // Vertical moves within the same column
            for (int ny : {y - 1, y + 1}) {
                if (ny &gt;= 1 && ny &lt;= 8 && safe[k][ny])
                    adj[id(k, y)].push_back({id(k, ny), 1});
            }
            // Moves to next column k + 1
            if (k + 1 &lt; K) {
                long long gap = xs[k + 1] - xs[k];
                if (gap == 1) {
                    for (int ny = max(1, y - 1); ny &lt;= min(8, y + 1); ++ny) {
                        if (safe[k + 1][ny]) {
                            adj[id(k, y)].push_back({id(k + 1, ny), 1});
                            adj[id(k + 1, ny)].push_back({id(k, y), 1});
                        }
                    }
                } else {
                    if (safe[k + 1][y]) {
                        adj[id(k, y)].push_back({id(k + 1, y), gap});
                        adj[id(k + 1, y)].push_back({id(k, y), gap});
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    int kS = lower_bound(xs.begin(), xs.end(), xS) - xs.begin();
    int kT = lower_bound(xs.begin(), xs.end(), xT) - xs.begin();
    int start = id(kS, yS), target = id(kT, yT);

    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist(K * 8, INF);
    priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
    dist[start] = 0;
    pq.push({0, start});

    while (!pq.empty()) {
        auto [d, u] = pq.top();
        pq.pop();
        if (d &gt; dist[u]) continue;
        if (u == target) break;
        for (auto [v, w] : adj[u]) {
            if (dist[u] + w &lt; dist[v]) {
                dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
                pq.push({dist[v], v});
            }
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; (dist[target] == INF ? -1 : dist[target]) &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.5 [Advanced #117] Luogu P2046 — [NOI2010] 海拔 (Altitude)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P2046
Constraints: \(N \le 500\) (\((N+1) \times (N+1)\) intersections, \(4 N (N+1)\) directed streets with traffic \(w_{uv} \in [0, 10^6]\)). Time Limit: \(1.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Each intersection \(v \in \{0, \dots, N\}^2\) must be assigned a real altitude \(h_v \in [0, 1]\) with \(h_{(0,0)} = 0\) and \(h_{(N,N)} = 1\) to minimize total uphill energy \(\sum_{(u \to v)} w_{uv} \max(0, h_v - h_u)\). As proved in Section 3, the combination of a continuous homogeneous 1-Lipschitz penalty \(\max(0, h_v - h_u)\) and a \(2.6 \times 10^5\)-vertex directed grid signals a two-stage reduction: Coarea continuous-to-binary collapse (Theorem 3.1) followed by \((s, t)\)-Planar Directed Dual Dijkstra (Theorem 2.2).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Topological Representation
By Theorem 3.1 (Section 3.2):
\[\min_{\substack{\mathbf{h} \in \mathbb{R}^V \\ h_s = 0, \, h_t = 1}} \sum_{(u \to v) \in E} w_{uv} \max(0, h_v - h_u) = \min_{\substack{x \in \{0, 1\}^V \\ x_s = 1, \, x_t = 0}} \sum_{(u \to v) \in E} w_{uv}\, x_u(1 - x_v) = \text{MinCut}_G(s, t).\]
Here \(x_u = 1\) corresponds to altitude \(h_u = 0\) (the \(V_S\) bank containing \(s = (0, 0)\)) and \(x_v = 0\) corresponds to altitude \(h_v = 1\) (the \(V_T\) bank containing \(t = (N, N)\)).


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Compilation)
Using Convention I (\(S^*\) on Bottom-Left boundary, \(T^*\) on Top-Right boundary, \(90^\circ\) CCW rotation of every directed primal street \(u \to v\)), the exact input order of [NOI2010] 海拔 is:

West \(\to\) East streets ((r, c-1) -&gt; (r, c)): \(N + 1\) rows (\(r = 0 \dots N\)), \(N\) streets per row (\(c = 1 \dots N\)). \(90^\circ\) CCW rotation of East is North: face(r + 1, c) -&gt; face(r, c).
North \(\to\) South streets ((r-1, c) -&gt; (r, c)): \(N\) rows (\(r = 1 \dots N\)), \(N + 1\) streets per row (\(c = 0 \dots N\)). \(90^\circ\) CCW rotation of South is East: face(r, c) -&gt; face(r, c + 1).
East \(\to\) West streets ((r, c) -&gt; (r, c-1)): \(N + 1\) rows (\(r = 0 \dots N\)), \(N\) streets per row (\(c = 1 \dots N\)). \(90^\circ\) CCW rotation of West is South: face(r, c) -&gt; face(r + 1, c).
South \(\to\) North streets ((r, c) -&gt; (r-1, c)): \(N\) rows (\(r = 1 \dots N\)), \(N + 1\) streets per row (\(c = 0 \dots N\)). \(90^\circ\) CCW rotation of North is West: face(r, c + 1) -&gt; face(r, c).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard
We already traced both the \(2 \times 2\) directed grid (Section 2.4) and the fractional level-set integral (Section 3.4). Remember FM-15A and FM-15B: every dual arc is strictly one-way (add_arc(u, v, w)), never undirected!






