    };

    // 1. West -&gt; East: (r, c-1) -&gt; (r, c) =&gt; Dual North: face(r+1, c) -&gt; face(r, c)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 1; c &lt;= N; ++c) {
            int w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            add_arc(face(r + 1, c), face(r, c), w);
        }
    }
    // 2. North -&gt; South: (r-1, c) -&gt; (r, c) =&gt; Dual East: face(r, c) -&gt; face(r, c+1)
    for (int r = 1; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt;= N; ++c) {
            int w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            add_arc(face(r, c), face(r, c + 1), w);
        }
    }
    // 3. East -&gt; West: (r, c) -&gt; (r, c-1) =&gt; Dual South: face(r, c) -&gt; face(r+1, c)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 1; c &lt;= N; ++c) {
            int w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            add_arc(face(r, c), face(r + 1, c), w);
        }
    }
    // 4. South -&gt; North: (r, c) -&gt; (r-1, c) =&gt; Dual West: face(r, c+1) -&gt; face(r, c)
    for (int r = 1; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt;= N; ++c) {
            int w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            add_arc(face(r, c + 1), face(r, c), w);
        }
    }

    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist(T + 1, INF);
    priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
    dist[S] = 0;
    pq.push({0, S});

    while (!pq.empty()) {
        auto [d, u] = pq.top();
        pq.pop();
        if (d &gt; dist[u]) continue;
        if (u == T) break;
        for (auto [v, w] : adj[u]) {
            if (dist[u] + w &lt; dist[v]) {
                dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
                pq.push({dist[v], v});
            }
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; dist[T] &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.6 [Advanced #118] LOJ / ICPC Reif Benchmark — Interior \(s\)-\(t\) Planar Minimum Cut

Problem Archetype: Given an undirected planar graph \(G = (V, E, w)\) (\(V \le 5 \cdot 10^4\)) embedded in the plane (for instance, an \(R \times C\) cylindrical/annular grid or general planar mesh where \(s\) is an interior vertex on the inner ring and \(t\) is on the outer ring), compute \(\text{MinCut}_G(s, t)\) in \(O(V \log^2 V)\) time using Reif’s Cut-Path Duality (Theorem 5.1).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Neither \(s\) nor \(t\) shares a common face, so the graph is not \((s, t)\)-planar. Any minimum \((s, t)\)-cut in \(G\) is a minimum-weight separating cycle \(C^*\) in \(G^*\) that encloses \(s\) and excludes \(t\).


Step 2: Intermediate Topological & Algebraic Representation
Let \(P = (e_1, e_2, \dots, e_L)\) be a shortest unweighted \((s, t)\)-path in \(G\). Slicing the dual graph \(G^*\) along \(P^*\) splits each crossed dual edge \(e_i^*\) into a Left-bank portal \(e_{i, L}^*\) and a Right-bank portal \(e_{i, R}^*\). By Theorem 5.1:
\[\text{MinCut}_G(s, t) = \min_{1 \le i \le L} \text{dist}_{G_{\text{slice}}^*}\big(e_{i, L}^*, e_{i, R}^*\big),\]
and the optimal cycles \(C_i^* = \text{ShortestPath}_{G_{\text{slice}}^*}(e_{i, L}^*, e_{i, R}^*)\) are pairwise non-crossing.


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Divide-and-Conquer Dijkstra on Annular / Sliced Dual Graphs)
When \(G^*\) is sliced along \(P^*\), how do we implement Reif’s divide-and-conquer cleanly without complex planar subgraph splitting?

Notice that on an annular / concentric radial grid (where \(P\) is a radial ray of length \(L\) from the inner ring \(s\) to the outer ring \(t\)), slicing \(G^*\) along \(P^*\) unrolls the annulus into a planar strip \([0, L-1] \times [0, K]\) where Left-bank portals \((i, 0)\) sit on the top boundary of the strip and Right-bank portals \((i, K)\) sit on the bottom boundary of the strip!
In this unrolled planar strip, when we compute the shortest path \(C_{\text{mid}}^*\) from \((\text{mid}, 0)\) to \((\text{mid}, K)\), every vertex on \(C_{\text{mid}}^*\) separates indices \(i &lt; \text{mid}\) from indices \(i &gt; \text{mid}\). Even more generally, on any planar graph where we run Divide-and-Conquer Dijkstra over portal pairs \((e_{i, L}^*, e_{i, R}^*)\), we can pass the active vertex list V_active to each recursive call and partition V_active along the shortest path tree / cycle \(C_{\text{mid}}^*\)!



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Failure-Mode Guard: When cutting \(G^*\) along \(P^* = (e_1^*, \dots, e_L^*)\), where do we place the weight \(w(e_i)\) of the crossed primal edge \(e_i\)?

If \(e_i\) separates dual face \(f_{i, L}\) (on the left of \(P\)) from \(f_{i, R}\) (on the right of \(P\)), then in \(G^* \setminus P^*\) we simply delete the edges \(e_1^*, \dots, e_L^*\) from \(G^*\), and for each \(i \in \{1, \dots, L\}\) we query: \[w(e_i) + \text{dist}_{G^* \setminus P^*}\big(f_{i, L}, f_{i, R}\big)!\]
Look how clean that is: deleting \(P^*\) from \(G^*\) requires zero vertex duplication, and \(w(e_i) + \text{dist}_{G^* \setminus P^*}(f_{i, L}, f_{i, R})\) is the exact weight of the shortest cycle in \(G^*\) that crosses \(P^*\) solely at \(e_i^*\)! (And if a cycle crosses \(P^*\) at multiple edges \(e_{i_1}^*, e_{i_2}^*, \dots\), we also include shortcut edges \((f_{i, L}, f_{i, R})\) of weight \(w(e_i)\) for \(i \neq m\), or use the 2-layer parity graph \(G^* \times \{0, 1\}\)!)








NoteProgressive Hints for #118 (Reif’s Interior Planar Cut)






Hint 1: Choose a simple primal \((s, t)\)-path \(P = (e_1, \dots, e_L)\) where each primal edge \(e_i\) separates dual face \(f_{i, L}\) (Left of \(P\)) from \(f_{i, R}\) (Right of \(P\)).
Hint 2: Build the \(\mathbb{Z}_2\)-Parity Double Cover \(G^* \times \{0, 1\}\): every dual edge \(e^* \notin P^*\) connects \((u^*, p) \leftrightarrow (v^*, p)\) on the same parity layer \(p \in \{0, 1\}\), while every dual edge \(e_i^* \in P^*\) crosses layers \((f_{i, L}, p) \leftrightarrow (f_{i, R}, p \oplus 1)\) with weight \(w(e_i)\).
Hint 3: Then the shortest \(s\)-\(t\) separating cycle through \(f_{i, L}\) has weight \(\text{dist}_{G^* \times \{0, 1\}}\big((f_{i, L}, 0), (f_{i, L}, 1)\big)\), and by Reif’s non-crossing property on the unrolled strip, Divide-and-Conquer Dijkstra finds \(\min_{1 \le i \le L}\) in \(O(V \log^2 V)\) time!










TipFull C++ Reference Implementation for #118 (Reif’s Divide-and-Conquer Planar Cut on Annular/Concentric Grids)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 1e18;

// Unrolled Planar Strip representation of an Annular / Interior-s-t Planar Dual Graph:
// R rings (0 &lt;= r &lt; R, representing the L edges along the radial primal s-t path P)
// C sectors (0 &lt;= c &lt;= C, where c = 0 is the Left bank of P and c = C is the Right bank of P,
// linked across the cut P at column c = 0 / c = C by primal radial edge weight radial_w[r]).
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int R, C;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; R &gt;&gt; C)) return 0;

    // Vertex (r, c) in G* \ P* for 0 &lt;= r &lt; R, 0 &lt;= c &lt;= C
    // where (r, 0) = f_{r, L} and (r, C) = f_{r, R} are the two faces separated by e_r in P.
    int V = R * (C + 1);
    auto id = [&](int r, int c) { return r * (C + 1) + c; };

    vector&lt;vector&lt;pair&lt;int, long long&gt;&gt;&gt; adj(V);
    auto add_edge = [&](int u, int v, long long w) {
        adj[u].push_back({v, w});
        adj[v].push_back({u, w});
    };

    // 1. Dual edges within each ring (circumferential steps in G* \ P*)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; R; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; C; ++c) {
            long long w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            add_edge(id(r, c), id(r, c + 1), w);
        }
    }
    // 2. Dual edges between adjacent rings (radial steps in G* \ P*)
    for (int r = 0; r + 1 &lt; R; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt;= C; ++c) {
            long long w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            add_edge(id(r, c), id(r + 1, c), w);
        }
    }
    // 3. Weights of the L = R primal edges e_r along the s-t path P
    vector&lt;long long&gt; radial_w(R);
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; R; ++r) cin &gt;&gt; radial_w[r];

    long long ans = INF;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist(V, INF);
    vector&lt;int&gt; par(V, -1), region_tag(V, -1);
    int timer = 0;

    // Reif's Divide-and-Conquer over portal range [l, r_hi] inside active vertex set `nodes`
    auto solve = [&](auto& self, int l, int r_hi, const vector&lt;int&gt;& nodes) -&gt; void {
        if (l &gt; r_hi || nodes.empty()) return;
        int mid = (l + r_hi) / 2;
        int src = id(mid, 0), dst = id(mid, C);

        ++timer;
        for (int u : nodes) {
            dist[u] = INF;
            par[u] = -1;
            region_tag[u] = timer;
        }

        priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
        if (region_tag[src] == timer) {
            dist[src] = 0;
            pq.push({0, src});
        }

        while (!pq.empty()) {
            auto [d, u] = pq.top();
            pq.pop();
            if (d &gt; dist[u]) continue;
            for (auto [v, w] : adj[u]) {
                if (region_tag[v] != timer) continue;
                if (dist[u] + w &lt; dist[v]) {
                    dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
                    par[v] = u;
                    pq.push({dist[v], v});
                }
            }
        }

        if (region_tag[dst] == timer && dist[dst] &lt; INF) {
            ans = min(ans, dist[dst] + radial_w[mid]);
        }

        if (l == r_hi) return;

        // Partition active nodes along the shortest path separator (or ring coordinate relative to mid)
        vector&lt;int&gt; left_nodes, right_nodes;
        for (int u : nodes) {
            int r_coord = u / (C + 1);
            if (r_coord &lt;= mid) left_nodes.push_back(u);
            if (r_coord &gt;= mid) right_nodes.push_back(u);
        }
        self(self, l, mid - 1, left_nodes);
        self(self, mid + 1, r_hi, right_nodes);
    };

    vector&lt;int&gt; all_nodes(V);
    iota(all_nodes.begin(), all_nodes.end(), 0);
    solve(solve, 0, R - 1, all_nodes);

    cout &lt;&lt; ans &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.7 [Mastery #119] Luogu P7916 — [CSP-S 2021] 交通规划 (Traffic Planning)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P7916
Constraints: \(N, M \le 500\), \(T \le 50\) queries, \(\sum k \le 50\) (up to \(k \le 50\) colored perimeter terminals per query). Time Limit: \(3.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Each query places \(k \le 50\) colored terminals (\(t_r \in \{0, 1\}\)) around the outer perimeter slot \(p_r \in [1, 2N + 2M]\) of a static \(N \times M\) undirected planar grid. As derived in Section 4, this is a Multi-Terminal Outer-Boundary Planar Cut, solvable in \(O\big(k \cdot NM \log(NM) + k^3\big)\) per query via Non-Crossing Dual Interval DP.


Step 2: Intermediate Topological & Algebraic Representation
Let us map the \(2N + 2M\) clockwise perimeter slots \(p \in [1, 2N + 2M]\) of [CSP-S 2021] 交通规划 to their adjacent interior dual face adj_face(p) and perimeter edge weight perim_w(p):

Top border (\(1 \le p \le M\)): column \(c = p\) (\(1 \le c \le M\)).

For \(1 \le p \le M - 1\), the perimeter grid edge between \((1, p)\) and \((1, p+1)\) borders interior face \(F_{1, p}\).
For \(p = M\) (the top-right corner \((1, M)\)), the corner turns clockwise without an interior face (adjacent face is 0, weight 0).

Right border (\(M + 1 \le p \le M + N\)): row \(r = p - M\) (\(1 \le r \le N\)).

For \(M + 1 \le p \le M + N - 1\), the perimeter grid edge between \((r, M)\) and \((r+1, M)\) borders interior face \(F_{r, M - 1}\).
For \(p = M + N\) (bottom-right corner \((N, M)\)), corner turn (weight 0).

Bottom border (\(M + N + 1 \le p \le 2M + N\)): column \(c = M - (p - (M + N)) + 1\) (\(M \ge c \ge 1\)).

For \(M + N + 1 \le p \le 2M + N - 1\), the perimeter grid edge between \((N, c-1)\) and \((N, c)\) borders interior face \(F_{N - 1, c - 1}\).
For \(p = 2M + N\) (bottom-left corner \((N, 1)\)), corner turn (weight 0).

Left border (\(2M + N + 1 \le p \le 2M + 2N\)): row \(r = N - (p - (2M + N)) + 1\) (\(N \ge r \ge 1\)).

For \(2M + N + 1 \le p \le 2M + 2N - 1\), the perimeter grid edge between \((r-1, 1)\) and \((r, 1)\) borders interior face \(F_{r - 1, 1}\).
For \(p = 2M + 2N\) (top-left corner \((1, 1)\)), corner turn (weight 0).




Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Query-by-Query Graph Restoration + Interval DP)

Sort the \(k\) terminals by their clockwise perimeter slot \(p_0 &lt; p_1 &lt; \dots &lt; p_{k-1}\).
Create \(k\) boundary interval vertices \(b_0^*, b_1^*, \dots, b_{k-1}^*\), where \(b_i^*\) covers the clockwise perimeter slots from \(p_i\) to \(p_{(i+1)\bmod k} - 1\):

Connect \(b_{(i-1+k)\bmod k}^* \leftrightarrow b_i^*\) with weight \(x_i\) (the stem edge capacity of terminal \(i\) at slot \(p_i\)).
For every perimeter slot \(s\) in the clockwise interval from \(p_i\) to \(p_{(i+1)\bmod k} - 1\) that has an interior face adj_face(s) (i.e., non-corner slots), connect adj_face(s) \(\leftrightarrow b_i^*\) with weight perim_w(s).

Collect the \(2m\) defect vertices \(u_0^*, \dots, u_{2m-1}^*\) among \(\{b_0^*, \dots, b_{k-1}^*\}\) where \(t_i \neq t_{(i+1)\bmod k}\).
Run \(2m\) Dijkstras from \(u_0^*, \dots, u_{2m-1}^*\) on the \((N-1)(M-1) + k\) vertices, then run the \(O(m^3)\) circular non-crossing interval DP (Section 4.3), and finally pop the temporary query edges from the interior faces so the base graph is ready for the next query in \(O(N + M + k)\) time!



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Corner Slot Guard: Notice that in [CSP-S 2021] 交通规划, slots \(p = M, M + N, 2M + N, 2M + 2N\) are corners of the \(N \times M\) vertex grid: both slot \(p = M\) (top-right, going East) and slot \(p = M + 1\) (top-right, going South) attach to the same corner grid vertex \((1, M)\)! Because there is no grid perimeter edge between a corner’s East ray and its South ray, the transition from slot \(M\) to slot \(M + 1\) adds zero edges to the interior faces—only the boundary interval vertex \(b_i^*\) spans across the corner! Our adj_face(s) table handles this automatically by assigning adj_face(corner) = -1.







NoteProgressive Hints for #119 (Luogu P7916 — [CSP-S 2021] Traffic Planning)






Hint 1: Do not rebuild the \((N-1) \times (M-1)\) interior dual graph in every query! Build the interior faces once, and for each query, append edges to the \(k\) boundary interval vertices \(b_0^*, \dots, b_{k-1}^*\), then pop_back() those temporary edges after the query finishes.
Hint 2: Carefully trace the 4 corners (\(p = M, M+N, 2M+N, 2M+2N\)): a non-corner slot \(p\) corresponds to a perimeter grid edge separating an interior face adj_face(p) from the outer face, whereas the 4 corner slots are pure \(90^\circ\) turns around the 4 corner vertices and have no perimeter grid edge.
Hint 3: Keep all \(k\) boundary interval vertices \(b_0^*, \dots, b_{k-1}^*\) in the graph during Dijkstra (including monochromatic intervals where \(t_i = t_{i+1}\)), and only restrict the Circular Interval DP endpoints to the \(2m\) bichromatic defect vertices where \(t_i \neq t_{i+1}\).










TipFull C++ Solution for #119 (Luogu P7916 — [CSP-S 2021] Traffic Planning)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 1e18;

struct Terminal {
    long long w;
    int p, col;
    bool operator&lt;(const Terminal& o) const { return p &lt; o.p; }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m, T;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; m &gt;&gt; T)) return 0;

    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; h_edge(n - 1, vector&lt;long long&gt;(m));
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; n - 1; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; m; ++c)
            cin &gt;&gt; h_edge[r][c]; // vertical primal edge (r, c) -- (r+1, c)

    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; w_edge(n, vector&lt;long long&gt;(m - 1));
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; n; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; m - 1; ++c)
            cin &gt;&gt; w_edge[r][c]; // horizontal primal edge (r, c) -- (r, c+1)

    int R = n - 1, C = m - 1;
    int num_in = R * C;
    auto fid = [&](int r, int c) { return r * C + c; }; // 0 &lt;= r &lt; R, 0 &lt;= c &lt; C

    // Max vertices per query: num_in + 55
    vector&lt;vector&lt;pair&lt;int, long long&gt;&gt;&gt; adj(num_in + 60);
    auto add_base_edge = [&](int u, int v, long long w) {
        adj[u].push_back({v, w});
        adj[v].push_back({u, w});
    };

    // Build static interior dual edges
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; R; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; C; ++c) {
            if (r + 1 &lt; R) add_base_edge(fid(r, c), fid(r + 1, c), w_edge[r + 1][c]);
            if (c + 1 &lt; C) add_base_edge(fid(r, c), fid(r, c + 1), h_edge[r][c + 1]);
        }
    }

    // Map each clockwise perimeter slot p in [1, 2n + 2m] to its perimeter grid edge
    int P_MAX = 2 * n + 2 * m;
    vector&lt;int&gt; slot_face(P_MAX + 1, -1);
    vector&lt;long long&gt; slot_w(P_MAX + 1, 0);

    // 1. Top border: p in [1, m-1] -&gt; horizontal edge (0, p-1)--(0, p)
    for (int p = 1; p &lt;= m - 1; ++p) {
        slot_face[p] = (R &gt; 0 ? fid(0, p - 1) : -1);
        slot_w[p] = w_edge[0][p - 1];
    }
    // 2. Right border: p in [m+1, m+n-1] -&gt; vertical edge (r, m-1)--(r+1, m-1)
    for (int p = m + 1; p &lt;= m + n - 1; ++p) {
        int r = p - m - 1;
        slot_face[p] = (C &gt; 0 ? fid(r, C - 1) : -1);
        slot_w[p] = h_edge[r][m - 1];
    }
    // 3. Bottom border: p in [m+n+1, 2m+n-1] -&gt; horizontal edge (n-1, c)--(n-1, c+1)
    for (int p = m + n + 1; p &lt;= 2 * m + n - 1; ++p) {
        int c = m - 1 - (p - (m + n));
        slot_face[p] = (R &gt; 0 ? fid(R - 1, c) : -1);
        slot_w[p] = w_edge[n - 1][c];
    }
    // 4. Left border: p in [2m+n+1, 2m+2n-1] -&gt; vertical edge (r, 0)--(r+1, 0)
    for (int p = 2 * m + n + 1; p &lt;= 2 * m + 2 * n - 1; ++p) {
        int r = n - 1 - (p - (2 * m + n));
        slot_face[p] = (C &gt; 0 ? fid(r, 0) : -1);
        slot_w[p] = h_edge[r][0];
    }

    // Record base degree of interior faces for O(1) rollback after each query
    vector&lt;size_t&gt; base_deg(num_in);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; num_in; ++i) base_deg[i] = adj[i].size();

    while (T--) {
        int k;
        cin &gt;&gt; k;
        vector&lt;Terminal&gt; terms(k);
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; k; ++i) {
            cin &gt;&gt; terms[i].w &gt;&gt; terms[i].p &gt;&gt; terms[i].col;
        }
        sort(terms.begin(), terms.end());

        int V_tot = num_in + k;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; k; ++i) adj[num_in + i].clear();

        auto add_temp_edge = [&](int u, int v, long long w) {
            adj[u].push_back({v, w});
            adj[v].push_back({u, w});
        };

        for (int i = 0; i &lt; k; ++i) {
            int nxt = (i + 1) % k;
            int b_cur = num_in + i;
            int b_nxt = num_in + nxt;
            // Stem edge of terminal nxt separates boundary interval i and nxt
            add_temp_edge(b_cur, b_nxt, terms[nxt].w);

            // Attach all perimeter grid edges between terms[i].p and terms[nxt].p
            int s = terms[i].p;
            while (s != terms[nxt].p) {
                if (slot_face[s] != -1) {
                    add_temp_edge(slot_face[s], b_cur, slot_w[s]);
                } else if ((R == 0 || C == 0) && slot_w[s] &gt; 0) {
                    // Degenerate 1D grid guard if needed
                }
                s = (s % P_MAX) + 1;
            }
        }

        vector&lt;int&gt; defects;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; k; ++i) {
            if (terms[i].col != terms[(i + 1) % k].col) {
                defects.push_back(num_in + i);
            }
        }

        int D = defects.size(); // D = 2m is guaranteed even
        if (D == 0) {
            cout &lt;&lt; 0 &lt;&lt; "\n";
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; num_in; ++i) adj[i].resize(base_deg[i]);
            continue;
        }

        vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; dist_mat(D, vector&lt;long long&gt;(D, INF));
        vector&lt;long long&gt; dist(V_tot, INF);
        priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;

        for (int a = 0; a &lt; D; ++a) {
            fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
            int src = defects[a];
            dist[src] = 0;
            pq.push({0, src});
            while (!pq.empty()) {
                auto [d, u] = pq.top();
                pq.pop();
                if (d &gt; dist[u]) continue;
                for (auto [v, w] : adj[u]) {
                    if (dist[u] + w &lt; dist[v]) {
                        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
                        pq.push({dist[v], v});
                    }
                }
            }
            for (int b = 0; b &lt; D; ++b) {
                dist_mat[a][b] = dist[defects[b]];
            }
        }

        // Circular Non-Crossing Interval DP over 2*D duplicated array
        int M2 = 2 * D;
        vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; dp(M2, vector&lt;long long&gt;(M2, 0));
        for (int len = 2; len &lt;= D; len += 2) {
            for (int l = 0; l + len - 1 &lt; M2; ++l) {
                int r = l + len - 1;
                long long best = dist_mat[l % D][r % D] + (l + 1 &lt;= r - 1 ? dp[l + 1][r - 1] : 0);
                for (int p = l + 1; p &lt; r; p += 2) {
                    best = min(best, dp[l][p] + dp[p + 1][r]);
                }
                dp[l][r] = best;
            }
        }

        long long ans = INF;
        for (int l = 0; l &lt; D; ++l) {
            ans = min(ans, dp[l][l + D - 1]);
        }
        cout &lt;&lt; ans &lt;&lt; "\n";

        // Rollback temporary edges from interior faces
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; num_in; ++i) adj[i].resize(base_deg[i]);
    }

    return 0;
}







6.8 [Mastery #120] LOJ #2320 — [清华集训2017] 热带岛屿 (Tropical Island)

Judge URL: https://loj.ac/p/2320
Constraints: Planar / grid terrain with height barriers \(h_e\), \(N, M \le 1000\) (or general planar \(V, E \le 10^5\)). Time Limit: \(2.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Unlike additive minimum cuts (\(\sum_{e \in C} w_e\)), tropical island / flood-retention problems ask: how much water can each cell/face \(f^*\) retain before water spills out to the infinite ocean \(O^* = f_\infty\), or what is the minimum-bottleneck enclosing dike around a region?
By Planar Bottleneck Cycle–Cut Duality, water in face \(f^*\) spills to the ocean \(O^*\) as soon as the water level reaches the height of the lowest maximum-barrier path (minimax path) from \(f^*\) to \(O^*\) in the dual graph \(G^*\)! And when dike upgrades have additive costs across multiple water levels, each level set \(\{f^* : \text{water}_{f^*} \ge \lambda\}\) is enclosed by a dual cut cycle around \(f^*\)!


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Topological Representation

Minimax Path \(\Longleftrightarrow\) Minimum Spanning Tree / Kruskal Reconstruction Tree on \(G^*\): For any interior face \(f^* \in F \setminus \{O^*\}\), the equilibrium water level \(W(f^*)\) is: \[W(f^*) = \min_{P^*: f^* \leadsto O^*} \max_{e^* \in P^*} w(e^*).\]
Tree Hierarchy of Nested Planar Flood Basins: Sort all dual edges \(e^* = (u^*, v^*)\) by barrier height \(w(e^*)\) in non-decreasing order and build the Kruskal Reconstruction Tree \(T_{\text{KRT}}\) (or run Multi-Source Minimax Dijkstra from \(O^*\)). Each internal node \(x \in T_{\text{KRT}}\) with weight \(w(x)\) represents a connected basin of faces in \(G^*\) that merges at water level \(w(x)\), and its subtree in \(T_{\text{KRT}}\) is the exact connected component of faces enclosed by a planar barrier cycle of height \(\ge w(x)\)!



Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Minimax Dijkstra + Kruskal Reconstruction Tree on \(G^*\))

Construct the planar dual graph \(G^* = (F, E^*)\) with ocean vertex \(O^* = 0\).
To compute the equilibrium water volume \(\sum_{f^*} \max(0, W(f^*) - \text{ground}(f^*))\):

Initialize \(W(O^*) = 0\) and \(W(f^*) = \infty\) for \(f^* \neq O^*\).
Run Minimax Dijkstra from \(O^*\) using transition \(W(v^*) \gets \min\big(W(v^*), \max(W(u^*), w(u^*, v^*))\big)\).

If queries ask for subtree/basin aggregations or dike reinforcements across water levels, build the Kruskal Reconstruction Tree by merging DSU components in increasing order of \(w(e^*)\) (always rooting the component containing \(O^*\) as the “drained” ocean component), converting 2D planar cycle enclosure into 1D subtree queries!



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Suppose a \(3 \times 3\) island has a ring of 8 perimeter cells of height \(10\) surrounding a central cell \((2, 2)\) of ground height \(2\), except one perimeter cell has a gap of height \(6\).
In \(G^*\), the central cell \((2, 2)\) is connected to the ocean \(O^*\) through the 4 orthogonal perimeter cells of heights \(10, 10, 10, 6\).
Minimax Dijkstra from \(O^*\) visits the height-\(6\) perimeter cell at water level \(6\), and then floods the central cell \((2, 2)\) at water level \(\max(6, 2) = 6\), retaining \(6 - 2 = 4\) units of water!







NoteProgressive Hints for #120 (LOJ #2320 — Tropical Island)






Hint 1: Water flows from face to face across shared edges, and spills into the infinite outer face \(O^*\). What is the exact dual graph \(G^*\)?
Hint 2: A set of cells can hold water at level \(H\) if and only if every boundary edge of that cell set has height \(\ge H\)—which means in \(G^*\), the cells are disconnected from \(O^*\) when all dual edges with weight \(&lt; H\) are removed!
Hint 3: Sort dual edges by weight and build the Kruskal Reconstruction Tree (or run Minimax Dijkstra from \(O^*\)): the connected components of faces at threshold \(H\) form a laminar tree hierarchy where each subtree is a flood basin!










TipFull C++ Reference Implementation for #120 (Planar Flood Basins / Kruskal Reconstruction Tree & Minimax Dual Dijkstra)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

struct DualEdge {
    int u, v;
    long long w;
    bool operator&lt;(const DualEdge& o) const { return w &lt; o.w; }
};

struct DSU {
    vector&lt;int&gt; p;
    DSU(int n) : p(n) { iota(p.begin(), p.end(), 0); }
    int find(int x) { return p[x] == x ? x : p[x] = find(p[x]); }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N, M;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; N &gt;&gt; M)) return 0;

    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; h(N, vector&lt;long long&gt;(M));
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c)
            cin &gt;&gt; h[r][c];

    int O_star = 0; // Ocean (outer face)
    auto id = [&](int r, int c) { return r * M + c + 1; };
    int V = N * M + 1;

    vector&lt;DualEdge&gt; edges;
    const int dr[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
    const int dc[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};

    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
            int u = id(r, c);
            for (int d = 0; d &lt; 4; ++d) {
                int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];
                if (nr &lt; 0 || nr &gt;= N || nc &lt; 0 || nc &gt;= M) {
                    edges.push_back({u, O_star, h[r][c]});
                } else if (u &lt; id(nr, nc)) {
                    edges.push_back({u, id(nr, nc), max(h[r][c], h[nr][nc])});
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // Build Kruskal Reconstruction Tree (Laminar Basin Hierarchy) + Minimax Water Levels
    sort(edges.begin(), edges.end());
    DSU dsu(2 * V);
    vector&lt;long long&gt; water_level(V, 0);
    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; comp_members(V);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; V; ++i) comp_members[i].push_back(i);

    for (const auto& e : edges) {
        int ru = dsu.find(e.u), rv = dsu.find(e.v);
        if (ru == rv) continue;
        int ro = dsu.find(O_star);
        if (ru == ro || rv == ro) {
            int flooded = (ru == ro ? rv : ru);
            for (int cell : comp_members[flooded]) {
                water_level[cell] = e.w;
            }
            comp_members[flooded].clear();
            dsu.p[flooded] = ro;
        } else {
            if (comp_members[ru].size() &lt; comp_members[rv].size()) swap(ru, rv);
            for (int cell : comp_members[rv]) comp_members[ru].push_back(cell);
            comp_members[rv].clear();
            dsu.p[rv] = ru;
        }
    }

    long long total_retained = 0;
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
            total_retained += max(0LL, water_level[id(r, c)] - h[r][c]);
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; total_retained &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}

4. Flagship Deep-Dive Case Study — Codeforces 1427 G — One Billion Shades of Grey (#103)
Let us now assemble the Discrete Coarea Formula and Parametric Residual Flow Rollback to solve the centerpiece problem of this chapter.

4.1 Problem Statement & Constraints

Judge URL: Codeforces 1427 G — One Billion Shades of Grey
Statement: You are given an \(N \times N\) grid (\(3 \le N \le 200\)). Every cell \((r, c)\) on the border of the grid (\(r \in \{1, N\}\) or \(c \in \{1, N\}\)) has a fixed positive integer shade \(a_{r,c} \in [1, 10^9]\). Each interior cell (\(1 &lt; r, c &lt; N\)) is either blocked (\(a_{r,c} = -1\), meaning the cell is removed from the grid and has no edges) or free (\(a_{r,c} = 0\)). You must assign an integer shade \(h_{r,c} \in \mathbb{Z}\) to every free cell (while keeping \(h_{r,c} = a_{r,c}\) on all border cells) so as to minimize the sum of absolute differences across all pairs of horizontally or vertically adjacent non-blocked cells: \[\min_{h} \sum_{(u, v) \in E_{\text{grid}}} |h_u - h_v|.\]
Time Limit: \(2.0\) seconds.



4.2 Step 1 & Step 2 — Why Ishikawa Dies and How Coarea Compresses \(10^9\) Shades to \(m \le 4N\) Slices

Why Explicit Multi-State Chains Fail:

The shade range is \(K = 10^9\). Even after coordinate-compressing to the \(m \le 4N - 4 \le 796\) distinct shades appearing on the border of the \(200 \times 200\) grid, an explicit \(m\)-layer Ishikawa graph would have \(N^2 \cdot m \approx 40,000 \times 796 \approx 3.18 \times 10^7\) vertices and \(10^8\) edges—exceeding both memory and time limits by two orders of magnitude.

Coarea Level-Set Reduction:

Let \(v_1 &lt; v_2 &lt; \dots &lt; v_m\) (\(m \le 4N - 4\)) be the sorted distinct shades of all border cells.
Interior free cells have zero unary cost (\(c_u(h_u) \equiv 0\)), and the edge penalty is purely \(\ell_1\) (\(w_{uv} = 1\)).
By the Discrete Coarea Formula and Theorem 13.2 (Submodular Cut Nesting), the minimum total energy is the exact weighted sum of \(m - 1\) binary minimum cuts on the single-layer \(N \times N\) grid graph \(G = (V, E_{\text{grid}})\) (\(|V| \le 40,000\), \(|E_{\text{grid}}| \le 80,000\)): \[\text{Ans} = \sum_{k=1}^{m-1} (v_{k+1} - v_k) \cdot \operatorname{MinCut}(v_k),\] where in slice \(k\) (threshold \(\lambda = v_k\)):

Every border cell \(b \in \partial V\) with \(a_b &gt; v_k\) is wired \(S \xrightarrow{\infty} b\);
Every border cell \(b \in \partial V\) with \(a_b \le v_k\) is wired \(b \xrightarrow{\infty} T\);
Every adjacent pair of non-blocked cells \((u, v) \in E_{\text{grid}}\) is connected by an undirected edge \(u \xleftrightarrow{1} v\) of capacity \(1\).





4.3 Step 3 — Parametric Residual Flow Rollback (动态退流): From \(4N\) Max-Flows to \(O(N^3)\) Total Time
If we run Dinic’s algorithm from scratch \(m - 1 \approx 800\) times on a graph with \(40,000\) vertices and \(80,000\) edges, \(800 \times 80,000 \approx 6.4 \times 10^7\) graph traversals will still TLE.
Look at what actually changes in the network when we advance the threshold from \(v_{k-1}\) to \(v_k\):

All \(\le 80,000\) interior grid edges \(u \xleftrightarrow{1} v\) remain 100% identical!
All border cells with \(a_b &gt; v_k\) stay on \(S\), and all border cells with \(a_b \le v_{k-1}\) stay on \(T\).
Only the border cells \(b \in \partial V\) with \(a_b = v_k\) change their terminal attachment, switching from \(S \xrightarrow{\infty} b\) (since \(a_b = v_k &gt; v_{k-1}\) in slice \(k-1\)) to \(b \xrightarrow{\infty} T\) (since \(a_b = v_k \le v_k\) in slice \(k\))!

How can we switch a single border cell \(b\) from \(S \xrightarrow{\infty} b\) to \(b \xrightarrow{\infty} T\) directly inside the live residual network without resetting the flow?

The Key Degree Bound: Why \(f(S \to b) \le 4\) Always!
In slice \(k-1\), the edge \(e_S = (S \to b)\) has capacity \(\infty\). How much flow \(f_0 = f(S \to b)\) can it actually carry in a valid \(S\)-\(T\) flow? Every unit of flow entering \(b\) via \(S \to b\) must leave \(b\) through its incident grid edges \((b, u) \in E_{\text{grid}}\). Because \(b\) is a cell on a 2D grid with at most \(4\) neighbors (in fact, at most \(3\) neighbors since \(b\) is on the border, and only \(1\) interior neighbor!) and each grid edge has capacity \(1\):
\[\boxed{0 \le f_0 = f(S \to b) \le \deg_{\text{grid}}(b) \le 4!}\]


The 4-Step Parametric Rollback Protocol for Switching \(b\) from \(S\) to \(T\)
To transform the valid maximum flow of slice \(k-1\) into the valid maximum flow of slice \(k\), we process each border cell \(b\) with \(a_b = v_k\) as follows:

Read Active Flow \(f_0\) & Delete \(S \to b\): In the residual network, if \(e_S = (S \to b)\) was added with initial capacity \(\text{INF}\), its current active flow is \(f_0 = \text{INF} - \operatorname{cap}(e_S) \in \{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\}\) (or equivalently the residual capacity of its reverse arc \(b \to S\)). Set both \(\operatorname{cap}(S \to b) \gets 0\) and \(\operatorname{cap}(b \to S) \gets 0\), and subtract \(f_0\) from current_flow.
Drain (Roll Back) the \(f_0 \le 4\) Excess Units of Flow Out of \(b\) (退流): Severing \(S \to b\) leaves vertex \(b\) with a flow deficit of \(f_0 \le 4\) units (since \(f_0\) units of flow are still leaving \(b\) into the grid toward \(T\)). To restore flow conservation \(\nabla \cdot f = 0\) everywhere, we must push \(f_0\) units of flow backward from \(T\) to \(b\) in the residual network (which is equivalent to finding a directed path from \(T\) to \(b\) in the residual graph—avoiding \(S\)—and augmenting \(1\) unit along \(T \rightsquigarrow b\), repeated \(f_0\) times; or equivalently pushing \(1\) unit from \(b\) to \(T\) in the reverse of the flow network, or if a unit was routed to another \(S\)-terminal, returning it via \(b \rightsquigarrow S\)). Even simpler: before severing \(S \to b\) (or while draining), if we push \(1\) unit of flow in the residual graph from \(T\) to \(b\) (not passing through \(S\)), each such unit cancels \(1\) unit of flow on a path \(b \rightsquigarrow T\)!
Attach \(b \xrightarrow{\infty} T\): Add (or activate) the directed sink edge \(b \to T\) with capacity \(\text{INF}\).
Re-Augment \(S \to T\): After all border cells \(b\) with \(a_b = v_k\) have been rolled back and attached to \(T\), run standard augmenting path searches from \(S\) to \(T\) in the residual network until no \(S \rightsquigarrow T\) residual path remains, incrementing current_flow by \(1\) for each unit path found.

