#097 (SS-1) — AtCoder ABC 347 G: Grid Coloring 2 (with Prerequisite Warmup ABC 326 G: Unlock Achievement)

Judge URLs:

Primary: AtCoder ABC 347 G — Grid Coloring 2 ([JA])
Warmup Prerequisite: AtCoder ABC 326 G — Unlock Achievement ([JA])

Statement & Constraints (ABC 347 G): You are given an \(N \times N\) grid (\(2 \le N \le 20\)) where each cell \((r, c)\) has an input integer \(A_{r,c} \in \{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\}\). If \(A_{r,c} \ge 1\), the cell’s value is fixed to \(x_{r,c} = A_{r,c}\); if \(A_{r,c} = 0\), you must choose an integer \(x_{r,c} \in \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\}\). Minimize the total quadratic roughness across all pairs of horizontally or vertically adjacent cells: \[\min_{x \in \{1..5\}^{N \times N}} \sum_{(u, v) \in E_{\text{grid}}} (x_u - x_v)^2.\] (In the companion warmup ABC 326 G, \(N \le 50\) skills start at level \(1\) and can be upgraded up to level \(5\) at cost \(C_i\) per level; achievement \(j\) pays \(A_j\) if \(\text{lv}_i \ge L_{j,i}\) for all \(i\). Both problems share the exact same 4-node unary threshold backbone \(z_{u,a} = [x_u \ge a]\) for \(a \in \{2, 3, 4, 5\}\)!)


4-Step Compilation (ABC 347 G)

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): Each cell has \(K = 5\) ordered states and the edge penalty is \((x_u - x_v)^2\). Since \(g(d) = d^2\) has strictly positive second difference \(\Delta^2 g(d) = 2 &gt; 0\), Coarea level-set decoupling does not apply, and \(K = 5\) is tiny (\(4N^2 \le 1600\) threshold vertices). This is the textbook signature of Ishikawa’s Convex Penalty Construction.
Step 2 (Algebraic Representation): Encode \(x_u \in \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\}\) via four threshold bits \(z_{u,a} = [x_u \ge a] \in \{0, 1\}\) for \(a \in \{2, 3, 4, 5\}\). By Section 2.3 (Form 2, Zero-Offset Downward Wiring): \[(x_u - x_v)^2 = \sum_{a=2}^5 |z_{u,a} - z_{v,a}| + 2 \sum_{2 \le b &lt; a \le 5} \Big(z_{u,a}(1 - z_{v,b}) + z_{v,a}(1 - z_{u,b})\Big).\]
Step 3 (Mechanical Graph Compilation):

Create \(4\) vertices \(v_{u,2}, v_{u,3}, v_{u,4}, v_{u,5}\) per grid cell \(u\), with reverse \(\infty\)-locks \(v_{u,a+1} \xrightarrow{\infty} v_{u,a}\) for \(a \in \{2, 3, 4\}\).
If cell \(u\) is fixed to \(A_u \in \{1, \dots, 5\}\), force \(z_{u,a} = 1\) for \(2 \le a \le A_u\) via \(S \xrightarrow{\infty} v_{u,a}\), and force \(z_{u,a} = 0\) for \(A_u &lt; a \le 5\) via \(v_{u,a} \xrightarrow{\infty} T\).
For each grid edge \((u, v) \in E_{\text{grid}}\), add bidirectional edges \(v_{u,a} \xleftrightarrow{1} v_{v,a}\) (cap \(1\)) for \(a \in \{2..5\}\), and directed downward edges \(v_{u,a} \xrightarrow{2} v_{v,b}\) and \(v_{v,a} \xrightarrow{2} v_{u,b}\) (cap \(2\)) for \(2 \le b &lt; a \le 5\).

Step 4 (Reconstruction & Failure Guard): After running Dinic’s algorithm, find the inclusion-minimal cut \(S^*\) (vertices reachable from \(S\) in the residual graph). Then \(x_u^* = 1 + \sum_{a=2}^5 [v_{u,a} \in S^*]\). Failure Guard: The downward cross-edges \(v_{u,a} \to v_{v,b}\) (\(a &gt; b\)) must be directed from higher threshold \(a\) to lower threshold \(b\); making them undirected would sever them when \(x_u = x_v\)!







Tip3-Tier Progressive Hints (#097 — ABC 347 G)






Hint 1: Represent each cell \(u\)’s color \(x_u \in \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\}\) using 4 binary threshold nodes \(v_{u,a} = [x_u \ge a]\) for \(a \in \{2, 3, 4, 5\}\), linked by reverse \(\infty\)-edges \(v_{u,a+1} \xrightarrow{\infty} v_{u,a}\).
Hint 2: Expand \((x_u - x_v)^2\) in terms of \(z_{u,a}\) and \(z_{v,b}\). When \(x_u &gt; x_v\), how many pairs \((a, b)\) with \(a = b\) and with \(a &gt; b\) satisfy \(x_v &lt; b \le a \le x_u\)?
Hint 3: Wire capacity \(1\) between same-level nodes \(v_{u,a} \leftrightarrow v_{v,a}\) (\(a \in \{2..5\}\)) and directed capacity \(2\) from higher to lower thresholds \(v_{u,a} \to v_{v,b}\) and \(v_{v,a} \to v_{u,b}\) (\(2 \le b &lt; a \le 5\)). Reconstruct \(x_u = 1 + \sum_{a=2}^5 [v_{u,a} \in S]\).










NoteComplete Solution & C++ Code (#097 — ABC 347 G & ABC 326 G)





// AtCoder ABC 347 G — Grid Coloring 2 (#097)
// 5-State Ishikawa Quadratic Cut with Reconstruction (Zero-Offset Form)
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;queue&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;

using namespace std;

constexpr long long INF = 1e15;

struct Dinic {
    struct Edge { int to, rev; long long cap; };
    int n;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; G;
    vector&lt;int&gt; level, ptr;

    explicit Dinic(int n) : n(n), G(n), level(n), ptr(n) {}

    void add_edge(int u, int v, long long cap) {
        G[u].push_back({v, static_cast&lt;int&gt;(G[v].size()), cap});
        G[v].push_back({u, static_cast&lt;int&gt;(G[u].size()) - 1, 0});
    }

    bool bfs(int s, int t) {
        fill(level.begin(), level.end(), -1);
        queue&lt;int&gt; q;
        level[s] = 0;
        q.push(s);
        while (!q.empty()) {
            int u = q.front();
            q.pop();
            for (const auto& e : G[u]) {
                if (e.cap &gt; 0 && level[e.to] == -1) {
                    level[e.to] = level[u] + 1;
                    q.push(e.to);
                }
            }
        }
        return level[t] != -1;
    }

    long long dfs(int u, int t, long long pushed) {
        if (pushed == 0 || u == t) return pushed;
        for (int& cid = ptr[u]; cid &lt; static_cast&lt;int&gt;(G[u].size()); ++cid) {
            auto& e = G[u][cid];
            if (level[u] + 1 != level[e.to] || e.cap == 0) continue;
            long long tr = dfs(e.to, t, min(pushed, e.cap));
            if (tr == 0) continue;
            e.cap -= tr;
            G[e.to][e.rev].cap += tr;
            return tr;
        }
        return 0;
    }

    long long max_flow(int s, int t) {
        long long flow = 0;
        while (bfs(s, t)) {
            fill(ptr.begin(), ptr.end(), 0);
            while (long long pushed = dfs(s, t, INF)) flow += pushed;
        }
        return flow;
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; N)) return 0;

    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; A(N, vector&lt;int&gt;(N));
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; N; ++c)
            cin &gt;&gt; A[r][c];

    int S = 4 * N * N, T = S + 1;
    Dinic dinic(T + 1);

    // node(r, c, a) for a in {2, 3, 4, 5}
    auto node = [&](int r, int c, int a) {
        return (r * N + c) * 4 + (a - 2);
    };

    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; N; ++c) {
            // Reverse infinity locks: v_{u, a+1} -&gt; v_{u, a}
            for (int a = 2; a &lt;= 4; ++a) {
                dinic.add_edge(node(r, c, a + 1), node(r, c, a), INF);
            }
            // Pin fixed cells
            if (A[r][c] &gt; 0) {
                for (int a = 2; a &lt;= 5; ++a) {
                    if (A[r][c] &gt;= a) dinic.add_edge(S, node(r, c, a), INF);
                    else dinic.add_edge(node(r, c, a), T, INF);
                }
            }
            // Pairwise Ishikawa quadratic edges to right and down neighbors
            const int dr[2] = {1, 0}, dc[2] = {0, 1};
            for (int d = 0; d &lt; 2; ++d) {
                int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];
                if (nr &gt;= N || nc &gt;= N) continue;
                for (int a = 2; a &lt;= 5; ++a) {
                    dinic.add_edge(node(r, c, a), node(nr, nc, a), 1);
                    dinic.add_edge(node(nr, nc, a), node(r, c, a), 1);
                    for (int b = 2; b &lt; a; ++b) {
                        dinic.add_edge(node(r, c, a), node(nr, nc, b), 2);
                        dinic.add_edge(node(nr, nc, a), node(r, c, b), 2);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    dinic.max_flow(S, T);

    // Reconstruct optimal grid coloring from residual reachability (level[v] != -1)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; N; ++c) {
            int val = 1;
            for (int a = 2; a &lt;= 5; ++a) {
                if (dinic.level[node(r, c, a)] != -1) ++val;
            }
            cout &lt;&lt; val &lt;&lt; (c + 1 == N ? '\n' : ' ');
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
