#102 (Adv-2) — AtCoder ARC 125 E: Snack (Symbolic Parametric Min-Cut Evaluation over \(k = |S \cap L|\))

Judge URL: AtCoder ARC 125 E — Snack ([JA])
Statement & Constraints: You have \(N\) types of snacks (\(1 \le N \le 2 \cdot 10^5\)) and \(M\) children (\(1 \le M \le 2 \cdot 10^5\)). Snack \(i\) has total supply \(A_i \ge 1\). Child \(j\) can receive at most \(B_j\) pieces of snack \(i\) (for each \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\)) and at most \(C_j\) pieces of snack in total across all types. Find the maximum total number of snacks that can be distributed.


4-Step Compilation

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): The constraints describe a standard bipartite Max-Flow network with source \(S\), \(N\) snack vertices \(L = \{1, \dots, N\}\) (\(S \xrightarrow{A_i} i\)), \(M\) child vertices \(R = \{1, \dots, M\}\) (\(j \xrightarrow{C_j} T\)), and complete bipartite arcs \(i \xrightarrow{B_j} j\) for all \((i, j) \in L \times R\). Since \(N, M = 2 \cdot 10^5\), building the \(N \times M = 4 \cdot 10^{10}\) edges is impossible. However, notice the symmetry: the capacity of edge \((i, j)\) is \(B_j\), which depends only on \(j\) and not on \(i\)!
Step 2 (Algebraic Representation via Min-Cut Duality): Let \(S_L \subseteq L\) be the subset of snack vertices placed on the \(S\)-side of a minimum \(S\)-\(T\) cut, and let \(k = |S_L| \in \{0, 1, \dots, N\}\) be its cardinality.

The snack supply edges severed by \(L \setminus S_L\) contribute \(\sum_{i \notin S_L} A_i\). For a fixed cardinality \(k = |S_L|\), this sum is minimized by choosing \(L \setminus S_L\) to be the \(N - k\) smallest values of \(A_i\)!
Once \(k = |S_L|\) is fixed, each child \(j \in \{1, \dots, M\}\) independently chooses whether to sit in \(T\) (severing all \(k\) incoming edges from \(S_L\), each of capacity \(B_j\), at total cost \(k \cdot B_j\)) or sit in \(S\) (severing \(j \to T\) at cost \(C_j\)). Therefore, by Max-Flow Min-Cut duality, the maximum flow equals the minimum over the single integer parameter \(k \in \{0, 1, \dots, N\}\) of the 1D parametric cut function: \[\boxed{\operatorname{MaxFlow} = \min_{0 \le k \le N} \left( \sum_{r=1}^{N-k} A_{(r)} + \sum_{j=1}^M \min(k \cdot B_j, C_j) \right),}\] where \(A_{(1)} \le A_{(2)} \le \dots \le A_{(N)}\) is the sorted array \(A\).

Step 3 & Step 4 (Prefix-Sum Evaluation in \(O((N+M)\log N)\)): For each child \(j\), \(k \cdot B_j \le C_j \iff k \le \lfloor C_j / B_j \rfloor\). Sort the children by their threshold \(\tau_j = \lfloor C_j / B_j \rfloor\) (or bucket them into \(0 \dots N\)). As \(k\) sweeps from \(0\) to \(N\), maintain the sum of \(B_j\) for children with \(\tau_j \ge k\) and the sum of \(C_j\) for children with \(\tau_j &lt; k\). Every \(k \in \{0, \dots, N\}\) is evaluated in \(O(1)\) time!







Tip3-Tier Progressive Hints (#102 — ARC 125 E: Snack)






Hint 1: Formulate the problem as a minimum \(S\)-\(T\) cut where a subset \(S_L \subseteq \{1, \dots, N\}\) of snacks stays in \(S\).
Hint 2: Because every snack \(i \in S_L\) has the exact same edge capacity \(B_j\) to child \(j\), the optimal choice of \(R \cap S\) depends only on the size \(k = |S_L|\): child \(j\) pays \(\min(k \cdot B_j, C_j)\).
Hint 3: Sort \(A_1 \le \dots \le A_N\) to get the sum of the \(N - k\) smallest \(A_i\), and bucket children by \(\min(N + 1LL, C_j / B_j)\) to evaluate \(\sum_j \min(k B_j, C_j)\) for all \(k = 0 \dots N\) in \(O(N + M)\) time after sorting!










