int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N, M, K;
    while (cin &gt;&gt; N &gt;&gt; M &gt;&gt; K) {
        vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; w(N, vector&lt;long long&gt;(M));
        vector&lt;vector&lt;bool&gt;&gt; mandatory(N, vector&lt;bool&gt;(M, false));
        long long total = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
            for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
                cin &gt;&gt; w[r][c];
                total += w[r][c];
            }
        }
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; K; ++i) {
            int r, c;
            cin &gt;&gt; r &gt;&gt; c;
            --r; --c;
            mandatory[r][c] = true;
        }

        int S = 0, T = N * M + 1;
        Dinic dinic(T + 1);
        auto id = [&](int r, int c) { return r * M + c + 1; };
        int dr[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0}, dc[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};

        for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
            for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
                int u = id(r, c);
                long long cap = mandatory[r][c] ? INF : w[r][c];
                if ((r + c) % 2 == 0) {
                    dinic.add_edge(S, u, cap);
                    for (int d = 0; d &lt; 4; ++d) {
                        int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];
                        if (nr &gt;= 0 && nr &lt; N && nc &gt;= 0 && nc &lt; M) {
                            long long pen = 2LL * (w[r][c] & w[nr][nc]);
                            if (pen &gt; 0) dinic.add_edge(u, id(nr, nc), pen);
                        }
                    }
                } else {
                    dinic.add_edge(u, T, cap);
                }
            }
        }

        cout &lt;&lt; (total - dinic.max_flow(S, T)) &lt;&lt; "\n";
    }
    return 0;
}







#083 (Int-1): Luogu P1935 / P1791 [国家集训队] — 圈地计划 (Fencing Plan)

Judge: Luogu P1935 / Luogu P1791
Constraints: \(N, M \le 100\). Time Limit: \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Statement Summary: Each cell \((r, c)\) of an \(N \times M\) grid must be zoned as either Industrial (\(x_u = 1\)), earning profit \(A_u\), or Commercial (\(x_u = 0\)), earning profit \(B_u\). Furthermore, for every orthogonally adjacent pair of cells \((u, v)\), if they receive different zones (\(x_u \ne x_v\)), they earn an additional synergy bonus \(C_u + C_v\). Maximize the total profit.


1. Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Step 1)
At first glance, this looks like standard 2-label image segmentation (Boykov–Jolly). Look closely at the neighbor condition: the bonus \(w_{uv} = C_u + C_v\) is awarded when \(x_u \ne x_v\) (different zones), which means choosing the same zone (\(x_u = x_v\)) loses the bonus \(w_{uv}\)!


2. Intermediate Algebraic Representation (Step 2)
Starting from the maximum theoretical baseline
\[
\text{Total} = \sum_{u} (A_u + B_u) + \sum_{(u,v) \in E} (C_u + C_v),
\]
every cell \(u\) loses \(B_u\) if \(x_u = 1\) and loses \(A_u\) if \(x_u = 0\), while every adjacent edge \((u, v) \in E\) with weight \(w_{uv} = C_u + C_v\) has the lost-bonus penalty matrix:
\[
\psi_{uv}(x_u, x_v) = \begin{array}{c|cc}
x_u \setminus x_v & 0 & 1 \\
\hline
0 & w_{uv} & 0 \\
1 & 0 & w_{uv}
\end{array}
\quad \Longrightarrow \quad \Delta_{uv} = 0 + 0 - w_{uv} - w_{uv} = -2w_{uv} &lt; 0 \quad (\textbf{SUPERMODULAR!}).
\]


3. Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Step 3)
Because all interactions are 4-connected grid adjacencies, we partition by chessboard parity \(L = \{(r+c) \text{ even}\}\), \(R = \{(r+c) \text{ odd}\}\), and invert \(y_v = 1 - x_v\) on \(R\):

On \(u \in L\) (\(u \in S \iff x_u = 1\) Industrial, \(u \in T \iff x_u = 0\) Commercial):

Choosing Industrial (\(u \in S\)) forfeits Commercial profit \(B_u \implies\) edge \(u \xrightarrow{B_u} T\).
Choosing Commercial (\(u \in T\)) forfeits Industrial profit \(A_u \implies\) edge \(S \xrightarrow{A_u} u\) (or equivalently connect \(S \xrightarrow{A_u} u\) and \(u \xrightarrow{B_u} T\)).

On \(v \in R\) (\(v \in S \iff y_v = 1 \iff x_v = 0\) Commercial, \(v \in T \iff y_v = 0 \iff x_v = 1\) Industrial):

Choosing Commercial (\(v \in S\)) forfeits Industrial profit \(A_v \implies\) edge \(v \xrightarrow{A_v} T\) ( role of \(A_v, B_v\) swapped!).
Choosing Industrial (\(v \in T\)) forfeits Commercial profit \(B_v \implies\) edge \(S \xrightarrow{B_v} v\).

Across \((u \in L, v \in R)\):

Same original zone \((x_u = x_v)\) now means opposite cut sides \((x_u \ne y_v)\)!
Penalty \(w_{uv} = C_u + C_v\) for \((u \in S, v \in T)\) and \((v \in S, u \in T) \implies\) bidirectional edge \(u \xleftrightarrow{C_u + C_v} v\)!




4. Hand-Checkable Toy Trace (Step 4)
For a \(1 \times 2\) grid with \(u = (1,1) \in L\) (\(A_u = 10, B_u = 2\)) and \(v = (1,2) \in R\) (\(A_v = 9, B_v = 1\)), and \(C_u + C_v = 5\):

Total baseline \(= (10 + 2) + (9 + 1) + 5 = 27\).
If both are Industrial (\(x_u = x_v = 1\)), true profit is \(10 + 9 + 0 = 19\). In our cut, \(u \in S\) and \(v \in T\), which severs \(u \xrightarrow{B_u=2} T\), \(S \xrightarrow{B_v=1} v\), and \(u \xrightarrow{5} v\), for cut cost \(2 + 1 + 5 = 8 \implies 27 - 8 = 19\).
If \(u\) is Industrial (\(x_u = 1 \iff u \in S\)) and \(v\) is Commercial (\(x_v = 0 \iff v \in S\)), both \(u, v \in S\)! Only \(u \xrightarrow{B_u=2} T\) and \(v \xrightarrow{A_v=9} T\) are severed, for cut cost \(2 + 9 = 11 \implies 27 - 11 = 16\). Wait—why wasn’t \(u \leftrightarrow v\) severed? Because \(u\) and \(v\) have different original zones, so they keep the synergy bonus \(5\) (\(10 + 1 + 5 = 16\))!







NoteHint 1 — Spot the Anti-Ferromagnetic Trap





In standard segmentation, adjacent cells pay a penalty when they have different labels. Here, adjacent cells lose the bonus \(C_u + C_v\) when they have the same label! Why does that make \(\Delta_{uv} &lt; 0\)?









NoteHint 2 — Invert Odd Chessboard Cells





On even cells \((r+c) \equiv 0 \pmod 2\), let \(S\) mean Industrial and \(T\) mean Commercial. On odd cells \((r+c) \equiv 1 \pmod 2\), flip the meaning: let \(S\) mean Commercial and \(T\) mean Industrial!









NoteHint 3 — Swap Source/Sink Capacities on Odd Cells





Because the meaning of \(S\) and \(T\) is reversed on odd cells, an odd cell \(v\) has \(S \xrightarrow{B_v} v\) and \(v \xrightarrow{A_v} T\) (whereas an even cell \(u\) has \(S \xrightarrow{A_u} u\) and \(u \xrightarrow{B_u} T\)), and every adjacent pair \((u, v)\) gets a bidirectional edge of capacity \(C_u + C_v\).









NoteFull Solution & C++ Implementation — Luogu P1935 (圈地计划)





// Luogu P1935 — Fencing Plan (#083)
// Chessboard Bipartite Inversion for Anti-Ferromagnetic Neighbor Bonus
#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 1e18;

struct Dinic {
    struct Edge { int to, rev; long long cap; };
    int n;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; adj;
    vector&lt;int&gt; level, ptr;

    Dinic(int n) : n(n), adj(n), level(n), ptr(n) {}

    void add_edge(int u, int v, long long cap) {
        adj[u].push_back({v, (int)adj[v].size(), cap});
        adj[v].push_back({u, (int)adj[u].size() - 1, 0});
    }

    bool bfs(int s, int t) {
        fill(level.begin(), level.end(), -1);
        level[s] = 0;
        queue&lt;int&gt; q;
        q.push(s);
        while (!q.empty()) {
            int u = q.front(); q.pop();
            for (auto &e : adj[u]) {
                if (e.cap &gt; 0 && level[e.to] == -1) {
                    level[e.to] = level[u] + 1;
                    q.push(e.to);
                }
            }
        }
        return level[t] != -1;
    }

    long long dfs(int u, int t, long long pushed) {
        if (pushed == 0 || u == t) return pushed;
        for (int &cid = ptr[u]; cid &lt; (int)adj[u].size(); ++cid) {
            auto &e = adj[u][cid];
            if (level[u] + 1 != level[e.to] || e.cap == 0) continue;
            long long tr = dfs(e.to, t, min(pushed, e.cap));
            if (tr == 0) continue;
            e.cap -= tr;
            adj[e.to][e.rev].cap += tr;
            return tr;
        }
        return 0;
    }

    long long max_flow(int s, int t) {
        long long flow = 0;
        while (bfs(s, t)) {
            fill(ptr.begin(), ptr.end(), 0);
            while (long long pushed = dfs(s, t, INF)) flow += pushed;
        }
        return flow;
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N, M;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; N &gt;&gt; M)) return 0;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; A(N, vector&lt;long long&gt;(M));
    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; B(N, vector&lt;long long&gt;(M));
    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; C(N, vector&lt;long long&gt;(M));

    long long total = 0;
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) { cin &gt;&gt; A[r][c]; total += A[r][c]; }
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) { cin &gt;&gt; B[r][c]; total += B[r][c]; }
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) cin &gt;&gt; C[r][c];

    int S = 0, T = N * M + 1;
    Dinic dinic(T + 1);
    auto id = [&](int r, int c) { return r * M + c + 1; };
    int dr[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0}, dc[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};

    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
            int u = id(r, c);
            if ((r + c) % 2 == 0) {
                dinic.add_edge(S, u, A[r][c]);
                dinic.add_edge(u, T, B[r][c]);
            } else {
                // Inverted shore: S = Commercial (0), T = Industrial (1)
                dinic.add_edge(S, u, B[r][c]);
                dinic.add_edge(u, T, A[r][c]);
            }
            for (int d = 0; d &lt; 4; ++d) {
                int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];
                if (nr &gt; r || (nr == r && nc &gt; c)) {
                    if (nr &gt;= 0 && nr &lt; N && nc &gt;= 0 && nc &lt; M) {
                        long long w = C[r][c] + C[nr][nc];
                        total += w;
                        int v = id(nr, nc);
                        dinic.add_edge(u, v, w);
                        dinic.add_edge(v, u, w);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; (total - dinic.max_flow(S, T)) &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







#084 (Int-2): Luogu P5038 [SCOI2012] — 奇怪的游戏 (Strange Game)

Judge: Luogu P5038
Constraints: \(T \le 10\) testcases, \(N, M \le 40\), initial integers \(a_{r,c} \in [1, 10^9]\). Time Limit: \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Statement Summary: Given an \(N \times M\) grid with initial integers \(a_{r,c}\), in one operation you may pick any two orthogonally adjacent cells and increment both of their values by \(+1\). Find the minimum number of operations required to make all cells in the grid equal to the same final integer \(X\), or output -1 if impossible.


1. Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Step 1)
Every operation increments two orthogonally adjacent cells by \(+1\). On a chessboard coloring \(B = \{(r+c) \text{ even}\}\) and \(W = \{(r+c) \text{ odd}\}\), every operation adds \(+1\) to one black cell and one white cell! This immediately couples a global linear parity invariant with a bipartite flow/cut feasibility test.


2. Intermediate Algebraic Representation (Step 2)
Let \(n_B = |B|\), \(n_W = |W|\) be the counts of black and white cells, and \(S_B = \sum_{u \in B} a_u\), \(S_W = \sum_{v \in W} a_v\) be their initial sums. Suppose all cells reach final value \(X \ge \max_{r,c} a_{r,c}\). Then the total increments on \(B\) and \(W\) must be equal:
\[
\sum_{u \in B} (X - a_u) = \sum_{v \in W} (X - a_v) \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad (n_B - n_W) X = S_B - S_W,
\]
and the total number of operations is \(\sum_{u \in B} (X - a_u) = n_B X - S_B\) (which increases strictly with \(X\)).
This splits into two cases based on the parity of \(N \times M\):

Case A: \(N \times M\) is Odd (\(n_B - n_W = 1\)):

The equation \((n_B - n_W)X = S_B - S_W\) uniquely determines \(X = S_B - S_W\)!
If \(X &lt; \max_{r,c} a_{r,c}\), it is immediately impossible (-1); otherwise we just test feasibility of this single \(X\)!

Case B: \(N \times M\) is Even (\(n_B = n_W\)):

First, we must have \(S_B = S_W\) (otherwise -1).
Second, why is feasibility monotone in \(X\) when \(N \times M\) is even? Because any even-area \(N \times M\) rectangle can be tiled by disjoint \(1 \times 2\) dominoes! If \(X\) is feasible, we can increment every domino in the tiling by \(+1\) to show \(X + 1\) is also feasible! Thus we can binary search for the minimum feasible \(X \in [\max a_{r,c},\; 2 \cdot 10^{11}]\).




3. Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Step 3)
For a candidate target \(X\), let \(f_{uv} \ge 0\) be the number of times we operate on adjacent pair \((u \in B, v \in W)\). We need \(\sum_{v \sim u} f_{uv} = X - a_u\) for all \(u \in B\) and \(\sum_{u \sim v} f_{uv} = X - a_v\) for all \(v \in W\).
Compile into an \(s\)-\(t\) flow network:

Source \(S \xrightarrow{X - a_u} u\) for each black cell \(u \in B\).
Directed edge \(u \xrightarrow{\infty} v\) for each orthogonal pair \((u \in B, v \in W)\).
Sink edge \(v \xrightarrow{X - a_v} T\) for each white cell \(v \in W\).
Target \(X\) is feasible iff \(\text{MaxFlow}(S, T) = \sum_{u \in B} (X - a_u)\).



4. Hand-Checkable Toy Trace (Step 4)
Consider a \(1 \times 3\) grid \((1, 4, 2)\) (\(N \times M = 3\) odd):

\(B = \{(1,1)=1,\; (1,3)=2\} \implies n_B = 2, S_B = 3\).
\(W = \{(1,2)=4\} \implies n_W = 1, S_W = 4\).
Unique candidate: \(X = S_B - S_W = 3 - 4 = -1 &lt; 4 \implies\) immediately impossible (-1)!
If the grid is \((3, 4, 3)\), then \(S_B = 6, S_W = 4 \implies X = 6 - 4 = 2 &lt; 4 \implies\) still -1!
If the grid is \((3, 2, 3)\), then \(S_B = 6, S_W = 2 \implies X = 6 - 2 = 4 \ge 3\). Max flow is \((4-3) + (4-3) = 2 = 4 - 2\), feasible with \(2\) operations!







NoteHint 1 — The Chessboard Parity Invariant





Color the board like a chessboard. Every operation adds \(+1\) to one black cell and \(+1\) to one white cell. Write an equation relating the final target value \(X\), the counts \(n_B, n_W\), and the initial sums \(S_B, S_W\).









NoteHint 2 — Why Odd vs. Even Boards Behave Differently





When \(NM\) is odd, \(n_B - n_W = 1\), so \(X = S_B - S_W\) is uniquely fixed! When \(NM\) is even, \(n_B = n_W\), so \(S_B\) must equal \(S_W\), and any feasible \(X\) remains feasible for \(X + 1\) because an even board has a perfect domino tiling.









NoteHint 3 — Bipartite Flow Feasibility Test





To test a candidate \(X\), connect \(S \xrightarrow{X - a_u} u\) for \(u \in B\), \(u \xrightarrow{\infty} v\) for adjacent \((u \in B, v \in W)\), and \(v \xrightarrow{X - a_v} T\) for \(v \in W\), checking whether max flow saturates \(\sum_{u \in B} (X - a_u)\).









NoteFull Solution & C++ Implementation — Luogu P5038 (奇怪的游戏)





// Luogu P5038 [SCOI2012] — Strange Game (#084)
// Chessboard Parity Invariant + Domino-Monotone Binary Search + Bipartite Flow
#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 1e16;

struct Dinic {
    struct Edge { int to, rev; long long cap; };
    int n;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; adj;
    vector&lt;int&gt; level, ptr;

    Dinic(int n) : n(n), adj(n), level(n), ptr(n) {}

    void add_edge(int u, int v, long long cap) {
        adj[u].push_back({v, (int)adj[v].size(), cap});
        adj[v].push_back({u, (int)adj[u].size() - 1, 0});
    }

    bool bfs(int s, int t) {
        fill(level.begin(), level.end(), -1);
        level[s] = 0;
        queue&lt;int&gt; q;
        q.push(s);
        while (!q.empty()) {
            int u = q.front(); q.pop();
            for (auto &e : adj[u]) {
                if (e.cap &gt; 0 && level[e.to] == -1) {
                    level[e.to] = level[u] + 1;
                    q.push(e.to);
                }
            }
        }
        return level[t] != -1;
    }

    long long dfs(int u, int t, long long pushed) {
        if (pushed == 0 || u == t) return pushed;
        for (int &cid = ptr[u]; cid &lt; (int)adj[u].size(); ++cid) {
            auto &e = adj[u][cid];
            if (level[u] + 1 != level[e.to] || e.cap == 0) continue;
            long long tr = dfs(e.to, t, min(pushed, e.cap));
            if (tr == 0) continue;
            e.cap -= tr;
            adj[e.to][e.rev].cap += tr;
            return tr;
        }
        return 0;
    }

    long long max_flow(int s, int t) {
        long long flow = 0;
        while (bfs(s, t)) {
            fill(ptr.begin(), ptr.end(), 0);
            while (long long pushed = dfs(s, t, INF)) flow += pushed;
        }
        return flow;
    }
};

void solve() {
    int N, M;
    cin &gt;&gt; N &gt;&gt; M;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; a(N, vector&lt;long long&gt;(M));
    long long nB = 0, nW = 0, sB = 0, sW = 0, mx = 0;
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
            cin &gt;&gt; a[r][c];
            mx = max(mx, a[r][c]);
            if ((r + c) % 2 == 0) { nB++; sB += a[r][c]; }
            else { nW++; sW += a[r][c]; }
        }
    }

    auto check = [&](long long X) -&gt; bool {
        if (X &lt; mx) return false;
        int S = 0, T = N * M + 1;
        Dinic dinic(T + 1);
        auto id = [&](int r, int c) { return r * M + c + 1; };
        int dr[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0}, dc[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};
        long long need = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
            for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
                int u = id(r, c);
                if ((r + c) % 2 == 0) {
                    need += X - a[r][c];
                    dinic.add_edge(S, u, X - a[r][c]);
                    for (int d = 0; d &lt; 4; ++d) {
                        int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];
                        if (nr &gt;= 0 && nr &lt; N && nc &gt;= 0 && nc &lt; M)
                            dinic.add_edge(u, id(nr, nc), INF);
                    }
                } else {
                    dinic.add_edge(u, T, X - a[r][c]);
                }
            }
        }
        return dinic.max_flow(S, T) == need;
    };

    if (nB != nW) {
        long long X = (sB - sW) / (nB - nW);
        if (check(X)) cout &lt;&lt; (nB * X - sB) &lt;&lt; "\n";
        else cout &lt;&lt; -1 &lt;&lt; "\n";
    } else {
        if (sB != sW) {
            cout &lt;&lt; -1 &lt;&lt; "\n";
            return;
        }
        long long lo = mx, hi = 200000000000LL, ans = -1;
        while (lo &lt;= hi) {
            long long mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (check(mid)) { ans = mid; hi = mid - 1; }
            else lo = mid + 1;
        }
        if (ans == -1) cout &lt;&lt; -1 &lt;&lt; "\n";
        else cout &lt;&lt; (nB * ans - sB) &lt;&lt; "\n";
    }
}

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int T;
    if (cin &gt;&gt; T) while (T--) solve();
    return 0;
}







#085 (Adv-1): AtCoder ABC 193 F — Zebraness

Judge: AtCoder abc193_f
Constraints: \(1 \le N \le 100\), grid characters \(c_{r,j} \in \{\texttt{'B'}, \texttt{'W'}, \texttt{'?'}\}\). Time Limit: \(2.0\text{ s}\).
Statement Summary: Each '?' cell in an \(N \times N\) grid must be painted either Black (B) or White (W). Maximize the Zebraness—the number of orthogonally adjacent pairs of cells that have different colors.


1. Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Step 1)
With \(N \le 100\), there are \(N^2 \le 10^4\) binary variables and \(2N(N-1)\) orthogonal grid edges. Maximizing the count of adjacent pairs with different colors is equivalent to subtracting the minimum number of adjacent pairs with the same color from the total edge count \(2N(N-1)\).


2. Intermediate Algebraic Representation (Step 2)
Let \(x_u \in \{0, 1\}\) be the color of cell \(u\) (\(1 = \texttt{'B'}\), \(0 = \texttt{'W'}\)). We want to minimize the same-color penalty:
\[
\min_{x \in \{0,1\}^{N^2}} \sum_{(u,v) \in E} \big( x_u x_v + (1 - x_u)(1 - x_v) \big) \quad \text{subject to pre-colored cells.}
\]
Each edge \((u, v) \in E\) has penalty matrix \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}\) with \(\Delta_{uv} = -2 &lt; 0\) (supermodular).


3. Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Step 3)
Invert odd chessboard cells \(v \in R\) (\(\tilde{x}_v = 1 - x_v\)), which is equivalent to conceptually flipping the color 'B' \(\leftrightarrow\) 'W' on all odd cells \((r + c) \equiv 1 \pmod 2\):

Define unified variable \(z_{r,c} \in \{0, 1\}\) by:

On even cells \((r+c) \equiv 0 \pmod 2\): \(z_u = 1 \iff \texttt{'B'}\), \(z_u = 0 \iff \texttt{'W'}\).
On odd cells \((r+c) \equiv 1 \pmod 2\): \(z_v = 1 \iff \texttt{'W'}\), \(z_v = 0 \iff \texttt{'B'}\).

Now two adjacent cells \(u \in L\) and \(v \in R\) have the same original color (\(x_u = x_v\)) if and only if they have different unified states (\(z_u \ne z_v\))!
Pre-colored cells:

If \(z_u\) is forced to \(1\), add \(S \xrightarrow{\infty} u\) (cannot be placed in \(T\)).
If \(z_u\) is forced to \(0\), add \(u \xrightarrow{\infty} T\) (cannot be placed in \(S\)).

Adjacent cells \((u, v) \in E\): Add bidirectional unit edge \(u \xleftrightarrow{1} v\) (penalizing \(z_u \ne z_v\)).



4. Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Complexity Remark (Step 4)
On a \(2 \times 2\) grid \(\begin{pmatrix} \texttt{B} & \texttt{?} \\ \texttt{?} & \texttt{W} \end{pmatrix}\):

Both \((0,0) = \texttt{'B'}\) and \((1,1) = \texttt{'W'}\) lie on the even diagonal \((r+c) \equiv 0 \pmod 2\), so \(z_{0,0} = 1\) (\(S \xrightarrow{\infty} (0,0)\)) and \(z_{1,1} = 0\) (\((1,1) \xrightarrow{\infty} T\)).
The two '?' cells \((0,1)\) and \((1,0)\) provide two edge-disjoint paths of length \(2\) from \((0,0)\) to \((1,1)\), so the minimum cut is \(2\), yielding Zebraness \(4 - 2 = 2\).
Complexity: Because all internal edges have unit capacity \(1\) and vertex degree \(\le 4\), Dinic’s algorithm runs in \(O(N^3)\) time (\(\le 10^6\) operations, &lt; 15 ms).







NoteHint 1 — From Zebra Coloring to Monochromatic Coloring





Imagine a perfect chessboard where even cells are Black and odd cells are White: its Zebraness is the maximum possible \(2N(N-1)\). What happens if you swap the labels 'B' and 'W' on all odd cells \((r + c) \equiv 1 \pmod 2\)?









NoteHint 2 — Reformulating the Penalty After Odd-Cell Flip





After swapping 'B' \(\leftrightarrow\) 'W' on all odd cells, two adjacent cells had different original colors if and only if they now have the same flipped color!









NoteHint 3 — Minimum Cut Network





Lock each pre-colored cell to \(S\) (if its flipped color is 'B') or \(T\) (if its flipped color is 'W') with capacity \(\infty\), connect all orthogonally adjacent cells with bidirectional edges of capacity \(1\), and output \(2N(N-1) - \text{MaxFlow}(S, T)\).









