Problem #065 — Luogu P2762: 太空飞行计划问题 (Space Flight Planning)

Canonical Slot: Ch 09 — Stepping Stone 1 (SS-1) | Judge URL: Luogu P2762 (网络流 24 题 #02)
Constraints: \(1 \le M \le 50\) experiments, \(1 \le N \le 50\) instruments; sponsor payouts \(p_i \in [1, 10^4]\), instrument costs \(c_j \in [1, 10^4]\); Time Limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Statement: A space agency is planning \(M\) candidate experiments using \(N\) candidate instruments. Conducting experiment \(i \in \{1, \dots, M\}\) yields revenue \(p_i &gt; 0\) from its sponsor, but requires a specific subset of instruments \(I_i \subseteq \{1, \dots, N\}\) to be onboard. Purchasing instrument \(j \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) costs \(c_j &gt; 0\), and once purchased, instrument \(j\) can be shared by any number of experiments at no extra cost. Choose a subset of experiments and their required instruments to maximize the net profit, and print: (1) the \(1\)-based indices of the chosen experiments, (2) the \(1\)-based indices of the chosen instruments, and (3) the maximum net profit.







NoteHint 1 — Formulating Shared Instrument Costs as Implications





Why does greedy selection fail? Because an expensive instrument \(j\) might be required by three different experiments \(i_1, i_2, i_3\), none of which can afford \(c_j\) alone, but whose combined revenue \(p_{i_1} + p_{i_2} + p_{i_3} &gt; c_j\) is profitable. Introduce binary variables \(x_i \in \{0, 1\}\) for experiments and \(y_j \in \{0, 1\}\) for instruments: selecting experiment \(i\) (\(x_i = 1\)) forces purchasing every required instrument \(j \in I_i\) (\(y_j = 1\)).









NoteHint 2 — Optimistic Baseline & Bipartite Closure Edges





Pre-claim all experiment revenues \(W^+ = \sum_{i=1}^M p_i\). Then every unchosen experiment \(i\) (\(x_i = 0\)) forfeits \(p_i(1 - x_i)\), every purchased instrument \(j\) (\(y_j = 1\)) pays \(c_j y_j\), and each requirement \(j \in I_i\) enforces \(\infty \cdot x_i(1 - y_j)\). Wire \(S \xrightarrow{p_i} \text{Exp}_i \xrightarrow{\infty} \text{Inst}_j \xrightarrow{c_j} T\).









NoteHint 3 — Reconstructing the Chosen Set & Zero-Gain Tie-Breaking





After running Dinic to termination, do not check which individual edges \((S \to \text{Exp}_i)\) or \((\text{Inst}_j \to T)\) are saturated (Section 2.3)! Instead, run a single BFS from \(S\) in the final residual network \(G_{f^*}\) using only edges with positive residual capacity \(c_{f^*}(e) &gt; 0\). Every experiment and instrument visited by this BFS belongs to the canonical minimal closure \(C_{\min}^* = S_{\min} \setminus \{S\}\).









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & C++ Solution — #065 (Luogu P2762)






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
This is the archetype of Picard’s Project Selection Problem: positive-weight experiments \(i \in \{1, \dots, M\}\) depend on overlapping subsets of negative-weight instruments \(j \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) (\(N, M \le 50\)). Because all dependencies point strictly from experiments to instruments, \(G_{\text{dep}}\) is a bipartite DAG (Semantics A).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(x_i = [\text{Exp}_i \text{ chosen}] \in \{0, 1\}\) and \(y_j = [\text{Inst}_j \text{ bought}] \in \{0, 1\}\). We maximize:
\[\max_{\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}} \left(\sum_{i=1}^M p_i x_i - \sum_{j=1}^N c_j y_j\right) = \sum_{i=1}^M p_i - \min_{\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}} \left(\sum_{i=1}^M p_i(1 - x_i) + \sum_{j=1}^N c_j y_j + \sum_{i=1}^M \sum_{j \in I_i} \infty \cdot x_i(1 - y_j)\right).\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Vertex indexing: \(S = 0\), experiments \(\text{Exp}_i = i\) (\(1 \le i \le M\)), instruments \(\text{Inst}_j = M + j\) (\(1 \le j \le N\)), \(T = M + N + 1\).
Wire \(S \xrightarrow{p_i} i\) for each experiment \(i \in \{1, \dots, M\}\).
Wire \((M + j) \xrightarrow{c_j} T\) for each instrument \(j \in \{1, \dots, N\}\).
Wire \(i \xrightarrow{+\infty} (M + j)\) for each \(j \in I_i\).
Run Dinic to obtain \(\text{MaxFlow}(S, T)\), and extract \(S_{\min}\) via residual reachability from \(S\).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Zero-Weight Tie-Breaking Guard

Toy Trace: See Section 2.4 (\(E_1 = +10\) needing \(\{I_1, I_2\}\), \(E_2 = +12\) needing \(\{I_2\}\), \(I_1 = -8, I_2 = -9\)), where \(S \to E_1\) is saturated (\(c_{f^*}(S \to E_1) = 0\)) yet \(E_1 \in S_{\min}\) via the residual path \(S \to E_2 \to I_2 \to E_1\)!
Canonical \(S_{\min}\) Tie-Breaking: What if a bundle of experiments and instruments has net profit exactly \(0\) (e.g., \(p_1 = 10\) requiring \(I_1\) with \(c_1 = 10\))? On that bundle, \(S \xrightarrow{10} E_1 \xrightarrow{\infty} I_1 \xrightarrow{10} T\) is completely saturated on both \(S \to E_1\) and \(I_1 \to T\), so \(S\) cannot reach \(E_1\) or \(I_1\) in \(G_{f^*}\) unless some strictly profitable experiment shares \(I_1\). Wait: in Luogu P2762’s test data, one test case includes a zero-net-profit bundle where the judge expects the maximal closure \(S_{\max}\) (or either depending on judge version), whereas standard minimal closure \(S_{\min}\) excludes zero-gain bundles; in fact, on Luogu P2762, standard residual reachability vis[u] from \(S\) (c_f &gt; 0) passes all test cases once lines are parsed with std::stringstream.

// Complete Model Compilation & Residual Closure Extraction for #065 (Luogu P2762)
// Assumes standard Dinic&lt;long long&gt; with `dinic.add_edge(u, v, cap)`, `dinic.max_flow()`,
// and `dinic.get_S_min()` returning vector&lt;bool&gt; of vertices reachable from S via c_f &gt; 0.
void solve_space_flight(istream& in, ostream& out) {
    int M, N;
    if (!(in &gt;&gt; M &gt;&gt; N)) return;
    string line;
    getline(in, line); // consume trailing newline after N

    int S = 0, T = M + N + 1;
    const long long INF = 1e15;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; dinic(T + 1, S, T);
    long long W_plus = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= M; ++i) {
        getline(in, line);
        stringstream ss(line);
        long long p;
        ss &gt;&gt; p;
        W_plus += p;
        dinic.add_edge(S, i, p);
        int j;
        while (ss &gt;&gt; j) {
            dinic.add_edge(i, M + j, INF);
        }
    }
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= N; ++j) {
        long long c;
        in &gt;&gt; c;
        dinic.add_edge(M + j, T, c);
    }

    long long min_cut = dinic.max_flow();
    vector&lt;bool&gt; vis_S = dinic.get_S_min();

    bool first = true;
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= M; ++i) {
        if (vis_S[i]) {
            if (!first) out &lt;&lt; ' ';
            out &lt;&lt; i;
            first = false;
        }
    }
    out &lt;&lt; '\n';

    first = true;
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= N; ++j) {
        if (vis_S[M + j]) {
            if (!first) out &lt;&lt; ' ';
            out &lt;&lt; j;
            first = false;
        }
    }
    out &lt;&lt; '\n' &lt;&lt; (W_plus - min_cut) &lt;&lt; '\n';
}







Problem #066 — POJ 2987: Firing (Minimum-Cardinality Maximum-Weight Closure)

Canonical Slot: Ch 09 — Stepping Stone 2 (SS-2) | Judge URL: POJ 2987
Constraints: \(1 \le N \le 5000\) employees, \(0 \le M \le 60000\) direct-subordinate relations, signed firing benefits \(w_i \in [-10^7, 10^7]\); Time Limit \(5.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Statement: A company is downsizing. Firing employee \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) yields a signed net benefit \(w_i \in \mathbb{Z}\) (positive if employee \(i\)’s salary exceeds their productivity, negative if they are a net contributor) and forces you to also fire all direct and indirect subordinates of \(i\). Find a valid subset of employees \(C \subseteq \{1, \dots, N\}\) to fire that maximizes the total net benefit \(\sum_{i \in C} w_i\); if multiple subsets achieve the maximum benefit, output the minimum number of fired employees \(|C|\) along with the maximum benefit.







NoteHint 1 — Why Subordinate Chains Form a Standard Closure





Firing \(u\) forces firing \(v\) (\(x_u = 1 \implies x_v = 1\)). Notice that even though the problem statement says “direct and indirect subordinates”, you do not need to compute the transitive closure of the hierarchy! Wiring \(u \xrightarrow{+\infty} v\) for each of the \(M\) direct-subordinate edges automatically enforces indirect subordinates by cut transitivity.









NoteHint 2 — Method A: Algebraic Lexicographic Weight Scaling (\(w'_i = B \cdot w_i - 1\))





How do you maximize \(\sum_{i \in C} w_i\) as the primary objective and minimize \(|C| = \sum_{i \in C} 1\) as the secondary tie-breaker? Multiply every weight \(w_i\) by a base \(B = N + 1\) and subtract \(1\):
\[w'_i = (N + 1) w_i - 1.\]
Then for any closed set \(C\), \(\sum_{i \in C} w'_i = (N + 1) \sum_{i \in C} w_i - |C|\). Since \(0 \le |C| \le N &lt; N + 1\), maximizing \(\sum_{i \in C} w'_i\) lexicographically maximizes \(\sum_{i \in C} w_i\) first and minimizes \(|C|\) second!









NoteHint 3 — Method B: Why Canonical \(S_{\min}\) Solves It Without Scaling!





Recall Corollary 2.1 (Section 2.3): in the unscaled Picard network \(\mathcal{N}(G_{\text{dep}}, w)\), the residual reachability set \(C_{\min}^* = S_{\min} \setminus \{S\}\) is a subset of every maximum-weight closure \(C^*\) (\(C_{\min}^* \subseteq C^*\)). Therefore, \(|C_{\min}^*|\) is automatically the unique minimum cardinality among all maximum-weight closures, with zero capacity inflation!









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & C++ Solution — #066 (POJ 2987 — Firing)






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Each employee \(i\) has an arbitrary signed weight \(w_i \in \mathbb{Z}\), and firing \(u\) requires firing \(v\) for \((u \to v) \in E_{\text{dep}}\). This is a general-DAG Maximum-Weight Closure with a minimum-cardinality tie-breaker.


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation & Dual Equivalence of Tie-Breaking
We present both tie-breaking formulations so you can use either in contests:

Structural Lattice Proof (Corollary 2.1): Because the family of maximum-weight closures is closed under intersection (\(C_1 \cap C_2\)), there exists a unique inclusion-minimal optimal closure \(C_{\min}^* \subseteq C^*\), which therefore simultaneously minimizes \(|C^*|\) (and indeed minimizes \(\sum_{i \in C^*} \alpha_i\) for any non-negative vertex weights \(\alpha_i \ge 0\)). In Dinic’s final residual graph \(G_{f^*}\), \(C_{\min}^* = S_{\min} \setminus \{S\}\).
Algebraic Perturbation Proof (\(w'_i = (N + 1)w_i - 1\)): Replacing \(w_i \gets (N + 1)w_i - 1\) makes the optimal closure unique up to equal-size sets and allows reading off both values purely from the scalar flow value \(F' = (N + 1)W^+ - \text{MaxFlow}'\):

Let \(W'_{\max} = \sum_{w'_i &gt; 0} w'_i - \text{MaxFlow}'\). Since \(|C^*| \in \{0, \dots, N\}\), rounding \(W'_{\max}\) up to the nearest multiple of \(N + 1\) recovers both \(\sum_{i \in C^*} w_i = \lceil W'_{\max} / (N + 1) \rceil\) and \(|C^*| = (N + 1)\sum_{i \in C^*} w_i - W'_{\max}\)!




Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Build \(\mathcal{N}(G_{\text{dep}}, w)\) on \(N + 2\) vertices (\(S = 0\), employees \(1 \dots N\), \(T = N + 1\)):

If \(w_i &gt; 0\): add \(w_i\) to \(W^+\) and wire \(S \xrightarrow{w_i} i\).
If \(w_i &lt; 0\): wire \(i \xrightarrow{-w_i} T\).
If \(w_i = 0\): add no edge to \(S\) or \(T\) (since \(i\) is not connected to \(S\), unless a profitable superior \(u \in S_{\min}\) forces \(i\) via \(u \xrightarrow{\infty} i\), \(i\) will remain unreachable from \(S\) and stay outside \(S_{\min}\)!).
For each subordinate relation \((u, v)\): wire \(u \xrightarrow{+\infty} v\).

Run Dinic in 64-bit integers (long long), extract \(S_{\min}\), and count fired_cnt = count(vis_S[1..N]).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace: \(N = 3\) employees: \(w_1 = +5, w_2 = -5, w_3 = 0\), with \(1 \to 2\) (\(1\) supervises \(2\)) and \(3\) isolated.

Closed subsets are \(\emptyset\) (weight \(0\), size \(0\)), \(\{2\}\) (weight \(-5\)), \(\{1, 2\}\) (weight \(+5 - 5 = 0\), size \(2\)), \(\{3\}\) (weight \(0\), size \(1\)), and \(\{1, 2, 3\}\) (weight \(0\), size \(3\)).
Maximum weight is \(0\), achieved by \(\emptyset, \{3\}, \{1, 2\}, \{1, 2, 3\}\).
In \(\mathcal{N}\), \(S \xrightarrow{5} 1 \xrightarrow{\infty} 2 \xrightarrow{5} T\) pushes \(5\) units of flow, saturating \(S \to 1\). In \(G_{f^*}\), \(S\) can reach zero employee vertices (\(S_{\min} = \{S\}\)), so fired_cnt = 0 and max_profit = 0!

Failure-Mode Guard: Notice that \(\sum_{w_i &gt; 0} w_i \le 5000 \times 10^7 = 5 \cdot 10^{10}\), which overflows 32-bit signed int (2.14e9). Always use long long for capacities and flow sums!

// Complete Model Compilation for #066 (POJ 2987 — Firing) via Canonical S_min
void solve_firing(int N, int M, const vector&lt;long long&gt;& w,
                  const vector&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt;& edges, ostream& out) {
    int S = 0, T = N + 1;
    const long long INF = 1e16;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; dinic(T + 1, S, T);
    long long W_plus = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        if (w[i - 1] &gt; 0) {
            W_plus += w[i - 1];
            dinic.add_edge(S, i, w[i - 1]);
        } else if (w[i - 1] &lt; 0) {
            dinic.add_edge(i, T, -w[i - 1]);
        }
    }
    for (auto [u, v] : edges) {
        dinic.add_edge(u, v, INF);
    }

    long long max_profit = W_plus - dinic.max_flow();
    vector&lt;bool&gt; vis_S = dinic.get_S_min();
    int fired_cnt = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        if (vis_S[i]) ++fired_cnt;
    }
    out &lt;&lt; fired_cnt &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; max_profit &lt;&lt; '\n';
}







Problem #067 — Codeforces 1082 G: Petya and Graph

Canonical Slot: Ch 09 — Intermediate 1 (Int-1) | Judge URL: Codeforces 1082 G
Constraints: \(1 \le N \le 10^3\) vertices, \(0 \le M \le 10^3\) undirected edges; vertex weights \(a_v \in [1, 10^9]\), edge weights \(w_e \in [1, 10^9]\); Time Limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Statement: You are given an undirected graph \(G = (V, E)\) with positive vertex weights \(a_v\) and positive edge weights \(w_e\). The weight of an induced subgraph on a vertex subset \(C \subseteq V\) is defined as the sum of weights of all edges having both endpoints in \(C\), minus the sum of weights of all vertices in \(C\):
\[f(C) = \sum_{\substack{e = (u, v) \in E \\ u \in C \land v \in C}} w_e - \sum_{v \in C} a_v.\]
Find the maximum possible weight \(f(C)\) over all subsets \(C \subseteq V\) (including \(C = \emptyset\), where \(f(\emptyset) = 0\)).







NoteHint 1 — Turning Induced Edges into Project Nodes





An edge \(e = (u, v) \in E\) contributes \(+w_e &gt; 0\) to \(f(C)\) if and only if both \(u \in C\) and \(v \in C\). View each edge \(e \in E\) as a project of profit \(+w_e\) and each vertex \(v \in V\) as a machine of cost \(-a_v\)!









NoteHint 2 — Algebraic Linearization of \(w_e x_u x_v\)





For each edge \(e = (u, v)\), introduce an auxiliary binary indicator \(z_e \in \{0, 1\}\) (“claim edge \(e\)’s reward”) subject to the closure implications \(z_e = 1 \implies x_u = 1\) and \(z_e = 1 \implies x_v = 1\). At the optimum, \(z_e = x_u x_v\) holds automatically because \(w_e &gt; 0\).









NoteHint 3 — Bipartite Closure Topology & 64-Bit Overflow Check





Wire \(S \xrightarrow{w_e} e\) for all \(M\) edges, \(e \xrightarrow{+\infty} u\) and \(e \xrightarrow{+\infty} v\) for each \(e = (u, v)\), and \(v \xrightarrow{a_v} T\) for all \(N\) vertices. Since \(\sum_{e=1}^M w_e \le 10^3 \times 10^9 = 10^{12}\), use long long with INF = 1e16.









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & C++ Solution — #067 (Codeforces 1082 G)






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Even though the input is already a graph \(G = (V, E)\), the problem is not asking for a minimum cut of \(G\) itself! Instead, choosing edge \(e = (u, v)\) requires paying for both endpoints \(u\) and \(v\), which is a Bipartite Hyperedge Closure on the incidence graph between \(E\) (projects) and \(V\) (prerequisites). (When we divide edge weight by vertex count in Chapter 14, this exact bipartite closure network becomes Goldberg’s Maximum-Density Subgraph algorithm!)


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(x_v = [v \in C] \in \{0, 1\}\) for \(v \in V\) and \(z_e \in \{0, 1\}\) for \(e = (u, v) \in E\):
\[\begin{aligned}
\max_{\mathbf{x} \in \{0,1\}^V} \left(\sum_{e=(u,v) \in E} w_e x_u x_v - \sum_{v \in V} a_v x_v\right)
&= \max_{\substack{\mathbf{x} \in \{0,1\}^V, \, \mathbf{z} \in \{0,1\}^E \\ z_e \le x_u, \, z_e \le x_v}} \left(\sum_{e \in E} w_e z_e - \sum_{v \in V} a_v x_v\right) \\
&= \sum_{e \in E} w_e - \min_{\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{z}} \left(\sum_{e \in E} w_e(1 - z_e) + \sum_{v \in V} a_v x_v + \sum_{e=(u,v)} \infty\big(z_e(1 - x_u) + z_e(1 - x_v)\big)\right).
\end{aligned}\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Total vertices in flow network: \(N + M + 2 \le 2002\).
Index \(S = 0\), edge-nodes \(1 \dots M\), vertex-nodes \(M + 1 \dots M + N\), \(T = M + N + 1\).
For each edge \(e \in \{1, \dots, M\}\) connecting \((u, v)\) with weight \(w_e\):

Add \(w_e\) to \(W^+\); wire \(S \xrightarrow{w_e} e\), \(e \xrightarrow{+\infty} (M + u)\), and \(e \xrightarrow{+\infty} (M + v)\).

For each vertex \(v \in \{1, \dots, N\}\): wire \((M + v) \xrightarrow{a_v} T\).
Output \(W^+ - \text{MaxFlow}(S, T)\).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace: Triangle \(N = 3, M = 3\) with vertex costs \(a = (4, 4, 4)\) and edge weights \(w_{12} = 5, w_{23} = 5, w_{13} = 5\).

Any single edge \(\{u, v\}\) gives \(5 - (4 + 4) = -3 &lt; 0\).
All 3 vertices \(\{1, 2, 3\}\) give \((5 + 5 + 5) - (4 + 4 + 4) = 15 - 12 = +3 &gt; 0\).
In \(\mathcal{N}\), \(W^+ = 15\), and cutting \((M+1 \to T, 4), (M+2 \to T, 4), (M+3 \to T, 4)\) costs \(12\), giving \(15 - 12 = \mathbf{3}\).

Failure-Mode Guard: Self-loops or parallel edges in input graphs (if present in variations) are handled automatically without special cases because each edge \(e\) gets its own distinct vertex \(z_e\). Complexity: \(O((N + M)\sqrt{N + M})\) on bipartite closure topology.

// Complete Model Compilation for #067 (Codeforces 1082 G — Petya and Graph)
long long solve_petya_and_graph(int N, int M, const vector&lt;long long&gt;& a,
                                const vector&lt;tuple&lt;int, int, long long&gt;&gt;& edges) {
    int S = 0, T = M + N + 1;
    const long long INF = 1e16;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; dinic(T + 1, S, T);
    long long W_plus = 0;

    for (int v = 1; v &lt;= N; ++v) {
        dinic.add_edge(M + v, T, a[v - 1]);
    }
    for (int e = 1; e &lt;= M; ++e) {
        auto [u, v, w] = edges[e - 1];
        W_plus += w;
        dinic.add_edge(S, e, w);
        dinic.add_edge(e, M + u, INF);
        dinic.add_edge(e, M + v, INF);
    }
    return W_plus - dinic.max_flow();
}







Problem #068 — AtCoder ARC 085 E: MUL

Canonical Slot: Ch 09 — Intermediate 2 (Int-2) | Judge URL: AtCoder ARC 085 E (ABC 078 / ARC 085)
Constraints: \(1 \le N \le 100\), gem values \(a_i \in [-10^9, 10^9]\); Time Limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Statement: There are \(N\) gems labeled \(1, 2, \dots, N\) with signed values \(a_1, \dots, a_N \in \mathbb{Z}\) (some values may be negative). You may perform the following operation any number of times: choose a positive integer \(x \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) and smash all gems whose label is a multiple of \(x\) (\(x, 2x, 3x, \dots\)). You receive the sum of values of all gems that remain unsmashed at the end. Find the maximum possible sum you can receive.







NoteHint 1 — Why Divisibility Transitivity Eliminates ‘Button’ Vertices





As proved in Section 4.1, if gem \(i\) is smashed by some divisor operation \(d \mid i\), then every multiple \(j = k i\) is also a multiple of \(d\) and is already smashed! Thus a subset of gems \(K \subseteq \{1, \dots, N\}\) can be the set of surviving (unsmashed) gems if and only if \(K\) is closed under taking divisors: \(j \in K \land i \mid j \implies i \in K\).









NoteHint 2 — Checking the Implication Orientation (\(j \to i\) vs. \(i \to j\))





Using our primary convention \(x_i = 1 \iff i \in V_S\) (“gem \(i\) is kept”): - Can we keep \(1\) (\(x_1 = 1\)) and smash \(2\) (\(x_2 = 0\))? Yes (press button \(2\)). So \((x_1, x_2) = (1, 0)\) is legal. - Can we smash \(1\) (\(x_1 = 0\)) and keep \(2\) (\(x_2 = 1\))? No (pressing button \(1\) smashes \(2\)). So \((x_2, x_1) = (1, 0)\) is forbidden! Thus the \(\infty\)-arc points from multiple to divisor: \(j \xrightarrow{+\infty} i\) for \(i \mid j\)!









NoteHint 3 — Prime-Multiple Hasse Reduction (\(O(N \log \log N)\) Edges)





Instead of wiring \(j \xrightarrow{+\infty} i\) for all divisors \(i \mid j\), you only need to wire \(i \cdot p \xrightarrow{+\infty} i\) for prime factors \(p\) (or all \(j = k i \to i\) since \(N = 100\) has only \(\sum_{i=1}^{100} \lfloor 100/i \rfloor = 482\) pairs anyway!).









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & C++ Solution — #068 (AtCoder ARC 085 E — MUL)






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
\(N \le 100\) with \(a_i \in [-10^9, 10^9]\) and a global operation over index multiples \(x, 2x, 3x, \dots\). Because primes \(2, 3, 5, 7\) create a densely tangled divisibility poset (e.g., \(6, 10, 12, 15, 30\)), bitmask DP over active primes up to \(50\) (\(15\) primes, plus composite interactions) is cumbersome, whereas Divisibility Poset Closure (Section 4.1) compiles directly to a 102-vertex network!


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(x_i = [\text{gem } i \text{ is kept}] \in \{0, 1\}\) (\(i \in V_S \iff x_i = 1\)). Then:
\[\max_{\mathbf{x} \in \{0,1\}^N} \sum_{i=1}^N a_i x_i \quad \text{s.t.} \quad x_j = 1 \implies x_i = 1 \quad (\forall i \mid j)\]
\[= \sum_{a_i &gt; 0} a_i - \min_{\mathbf{x} \in \{0,1\}^N} \left(\sum_{a_i &gt; 0} a_i(1 - x_i) + \sum_{a_i &lt; 0} |a_i| x_i + \sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{k=2}^{\lfloor N/i \rfloor} \infty \cdot x_{ki}(1 - x_i)\right).\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Vertices: \(S = 0\), gems \(1 \dots N\), \(T = N + 1\).
For each \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\):

If \(a_i &gt; 0\): add \(a_i\) to \(W^+\) and wire \(S \xrightarrow{a_i} i\).
If \(a_i &lt; 0\): wire \(i \xrightarrow{-a_i} T\).
For each multiple \(j = 2i, 3i, \dots \le N\): wire \(j \xrightarrow{+\infty} i\).

Output \(W^+ - \text{MaxFlow}(S, T)\).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace: \(N = 4\), \(a = [2, -10, 5, 8]\).

Divisibility arcs: \(2 \xrightarrow{\infty} 1\), \(3 \xrightarrow{\infty} 1\), \(4 \xrightarrow{\infty} 2\), \(4 \xrightarrow{\infty} 1\).
Positive baseline \(W^+ = 2 + 5 + 8 = 15\).
Look at gem \(2\) (\(a_2 = -10\)) and gem \(4\) (\(a_4 = +8\)): keeping gem \(4\) (\(4 \in V_S\)) forces keeping gem \(2\) (\(2 \in V_S\)) via \(4 \xrightarrow{\infty} 2\), which costs \(|-10| = 10 &gt; 8\)!
Thus the minimum cut places \(4, 2 \in V_T\) (smashing \(\{2, 4\}\) by pressing button \(2\), severing \(S \xrightarrow{8} 4\) of cost \(8\)) and keeps \(\{1, 3\} \subseteq V_S\), giving net score \(15 - 8 = \mathbf{7}\) (\(a_1 + a_3 = 2 + 5 = 7\)).

Failure-Mode Guard: Never wire \(i \xrightarrow{\infty} ki\) when \(S\) represents kept gems (\(S \xrightarrow{a_i} i\) for \(a_i &gt; 0\))! Doing so would force gem \(4\) and gem \(2\) to be kept whenever gem \(1\) is kept!

// Complete Model Compilation for #068 (AtCoder ARC 085 E — MUL)
long long solve_arc085e_mul(int N, const vector&lt;long long&gt;& a) {
    int S = 0, T = N + 1;
    const long long INF = 1e16;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; dinic(T + 1, S, T);
    long long W_plus = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        if (a[i - 1] &gt; 0) {
            W_plus += a[i - 1];
            dinic.add_edge(S, i, a[i - 1]);
        } else if (a[i - 1] &lt; 0) {
            dinic.add_edge(i, T, -a[i - 1]);
        }
        for (int j = 2 * i; j &lt;= N; j += i) {
            // Keeping multiple j (j in S) requires keeping divisor i (i in S)
            dinic.add_edge(j, i, INF);
        }
    }
    return W_plus - dinic.max_flow();
}







Problem #069 — Luogu P4174 [NOI2006]: 最大获利 (Maximum Profit)

Canonical Slot: Ch 09 — Advanced 1 (Adv-1) | Judge URL: Luogu P4174 (Judge Mirrors: BZOJ 1497, SPOJ PROFIT)
Constraints: \(1 \le N \le 5000\) base stations, \(1 \le M \le 5 \cdot 10^4\) user demand groups; station build costs \(P_i \in [1, 100]\), demand revenues \(C_k \in [1, 100]\); Time Limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Statement: A telecom company can build any subset of \(N\) candidate cell towers, where building tower \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) costs \(P_i &gt; 0\). There are \(M\) user demand groups: group \(k \in \{1, \dots, M\}\) requires both tower \(a_k\) and tower \(b_k\) to be built, and if both are built, the company earns revenue \(C_k &gt; 0\). Maximize the company’s net profit (total revenue minus total tower construction cost).







NoteHint 1 — Why Pairwise Conjunction Needs an Auxiliary Hub (or Vertex-Edge Merging)





If you try to connect \(a_k\) and \(b_k\) directly without an auxiliary vertex, how do you represent \(+C_k x_{a_k} x_{b_k}\)? In this chapter, the most transparent method is to introduce an auxiliary binary variable \(z_k \in \{0, 1\}\) for demand group \(k\) with the closure implications \(z_k = 1 \implies x_{a_k} = 1\) and \(z_k = 1 \implies x_{b_k} = 1\). (Note: When \(M = \Theta(N^2)\) is a dense matrix as in [TJOI2015] 线性代数 (Luogu P3973), we can eliminate the \(M\) auxiliary vertices entirely via the identity \(1 - x_i x_j = (1 - x_i) + x_i(1 - x_j)\), which we formalize in Chapter 10!)









NoteHint 2 — Bipartite Closure Network & Dinic Complexity





Wire \(S \xrightarrow{C_k} \text{User}_k\), \(\text{User}_k \xrightarrow{+\infty} \text{Tower}_{a_k}\), \(\text{User}_k \xrightarrow{+\infty} \text{Tower}_{b_k}\), and \(\text{Tower}_i \xrightarrow{P_i} T\). Even though \(|V| = N + M + 2 \le 55,002\) and \(|E| = 3M + N \le 155,000\), this is a 2-layer bipartite closure network, on which Dinic runs in \(O(M\sqrt{N})\) time (\(&lt; 40\text{ ms}\))!









NoteHint 3 — Constant-Factor Orientation Tip (S -&gt; Tower -&gt; User -&gt; T)





In Dinic’s DFS, each user node \(\text{User}_k\) has out-degree \(2\) (pointing to \(a_k, b_k\)), whereas in the transposed network \(S \xrightarrow{P_i} \text{Tower}_i \xrightarrow{+\infty} \text{User}_k \xrightarrow{C_k} T\), each \(\text{User}_k\) has out-degree \(1\) (pointing only to \(T\)). Both orientations have the exact same minimum cut and pass well within \(50\text{ ms}\).









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & C++ Solution — #069 (Luogu P4174 — Maximum Profit)






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
We must choose a subset of \(N \le 5000\) cost-bearing stations to satisfy a subset of \(M \le 5 \cdot 10^4\) positive-revenue pair demands \((a_k, b_k, C_k)\). This is the large-scale benchmark for Bipartite Pair-Hub Closure (mathematically identical to #067 Petya and Graph, scaled up \(50\times\) to test \(O(M\sqrt{N})\) Dinic efficiency).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(x_i = [\text{Tower } i \text{ built}] \in \{0, 1\}\) and \(z_k = [\text{User } k \text{ served}] \in \{0, 1\}\):
\[\max_{\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{z}} \left(\sum_{k=1}^M C_k z_k - \sum_{i=1}^N P_i x_i\right) = \sum_{k=1}^M C_k - \min_{\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{z}} \left(\sum_{k=1}^M C_k(1 - z_k) + \sum_{i=1}^N P_i x_i + \sum_{k=1}^M \infty\big(z_k(1 - x_{a_k}) + z_k(1 - x_{b_k})\big)\right).\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Vertices: \(S = 0\), towers \(1 \dots N\), users \(N + 1 \dots N + M\), \(T = N + M + 1\).
For each tower \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\): wire \(i \xrightarrow{P_i} T\).
For each demand \(k \in \{1, \dots, M\}\): add \(C_k\) to \(W^+\), wire \(S \xrightarrow{C_k} (N + k)\), \((N + k) \xrightarrow{+\infty} a_k\), and \((N + k) \xrightarrow{+\infty} b_k\).
Output \(W^+ - \text{MaxFlow}(S, T)\).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Dense-Matrix Alternative Preview

Toy Trace: \(N = 3\) towers with \(P = (3, 4, 5)\); \(M = 2\) users: \((1, 2, C_1 = 6)\) and \((2, 3, C_2 = 6)\).

Baseline \(W^+ = 6 + 6 = 12\).
Building \(\{1, 2\}\) gives \(6 - (3 + 4) = -1 &lt; 0\); building \(\{2, 3\}\) gives \(6 - (4 + 5) = -3 &lt; 0\).
Building all three \(\{1, 2, 3\}\) shares Tower \(2\)’s cost \(P_2 = 4\), giving \((6 + 6) - (3 + 4 + 5) = 12 - 12 = 0\).
What if \(C_1 = 7, C_2 = 7\)? Then \(W^+ = 14\), cutting \((1 \to T, 3), (2 \to T, 4), (3 \to T, 5)\) costs \(12\), yielding \(14 - 12 = \mathbf{2}\).

Failure-Mode Guard (Sparse vs. Dense Pairwise Closure): Here \(M = 5 \cdot 10^4\) is sparse compared to \(\binom{5000}{2} \approx 1.25 \cdot 10^7\), so creating \(M\) auxiliary vertices is optimal. By contrast, in [TJOI2015] 线性代数 (Luogu P3973), where we maximize \(\sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{j=1}^N B_{i,j} x_i x_j - \sum_{i=1}^N C_i x_i\) for a dense \(500 \times 500\) matrix \(B_{i,j} \ge 0\), creating \(N^2 = 2.5 \cdot 10^5\) auxiliary vertices is unnecessary: using \(x_i - x_i x_j = x_i(1 - x_j)\), we wire \(S \xrightarrow{\sum_j B_{i,j}} i\), \(i \xrightarrow{C_i} T\), and \(i \xrightarrow{B_{i,j}} j\) on just \(N = 500\) vertices!

// Complete Model Compilation for #069 (Luogu P4174 — [NOI2006] Maximum Profit)
long long solve_maximum_profit(int N, int M, const vector&lt;int&gt;& P,
                               const vector&lt;tuple&lt;int, int, int&gt;&gt;& demands) {
    int S = 0, T = N + M + 1;
    const long long INF = 1e15;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; dinic(T + 1, S, T);
    long long W_plus = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        dinic.add_edge(i, T, P[i - 1]);
    }
    for (int k = 1; k &lt;= M; ++k) {
        auto [a, b, c] = demands[k - 1];
        W_plus += c;
        dinic.add_edge(S, N + k, c);
        dinic.add_edge(N + k, a, INF);
        dinic.add_edge(N + k, b, INF);
    }
    return W_plus - dinic.max_flow();
}







Problem #070 — Luogu P2805 [NOI2009]: 植物大战僵尸 (Plants vs. Zombies)

Canonical Slot: Ch 09 — Advanced 2 (Adv-2) | Judge URL: Luogu P2805
Constraints: Grid dimensions \(1 \le N \le 20\) rows, \(1 \le M \le 30\) columns (\(N M \le 600\) cells); plant scores \(w_{r,c} \in [-10^4, 10^4]\); Time Limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Statement: A lawn is an \(N \times M\) grid (\(0\)-indexed rows \(0 \dots N-1\) and columns \(0 \dots M-1\)). Each cell \((r, c)\) contains a plant with signed score \(w_{r,c} \in \mathbb{Z}\) (eating it grants \(w_{r,c}\) points; negative means eating it costs points) and a list of target cells \((r_1, c_1), \dots, (r_k, c_k)\) that plant \((r, c)\) attacks/protects.

Zombies can only enter each row from the right border (column \(M - 1\)) and advance leftward: to eat plant \((r, c)\), zombies must first eat all plants \((r, c + 1), (r, c + 2), \dots, (r, M - 1)\) to its right in the same row.
Furthermore, if plant \(v\) attacks cell \(u\), any zombie stepping onto cell \(u\) is instantly destroyed unless plant \(v\) has already been eaten! Choose a valid sequential eating strategy to maximize the total score of eaten plants.








NoteHint 1 — Building the Complete Causal Prerequisite Graph \(G_{\text{pre}}\)





What are ALL the prerequisites that must be eaten before cell \(u = (r, c)\) can be eaten? 1. If \(c + 1 &lt; M\), cell \((r, c + 1)\) must be eaten before \((r, c)\): add \((r, c + 1) \xrightarrow{\text{unlocks}} (r, c)\) to \(G_{\text{pre}}\). 2. If plant \(v\) attacks cell \(u\), then \(v\) must be eaten before \(u\): add \(v \xrightarrow{\text{unlocks}} u\) to \(G_{\text{pre}}\).









NoteHint 2 — Eliminating Cyclic Deadlocks via Kahn’s Topological Sort (FM-09)





Why can’t we run Picard’s closure directly on \(G_{\text{pre}}\)? Because of Broken-Gadget Counterexample FM-09 (Section 3.2): if two plants protect each other in a cycle (or if a row dependency and an attack edge form a cycle \((r, c+1) \to (r, c) \to (r, c+1)\)), neither plant can ever be eaten first, AND any plant causally downstream of that cycle is also permanently invincible! Run Kahn’s in-degree-\(0\) BFS on \(G_{\text{pre}}\) first!









NoteHint 3 — Reversing Surviving Edges for the Picard Closure Network





Only vertices visited by Kahn’s BFS (vis_kahn[u] == true) are physically eatable. On those surviving vertices, if \((v \xrightarrow{\text{unlocks}} u) \in E_{\text{pre}}\), then eating \(u\) (\(x_u = 1\)) requires eating \(v\) (\(x_v = 1\)), so you wire the reversed edge \(u \xrightarrow{+\infty} v\) in Dinic!









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & C++ Solution — #070 (Luogu P2805 — Plants vs. Zombies)






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
This is the definitive benchmark for Sequential Causal Closure (Semantics B, Section 3): eating a plant requires eating its right-hand row neighbor and all of its protectors first, and cycles in the prerequisite graph cause permanent physical deadlock (FM-09).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(U = \{0, \dots, NM - 1\}\) where cell \((r, c)\) has ID \(u = r M + c\). 1. Causal Graph \(G_{\text{pre}} = (U, E_{\text{pre}})\): \((v \to u) \in E_{\text{pre}}\) iff \(v\) must be eaten before \(u\). 2. Reachability Filter: \(U_{\text{valid}} \subseteq U\) is the set of vertices popped during Kahn’s topological sort on \(G_{\text{pre}}\). 3. Closure Optimization on \(U_{\text{valid}}\): Let \(x_u = [u \text{ eaten}] \in \{0, 1\}\) for \(u \in U_{\text{valid}}\). Then \((v \to u) \in E_{\text{pre}}(U_{\text{valid}})\) enforces \(x_u = 1 \implies x_v = 1\), compiled as \(\infty \cdot x_u(1 - x_v)\) (\(u \xrightarrow{+\infty} v\)).


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Map \((r, c) \mapsto u = r M + c \in [0, NM - 1]\).
For each cell \(u = (r, c)\):

If \(c + 1 &lt; M\), add directed edge \((r M + c + 1) \to u\) to \(G_{\text{pre}}\).
For each target cell \((tr, tc)\) attacked by \(u\), add directed edge \(u \to (tr \cdot M + tc)\) to \(G_{\text{pre}}\).

Run Kahn’s BFS on \(G_{\text{pre}}\) using in_deg[0 .. NM-1] to compute valid[0 .. NM-1].
Build \(\mathcal{N}\) on \(NM + 2\) vertices (\(S = NM, T = NM + 1\)):

For each \(u\) with valid[u] == true:

If \(w_u &gt; 0\): add \(w_u\) to \(W^+\) and wire \(S \xrightarrow{w_u} u\).
If \(w_u &lt; 0\): wire \(u \xrightarrow{-w_u} T\).
For each \((v \to u) \in E_{\text{pre}}\) with valid[v] == true: wire \(u \xrightarrow{+\infty} v\).


Output \(W^+ - \text{MaxFlow}(S, T)\).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace: See Section 3.2 (FM-09), where \(u_2 \leftrightarrow u_3\) deadlocks and blocks \(u_1\) (\(w(u_1) = +1000\)). Kahn’s BFS visits only \(u_4\), outputting \(+50\) instead of the unpruned \(+1030\) or the Tarjan-SCC \(+1050\).
Failure-Mode Guard: Double-check the direction of both edges:

In \(G_{\text{pre}}\) (Kahn’s BFS): protector -&gt; protected and (r, c+1) -&gt; (r, c).
In \(\mathcal{N}\) (Dinic): protected -&gt; protector and (r, c) -&gt; (r, c+1) with capacity \(+\infty\).


// Complete Two-Phase Causal Pruning + Closure Solver for #070 (Luogu P2805)
long long solve_pvz(int N, int M, const vector&lt;long long&gt;& score,
                    const vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;& attacks) {
    int V = N * M;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; adj_pre(V);
    vector&lt;int&gt; in_deg(V, 0);

    auto add_pre = [&](int protector, int target) {
        adj_pre[protector].push_back(target);
        in_deg[target]++;
    };

    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
            int u = r * M + c;
            if (c + 1 &lt; M) {
                add_pre(r * M + (c + 1), u); // must eat (r, c+1) before (r, c)
            }
            for (int target : attacks[u]) {
                add_pre(u, target); // u protects target -&gt; must eat u before target
            }
        }
    }

    // Phase 1: Kahn's Topological BFS on G_pre to prune cycles and their descendants
    vector&lt;bool&gt; valid(V, false);
    queue&lt;int&gt; q;
    for (int u = 0; u &lt; V; ++u) {
        if (in_deg[u] == 0) q.push(u);
    }
    while (!q.empty()) {
        int u = q.front();
        q.pop();
        valid[u] = true;
        for (int nxt : adj_pre[u]) {
            if (--in_deg[nxt] == 0) q.push(nxt);
        }
    }

    // Phase 2: Build Picard Closure Network ONLY on Kahn-validated vertices
    int S = V, T = V + 1;
    const long long INF = 1e15;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; dinic(V + 2, S, T);
    long long W_plus = 0;

    for (int u = 0; u &lt; V; ++u) {
        if (!valid[u]) continue;
        if (score[u] &gt; 0) {
            W_plus += score[u];
            dinic.add_edge(S, u, score[u]);
        } else if (score[u] &lt; 0) {
            dinic.add_edge(u, T, -score[u]);
        }
        for (int nxt : adj_pre[u]) {
            if (valid[nxt]) {
                // Eating nxt requires eating u -&gt; closure edge nxt -&gt; u of cap INF
                dinic.add_edge(nxt, u, INF);
            }
        }
    }
    return W_plus - dinic.max_flow();
}







Problem #071 — Luogu P3749 [SHOI2017]: 寿司餐厅 (Sushi Restaurant)

Canonical Slot: Ch 09 — Mastery 1 (Mas-1) | Judge URL: Luogu P3749 ([六省联考2017])
Constraints: \(1 \le N \le 100\) sushi plates, \(m \in \{0, 1\}\), sushi type codes \(1 \le a_i \le 1000\), interval scores \(d_{l,r} \in [-10^3, 10^3]\); Time Limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Statement: A conveyor belt holds \(N\) sushi plates in a row, where plate \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) has sushi type \(a_i\).

If you choose to eat a contiguous interval of plates \([l, r]\) (\(1 \le l \le r \le N\)), you automatically select all sub-intervals \([l', r'] \subseteq [l, r]\) as well. Each selected interval \([l, r]\) grants a signed satisfaction score \(d_{l,r} \in \mathbb{Z}\) (counted at most once even if covered by multiple larger intervals).
If you eat \(c \ge 1\) plates of sushi type \(x\) in total, you must pay a sushi bill of \(m x^2 + c x\). Maximize the net profit (total satisfaction score minus total sushi bill).








NoteHint 1 — Compressing Interval Inclusion to a 2-Out-Degree Pyramid (Section 4.2)





Treat each interval \([l, r]\) (\(1 \le l \le r \le N\)) as a vertex in the closure graph. Since \([l, r] \implies [l + 1, r]\) and \([l, r] \implies [l, r - 1]\) transitively generate every sub-interval \([l', r'] \subseteq [l, r]\), you only need two \(\infty\)-edges out of each \([l, r]\) (\(l &lt; r\))!









NoteHint 2 — Splitting \(m x^2 + c x\) into Per-Plate and Per-Type Costs





When is plate \(i\) physically eaten? Precisely when the length-\(1\) interval \([i, i]\) is selected! - The linear cost \(c \cdot x = \sum_{i \text{ eaten}} a_i\) charges \(a_i\) once for each eaten plate \(i\): simply set the net weight of node \([i, i]\) to \(w_{i,i} = d_{i,i} - a_i\)! - The quadratic cost \(m \cdot x^2\) is paid once per distinct sushi type \(x\) if at least one plate of type \(x\) is eaten: create a type hub \(\text{Type}_x\) with edge \(\text{Type}_x \xrightarrow{m x^2} T\), and wire \([i, i] \xrightarrow{+\infty} \text{Type}_{a_i}\)!









NoteHint 3 — Vertex & Edge Budget Check





There are \(\binom{N+1}{2} \le 5050\) interval vertices and \(\le 1000\) type vertices (\(\le 6052\) vertices total), with \(\le 15,000\) directed edges. Dinic solves this network in \(&lt; 15\text{ ms}\).









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & C++ Solution — #071 (Luogu P3749 — Sushi Restaurant)






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Contiguous interval inclusion (\([l, r]\) forces all sub-intervals \([l', r'] \subseteq [l, r]\)) combined with overlapping global item-type taxes \(m x^2\) breaks interval DP (dp[l][r] cannot merge two intervals without double-counting shared sushi types \(x\) or overlapping sub-intervals). This is the canonical Interval-Triangle Pyramid Closure (Section 4.2).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(x_{l,r} \in \{0, 1\}\) indicate selecting interval \([l, r]\) (\(1 \le l \le r \le N\)), and let \(y_v \in \{0, 1\}\) indicate activating sushi type \(v \in \{1, \dots, 1000\}\). Define modified interval weights:
\[\tilde{w}_{l,r} = \begin{cases} d_{l,r} & \text{if } l &lt; r, \\ d_{i,i} - a_i & \text{if } l = r = i. \end{cases}\]
We maximize:
\[\max_{\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}} \left(\sum_{1 \le l \le r \le N} \tilde{w}_{l,r} x_{l,r} - \sum_{v=1}^{1000} m v^2 y_v\right) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \begin{cases} x_{l,r} \le x_{l+1,r} \;\land\; x_{l,r} \le x_{l,r-1} & (1 \le l &lt; r \le N), \\ x_{i,i} \le y_{a_i} & (1 \le i \le N). \end{cases}\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Assign unique node IDs id[l][r] for \(1 \le l \le r \le N\) (\(1 \dots \binom{N+1}{2}\)) and type_id[v] for each distinct sushi type \(v\) present in \(a_1, \dots, a_N\).
For each interval \([l, r]\):

If \(\tilde{w}_{l,r} &gt; 0\): add \(\tilde{w}_{l,r}\) to \(W^+\) and wire \(S \xrightarrow{\tilde{w}_{l,r}} \text{id}[l][r]\).
If \(\tilde{w}_{l,r} &lt; 0\): wire \(\text{id}[l][r] \xrightarrow{-\tilde{w}_{l,r}} T\).
If \(l &lt; r\): wire \(\text{id}[l][r] \xrightarrow{+\infty} \text{id}[l+1][r]\) and \(\text{id}[l][r] \xrightarrow{+\infty} \text{id}[l][r-1]\).
If \(l = r\): wire \(\text{id}[l][l] \xrightarrow{+\infty} \text{type\_id}[a_l]\).

For each distinct sushi type \(v\): if \(m = 1\), wire \(\text{type\_id}[v] \xrightarrow{v^2} T\).
Output \(W^+ - \text{MaxFlow}(S, T)\).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace: \(N = 2, m = 1\), types \(a = [2, 2]\). Scores: \(d_{1,1} = 5, d_{2,2} = 5, d_{1,2} = -1\).

Singleton net weights: \(\tilde{w}_{1,1} = 5 - 2 = +3\), \(\tilde{w}_{2,2} = 5 - 2 = +3\); pair weight \(\tilde{w}_{1,2} = -1\).
Type hub \(\text{Type}_2\) has cost \(1 \cdot 2^2 = 4\) (\(\text{Type}_2 \xrightarrow{4} T\)).
Since \([1, 1] \to \text{Type}_2\) and \([2, 2] \to \text{Type}_2\) share the single type hub, choosing \(\{[1, 1], [2, 2], \text{Type}_2\}\) (without choosing \([1, 2]\)!) gains \(3 + 3 - 4 = \mathbf{2}\)!
Notice that eating plate \(1\) and plate \(2\) individually does not force eating the interval \([1, 2]\) (whose implication \([1, 2] \to [1, 1], [2, 2]\) points downward, not upward!).

Failure-Mode Guard: Never add reverse edges \([l+1, r] \land [l, r-1] \implies [l, r]\)! The problem statement says choosing \([l, r]\) includes sub-intervals of \([l, r]\); eating \([1, 1]\) and \([2, 2]\) separately does NOT force you to take a negative \(d_{1,2} &lt; 0\)!

// Complete Model Compilation for #071 (Luogu P3749 — [SHOI2017] Sushi Restaurant)
long long solve_sushi_restaurant(int N, int m, const vector&lt;int&gt;& a,
                                 const vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt;& d) {
    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; id(N + 1, vector&lt;int&gt;(N + 1, 0));
    int node_cnt = 0;
    for (int l = 1; l &lt;= N; ++l)
        for (int r = l; r &lt;= N; ++r)
            id[l][r] = ++node_cnt;

    int max_a = *max_element(a.begin(), a.end());
    vector&lt;int&gt; type_id(max_a + 1, 0);
    for (int x : a) {
        if (!type_id[x]) type_id[x] = ++node_cnt;
    }

    int S = 0, T = node_cnt + 1;
    const long long INF = 1e15;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; dinic(T + 1, S, T);
    long long W_plus = 0;

    for (int l = 1; l &lt;= N; ++l) {
        for (int r = l; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
            long long w = d[l][r] - (l == r ? a[l - 1] : 0);
            if (w &gt; 0) {
                W_plus += w;
                dinic.add_edge(S, id[l][r], w);
            } else if (w &lt; 0) {
                dinic.add_edge(id[l][r], T, -w);
            }
            if (l &lt; r) {
                dinic.add_edge(id[l][r], id[l + 1][r], INF);
                dinic.add_edge(id[l][r], id[l][r - 1], INF);
            } else {
                dinic.add_edge(id[l][l], type_id[a[l - 1]], INF);
            }
        }
    }
    if (m == 1) {
        for (int x = 1; x &lt;= max_a; ++x) {
            if (type_id[x]) {
                dinic.add_edge(type_id[x], T, 1LL * x * x);
            }
        }
    }
    return W_plus - dinic.max_flow();
}







Problem #072 — Codeforces 103 E: Buying Sets

Canonical Slot: Ch 09 — Mastery 2 (Mas-2) | Judge URL: Codeforces 103 E
Constraints: \(1 \le N \le 300\) sets over universe \(\{1, \dots, N\}\), set purchase costs \(w_i \in [-10^6, 10^6]\); Time Limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Statement: You are given \(N\) subsets \(A_1, \dots, A_N \subseteq \{1, \dots, N\}\), where buying set \(A_i\) costs \(w_i \in \mathbb{Z}\) (if \(w_i &lt; 0\), buying \(A_i\) earns \(-w_i\) profit). It is guaranteed that for every \(k \in \{1, \dots, N\}\), the union of any \(k\) subsets contains at least \(k\) elements (\(\big|\bigcup_{i \in I} A_i\big| \ge |I|\)). Find a subcollection of sets \(I \subseteq \{1, \dots, N\}\) whose union contains exactly \(|I|\) elements (\(\big|\bigcup_{i \in I} A_i\big| = |I|\)) that minimizes the total cost \(\sum_{i \in I} w_i\) (note that \(I = \emptyset\) is valid with cost \(0\)).







NoteHint 1 — Exploiting the Hall Guarantee \(|N(I)| \ge |I|\)





Because every subcollection \(I \subseteq \{1, \dots, N\}\) satisfies \(|N(I)| \ge |I|\), the excess \(|N(I)| - |I|\) is always non-negative (\(\ge 0\)), and equals \(0\) if and only if \(|N(I)| = |I|\)!









NoteHint 2 — Method 1: Uniform Pedestal Shift +M (ゲタを履かせる)





As derived in Section 4.3, set \(M = 10^9 &gt; N \max |w_i| = 3 \cdot 10^8\). Give each set \(i\) a positive closure weight \(M - w_i &gt; 0\) (\(S \xrightarrow{M - w_i} \text{Set}_i\)), each element \(j \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) a negative closure weight \(-M\) (\(\text{Elem}_j \xrightarrow{M} T\)), and wire \(\text{Set}_i \xrightarrow{+\infty} \text{Elem}_j\) for all \(j \in A_i\). Then any closed set \((I, J)\) has weight \(-(|J| - |I|)M - \sum_{i \in I} w_i\), which is forced to have \(|J| = |N(I)| = |I|\)!









NoteHint 3 — Method 2: Hall Perfect Matching + \(N\)-Vertex Closure





Alternatively, find a perfect matching \(M(i) \in A_i\) first, and wire \(i \xrightarrow{+\infty} M^{-1}(e)\) for all \(e \in A_i\) on an \(N\)-vertex closure network with weights \(-w_i\) (Section 4.3 Method 2). Both methods run in \(&lt; 15\text{ ms}\)!









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & C++ Solution — #072 (Codeforces 103 E — Buying Sets)






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
“Union of \(k\) sets has size \(\ge k\) for all \(k\)” is the verbatim statement of Hall’s Marriage Condition (Chapter 8). Combining Hall’s non-negative excess \(|N(I)| - |I| \ge 0\) with an exact-equality requirement \(|N(I)| = |I|\) and signed set costs \(w_i\) triggers Hall-Shifted Bipartite Closure (Section 4.3).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation (+M Pedestal Shift)
Let \(x_i = [i \in I] \in \{0, 1\}\) for sets and \(y_j = [j \in J] \in \{0, 1\}\) for elements, with \(M = 10^9\):
\[\max_{\substack{\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y} \in \{0,1\}^N \\ x_i \le y_j \; (\forall j \in A_i)}} \left(\sum_{i=1}^N (M - w_i) x_i - \sum_{j=1}^N M y_j\right) = \sum_{i=1}^N (M - w_i) - \text{MinCut}(S, T).\]
Since \(\max \text{Closure} = \max_{|N(I)| = |I|} \sum_{i \in I} (-w_i) = -\min_{|N(I)| = |I|} \sum_{i \in I} w_i\), the minimum cost \(\sum_{i \in I^*} w_i\) is simply:
\[\min_{|N(I)| = |I|} \sum_{i \in I} w_i = \text{MinCut}(S, T) - \sum_{i=1}^N (M - w_i).\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Set \(M = 10^9\), INF = 1e16.
Vertices: \(S = 0\), sets \(1 \dots N\), elements \(N + 1 \dots 2N\), \(T = 2N + 1\).
For each set \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\):

Add \(M - w_i\) to \(W^+\); wire \(S \xrightarrow{M - w_i} i\).
For each element \(j \in A_i\): wire \(i \xrightarrow{+\infty} (N + j)\).

For each element \(j \in \{1, \dots, N\}\): wire \((N + j) \xrightarrow{M} T\).
Output \(\text{MaxFlow}(S, T) - W^+\).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace (\(N = 3\)):

\(A_1 = \{1, 2\}\) (\(w_1 = -10\)), \(A_2 = \{2, 3\}\) (\(w_2 = +4\)), \(A_3 = \{3\}\) (\(w_3 = -5\)).
Check all subsets \(I\) with \(|N(I)| = |I|\):

\(I = \emptyset \implies \text{cost } 0\).
\(I = \{1\} \implies N(\{1\}) = \{1, 2\}\) (size \(2 &gt; 1\), invalid!).
\(I = \{3\} \implies N(\{3\}) = \{3\}\) (size \(1 = 1\), valid, cost \(-5\)).
\(I = \{2, 3\} \implies N(\{2, 3\}) = \{2, 3\}\) (size \(2 = 2\), valid, cost \(4 - 5 = -1\)).
\(I = \{1, 2, 3\} \implies N(\{1, 2, 3\}) = \{1, 2, 3\}\) (size \(3 = 3\), valid, cost \(-10 + 4 - 5 = \mathbf{-11}\)).

With \(M = 100\): set weights are \(W_1 = 110, W_2 = 96, W_3 = 105\) (\(W^+ = 311\)), element costs are \(100\) each.
Choosing \(I = \{1\}\) (\(J = \{1, 2\}\)) gives \(110 - 200 = -90 &lt; 0\) (rejected!).
Choosing \(I = \{1, 2, 3\}\) (\(J = \{1, 2, 3\}\)) severs the three element edges to \(T\) (cut \(= 300\)), giving \(\text{MinCut} - W^+ = 300 - 311 = \mathbf{-11}\)!

Failure-Mode Guard: In the \(+M\) pedestal method, \(M\) must strictly exceed \(N \cdot \max_i |w_i| = 300 \times 10^6 = 3 \cdot 10^8\) (so \(M = 10^9\) is safe, whereas \(M = 10^7\) fails when \(300\) sets each have \(w_i = -10^6\)). And because \(N \cdot M = 3 \cdot 10^{11}\), all capacities and flow variables must be long long!

// Complete Model Compilation for #072 (Codeforces 103 E — Buying Sets) via +M Pedestal Shift
long long solve_buying_sets(int N, const vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;& sets,
                            const vector&lt;long long&gt;& w) {
    int S = 0, T = 2 * N + 1;
    const long long M_PEDESTAL = 1e9; // &gt; N * max|w_i| = 3e8
    const long long INF = 1e16;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; dinic(T + 1, S, T);
    long long W_plus = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        long long cap_s = M_PEDESTAL - w[i - 1];
        W_plus += cap_s;
        dinic.add_edge(S, i, cap_s);
        for (int elem : sets[i - 1]) {
            dinic.add_edge(i, N + elem, INF);
        }
    }
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= N; ++j) {
        dinic.add_edge(N + j, T, M_PEDESTAL);
    }
    // Minimum cost = -(W_plus - min_cut) = min_cut - W_plus
    return dinic.max_flow() - W_plus;
}

4. König’s Line Coloring Theorem (\(\chi'(G) = \Delta\)): \(O(VE)\) Kempe-Chain Augmentation & \(O(E \log \Delta)\) Eulerian Halving
In a general multigraph \(G\), Vizing’s Theorem bounds the edge chromatic number \(\chi'(G)\) (the minimum number of colors needed so no two adjacent edges share a color) between \(\Delta(G)\) and \(\Delta(G) + \mu(G)\). In a bipartite multigraph, however, there is zero gap between the trivial lower bound \(\Delta(G)\) and the chromatic index!

4.1 König’s Line Coloring Theorem via \(\Delta\)-Regularization






ImportantTheorem 7.2 — König’s Line Coloring Theorem (1916)



For every bipartite multigraph \(G = (L \sqcup R, E)\) with maximum vertex degree \(\Delta = \max_{v \in L \cup R} \deg_G(v)\), the minimum number of colors required to properly edge-color \(G\) (equivalently, to partition \(E\) into matchings) is:
\[\chi'(G) = \Delta(G).\]



Proof via \(\Delta\)-Regular Embedding
We can embed any bipartite multigraph \(G = (L \sqcup R, E)\) of maximum degree \(\Delta\) into a \(\Delta\)-regular bipartite multigraph \(G_{\text{reg}}\) as follows:

Pad the smaller side with isolated dummy vertices until \(|L| = |R| = N\).
While there exists a vertex \(u \in L\) with \(\deg(u) &lt; \Delta\), the sum of degrees satisfies \(\sum_{v \in R} \deg(v) = \sum_{x \in L} \deg(x) &lt; \Delta N\), so by the Pigeonhole Principle there must also exist some \(v \in R\) with \(\deg(v) &lt; \Delta\). Add a dummy edge \((u, v)\) and repeat until every vertex has degree \(\Delta\).
By Theorem 7.1 (Birkhoff–von Neumann), \(G_{\text{reg}}\) decomposes into \(\Delta\) edge-disjoint perfect matchings \(M_1, \dots, M_\Delta\). Restricting each \(M_c\) to the original edges \(E(G)\) partitions \(E(G)\) into \(\Delta\) matchings (color classes)! \(\blacksquare\)





4.2 Engine 2: Online \(O(VE)\) Kempe-Chain Augmentation (CF 600 F — Edge Coloring of Bipartite Graph)

Judge URL: https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/600/F
Constraints: \(|L| = a \le 1000\), \(|R| = b \le 1000\), \(M \le 10^5\) edges, Time Limit \(1.0\text{ s}\). Color all \(M\) edges using the minimum number of colors \(D = \Delta\).


1. Why Iterative Dinic Peeling TLEs Here
If we embed \(G\) into a \(\Delta\)-regular graph and run Hopcroft–Karp \(\Delta \le 1000\) times on \(M = 10^5\) edges, the total work is \(O(\Delta \cdot M \sqrt{V}) \approx 1000 \times 10^5 \times 45 \approx 4.5 \times 10^9\) operations—a guaranteed Time Limit Exceeded!
Instead, we can color the edges online one by one in \(O(|L| + |R|)\) time per edge (\(O(VE)\) total) using Kempe-Chain Alternating Paths.


2. 4-Step Compilation of Online Kempe-Chain Recoloring

Step 1 — Algebraic State Invariant: Fix the palette of colors \(\{1, 2, \dots, \Delta\}\). Maintain a lookup table \(\text{who}[z][c]\) storing the neighbor of vertex \(z\) along the unique incident edge of color \(c\) (or \(0\) if color \(c\) is currently free at \(z\)). Before inserting a new edge \(e = (u, v)\) (\(u \in L, v \in R\)), both \(u\) and \(v\) have current degree \(\le \Delta - 1\) in the already-colored subgraph. Therefore:

\(u\) is missing at least one free color \(c_u \in \{1, \dots, \Delta\}\) (\(\text{who}[u][c_u] = 0\)).
\(v\) is missing at least one free color \(c_v \in \{1, \dots, \Delta\}\) (\(\text{who}[v][c_v] = 0\)).

Step 2 — Easy Case vs. Kempe-Chain Conflict:

If color \(c_u\) is also free at \(v\) (\(\text{who}[v][c_u] = 0\)), simply color \(e = (u, v)\) with \(c_u\) in \(O(1)\) time!
Otherwise, \(v\) already has an incident edge of color \(c_u\), while \(c_v\) is free at \(v\). Consider the subgraph \(H(c_u, c_v)\) formed by all currently colored edges of colors \(c_u\) and \(c_v\). Because every vertex has at most one incident edge of color \(c_u\) and at most one of color \(c_v\), every vertex in \(H(c_u, c_v)\) has degree \(\le 2\), so \(H(c_u, c_v)\) is a collection of disjoint paths and even cycles!

Step 3 — Why the Kempe Chain from \(v\) Can Never Fail on a Bipartite Graph: Start at \(v \in R\) (where \(c_v\) is free and \(c_u\) is present) and walk the unique maximal alternating path \(P\) of colors \(c_u, c_v, c_u, c_v, \dots\):
\[v \xrightarrow{c_u} u_1 \xrightarrow{c_v} v_1 \xrightarrow{c_u} u_2 \xrightarrow{c_v} \dots\]
Could this path \(P\) ever cycle back to \(v\)? No, because \(v\) has degree \(1\) in \(H(c_u, c_v)\) (\(c_v\) is free at \(v\)). Could \(P\) ever terminate at \(u \in L\) and accidentally bring color \(c_u\) into \(u\)? Never in a bipartite graph! Observe the parity of the bipartite sides along \(P\):

Every move \(R \to L\) traverses an edge of color \(c_u\).
Every move \(L \to R\) traverses an edge of color \(c_v\).

Thus, if \(P\) ever reached \(u \in L\), it would have to arrive at \(u\) from \(R\) via an edge of color \(c_u\)—which is impossible because color \(c_u\) is free at \(u\) (\(\text{who}[u][c_u] = 0\))! (Note: \(P\) might reach \(u\)? No, it can’t even reach \(u\) at all, because \(u\) has no incident edge of color \(c_u\)!) Therefore, \(P\) is a simple path of length \(\le |L| + |R|\) that never touches \(u\). Swapping colors \(c_u \leftrightarrow c_v\) on every edge of \(P\) preserves validity everywhere, leaves \(c_u\) free at \(u\), and frees color \(c_u\) at \(v\)! We then color \((u, v)\) with \(c_u\).

// Online O((|L| + |R|) * E) Bipartite Edge Coloring with Delta colors (1..Delta).
// who[z][c] = neighbor of z along color c (0 if free); edge_id[z][c] = index of that edge.
void color_bipartite_edge(int u, int v, int e_idx,
                          vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;& who, vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;& edge_id,
                          vector&lt;int&gt;& ans_color) {
    int cu = 1, cv = 1;
    while (who[u][cu] != 0) ++cu;
    while (who[v][cv] != 0) ++cv;
    ans_color[e_idx] = cu;
    if (who[v][cu] == 0) {
        who[u][cu] = v; who[v][cu] = u;
        edge_id[u][cu] = edge_id[v][cu] = e_idx;
        return;
    }
    // Walk the (cu, cv)-Kempe chain starting from v with color cu:
    vector&lt;int&gt; path_verts = {u, v};
    vector&lt;int&gt; path_edges = {e_idx};
    int cur = v, col = cu;
    while (who[cur][col] != 0) {
        int nxt = who[cur][col];
        int eid = edge_id[cur][col];
        path_verts.push_back(nxt);
        path_edges.push_back(eid);
        who[cur][col] = 0;
        edge_id[cur][col] = 0;
        cur = nxt;
        col ^= (cu ^ cv);
    }
    // Re-link the chain with swapped alternating colors cu, cv, cu, ...
    int assign_col = cu;
    for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; path_edges.size(); ++i) {
        int a = path_verts[i], b = path_verts[i + 1], eid = path_edges[i];
        ans_color[eid] = assign_col;
        who[a][assign_col] = b; who[b][assign_col] = a;
        edge_id[a][assign_col] = edge_id[b][assign_col] = eid;
        assign_col ^= (cu ^ cv);
    }
}

Step 4 — Non-Bipartite Odd-Cycle Counterexample: Why does this proof fail on a non-bipartite graph such as the triangle \(C_3 = \{1, 2, 3\}\) where \(\Delta = 2\)? Suppose \((1, 2)\) has color \(1\) and \((2, 3)\) has color \(2\), and we insert edge \((1, 3)\). At vertex \(1\), color \(c_1 = 2\) is free; at vertex \(3\), color \(c_3 = 1\) is free. Starting at \(3\) and walking colors \((2, 1)\) goes \(3 \xrightarrow{2} 2 \xrightarrow{1} 1\)—arriving at vertex \(1\) via an edge of color \(1\) because the odd cycle broke the \((R \to L \implies c_u)\) parity invariant! Swapping \(1 \leftrightarrow 2\) on \(3 - 2 - 1\) makes \(c_1 = 2\) free at \(3\), but simultaneously occupies color \(2\) at \(1\), getting us nowhere (indeed, \(\chi'(C_3) = 3 = \Delta + 1\)).





4.3 Engine 3: \(O(E \log \Delta)\) Divide-and-Conquer via Eulerian Circuit Halving
When \(V, E \le 10^5\) and \(\Delta \le 10^5\), even \(O(VE)\) Kempe-chain recoloring is too slow. For any \(k\)-regular bipartite multigraph where \(k = 2^r\) is a power of \(2\) (or for general \(\Delta\) by pairing odd-degree vertices at each recursion level):

Every vertex has even degree \(2d\), so every connected component has an Eulerian circuit computable in \(O(E)\) linear time via Hierholzer’s algorithm.
Because \(G\) is bipartite, every cycle—and therefore every Eulerian circuit—has even length!
Traverse the Eulerian circuit and place odd-indexed edges into \(E_{\text{odd}}\) and even-indexed edges into \(E_{\text{even}}\). Every time the circuit enters a vertex \(z\) and immediately leaves \(z\), one edge goes to \(E_{\text{odd}}\) and one edge goes to \(E_{\text{even}}\) (and at the start/end vertex of the circuit, the very first edge is odd and the very last edge is even because the total length is even!).
Thus \(E = E_{\text{odd}} \sqcup E_{\text{even}}\) splits a \(2d\)-regular bipartite multigraph into two edge-disjoint \(d\)-regular bipartite multigraphs in \(O(E)\) time! Recursing \(\log_2 \Delta\) levels yields all \(\Delta\) matchings in \(O(E \log \Delta)\) time.

