Every problem below is compiled using the 4-Step Zero-Magic-Gadgets Protocol: (1) Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal, (2) Intermediate Algebraic Representation, (3) Mechanical Graph Compilation, and (4) Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard.


#049 — [ZJOI2007] 矩阵游戏 (Matrix Game)

Tier: Stepping Stone 1 (SS-1) | Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P1129
Constraints: \(T \le 20\) test cases; \(N \times N\) binary matrix \(A_{r,c} \in \{0, 1\}\) with \(N \le 200\); Time Limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Summary: Given an \(N \times N\) chessboard of black (\(1\)) and white (\(0\)) cells, you may swap any two rows or swap any two columns any number of times. Determine whether it is possible to place \(N\) black cells on the main diagonal \((1, 1), (2, 2), \dots, (N, N)\).







NoteHint 1 — What Invariant Survives Row and Column Swaps?





Suppose two black cells start in the same row. Can any sequence of row swaps or column swaps ever place them in different rows? What must be true of the \(N\) black cells that eventually land on \((1, 1), (2, 2), \dots, (N, N)\)?









NoteHint 2 — Bipartite Incidence Graph





Row swaps and column swaps preserve “same row” and “same column” relationships—they simply relabel the rows and columns! Build a bipartite graph between Rows \(L = \{1, \dots, N\}\) and Columns \(R = \{1, \dots, N\}\) with an edge \((r, c)\) whenever \(A_{r,c} = 1\). What does a transversal of \(N\) diagonal ones correspond to?









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & Solution — #049 [ZJOI2007] 矩阵游戏






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
The operations act globally on entire rows and entire columns (\(A \mapsto P A Q\) for permutation matrices \(P, Q\)). Placing \(N\) ones onto the main diagonal \((1, 1), \dots, (N, N)\) requires selecting \(N\) ones from \(A\) such that no two share a row and no two share a column.


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(x_{r,c} \in \{0, 1\}\) indicate whether cell \((r, c)\) is selected to become one of the \(N\) final main-diagonal entries. Since row and column permutations are independent, such a permutation pair \((\sigma, \tau) \in S_N \times S_N\) exists if and only if there is a feasible solution to:
\[x_{r,c} \le A_{r,c} \;\; (\forall r, c), \qquad \sum_{c=1}^N x_{r,c} = 1 \;\; (\forall r \in \{1, \dots, N\}), \qquad \sum_{r=1}^N x_{r,c} = 1 \;\; (\forall c \in \{1, \dots, N\}).\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Left Vertices \(L\): \(\{R_1, \dots, R_N\}\) (one per row).
Right Vertices \(R\): \(\{C_1, \dots, C_N\}\) (one per column).
Edges \(E\): Add undirected matching edge \((R_r, C_c)\) iff \(A_{r,c} = 1\).
Target Invariant: Output Yes if and only if \(\nu(G) = N\) (a perfect matching exists).

// Core Modelling Snippet for #049 ([ZJOI2007] Matrix Game)
bool solve_matrix_game(int N, const vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;& A) {
    Dinic Flow(2 * N + 2);
    int S = 0, T = 2 * N + 1;
    for (int r = 1; r &lt;= N; ++r) Flow.add_edge(S, r, 1);
    for (int c = 1; c &lt;= N; ++c) Flow.add_edge(N + c, T, 1);
    for (int r = 1; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 1; c &lt;= N; ++c) {
            if (A[r - 1][c - 1] == 1) {
                Flow.add_edge(r, N + c, 1);
            }
        }
    }
    return Flow.max_flow(S, T) == N;
}


Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace (\(N = 2\)): For \(A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}\), row \(2\) has degree \(0\), so \(\nu(G) = 1 &lt; 2\) (No). For \(A = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}\), edges \((R_1, C_2)\) and \((R_2, C_1)\) give \(\nu(G) = 2\) (Yes).
Failure-Mode Guard:

Remember to clear the graph / adjacency lists between the \(T \le 20\) test cases.
Do not assume that finding \(\nu(G) = N\) requires only row swaps or only column swaps—in fact, either row swaps alone or column swaps alone already suffice once a perfect matching exists, because \(\sigma \circ \tau^{-1}\) can be absorbed into one side!

Complexity: \(O(T \cdot N^{2.5})\) via Dinic or \(O(T \cdot N^3)\) via Kuhn’s algorithm.








#050 — Luogu P2071: 座位安排 (Seat Arrangement)

Tier: Stepping Stone 2 (SS-2) | Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P2071
Constraints: \(N \le 2000\) rows of seats (\(2N\) passengers total); each row holds at most \(2\) passengers; Time Limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Summary: An airplane has \(N\) rows of seats, with \(2\) seats per row, and \(2N\) passengers boarding. Each passenger \(i \in \{1, \dots, 2N\}\) is willing to sit only in row \(r_{i,1}\) or row \(r_{i,2}\). Compute the maximum number of passengers who can be seated.







NoteHint 1 — From Unit Matching to Capacitated b-Matching





Each passenger \(i \in L\) can take at most \(1\) seat, while each row \(r \in R\) can accept up to \(b_r = 2\) passengers. How can you represent \(b_r = 2\) in an \(S\)-\(T\) flow network without cloning vertices?









NoteHint 2 — Direct Terminal Capacity vs. Seat Cloning





In Dinic’s algorithm, wire \(S \to \text{Passenger}_i\) with capacity \(1\), \(\text{Passenger}_i \to \text{Row}_{r}\) with capacity \(1\), and \(\text{Row}_r \to T\) with capacity \(2\)! (Alternatively, in Kuhn’s algorithm, represent each row \(r\) as two seat vertices \(\text{Seat}_{r,1}\) and \(\text{Seat}_{r,2}\).)









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & Solution — #050 Luogu P2071: 座位安排






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Each passenger has two allowable row choices \((r_{i,1}, r_{i,2})\) and unit demand (\(a_i = 1\)), while each row \(r \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) has capacity \(b_r = 2\). This is a \((1, 2)\)-capacitated bipartite \(b\)-matching.


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(x_{i, r} \in \{0, 1\}\) indicate whether passenger \(i \in \{1, \dots, 2N\}\) is assigned to row \(r \in \{r_{i,1}, r_{i,2}\}\). We maximize:
\[\max \sum_{i=1}^{2N} \sum_{r \in \{r_{i,1}, r_{i,2}\}} x_{i,r} \quad \text{subject to} \quad \sum_{r} x_{i,r} \le 1 \;\; (\forall i \in \{1, \dots, 2N\}), \qquad \sum_{i} x_{i,r} \le 2 \;\; (\forall r \in \{1, \dots, N\}).\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Vertices: Source \(S = 0\), Passengers \(L = \{1, \dots, 2N\}\), Rows \(R = \{2N + 1, \dots, 3N\}\), Sink \(T = 3N + 1\).
Edges:

\(S \xrightarrow{\text{cap } 1} i\) for each passenger \(i \in \{1, \dots, 2N\}\).
\(i \xrightarrow{\text{cap } 1} (2N + r_{i,1})\) and (if \(r_{i,2} \neq r_{i,1}\)) \(i \xrightarrow{\text{cap } 1} (2N + r_{i,2})\).
\((2N + r) \xrightarrow{\text{cap } 2} T\) for each row \(r \in \{1, \dots, N\}\).


// Core Modelling Snippet for #050 (Luogu P2071 Seat Arrangement)
int solve_seats(int N, const vector&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt;& prefs) {
    int S = 0, T = 3 * N + 1;
    Dinic Flow(T + 1);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 2 * N; ++i) {
        Flow.add_edge(S, i, 1);
        auto [r1, r2] = prefs[i - 1];
        Flow.add_edge(i, 2 * N + r1, 1);
        if (r2 != r1) {
            Flow.add_edge(i, 2 * N + r2, 1);
        }
    }
    for (int r = 1; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
        Flow.add_edge(2 * N + r, T, 2);
    }
    return Flow.max_flow(S, T);
}


Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace (\(N = 1\), \(2\) passengers): Both passengers prefer row \((1, 1)\). Passenger \(1\) sends \(1\) unit of flow \(S \to 1 \to \text{Row}_1 \to T\), and Passenger \(2\) sends \(1\) unit \(S \to 2 \to \text{Row}_1 \to T\), saturating the capacity-\(2\) arc \(\text{Row}_1 \to T\) for a total of \(2\) seated passengers.
Failure-Mode Guard (Duplicate Row Preferences): What if a passenger lists the same row twice (\(r_{i,1} = r_{i,2}\))? In our Dinic network, even if you forgot the if (r2 != r1) check, the capacity-\(1\) bottleneck on \(S \to i\) prevents passenger \(i\) from taking both seats in row \(r_{i,1}\). However, if you invert the formulation or use degree-counting arrays, duplicate edges between \((i, r)\) can corrupt degree bounds—always deduplicate \((r_{i,1}, r_{i,2})\) on input!
Complexity: \(|V| = 3N + 2 \le 6002\), \(|E| \le 7N \le 14000\). Dinic runs in \(O(N\sqrt{N})\) time (\(&lt; 0.02\text{ s}\)).








#051 — Codeforces 387D: George and Interesting Graph

Tier: Intermediate 1 (Int-1) | Judge URL: https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/387/D
Constraints: \(N \le 500\) vertices, \(M \le 1000\) directed edges (self-loops \(u \to u\) allowed, no parallel edges); Time Limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Summary: A directed graph is called interesting if there exists a center vertex \(c \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) such that:

For every vertex \(u \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) (including \(u = c\)), both directed edges \(c \to u\) and \(u \to c\) exist (note that for \(u = c\), this is a single self-loop \(c \to c\)).
Every non-center vertex \(u \neq c\) has total in-degree \(2\) and total out-degree \(2\) in the entire graph.

Compute the minimum number of edge additions and edge deletions needed to make the given graph interesting.







NoteHint 1 — Fix the Center Vertex c First





Since \(N \le 500\) and \(M \le 1000\) are small, we can afford to test all \(N\) candidates for the center vertex \(c \in \{1, \dots, N\}\). Once \(c\) is fixed, all edges incident to \(c\) are completely determined! What condition must the remaining edges on \(V \setminus \{c\}\) satisfy?









NoteHint 2 — Residual 1-Factor on Non-Center Vertices





Once \(c \to u\) and \(u \to c\) are present for every \(u \neq c\), each \(u \in V \setminus \{c\}\) already has \(1\) incoming edge from \(c\) and \(1\) outgoing edge to \(c\). To reach total in-degree \(2\) and out-degree \(2\), the subgraph on \(V \setminus \{c\}\) must have in-degree \(1\) and out-degree \(1\) at every vertex—which is a Perfect Matching between \((V \setminus \{c\})_{\text{out}}\) and \((V \setminus \{c\})_{\text{in}}\)!









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & Solution — #051 CF 387D: George and Interesting Graph






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
The problem couples a global star vertex \(c\) with a uniform degree-\(2\) constraint on all other vertices. Because \(N \le 500\) and \(M \le 1000\), enumerating the center \(c\) decouples the star edges from the remaining \((N - 1)\) vertices, where in-degree \(1\) and out-degree \(1\) is a standard bipartite matching between out-ports and in-ports!


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Fix a candidate center \(c \in \{1, \dots, N\}\):

Center Star Edges: There are exactly \(2N - 1\) required edges incident to \(c\): the self-loop \((c \to c)\) plus \((c \to u)\) and \((u \to c)\) for the \(N - 1\) vertices \(u \neq c\). Let \(k_c\) be the number of these \(2N - 1\) edges that already exist in \(E\). We must add \((2N - 1) - k_c\) missing center edges.
Non-Center Matching Edges: There are \(M_{\text{rem}} = M - k_c\) existing edges in \(E\) that have neither endpoint equal to \(c\). In the final graph, the edges on \(V \setminus \{c\}\) must form a directed \(1\)-factor (\(N - 1\) edges with out-degree \(1\) and in-degree \(1\) on \(V \setminus \{c\}\)). If the maximum bipartite matching inside the existing \(M_{\text{rem}}\) edges has size \(\nu_c\), we keep those \(\nu_c\) edges, delete the remaining \(M_{\text{rem}} - \nu_c\) unused existing edges, and add \((N - 1) - \nu_c\) new edges to complete the perfect matching!

Summing additions and deletions for fixed \(c\) gives:
\[\operatorname{Cost}(c) = \underbrace{(2N - 1 - k_c)}_{\text{add center edges}} + \underbrace{(M - k_c - \nu_c)}_{\text{delete extra non-center edges}} + \underbrace{(N - 1 - \nu_c)}_{\text{add missing matching edges}} = 3N + M - 2 - 2k_c - 2\nu_c.\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)
For each \(c \in \{1, \dots, N\}\):

Build bipartite graph \(B_c = (L \sqcup R, E_c)\) with \(L = \{u_{\text{out}} : u \neq c\}\) and \(R = \{v_{\text{in}} : v \neq c\}\).
Scan all \(M\) input edges \((u \to v)\):

If \(u = c\) or \(v = c\), increment \(k_c\).
Otherwise (\(u \neq c\) and \(v \neq c\)), add bipartite edge \((u_{\text{out}}, v_{\text{in}})\) to \(B_c\).

Compute \(\nu_c = \operatorname{MaxMatching}(B_c)\) via Dinic / Hopcroft–Karp and minimize \(3N + M - 2 - 2k_c - 2\nu_c\).

// Core Modelling Snippet for #051 (CF 387D George and Interesting Graph)
int solve_interesting_graph(int N, const vector&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt;& edges) {
    int M = edges.size(), best_cost = 1e9;
    for (int c = 1; c &lt;= N; ++c) {
        int kc = 0, S = 0, T = 2 * N + 1;
        Dinic Flow(T + 1);
        for (int u = 1; u &lt;= N; ++u) {
            if (u == c) continue;
            Flow.add_edge(S, u, 1);
            Flow.add_edge(N + u, T, 1);
        }
        for (auto [u, v] : edges) {
            if (u == c || v == c) {
                ++kc;
            } else {
                Flow.add_edge(u, N + v, 1);
            }
        }
        int nu_c = Flow.max_flow(S, T);
        int cost = (2 * N - 1 - kc) + (M - kc - nu_c) + (N - 1 - nu_c);
        best_cost = min(best_cost, cost);
    }
    return best_cost;
}


Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace (\(N = 2\), \(M = 1\) edge \(1 \to 2\)):

Test \(c = 1\): edge \(1 \to 2\) has \(u = 1 = c\), so \(k_1 = 1\), \(M_{\text{rem}} = 0\), \(\nu_1 = 0\). Cost \(= (3 - 1) + (0 - 0) + (1 - 0) = 3\) (add \(1 \to 1, 2 \to 1, 2 \to 2\)).

Failure-Mode Guard (The Self-Loop Double-Count Trap):

Notice that for \(u = c\), the edges \(c \to u\) and \(u \to c\) are the same single self-loop \(c \to c\)! Thus there are \(2N - 1\) required center edges, not \(2N\). Using if (u == c || v == c) ++kc; (with ||, not two separate ifs!) correctly counts \(c \to c\) once.
Notice that a non-center vertex \(u \neq c\) is allowed to have a self-loop \(u \to u\) as its matched edge \((u_{\text{out}}, u_{\text{in}})\) in \(B_c\)! Do not filter out self-loops \(u \to u\) when \(u \neq c\).

Complexity: \(N\) runs of Hopcroft–Karp on \(M \le 1000\) edges takes \(O(N \cdot M\sqrt{N}) \approx 500 \times 1000 \times 22 \approx 1.1 \times 10^7\) operations (\(&lt; 0.1\text{ s}\)).








#052 — [HNOI2013] 消毒 (Disinfection)

Tier: Intermediate 2 (Int-2) | Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P3231
Constraints: \(T \le 3\) test cases; 3D box dimensions \(A \times B \times C\) with \(A \cdot B \cdot C \le 5000\); Time Limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Summary: An \(A \times B \times C\) 3D grid contains infected unit cells \((x, y, z)\). In one operation, you may choose any sub-box of dimensions \(a \times b \times c\) and sterilize all cells inside it at a cost of \(\min(a, b, c)\) units of chemical. Find the minimum total cost to sterilize all infected cells.







NoteHint 1 — Why Every Optimal Operation Is a Thickness-1 Plane





If you pay \(\min(a, b, c)\) to sterilize an \(a \times b \times c\) box, why would you ever choose \(\min(a, b, c) &gt; 1\)? And if one dimension of your box is \(1\) (say \(a = 1\)), why not extend the other two dimensions to the full grid \(1 \times B \times C\) for the exact same cost of \(\min(1, B, C) = 1\)?









NoteHint 2 — Exploiting A * B * C &lt;= 5000





Every operation is simply a full axis-aligned plane (\(x = x_0\), \(y = y_0\), or \(z = z_0\)) of cost \(1\). Covering points in 3D with planes is NP-hard in general, but \(A \cdot B \cdot C \le 5000\) guarantees \(\min(A, B, C) \le \lfloor 5000^{1/3} \rfloor = 17\)! Brute-force the bitmask of planes along the shortest dimension, and project the remaining uncovered cells onto the other two dimensions!









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & Solution — #052 [HNOI2013] 消毒






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)

Cost Reduction to Thickness-1 Planes: Since \(\min(a, b, c)\) can be decomposed into \(\min(a, b, c)\) slices of thickness \(1\) along the shortest side of the sub-box, and expanding a \(1 \times b \times c\) slice to \(1 \times B \times C\) never increases its cost \(\min(1, B, C) = 1\), every operation is an axis-aligned plane \(x = x_0\), \(y = y_0\), or \(z = z_0\) of cost \(1\).
Dimension Collapse via Pigeonhole Bound: Since \(A \cdot B \cdot C \le 5000\), permuting coordinates so \(A = \min(A, B, C)\) guarantees \(A \le 17\) (\(2^{17} = 131,072\)).



Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(M \subseteq \{1, \dots, A\}\) be the subset of \(x\)-planes purchased directly (cost \(|M|\)). Every infected cell \((x, y, z)\) with \(x \in M\) is already sterilized. The remaining infected cells with \(x \notin M\) must satisfy \(v_y + w_z \ge 1\), which is a 2D Minimum Vertex Cover on \(Y \times Z\):
\[\text{Ans} = \min_{M \subseteq \{1, \dots, A\}} \Big( |M| + \nu(G_M) \Big), \qquad E(G_M) = \big\{ (y, z) \in Y \times Z : \exists x \notin M \text{ s.t. } (x, y, z) \text{ is infected} \big\}.\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Reorient \((A, B, C)\) so \(A \le B \le C\) (giving \(A \le 17\) and \(B \le \lfloor \sqrt{5000} \rfloor = 70\)).
For each unique \((y, z)\) pair that contains at least one infected cell, precompute a bitmask \(\text{x\_mask}(y, z) \in [1, 2^A)\) whose \(x\)-th bit is \(1\) iff \((x, y, z)\) is infected.
For each bitmask \(M \in [0, 2^A)\):

Pruning: If \(\operatorname{popcount}(M) \ge \text{best\_ans}\), skip \(M\) immediately.
Build the 2D bipartite graph \(G_M\) on \(L = \{1, \dots, B\}\) and \(R = \{1, \dots, C\}\) by adding edge \((y, z)\) iff \((\text{x\_mask}(y, z) \mathbin{\&} \sim M) \neq 0\).
Run Kuhn’s algorithm (or Dinic), aborting early if \(\operatorname{popcount}(M) + \text{current\_matching} \ge \text{best\_ans}\).


// Core Modelling Snippet for #052 ([HNOI2013] Disinfection)
// Assumes coordinates are rotated so A &lt;= B &lt;= C (A &lt;= 17, B &lt;= 70).
// items = list of (y, z, x_bitmask) for all non-empty (y, z) columns.
int solve_disinfection(int A, int B, int C, const vector&lt;tuple&lt;int, int, int&gt;&gt;& items) {
    int best_ans = A; // Purchasing all A x-planes is always a valid cover of cost A!
    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; adj(B + 1);
    vector&lt;int&gt; matchR(C + 1, 0), vis(C + 1, 0);
    int timer = 0;

    auto dfs = [&](auto& self, int u) -&gt; bool {
        for (int v : adj[u]) {
            if (vis[v] == timer) continue;
            vis[v] = timer;
            if (!matchR[v] || self(self, matchR[v])) {
                matchR[v] = u;
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    };

    for (int mask = 0; mask &lt; (1 &lt;&lt; A); ++mask) {
        int cost = __builtin_popcount(mask);
        if (cost &gt;= best_ans) continue;
        for (int y = 1; y &lt;= B; ++y) adj[y].clear();
        for (auto [y, z, x_bits] : items) {
            if (x_bits & ~mask) adj[y].push_back(z);
        }
        fill(matchR.begin(), matchR.end(), 0);
        for (int y = 1; y &lt;= B; ++y) {
            ++timer;
            if (dfs(dfs, y)) {
                if (++cost &gt;= best_ans) break; // Early branch-and-bound abort!
            }
        }
        best_ans = min(best_ans, cost);
    }
    return best_ans;
}


Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace: Infected cells at \((1, 1, 1), (1, 2, 2), (2, 1, 2)\).

Mask \(M = \{1\}\) (sterilize plane \(x = 1\), cost \(1\)): only \((2, 1, 2)\) survives, projecting to a single edge \((y=1, z=2)\) with \(\nu = 1\), total cost \(1 + 1 = 2\).

Failure-Mode Guard: Initialize best_ans = A (since buying all \(A\) planes along the \(x\)-axis costs \(A\)) and abort Kuhn’s loop as soon as cost &gt;= best_ans. Without rotating the axes so \(A = \min(A, B, C) \le 17\) and \(B \le 70\), \(2^A\) can be \(2^{5000}\)!
Complexity: \(O(2^{\min(A,B,C)} \cdot BC)\) with strong branch-and-bound pruning (\(\approx 0.15\text{ s}\) for \(T = 3\)).








#053 — Codeforces 512E: Fox And Polygon

Tier: Advanced 1 (Adv-1) | Judge URL: https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/512/E
Constraints: \(N \le 1000\) vertices on a convex \(N\)-gon; \(N - 3\) initial diagonals \(T_1\) and \(N - 3\) target diagonals \(T_2\); at most \(20,000\) diagonal flips allowed (tight bound \(\le 2N - 6\)); Time Limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Summary: Given two triangulations \(T_1\) and \(T_2\) of a convex \(N\)-gon, you can perform diagonal flips: pick a diagonal \((a, c)\) shared by two triangles \((a, b, c)\) and \((a, c, d)\), remove \((a, c)\), and insert the cross-diagonal \((b, d)\). Output a sequence of at most \(20,000\) flips transforming \(T_1\) into \(T_2\).







NoteHint 1 — Meet in the Middle at a Canonical Hub Triangulation





Instead of trying to morph \(T_1\) directly into \(T_2\), choose a single canonical triangulation \(T_{\text{fan}(1)}\)—the Fan Triangulation centered at vertex \(1\), where all \(N - 3\) diagonals are \((1, 3), (1, 4), \dots, (1, N - 1)\). If you can reduce any triangulation \(T \to T_{\text{fan}(1)}\) in \(\le N - 3\) flips, how do you combine \(T_1 \to T_{\text{fan}(1)}\) and \(T_2 \to T_{\text{fan}(1)}\)?









NoteHint 2 — Greedy Fan Degree Augmentation & Reverse Flip Polarity





Whenever vertex \(1\) has degree \(&lt; N - 1\) in the current triangulation, there exists some triangle \((1, a, c)\) where \((a, c)\) is a non-fan diagonal (\(1 &lt; a &lt; c\)). If \((a, b, c)\) is the unique other triangle sharing \((a, c)\) (\(a &lt; b &lt; c\)), flipping \((a, c) \mapsto (1, b)\) strictly increases the degree of vertex \(1\) by \(+1\)! When reversing the path \(T_2 \to T_{\text{fan}(1)}\), remember that a forward step flip (a, c) -&gt; creates (1, b) becomes the backward step flip (1, b) -&gt; creates (a, c)!









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & Solution — #053 CF 512E: Fox And Polygon






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
The space of triangulations of a convex \(N\)-gon (the \(N-2\)-dimensional associahedron) is connected by diagonal flips, and every triangulation has a hub-star canonical form \(T_{\text{fan}(1)}\) where vertex \(1\) is connected to every vertex \(3, 4, \dots, N - 1\).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Maintain the \(N \times N\) boolean adjacency matrix adj[u][v] of polygon boundary edges plus current diagonals.

A triangulation equals \(T_{\text{fan}(1)}\) iff \(\deg(1) = N - 1\) (vertex \(1\) is adjacent to all \(v \in \{2, \dots, N\}\)).
Whenever \(\deg(1) &lt; N - 1\), there exist two consecutive neighbors \(a &lt; c\) of vertex \(1\) in clockwise order (\(c &gt; a + 1\)) such that \((a, c)\) is a diagonal (so \((1, a, c)\) is a triangle, and no \(b \in (a, c)\) is currently connected to \(1\)).
Inside the arc \([a, c]\), there is a unique vertex \(b \in (a, c)\) such that adj[a][b] && adj[b][c]. Flipping \((a, c) \mapsto (1, b)\) replaces adj[a][c] = 0 with adj[1][b] = 1, increasing \(\deg(1)\) by \(+1\).



Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Compilation)
Write a helper to_fan(adj) that returns the list of flipped pairs ((a, c), (1, b)):

Compute ops1 = to_fan(T1) and ops2 = to_fan(T2).
Reverse ops2.
Output the old diagonal (a, c) for each step in ops1 (as required by \(T_1 \to T_{\text{fan}(1)}\)), followed by the new diagonal (1, b) for each reversed step in ops2 (which is the diagonal currently present in \(T_{\text{fan}(1)} \to T_2\)!).

// Core Modelling Snippet for #053 (CF 512E Fox And Polygon)
// Returns vector of {old_diag, new_diag} transforming adj into Fan(1).
vector&lt;pair&lt;pair&lt;int,int&gt;, pair&lt;int,int&gt;&gt;&gt; to_fan(int N, vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; adj) {
    vector&lt;pair&lt;pair&lt;int,int&gt;, pair&lt;int,int&gt;&gt;&gt; ops;
    while (true) {
        int a = -1, c = -1;
        // Find two consecutive neighbors a &lt; c of vertex 1 with c &gt; a + 1
        int prev_nb = 2;
        for (int v = 3; v &lt;= N; ++v) {
            if (adj[1][v]) {
                if (v &gt; prev_nb + 1) { a = prev_nb; c = v; break; }
                prev_nb = v;
            }
        }
        if (a == -1) break; // All fan diagonals (1, 3)..(1, N-1) are present!
        int b = -1;
        for (int k = a + 1; k &lt; c; ++k) {
            if (adj[a][k] && adj[k][c]) { b = k; break; }
        }
        adj[a][c] = adj[c][a] = 0;
        adj[1][b] = adj[b][1] = 1;
        ops.push_back({{a, c}, {1, b}});
    }
    return ops;
}


Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace (\(N = 4\)): \(T_1\) has diagonal \((2, 4)\) and \(T_2\) has diagonal \((1, 3)\).

to_fan(T1): neighbors of \(1\) are \(2, 4\) (\(4 &gt; 2 + 1\)), with \(b = 3\) satisfying adj[2][3] && adj[3][4]. Flip \((2, 4) \mapsto (1, 3)\) in \(1\) step.
to_fan(T2): \(1\) is already connected to \(2, 3, 4\), so \(0\) steps.

Failure-Mode Guard (Reverse-Phase Diagonal Swap): In CF 512E, each operation in the output is specified by printing the two endpoints of the diagonal currently being removed. During Phase 1 (\(T_1 \to T_{\text{fan}(1)}\)), you print op.first = (a, c). During Phase 2 (\(T_{\text{fan}(1)} \to T_2\), iterating through ops2 in reverse), the diagonal currently present on the board is op.second = (1, b)! Printing op.first in Phase 2 is the #1 WA bug on this problem.
Complexity: At most \(2(N - 3) \le 1994\) flips; each step scans \(O(N)\), totaling \(O(N^2)\) time (\(&lt; 0.03\text{ s}\)).








#054 — AtCoder AGC 029 F: Construction of a Tree

Tier: Advanced 2 (Adv-2) | Judge URL: https://atcoder.jp/contests/agc029/tasks/agc029_f
Constraints: \(N \le 10^5\) vertices; \(N - 1\) subsets \(E_1, \dots, E_{N-1} \subseteq \{1, \dots, N\}\) with \(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1} |E_i| \le 2 \cdot 10^5\); Time Limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Summary: You are given \(N - 1\) vertex subsets \(E_1, \dots, E_{N-1}\). For each \(i \in \{1, \dots, N - 1\}\), choose two distinct vertices \(u_i, v_i \in E_i\) and add the undirected edge \((u_i, v_i)\) so that the resulting \(N - 1\) edges form a spanning tree on \(\{1, \dots, N\}\), or output -1 if no such tree exists.







NoteHint 1 — Root the Tree at Vertex 1 to Create a Bijection





Suppose a valid spanning tree \(T\) exists. Root \(T\) at vertex \(r = 1\). Then every non-root vertex \(v \in \{2, \dots, N\}\) has a unique parent edge \(e_i = (u_i \to v)\) entering \(v\)! What does the correspondence \(E_i \longleftrightarrow v_i\) form in the bipartite incidence graph between \(\{E_1, \dots, E_{N-1}\}\) and \(\{2, \dots, N\}\)?









NoteHint 2 — Matching Gives the Child v_i; BFS from Root 1 Gives the Parent u_i





First find a perfect matching \(M\) of size \(N - 1\) between \(\{E_1, \dots, E_{N-1}\}\) and \(\{2, \dots, N\}\) (excluding root \(1\)!). Let \(v_i = M(E_i) \in E_i \setminus \{1\}\) be the matched child for hyperedge \(E_i\). To avoid cycles, start a BFS queue with visited set \(V_{\text{vis}} = \{1\}\): whenever a visited vertex \(u\) belongs to an unused hyperedge \(E_i\), set \(E_i\)’s edge to \((u, M(E_i))\) and push \(M(E_i)\) into the queue!









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & Solution — #054 AGC 029 F: Construction of a Tree






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
By Rado’s Theorem / Hall’s Condition on Graphic Matroids, \(N - 1\) hyperedges \(E_1, \dots, E_{N-1}\) can realize a spanning tree if and only if every non-empty subset \(S \subseteq \{1, \dots, N-1\}\) spans at least \(|S| + 1\) vertices:
\[\forall \emptyset \neq S \subseteq \{1, \dots, N - 1\}, \qquad \left| \bigcup_{i \in S} E_i \right| \ge |S| + 1.\]
Rooting at vertex \(1\) turns this \(+1\) surplus into a standard bipartite perfect matching on \(V \setminus \{1\}\) plus a single BFS connectivity sweep!


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation

Build bipartite graph \(G = (L \sqcup R, E_{\text{bip}})\) with \(L = \{E_1, \dots, E_{N-1}\}\) (\(N - 1\) hyperedges) and \(R = \{2, 3, \dots, N\}\) (\(N - 1\) non-root vertices), connecting \((E_i, v)\) whenever \(v \in E_i\) and \(v \ge 2\).
If \(\nu(G) &lt; N - 1\), no tree can exist (output -1).
Otherwise, let \(M(E_i) = v_i \in \{2, \dots, N\}\) be the perfect matching. Seed a BFS queue at \(u = 1\). Whenever \(u\) is popped, for every unused hyperedge \(E_i \ni u\), orient \(E_i\) as \((u, v_i)\), mark \(E_i\) used, and push \(v_i = M(E_i)\) into the BFS queue.



Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Proof that BFS Never Lies)
Wait—what if our BFS stalls before visiting all \(N - 1\) hyperedges, even though some other perfect matching \(M'\) might have worked? Theorem: If the BFS stalls with unvisited hyperedge set \(S_{\text{unvis}} \neq \emptyset\), then no spanning tree exists for ANY matching! Proof: Since \(M\) is a bijection between \(L\) and \(R\), the set of unvisited vertices \(V_{\text{unvis}} = \{ M(E_i) : i \in S_{\text{unvis}} \}\) has size \(|V_{\text{unvis}}| = |S_{\text{unvis}}|\). Because the BFS stalled, no hyperedge \(E_i\) (\(i \in S_{\text{unvis}}\)) contains any visited vertex in \(V \setminus V_{\text{unvis}}\). Therefore:
\[\bigcup_{i \in S_{\text{unvis}}} E_i \subseteq V_{\text{unvis}} \implies \left| \bigcup_{i \in S_{\text{unvis}}} E_i \right| \le |V_{\text{unvis}}| = |S_{\text{unvis}}| &lt; |S_{\text{unvis}}| + 1,\]
which directly violates Rado’s tree condition (\(|S_{\text{unvis}}|\) edges confined inside \(\le |S_{\text{unvis}}|\) vertices must contain a cycle)!
// Core Modelling Snippet for #054 (AGC 029 F Construction of a Tree)
// Sets E_sets[1..N-1] contain vertices in 1..N.
bool solve_agc029f(int N, const vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;& E_sets, vector&lt;pair&lt;int,int&gt;&gt;& ans) {
    int S = 0, T = 2 * N - 1;
    Dinic Flow(T + 1);
    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; belongs_to(N + 1);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N - 1; ++i) {
        Flow.add_edge(S, i, 1);
        for (int v : E_sets[i]) {
            belongs_to[v].push_back(i);
            if (v &gt;= 2) Flow.add_edge(i, (N - 1) + (v - 1), 1);
        }
    }
    for (int v = 2; v &lt;= N; ++v) Flow.add_edge((N - 1) + (v - 1), T, 1);
    if (Flow.max_flow(S, T) &lt; N - 1) return false;

    vector&lt;int&gt; match_v(N, 0);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N - 1; ++i) {
        for (auto& e : Flow.adj[i]) {
            if (e.to &gt;= N && e.to &lt; T && e.cap == 0) {
                match_v[i] = (e.to - (N - 1)) + 1;
            }
        }
    }
    ans.resize(N);
    vector&lt;int&gt; used_set(N, 0);
    queue&lt;int&gt; q;
    q.push(1);
    int used_cnt = 0;
    while (!q.empty()) {
        int u = q.front(); q.pop();
        for (int i : belongs_to[u]) {
            if (!used_set[i]) {
                used_set[i] = 1;
                ++used_cnt;
                int v = match_v[i];
                ans[i] = {u, v};
                q.push(v);
            }
        }
    }
    return used_cnt == N - 1;
}


Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace (\(N = 3\), \(E_1 = \{2, 3\}, E_2 = \{2, 3\}\)):

Matching \(M\) pairs \(E_1 \leftrightarrow 2\) and \(E_2 \leftrightarrow 3\) (\(\nu = 2 = N - 1\)).
However, vertex \(1\) belongs to zero hyperedges (belongs_to[1] is empty), so the BFS queue pops \(1\) and immediately terminates with used_cnt = 0 &lt; 2, outputting -1!

Failure-Mode Guard: Never add bipartite matching edges to vertex \(1\)! Vertex \(1\) must remain unmatched in \(R\) so that it acts as the root of the tree and seeds the BFS.
Complexity: \(O(\sum |E_i| \sqrt{N})\) for Hopcroft–Karp / Dinic + \(O(\sum |E_i|)\) for the BFS (\(\approx 0.15\text{ s}\)).








#055 — Codeforces 1592F1: Alice and Recoloring 1 (with Bridge to CF 1592F2)

Tier: Mastery 1 (Mas-1) | Judge URL: https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1592/F1
Constraints: \(N, M \le 500\); \(N \times M\) grid of W (\(0\)) and B (\(1\)) cells; Time Limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Summary: You want to turn all B (\(1\)) cells into W (\(0\)) cells using four types of subgrid bit-flip (XOR \(1\)) operations:

Op 1 (Cost 1): Flip top-left prefix rectangle \([1..r] \times [1..c]\).
Op 2 (Cost 2): Flip bottom-left rectangle \([r..N] \times [1..c]\).
Op 3 (Cost 4): Flip top-right rectangle \([1..r] \times [c..M]\).
Op 4 (Cost 3 in F1; Cost 2 in F2): Flip bottom-right suffix rectangle \([r..N] \times [c..M]\).

Find the minimum total cost to make all cells \(0\).







NoteHint 1 — Eliminate Redundant Operations & Apply 2D XOR Difference





First, notice that Op 2 (cost \(2\)) is equivalent to two Op 1 flips (\([1..N, 1..c] \oplus [1..r-1, 1..c]\), cost \(1+1=2\)), and Op 3 (cost \(4\)) is equivalent to two Op 1 flips (cost \(2 &lt; 4\)), so Op 2 and Op 3 are completely useless! Now apply the 2D Suffix XOR Difference Transform \(c_{r,c} = a_{r,c} \oplus a_{r+1,c} \oplus a_{r,c+1} \oplus a_{r+1,c+1}\) (where \(a_{r,c} = 0\) outside \([1..N] \times [1..M]\)). What do Op 1 and Op 4 do to the matrix \(c\)?









NoteHint 2 — Single-Cell Toggle vs. 4-Corner Rectangle Toggle in c





In the transformed matrix \(c\):

Op 1 at \((r, c)\) (cost \(1\)) toggles only the single entry \(c_{r,c}\)!
Op 4 at \((r+1, c+1)\) (\(1 \le r &lt; N, 1 \le c &lt; M\)) toggles the four corners \(c_{r,c}, c_{r,M}, c_{N,c}, c_{N,M}\)!

In F1 (where Op 4 costs \(3\)), when does Op 4 beat Op 1, and why can you never profitably use Op 4 twice? (And what happens in F2 when Op 4 costs \(2\)?)









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & Solution — #055 CF 1592F1 (& F2 Bridge)






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Whenever operations toggle prefix/suffix rectangles on a binary matrix, apply the 2D XOR Difference Transform:
\[c_{r,c} := a_{r,c} \oplus a_{r+1,c} \oplus a_{r,c+1} \oplus a_{r+1,c+1} \qquad (1 \le r \le N, \; 1 \le c \le M),\]
with boundary convention \(a_{r,c} = 0\) for \(r &gt; N\) or \(c &gt; M\). Notice that \(a \equiv 0 \iff c \equiv 0\).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Under this bijection between \(a\) and \(c\):

Op 1 on \([1..r] \times [1..c]\) (cost \(1\)) flips only the single cell \(c_{r,c}\)! Thus, using only Op 1, the exact cost to clear \(c\) is \(\text{BaseCost} = \sum_{r=1}^N \sum_{c=1}^M c_{r,c}\).
Op 4 on \([r+1..N] \times [c+1..M]\) (\(1 \le r &lt; N, 1 \le c &lt; M\)) simultaneously flips the four entries:
\[c_{r,c}, \quad c_{r,M}, \quad c_{N,c}, \quad c_{N,M}.\]

Now compare F1 (Op 4 costs \(3\)) and F2 (Op 4 costs \(2\)):

In F1 (Op 4 costs \(3\)):

One Op 4 at \((r, c)\) can flip at most \(4\) ones in \(c\) for cost \(3\), saving at most \(4 - 3 = 1\) compared to four Op 1s—and it saves \(+1\) if and only if all four entries \(c_{r,c} = c_{r,M} = c_{N,c} = c_{N,M} = 1\)!
What if we use Op 4 twice, at \((r_1, c_1)\) and \((r_2, c_2)\)? Both operations flip \(c_{N,M}\), so they cancel out on \(c_{N,M}\) and flip at most \(6\) distinct cells for cost \(3 + 3 = 6\)—saving \(6 - 6 = 0\)! In general, \(k \ge 2\) uses of Op 4 never save more than \(1\). Thus in F1, we use Op 4 at most once (\(O(NM)\) scan)!

Bridge to F2 (CF 1592F2, where Op 4 costs \(2\)):

When Op 4 costs \(2\), if \(c_{r,c} = c_{r,M} = c_{N,c} = 1\), applying Op 4 at \((r, c)\) clears the \(3\) cells \(c_{r,c}, c_{r,M}, c_{N,c}\) (and toggles \(c_{N,M}\)), reducing the sum \(c_{r,c} + c_{r,M} + c_{N,c}\) from \(3\) to \(0\) at cost \(2\) (saving \(+1\) per operation), provided no two chosen \((r, c)\) share a row \(r\) or column \(c\)!
Thus in F2, we build a bipartite graph between Rows \(L = \{1, \dots, N-1\}\) and Columns \(R = \{1, \dots, M-1\}\) with edge \((r, c)\) iff \(c_{r,c} = c_{r,M} = c_{N,c} = 1\), find \(\nu = \operatorname{MaxMatching}(G)\), and update \(c_{N,M} \gets c_{N,M} \oplus (\nu \bmod 2)\)!




Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Code for Both F1 and F2)
// Core Modelling Snippet for #055 (CF 1592F1 in O(NM) & CF 1592F2 via Bipartite Matching)
int solve_f1(int N, int M, const vector&lt;string&gt;& grid) {
    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; a(N + 2, vector&lt;int&gt;(M + 2, 0));
    for (int r = 1; r &lt;= N; ++r)
        for (int c = 1; c &lt;= M; ++c)
            a[r][c] = (grid[r - 1][c - 1] == 'B');

    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; diff(N + 1, vector&lt;int&gt;(M + 1, 0));
    int base_cost = 0;
    for (int r = 1; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 1; c &lt;= M; ++c) {
            diff[r][c] = a[r][c] ^ a[r + 1][c] ^ a[r][c + 1] ^ a[r + 1][c + 1];
            base_cost += diff[r][c];
        }
    }
    for (int r = 1; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 1; c &lt; M; ++c) {
            if (diff[r][c] && diff[r][M] && diff[N][c] && diff[N][M]) {
                return base_cost - 1; // At most one cost-3 op can ever save +1!
            }
        }
    }
    return base_cost;
}


Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace (\(2 \times 2\) all-B grid): Only \(a_{1,1}=a_{1,2}=a_{2,1}=a_{2,2}=1\), so \(c_{2,2} = 1\) and all other \(c_{r,c} = 0\). Base cost \(= 1\) (one Op 1 on \([1..2] \times [1..2]\)).
Failure-Mode Guard: Note index shift: Op 4 on suffix \([r+1..N] \times [c+1..M]\) toggles row \(r\) (not \(r+1\)!) and column \(c\) (not \(c+1\)!) in the bottom-right difference array \(c_{r,c} = a_{r,c} \oplus a_{r+1,c} \oplus a_{r,c+1} \oplus a_{r+1,c+1}\).
Complexity: \(O(NM)\) for F1; \(O(NM\sqrt{N + M})\) via Hopcroft–Karp for F2.








#056 — Codeforces 1783F: Double Sort II

Tier: Mastery 2 (Mas-2) | Judge URL: https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1783/F
Constraints: \(N \le 3000\); two permutations \(a, b\) of \(\{1, \dots, N\}\); Time Limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).
Problem Summary: Given two permutations \(a\) and \(b\) of \(\{1, \dots, N\}\), in one operation you may choose a value \(v \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) and simultaneously swap \(a_v\) with the current position of \(v\) in \(a\), and swap \(b_v\) with the current position of \(v\) in \(b\) (placing \(v\) into its home position \(a_v = v\) and \(b_v = v\) in both permutations!). Minimize the number of operations needed to sort both \(a\) and \(b\), and print the chosen values \(v\).







NoteHint 1 — Maximize Untouched Elements Instead of Minimizing Operations





Notice that operating on value \(v\) immediately puts \(v\) into its sorted slot \(a_v = v, b_v = v\) and splices \(v\) out of its permutation cycle in both \(a\) and \(b\). In a single permutation \(a\) with a cycle \(C\) of length \(L\), you only need \(L - 1\) operations to sort the cycle—leaving \(1\) element of \(C\) completely untouched!









NoteHint 2 — Cycle-to-Cycle Bipartite Matching





Decompose \(a\) into disjoint cycles \(A_1, \dots, A_{p}\) and \(b\) into disjoint cycles \(B_1, \dots, B_{q}\). A subset of values \(U \subseteq \{1, \dots, N\}\) can be left untouched if and only if \(U\) contains at most \(1\) element from each cycle \(A_i\) and at most \(1\) element from each cycle \(B_j\)! What is each element \(v \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) in the bipartite graph between \(\{A_1, \dots, A_p\}\) and \(\{B_1, \dots, B_q\}\)?









TipFull 4-Step Compilation & Solution — #056 CF 1783F: Double Sort II






Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Each element \(v \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) belongs to a unique cycle \(\operatorname{cyc}_a(v)\) in permutation \(a\) and a unique cycle \(\operatorname{cyc}_b(v)\) in permutation \(b\). Leaving an element \(v\) untouched requires it to be the unique surviving representative of \(\operatorname{cyc}_a(v)\) and of \(\operatorname{cyc}_b(v)\)—a Dual Equivalence-Class Incidence (\(\implies\) Bipartite Matching where elements \(v\) are edges!).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(x_v \in \{0, 1\}\) indicate whether element \(v \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) is left untouched (never operated on). Because operating on all elements of a cycle except at most one automatically places the last element into its correct home position, a choice of untouched elements \(x_v\) sorts both \(a\) and \(b\) if and only if:
\[\sum_{v \in A_i} x_v \le 1 \quad (\forall \text{ cycle } A_i \text{ of } a), \qquad \sum_{v \in B_j} x_v \le 1 \quad (\forall \text{ cycle } B_j \text{ of } b).\]
Therefore, the minimum number of operations is:
\[\text{Min Ops} = N - \max \sum_{v=1}^N x_v = N - \nu(G).\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Decompose \(a\) into cycles \(A_1, \dots, A_p\) (\(L\)) and \(b\) into cycles \(B_1, \dots, B_q\) (\(R\)).
For each value \(v \in \{1, \dots, N\}\), add a bipartite edge \(e_v = (\operatorname{cyc}_a(v), \operatorname{cyc}_b(v))\) with label \(v\).
Run Dinic / Hopcroft–Karp to find a maximum matching \(M^*\) of size \(\nu(G)\).
The matched edges \(e_v \in M^*\) are the elements we never touch. Output the remaining \(N - |M^*|\) unmatched values \(v\) in any order!

// Core Modelling Snippet for #056 (CF 1783F Double Sort II)
vector&lt;int&gt; solve_double_sort(int N, const vector&lt;int&gt;& a, const vector&lt;int&gt;& b) {
    vector&lt;int&gt; cycA(N + 1, 0), cycB(N + 1, 0);
    int cntA = 0, cntB = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        if (!cycA[i]) {
            ++cntA;
            for (int cur = i; !cycA[cur]; cur = a[cur - 1]) cycA[cur] = cntA;
        }
        if (!cycB[i]) {
            ++cntB;
            for (int cur = i; !cycB[cur]; cur = b[cur - 1]) cycB[cur] = cntB;
        }
    }
    int S = 0, T = cntA + cntB + 1;
    Dinic Flow(T + 1);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= cntA; ++i) Flow.add_edge(S, i, 1);
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= cntB; ++j) Flow.add_edge(cntA + j, T, 1);

    vector&lt;int&gt; edge_idx(N + 1);
    for (int v = 1; v &lt;= N; ++v) {
        edge_idx[v] = Flow.add_edge(cycA[v], cntA + cycB[v], 1);
    }
    Flow.max_flow(S, T);

    vector&lt;int&gt; ops;
    for (int v = 1; v &lt;= N; ++v) {
        // If edge for v was NOT saturated in the matching, we must operate on v!
        if (Flow.edges[edge_idx[v]].cap == 1) {
            ops.push_back(v);
        }
    }
    return ops;
}


Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Toy Trace (\(N = 3\), \(a = [2, 1, 3]\), \(b = [1, 3, 2]\)):

Cycles of \(a\): \(A_1 = \{1, 2\}\), \(A_2 = \{3\}\). Cycles of \(b\): \(B_1 = \{1\}\), \(B_2 = \{2, 3\}\).
Edges: \(e_1 = (A_1, B_1)\), \(e_2 = (A_1, B_2)\), \(e_3 = (A_2, B_2)\).
Maximum matching \(M^* = \{e_1, e_3\}\) has size \(2\), leaving \(\{1, 3\}\) untouched and operating only on \(v = 2\) (\(3 - 2 = 1\) operation!).

Failure-Mode Guard:

Do not output the matched edges—the matched edges are the untouched elements, so you must output the \(N - |M^*|\) unmatched elements!
Notice that the order in which you operate on the \(N - |M^*|\) unmatched elements does not matter, because each operation on \(v\) swaps \(v\) with whatever element currently sits at index \(v\), splicing \(v\) into a 1-cycle \((v)\) while preserving the cycle structure of all remaining elements!

Complexity: \(|V| \le 2N + 2\), \(|E| = 3N\). Dinic runs in \(O(N\sqrt{N})\) time (\(&lt; 0.03\text{ s}\)).

5. Black-Box Algorithmic Contract & Recognition Table

5.1 Canonical \(O(V + E)\) König Set Recovery Implementation Contract
Assuming you have already run Dinic’s algorithm (Ch 01) on the canonical network \(\mathcal{N}\) where middle arcs \(u \to v\) have capacity \(+\infty\), the following clean \(O(V + E)\) helper extracts all four canonical sets (MVC, MIS, matched pairs \(M^*\), and MEC when unweighted) from the residual graph:
// Contract:
// 1. Network has source `s`, sink `t`, left vertices `L`, right vertices `R`.
// 2. Every original edge (u, v) in E was added as a directed arc u -&gt; v with capacity INF,
//    so forward residual capacity c_f(u, v) &gt; 0 ALWAYS holds after Max-Flow.
struct KonigDecomposition {
    vector&lt;int&gt; mvc_L, mvc_R; // Minimum (Weighted) Vertex Cover: (L \ S_min) U (R ^ S_min)
    vector&lt;int&gt; mis_L, mis_R; // Maximum (Weighted) Independent Set: (L ^ S_min) U (R \ S_min)
};

template &lt;typename FlowNetwork&gt;
KonigDecomposition extract_konig_sets(
    int s, int num_nodes,
    const vector&lt;int&gt;& L, const vector&lt;int&gt;& R,
    const FlowNetwork& net) 
{
    vector&lt;bool&gt; in_S(num_nodes, false);
    queue&lt;int&gt; q;
    q.push(s);
    in_S[s] = true;
    while (!q.empty()) {
        int u = q.front();
        q.pop();
        for (const auto& e : net.adj[u]) {
            if (e.cap_residual &gt; 0 && !in_S[e.to]) {
                in_S[e.to] = true;
                q.push(e.to);
            }
        }
    }

    KonigDecomposition res;
    for (int u : L) {
        if (!in_S[u]) res.mvc_L.push_back(u); // s -&gt; u severed: u in L \ S_min (in MVC)
        else          res.mis_L.push_back(u); // s -&gt; u intact:  u in L ^ S_min (in MIS)
    }
    for (int v : R) {
        if (in_S[v])  res.mvc_R.push_back(v); // v -&gt; t severed: v in R ^ S_min (in MVC)
        else          res.mis_R.push_back(v); // v -&gt; t intact:  v in R \ S_min (in MIS)
    }
    return res;
}


