#073 (SS-1) — POJ 3469: Dual Core CPU

Judge URL: http://poj.org/problem?id=3469
Constraints: \(N \le 20\,000\) software modules, \(M \le 200\,000\) communication pairs; execution costs \(A_i, B_i \le 50\,000\), communication penalty \(w_k \le 50\,000\). Time Limit: 15.0 s (case-heavy input).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Each of \(N\) modules must be placed on either Core A or Core B. Module \(i\) costs \(A_i\) on Core A and \(B_i\) on Core B. Furthermore, for \(M\) given pairs \((u, v, w)\), executing \(u\) and \(v\) on different cores incurs a data-transfer penalty \(w &gt; 0\). We must minimize the total execution plus communication cost.
This is the canonical Pairwise Markov Random Field (MRF) / Metric Labeling problem for \(K = 2\) labels: unary assignment costs coupled with symmetric pairwise separation penalties \(w_{uv}|x_u - x_v|\). Because \(N = 20\,000\) and \(M = 200\,000\) on a general graph, treewidth is unbounded and only an \(O(M\sqrt{N})\) flow solver with compact memory layout can pass.


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(x_i \in \{0, 1\}\) indicate the core assignment of module \(i\):

\(x_i = 1\) (\(i \in V_S\)): Module \(i\) runs on Core B (incurring unary cost \(B_i\)).
\(x_i = 0\) (\(i \in V_T\)): Module \(i\) runs on Core A (incurring unary cost \(A_i\)).

For each communication pair \((u, v, w)\), the \(2 \times 2\) penalty matrix is \(E_{u,v} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & w \\ w & 0 \end{pmatrix}\), which satisfies \(A + D = 0 \le B + C = 2w\) (\(\Delta_{uv} = 2w &gt; 0\), strictly submodular). Summing over all modules and pairs gives the exact cut polynomial:
\[\Phi(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{i=1}^N \big(A_i(1 - x_i) + B_i x_i\big) + \sum_{(u, v, w)} \big(w\,x_u(1 - x_v) + w\,x_v(1 - x_u)\big).\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Unary Pre-Flow (差値正規化, Recipe 1): For each module \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\), let \(m_i = \min(A_i, B_i)\) and add \(m_i\) directly to base_cost:

If \(A_i &gt; B_i\), emit \(S \xrightarrow{A_i - B_i} i\).
If \(B_i &gt; A_i\), emit \(i \xrightarrow{B_i - A_i} T\). (This immediately eliminates half the source/sink edges and pre-pushes \(\sum_i \min(A_i, B_i)\) units of flow in \(O(N)\) time!)

Bidirectional Communication Arcs: For each pair \((u, v, w)\), emit a single adjacency-list edge pair \(u \leftrightarrow v\) where both the forward edge \(u \to v\) and its residual reverse edge \(v \to u\) are initialized with capacity \(w\) (add_undirected_edge(u, v, w)). This halves the memory footprint of the \(M\) communication edges!



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard
Suppose \(N = 3\) modules with \((A_1, B_1) = (10, 4)\), \((A_2, B_2) = (3, 12)\), \((A_3, B_3) = (8, 5)\), and one communication link \((1, 2, w = 5)\).

Recipe 1 Pre-Flow:

Module \(1\): \(m_1 = 4\), base_cost += 4, arc \(S \xrightarrow{6} 1\).
Module \(2\): \(m_2 = 3\), base_cost += 3, arc \(2 \xrightarrow{9} T\).
Module \(3\): \(m_3 = 5\), base_cost += 5, arc \(S \xrightarrow{3} 3\).
Total base_cost \(= 4 + 3 + 5 = 12\).

Communication Arc: \(1 \xleftrightarrow{5} 2\).
Min-Cut: Only a single augmenting path \(S \xrightarrow{6} 1 \xrightarrow{5} 2 \xrightarrow{9} T\) exists, pushing \(\min(6, 5, 9) = 5\) units of flow.
Total Minimum Cost: \(\text{base\_cost} + \text{MaxFlow} = 12 + 5 = 17\) (achieved by \(x_1 = 1\) [Core B, cost \(4\)], \(x_2 = 0\) [Core A, cost \(3\)], \(x_3 = 1\) [Core B, cost \(5\)], plus communication penalty \(5 \implies 4 + 3 + 5 + 5 = 17\)).


[!CAUTION] Failure-Mode Guard (POJ 3469): Adding only a one-way directed edge \(u \to v\) of capacity \(w\) penalizes \((x_u = 1, x_v = 0)\) but leaves \((x_u = 0, x_v = 1)\) with zero penalty! Always initialize rev.cap = w for symmetric separation costs.







Note3-Tier Progressive Hints (#073 — POJ 3469)






Hint 1: Express the cost of module \(i\) as \(A_i(1 - x_i) + B_i x_i\) and the communication penalty as \(w|x_u - x_v| = w\,x_u(1 - x_v) + w\,x_v(1 - x_u)\).
Hint 2: Use Recipe 1 (差値正規化) to extract \(\min(A_i, B_i)\) into a running sum so that each module needs only one edge to either \(S\) or \(T\).
Hint 3: Store each bidirectional communication link \((u, v, w)\) as a single forward/reverse edge pair with both capacities initialized to \(w\).










NoteC++ Model Compilation (#073 — POJ 3469: Dual Core CPU)





// Assumes reader's Dinic&lt;long long&gt; library with:
//   G.add_edge(u, v, forward_cap, reverse_cap = 0)
//   G.max_flow(S, T)
void solve_poj3469() {
    int N, M;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; N &gt;&gt; M)) return;
    int S = 0, T = N + 1;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; G(T + 1);

    long long base_cost = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        long long A, B;
        cin &gt;&gt; A &gt;&gt; B;
        long long m = min(A, B);
        base_cost += m;
        if (A &gt; B) {
            G.add_edge(S, i, A - B, 0); // Cut if i in V_T (Core A)
        } else if (B &gt; A) {
            G.add_edge(i, T, B - A, 0); // Cut if i in V_S (Core B)
        }
    }

    for (int k = 0; k &lt; M; ++k) {
        int u, v;
        long long w;
        cin &gt;&gt; u &gt;&gt; v &gt;&gt; w;
        // Bidirectional edge u &lt;-&gt; v of capacity w in both directions
        G.add_edge(u, v, w, w);
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; base_cost + G.max_flow(S, T) &lt;&lt; "\n";
}







#074 (SS-2) — Luogu P2057 [SHOI2007]: 善意的投票 (Well-Intentioned Vote)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P2057
Constraints: \(N \le 300\) children, \(M \le \binom{N}{2}\) friendship pairs, initial preference \(c_i \in \{0, 1\}\). Time Limit: 1.0 s.


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Each child \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) has an initial preference \(c_i \in \{0, 1\}\) (nap vs. no nap) and chooses a final vote \(x_i \in \{0, 1\}\). A conflict of \(+1\) occurs whenever a child votes against their own preference (\(x_i \neq c_i\)) and whenever two friends \(\{u, v\}\) cast opposite votes (\(x_u \neq x_v\)). Minimize total conflicts.
This is the unweighted Ferromagnetic Ising Model on an arbitrary friendship graph: a special case of #073 where all unary and pairwise capacities equal \(1\).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
For each child \(i\), \([x_i \neq c_i] = (1 - x_i)\) if \(c_i = 1\), and \([x_i \neq c_i] = x_i\) if \(c_i = 0\). For each friend pair \(\{u, v\}\), \([x_u \neq x_v] = x_u(1 - x_v) + x_v(1 - x_u)\). Thus:
\[\Phi(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{i : c_i = 1} (1 - x_i) + \sum_{i : c_i = 0} x_i + \sum_{\{u, v\} \in E} \big(x_u(1 - x_v) + x_v(1 - x_u)\big).\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

If \(c_i = 1\): add arc \(S \xrightarrow{1} i\) (severed if \(x_i = 0\)).
If \(c_i = 0\): add arc \(i \xrightarrow{1} T\) (severed if \(x_i = 1\)).
For each friend pair \(\{u, v\}\): add bidirectional unit edge \(u \xleftrightarrow{1} v\) (G.add_edge(u, v, 1, 1)).
Complexity: Because every arc in the network has unit capacity \(1\), Dinic’s algorithm runs in \(O(M\sqrt{N})\) time (\(\le 8 \cdot 10^5\) operations for \(N = 300\)).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard
Suppose \(N = 3\) with preferences \(c = (1, 1, 0)\) and friendships \(\{1, 3\}\) and \(\{2, 3\}\).

Arcs: \(S \xrightarrow{1} 1\), \(S \xrightarrow{1} 2\), \(3 \xrightarrow{1} T\), plus \(1 \xleftrightarrow{1} 3\) and \(2 \xleftrightarrow{1} 3\).
Augmenting paths \(S \to 1 \to 3 \to T\) (flow \(1\)), leaving \(3 \to T\) saturated! Vertex \(2\) has no augmenting path to \(T\).
Minimum cut \(= 1\) (child \(3\) flips their vote to \(x_3 = 1\) to agree with both friends \(1\) and \(2\), incurring \(1\) self-conflict instead of \(2\) friend conflicts).







Note3-Tier Progressive Hints (#074 — Luogu P2057)






Hint 1: Notice that voting against \(c_i = 1\) costs \((1 - x_i)\), while voting against \(c_i = 0\) costs \(x_i\).
Hint 2: Friend disagreement \([x_u \neq x_v]\) has penalty table \(\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}\), which is submodular with \(\Delta = 2\).
Hint 3: Wire \(S \xrightarrow{1} i\) for \(c_i = 1\), \(i \xrightarrow{1} T\) for \(c_i = 0\), and \(u \xleftrightarrow{1} v\) for each friend pair.










NoteC++ Model Compilation (#074 — Luogu P2057: 善意的投票)





void solve_p2057() {
    int N, M;
    cin &gt;&gt; N &gt;&gt; M;
    int S = 0, T = N + 1;
    Dinic&lt;int&gt; G(T + 1);

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        int c;
        cin &gt;&gt; c;
        if (c == 1) G.add_edge(S, i, 1, 0);
        else        G.add_edge(i, T, 1, 0);
    }
    for (int k = 0; k &lt; M; ++k) {
        int u, v;
        cin &gt;&gt; u &gt;&gt; v;
        G.add_edge(u, v, 1, 1); // Undirected capacity-1 conflict edge
    }
    cout &lt;&lt; G.max_flow(S, T) &lt;&lt; "\n";
}







#075 (Int-1) — Luogu P1361: 小M的作物 (Little M’s Crops)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P1361
Constraints: \(N \le 1000\) crops, \(M \le 1000\) synergy combinations; individual profits \(a_i, b_i \le 1000\), combination bonuses \(c_{k,1}, c_{k,2} \le 1000\). Time Limit: 1.0 s.


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Each crop \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) is planted in Field A (\(x_i = 1\), profit \(a_i\)) or Field B (\(x_i = 0\), profit \(b_i\)). In addition, \(M\) subset combinations \((C_k, c_{k,1}, c_{k,2})\) are given: if all crops in \(C_k\) are planted in Field A, we gain \(+c_{k,1}\); if all crops in \(C_k\) are planted in Field B, we gain \(+c_{k,2}\). Maximize total profit.
Because \(|C_k|\) can be arbitrary (\(2 \le |C_k| \le N\)), pairwise edges cannot represent the degree-\(|C_k|\) conjunctions (FM-10 Bug 1). This is the pure archetype for Theorem 10.2’s Dual Synergy Hubs (\(y_k\) and \(z_k\)).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Pre-claim all possible profits into \(W_{\text{base}} = \sum_{i=1}^N (a_i + b_i) + \sum_{k=1}^M (c_{k,1} + c_{k,2})\) (or use Recipe 1 on unary terms). By Theorem 10.2, the regret polynomial is:
\[\Phi(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{z}) = \sum_{i=1}^N \big(a_i(1 - x_i) + b_i x_i\big) + \sum_{k=1}^M \left[ c_{k,1}(1 - y_k) + \sum_{i \in C_k} \infty \cdot y_k(1 - x_i) \right] + \sum_{k=1}^M \left[ c_{k,2}\,z_k + \sum_{i \in C_k} \infty \cdot x_i(1 - z_k) \right].\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Vertex Allocation:

Crops: \(1 \dots N\).
Field A (All-in-S) hubs: \(y_k = N + k\) for \(k = 1 \dots M\).
Field B (All-in-T) hubs: \(z_k = N + M + k\) for \(k = 1 \dots M\).
Total vertices: \(N + 2M + 2 \le 3002\).

Arcs:

For each crop \(i\): add \(\max(a_i, b_i)\) to ans_base; if \(a_i &gt; b_i\), wire \(S \xrightarrow{a_i - b_i} i\), else if \(b_i &gt; a_i\), wire \(i \xrightarrow{b_i - a_i} T\).
For each combination \(k\): add \(c_{k,1} + c_{k,2}\) to ans_base; wire \(S \xrightarrow{c_{k,1}} y_k\), \(z_k \xrightarrow{c_{k,2}} T\), and for every \(i \in C_k\), wire \(y_k \xrightarrow{\infty} i\) and \(i \xrightarrow{\infty} z_k\).




Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard
Suppose \(N = 2\) crops with \((a_1, b_1) = (10, 0)\), \((a_2, b_2) = (0, 10)\), and \(M = 1\) combination \(C_1 = \{1, 2\}\) with \((c_{1,1}, c_{1,2}) = (6, 6)\).

ans_base \(= 10 + 10 + 6 + 6 = 32\).
Arcs: \(S \xrightarrow{10} 1\), \(2 \xrightarrow{10} T\), \(S \xrightarrow{6} y_1 \xrightarrow{\infty} \{1, 2\}\), and \(\{1, 2\} \xrightarrow{\infty} z_1 \xrightarrow{6} T\).
Look at the augmenting paths:

Path 1: \(S \xrightarrow{6} y_1 \xrightarrow{\infty} 2 \xrightarrow{10} T\) pushes \(6\) units of flow!
Path 2: \(S \xrightarrow{10} 1 \xrightarrow{\infty} z_1 \xrightarrow{6} T\) pushes \(6\) units of flow!
Total MaxFlow \(= 6 + 6 = 12\).

Maximum profit \(= 32 - 12 = 20\) (achieved by \(x_1 = 1, x_2 = 0\): we keep \(a_1 = 10\) and \(b_2 = 10\) while forfeiting both unanimity bonuses \(c_{1,1} = 6\) and \(c_{1,2} = 6\)). Notice how the two separate hubs \(y_1\) and \(z_1\) allowed \(1 \in V_S\) and \(2 \in V_T\) without creating an \(\infty\)-path \(1 \to 2\)!







Note3-Tier Progressive Hints (#075 — Luogu P1361)






Hint 1: Create two distinct auxiliary vertices \(y_k\) and \(z_k\) for each combination \(k \in \{1, \dots, M\}\).
Hint 2: For the Field A bonus \(c_{k,1}\), wire \(S \xrightarrow{c_{k,1}} y_k\) and \(y_k \xrightarrow{\infty} i\) for all \(i \in C_k\).
Hint 3: For the Field B bonus \(c_{k,2}\), wire \(i \xrightarrow{\infty} z_k\) for all \(i \in C_k\) and \(z_k \xrightarrow{c_{k,2}} T\). Never merge \(y_k\) and \(z_k\)!










NoteC++ Model Compilation (#075 — Luogu P1361: 小M的作物)





void solve_p1361() {
    int N;
    cin &gt;&gt; N;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; a(N + 1), b(N + 1);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; a[i];
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; b[i];

    int M;
    cin &gt;&gt; M;
    int S = 0, T = N + 2 * M + 1;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; G(T + 1);
    const long long INF = 1e15;

    long long ans_base = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        ans_base += max(a[i], b[i]);
        if (a[i] &gt; b[i]) G.add_edge(S, i, a[i] - b[i]);
        else if (b[i] &gt; a[i]) G.add_edge(i, T, b[i] - a[i]);
    }

    for (int k = 1; k &lt;= M; ++k) {
        int K;
        long long c1, c2;
        cin &gt;&gt; K &gt;&gt; c1 &gt;&gt; c2;
        ans_base += c1 + c2;
        int y_k = N + k;
        int z_k = N + M + k;
        G.add_edge(S, y_k, c1);
        G.add_edge(z_k, T, c2);
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; K; ++j) {
            int crop;
            cin &gt;&gt; crop;
            G.add_edge(y_k, crop, INF);
            G.add_edge(crop, z_k, INF);
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; ans_base - G.max_flow(S, T) &lt;&lt; "\n";
}







#076 (Int-2) — Luogu P4313 [SHOI2012]: 文理分科 (Arts and Sciences Division)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P4313
Constraints: \(N \times M \le 100 \times 100\) classroom grid (\(10^4\) students); individual happiness \(A_{r,c}, B_{r,c} \le 500\), 5-cell cross unanimity bonuses \(S^{\text{art}}_{r,c}, S^{\text{sci}}_{r,c} \le 500\). Time Limit: 1.0 s.


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Each student at \((r, c)\) chooses Arts (\(x_{r,c} = 1\), reward \(A_{r,c}\)) or Science (\(x_{r,c} = 0\), reward \(B_{r,c}\)). Furthermore, each student \((r, c)\) receives an extra bonus \(S^{\text{art}}_{r,c}\) if \((r, c)\) and all of its 4-connected orthogonal neighbors choose Arts, and \(S^{\text{sci}}_{r,c}\) if that entire \(\le 5\)-cell cross chooses Science. Maximize total happiness.
This is the classic 2D grid realization of #075: each cell \((r, c)\) defines a higher-order conjunction over the cross stencil
\[N^+[r, c] = \{(r, c), (r-1, c), (r+1, c), (r, c-1), (r, c+1)\} \cap \text{Grid},\]
of size \(3 \le |N^+[r, c]| \le 5\).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(u = \text{id}(r, c) \in \{1, \dots, NM\}\). The total happiness is:
\[\Psi(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{u=1}^{NM} \left[ A_u x_u + B_u(1 - x_u) + S^{\text{art}}_u \prod_{v \in N^+[u]} x_v + S^{\text{sci}}_u \prod_{v \in N^+[u]} (1 - x_v) \right].\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Vertex Allocation (\(3NM + 2 \le 30\,002\) vertices):

Student node \(u \in [1, NM]\).
Arts cross-hub \(y_u = NM + u \in [NM + 1, 2NM]\).
Science cross-hub \(z_u = 2NM + u \in [2NM + 1, 3NM]\).

Arcs (\(\le 13NM\) directed arcs):

Apply Recipe 1 to \((A_u, B_u)\) at each student \(u\).
If \(S^{\text{art}}_u &gt; 0\): add \(S^{\text{art}}_u\) to ans_base, wire \(S \xrightarrow{S^{\text{art}}_u} y_u\), and wire \(y_u \xrightarrow{\infty} v\) for all \(v \in N^+[u]\) (including \(v = u\) itself!).
If \(S^{\text{sci}}_u &gt; 0\): add \(S^{\text{sci}}_u\) to ans_base, wire \(z_u \xrightarrow{S^{\text{sci}}_u} T\), and wire \(v \xrightarrow{\infty} z_u\) for all \(v \in N^+[u]\) (including \(v = u\) itself!).




Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

[!WARNING] Failure-Mode Guard (Luogu P4313): The #1 implementation bug on 文理分科 is looping over the \(4\) direction offsets dr = {-1, 1, 0, 0}, dc = {0, 0, -1, 1} while forgetting to include the center cell (r, c) (dr = 0, dc = 0) in the stencil \(N^+[r, c]\)! If you omit \(y_u \xrightarrow{\infty} u\), student \(u\) can choose Science (\(x_u = 0\)) while their \(4\) neighbors choose Arts (\(x_v = 1\)) and still falsely collect the Arts unanimity bonus \(S^{\text{art}}_u\)! Always use a 5-element direction array dr = {0, -1, 1, 0, 0}.







Note3-Tier Progressive Hints (#076 — Luogu P4313)






Hint 1: Treat each cell \((r, c)\)’s 5-cell cross neighborhood \(N^+[r, c]\) as a subset in Luogu P1361.
Hint 2: Make sure your direction array has \(5\) entries dr = {0, -1, 1, 0, 0}, dc = {0, 0, 0, -1, 1} so the center student \((r, c)\) is linked to their own hubs \(y_{r,c}\) and \(z_{r,c}\).
Hint 3: With \(30\,002\) vertices and \(\sim 1.3 \cdot 10^5\) edges, Dinic with Recipe 1 unary normalization runs in \(&lt; 30\text{ ms}\).










NoteC++ Model Compilation (#076 — Luogu P4313: 文理分科)





void solve_p4313() {
    int N, M;
    cin &gt;&gt; N &gt;&gt; M;
    int NM = N * M;
    auto id = [&](int r, int c) { return r * M + c + 1; };

    vector&lt;long long&gt; A(NM + 1), B(NM + 1), SA(NM + 1), SB(NM + 1);
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) cin &gt;&gt; A[id(r, c)];
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) cin &gt;&gt; B[id(r, c)];
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) cin &gt;&gt; SA[id(r, c)];
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) cin &gt;&gt; SB[id(r, c)];

    int S = 0, T = 3 * NM + 1;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; G(T + 1);
    const long long INF = 1e15;
    long long ans_base = 0;

    for (int u = 1; u &lt;= NM; ++u) {
        ans_base += max(A[u], B[u]);
        if (A[u] &gt; B[u]) G.add_edge(S, u, A[u] - B[u]);
        else if (B[u] &gt; A[u]) G.add_edge(u, T, B[u] - A[u]);
    }

    const int dr[5] = {0, -1, 1, 0, 0};
    const int dc[5] = {0, 0, 0, -1, 1};

    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
            int u = id(r, c);
            int y_u = NM + u;
            int z_u = 2 * NM + u;
            if (SA[u] &gt; 0) {
                ans_base += SA[u];
                G.add_edge(S, y_u, SA[u]);
                for (int d = 0; d &lt; 5; ++d) {
                    int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];
                    if (0 &lt;= nr && nr &lt; N && 0 &lt;= nc && nc &lt; M)
                        G.add_edge(y_u, id(nr, nc), INF);
                }
            }
            if (SB[u] &gt; 0) {
                ans_base += SB[u];
                G.add_edge(z_u, T, SB[u]);
                for (int d = 0; d &lt; 5; ++d) {
                    int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];
                    if (0 &lt;= nr && nr &lt; N && 0 &lt;= nc && nc &lt; M)
                        G.add_edge(id(nr, nc), z_u, INF);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; ans_base - G.max_flow(S, T) &lt;&lt; "\n";
}







#077 (Adv-1) — Luogu P1646 [国家集训队]: happiness

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P1646
Constraints: \(N \times M \le 100 \times 100\) classroom grid; unary happiness \(A_{r,c}, B_{r,c} \le 1000\), adjacent-pair same-choice bonuses \(E^{\text{art}}_{e}, E^{\text{sci}}_{e} \le 1000\) for all horizontal and vertical grid edges \(e = \{u, v\}\). Time Limit: 1.0 s.


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Unlike Luogu P4313 (文理分科, #076), where bonuses are awarded to 5-cell crosses (\(|C| = 5\)), in Luogu P1646 (happiness) bonuses are awarded to 2-cell adjacent deskmate pairs \(e = \{u, v\}\) (\(|C| = 2\)): if both \(u\) and \(v\) choose Arts (\(x_u = x_v = 1\)), they gain \(E^{\text{art}}_e\); if both choose Science (\(x_u = x_v = 0\)), they gain \(E^{\text{sci}}_e\).
Because \(|C| = 2\), every synergy term is quadratic! This gives us a direct head-to-head comparison between:

Method A (Auxiliary Hub Compilation): Creating \(2\) hubs per grid edge (\(\approx 4NM\) extra vertices and \(12NM\) extra directed arcs), vs.
Method B (Hu Botao’s Symmetric Half-Edge Compilation / 合并点边法): Eliminating all auxiliary vertices by absorbing \(\frac{1}{2}E^{\text{art}}_e\) and \(\frac{1}{2}E^{\text{sci}}_e\) into the endpoint unary capacities of \(u\) and \(v\) and placing a single undirected edge \(u \leftrightarrow v\) of capacity \(\frac{1}{2}(E^{\text{art}}_e + E^{\text{sci}}_e)\)!



Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation (合并点边法 with \(2\times\) Scaling)
For each grid edge \(e = \{u, v\}\), the \(2 \times 2\) reward table is \(\Pi_e = \begin{pmatrix} E^{\text{sci}}_e & 0 \\ 0 & E^{\text{art}}_e \end{pmatrix}\). Subtracting from \(E^{\text{art}}_e + E^{\text{sci}}_e\) gives the submodular regret matrix:
\[E_e(x_u, x_v) = \begin{pmatrix} E^{\text{art}}_e & E^{\text{art}}_e + E^{\text{sci}}_e \\ E^{\text{art}}_e + E^{\text{sci}}_e & E^{\text{sci}}_e \end{pmatrix}, \qquad \Delta_e = E^{\text{art}}_e + E^{\text{sci}}_e \ge 0.\]
To keep every capacity an exact integer without division by \(2\), multiply the entire objective by \(2\)! Then for each edge \(e = \{u, v\}\):
\[2\,E_e(x_u, x_v) = E^{\text{art}}_e\big((1 - x_u) + (1 - x_v)\big) + E^{\text{sci}}_e(x_u + x_v) + \big(E^{\text{art}}_e + E^{\text{sci}}_e\big)\big(x_u(1 - x_v) + x_v(1 - x_u)\big).\]
Summing over all students \(u \in V_{\text{grid}}\) and all grid edges \(e \in E_{\text{grid}}\) yields:
\[2\Phi(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{u \in V_{\text{grid}}} \left[ \Big(2A_u + \sum_{e \ni u} E^{\text{art}}_e\Big)(1 - x_u) + \Big(2B_u + \sum_{e \ni u} E^{\text{sci}}_e\Big)x_u \right] + \sum_{e = \{u, v\} \in E_{\text{grid}}} \big(E^{\text{art}}_e + E^{\text{sci}}_e\big)|x_u - x_v|.\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Vertex Count: Only \(NM + 2 \le 10\,002\) vertices (\(0\) auxiliary hubs, a \(5\times\) reduction in vertex count compared to Method A!).
Scaled Accumulators:

Initialize \(p_u = 2 A_u\), \(q_u = 2 B_u\) for each cell \(u\), and scaled_base \(= 2\sum_u (A_u + B_u)\).
For each grid edge \(e = \{u, v\}\) with bonuses \((E^{\text{art}}_e, E^{\text{sci}}_e)\):

scaled_base \(\mathrel{+}= 2(E^{\text{art}}_e + E^{\text{sci}}_e)\).
\(p_u \mathrel{+}= E^{\text{art}}_e, \; p_v \mathrel{+}= E^{\text{art}}_e\).
\(q_u \mathrel{+}= E^{\text{sci}}_e, \; q_v \mathrel{+}= E^{\text{sci}}_e\).
Emit undirected grid edge \(u \xleftrightarrow{E^{\text{art}}_e + E^{\text{sci}}_e} v\).

Finally, apply Recipe 1 (差値正規化) to \((p_u, q_u)\) at each cell \(u\), run Dinic, and output (scaled_base - MaxFlow) / 2!




Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard
Suppose a \(1 \times 2\) grid has \(A = (10, 0)\), \(B = (0, 10)\), and edge \(e = \{1, 2\}\) with \(E^{\text{art}}_e = 5, E^{\text{sci}}_e = 7\).

scaled_base \(= 2(10 + 10) + 2(5 + 7) = 64\).
Vertex \(1\): \(p_1 = 2(10) + 5 = 25\), \(q_1 = 2(0) + 7 = 7 \implies m_1 = 7\), arc \(S \xrightarrow{18} 1\).
Vertex \(2\): \(p_2 = 2(0) + 5 = 5\), \(q_2 = 2(10) + 7 = 27 \implies m_2 = 5\), arc \(2 \xrightarrow{22} T\).
Edge \(1 \leftrightarrow 2\): capacity \(5 + 7 = 12\).
Minimum cut: \(m_1 + m_2 + \min(18, 12, 22) = 7 + 5 + 12 = 24\).
Maximum happiness: \((64 - 24) / 2 = 20\) (exact integer!).


[!CAUTION] Failure-Mode Guard (Luogu P1646): Notice that in our trace, \(E^{\text{art}}_e = 5\) and \(E^{\text{sci}}_e = 7\) are both odd! If you did not multiply capacities by \(2\) and instead used integer division 5 / 2 = 2, you would lose \(1\) unit of capacity per odd edge and get WA. Always scale by \(2\times\) before building the integer network and divide by \(2\) at the very end!







Note3-Tier Progressive Hints (#077 — Luogu P1646)






Hint 1: Because every deskmate pair \(\{u, v\}\) has size \(2\), you can eliminate all \(4NM\) auxiliary pair-hubs using Symmetric Half-Edge Compilation (合并点边法).
Hint 2: Multiply all unary rewards \(A_u, B_u\) by \(2\). Then for each edge \(e = \{u, v\}\), add \(E^{\text{art}}_e\) to \(p_u\) and \(p_v\), add \(E^{\text{sci}}_e\) to \(q_u\) and \(q_v\), and add an undirected edge \(u \leftrightarrow v\) of capacity \(E^{\text{art}}_e + E^{\text{sci}}_e\).
Hint 3: Apply Recipe 1 (差値正規化) to \((p_u, q_u)\) at each cell, run Dinic, and divide the net scaled score by \(2\).










NoteC++ Model Compilation (#077 — Luogu P1646: happiness via 合并点边法)





void solve_p1646() {
    int N, M;
    cin &gt;&gt; N &gt;&gt; M;
    int NM = N * M;
    auto id = [&](int r, int c) { return r * M + c + 1; };

    vector&lt;long long&gt; p(NM + 1, 0), q(NM + 1, 0);
    long long scaled_base = 0;

    // 1. Unary Arts rewards A_{r,c} (scaled by 2)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
            long long a;
            cin &gt;&gt; a;
            p[id(r, c)] += 2 * a;
            scaled_base += 2 * a;
        }
    }
    // 2. Unary Science rewards B_{r,c} (scaled by 2)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
            long long b;
            cin &gt;&gt; b;
            q[id(r, c)] += 2 * b;
            scaled_base += 2 * b;
        }
    }

    // Store undirected edge capacities between adjacent cells
    // Vertical pairs: (r, c) &lt;-&gt; (r+1, c) for r in [0, N-2], c in [0, M-1]
    // Horizontal pairs: (r, c) &lt;-&gt; (r, c+1) for r in [0, N-1], c in [0, M-2]
    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; cap_down(N - 1, vector&lt;long long&gt;(M, 0));
    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; cap_right(N, vector&lt;long long&gt;(M - 1, 0));

    // 3. Vertical Arts bonuses (N-1 rows, M cols)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N - 1; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
            long long w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            int u = id(r, c), v = id(r + 1, c);
            scaled_base += 2 * w;
            p[u] += w; p[v] += w;
            cap_down[r][c] += w;
        }
    }
    // 4. Vertical Science bonuses (N-1 rows, M cols)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N - 1; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c) {
            long long w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            int u = id(r, c), v = id(r + 1, c);
            scaled_base += 2 * w;
            q[u] += w; q[v] += w;
            cap_down[r][c] += w;
        }
    }
    // 5. Horizontal Arts bonuses (N rows, M-1 cols)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M - 1; ++c) {
            long long w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            int u = id(r, c), v = id(r, c + 1);
            scaled_base += 2 * w;
            p[u] += w; p[v] += w;
            cap_right[r][c] += w;
        }
    }
    // 6. Horizontal Science bonuses (N rows, M-1 cols)
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M - 1; ++c) {
            long long w;
            cin &gt;&gt; w;
            int u = id(r, c), v = id(r, c + 1);
            scaled_base += 2 * w;
            q[u] += w; q[v] += w;
            cap_right[r][c] += w;
        }
    }

    int S = 0, T = NM + 1;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; G(T + 1);
    long long cut_offset = 0;

    for (int u = 1; u &lt;= NM; ++u) {
        long long m = min(p[u], q[u]);
        cut_offset += m;
        if (p[u] &gt; q[u]) G.add_edge(S, u, p[u] - q[u], 0);
        else if (q[u] &gt; p[u]) G.add_edge(u, T, q[u] - p[u], 0);
    }
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N - 1; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M; ++c)
            if (cap_down[r][c] &gt; 0)
                G.add_edge(id(r, c), id(r + 1, c), cap_down[r][c], cap_down[r][c]);
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; N; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; M - 1; ++c)
            if (cap_right[r][c] &gt; 0)
                G.add_edge(id(r, c), id(r, c + 1), cap_right[r][c], cap_right[r][c]);

    long long min_cut = cut_offset + G.max_flow(S, T);
    cout &lt;&lt; (scaled_base - min_cut) / 2 &lt;&lt; "\n";
}







#078 (Adv-2) — Luogu P1791 / BZOJ 2039 [2009国家集训队]: 人员雇佣 (Employee Hiring)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P1791 / https://darkbzoj.cc/problem/2039
Constraints: \(N \le 1000\) candidates, hiring costs \(A_i \le 10^9\), dense \(N \times N\) synergy matrix \(E_{i,j} \le 10^9\) (\(E_{i,j} = E_{j,i}, E_{i,i} = 0\)). Time Limit: 1.0 s.


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Hiring candidate \(i\) (\(x_i = 1\)) costs a salary \(A_i \ge 0\). For every ordered pair \((i, j)\):

If both \(i\) and \(j\) are hired (\(x_i = x_j = 1\)), the company gains \(+E_{i,j}\) (so an unordered pair \(\{i, j\}\) yields \(+2 E_{i,j}\) when both are hired).
If \(i\) is hired and \(j\) is NOT hired (\(x_i = 1, x_j = 0\)), the company suffers a friction penalty of \(-E_{i,j}\)!
If \(i\) is not hired (\(x_i = 0\)), \((i, j)\) contributes \(0\).

Why is this an Advanced milestone? Because \(N = 1000\) and the matrix \(E_{i,j}\) is completely dense (\(\binom{N}{2} \approx 5 \cdot 10^5\) pairs)! If you treat each pair \(\{i, j\}\) as an auxiliary hub node (Luogu P4174 最大获利 style), the network has \(N + \binom{N}{2} \approx 5 \cdot 10^5\) vertices and \(1.5 \cdot 10^6\) edges with huge capacities, which TLEs and MLEs. Instead, expanding the \(2 \times 2\) matrix collapses the entire problem onto \(N + 2 = 1002\) vertices!


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation (\(O(N)\) Vertex Degree-Collapse)
For each unordered pair \(\{i, j\}\) (\(1 \le i &lt; j \le N\)), combine the \((i, j)\) and \((j, i)\) contributions into a single \(2 \times 2\) reward matrix \(\Pi_{i,j}(x_i, x_j)\):



\(x_i \setminus x_j\)
\(x_j = 0\) (Unhired)
\(x_j = 1\) (Hired)




\(x_i = 0\) (Unhired)
\(0\)
\(-E_{i,j}\)


\(x_i = 1\) (Hired)
\(-E_{i,j}\)
\(+2 E_{i,j}\)



Let us apply the Asymmetric or Symmetric Cut Expansion directly to \(\Pi_{i,j}(x_i, x_j)\)! Using the identity \(2 x_i x_j = x_i + x_j - x_i(1 - x_j) - x_j(1 - x_i)\):
\[\begin{aligned}
\Pi_{i,j}(x_i, x_j) &= 2 E_{i,j} x_i x_j - E_{i,j} x_i(1 - x_j) - E_{i,j} x_j(1 - x_i) \\
&= E_{i,j}(x_i + x_j) - 2 E_{i,j}\big(x_i(1 - x_j) + x_j(1 - x_i)\big)!
\end{aligned}\]
Look at that miracle of cancellation:

Every fraction \(\frac{1}{2}\) vanished completely—all coefficients are exact integers!
When we sum \(E_{i,j}(x_i + x_j)\) over all unordered pairs \(1 \le i &lt; j \le N\), each candidate \(i\) simply collects their row sum \(D_i := \sum_{j=1}^N E_{i,j}\) as a unary profit \(+D_i x_i\)!
Each unordered pair \(\{i, j\}\) simply becomes an undirected edge \(i \leftrightarrow j\) of capacity \(2 E_{i,j}\)!

Therefore, the total net profit over all \(\mathbf{x} \in \{0, 1\}^N\) is:
\[\Psi(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{i=1}^N D_i - \left[ \sum_{i=1}^N D_i(1 - x_i) + \sum_{i=1}^N A_i x_i + \sum_{1 \le i &lt; j \le N} 2 E_{i,j}\big(x_i(1 - x_j) + x_j(1 - x_i)\big) \right].\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Vertices: \(S = 0\), candidates \(1 \dots N\), \(T = N + 1\) (\(1002\) vertices total!).
Baseline: \(W_{\text{base}} = \sum_{i=1}^N D_i = \sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{j=1}^N E_{i,j}\).
Unary Arcs (via Recipe 1): For each candidate \(i\), apply Recipe 1 to \(D_i(1 - x_i) + A_i x_i\):

Subtract \(\min(D_i, A_i)\) from \(W_{\text{base}}\).
If \(D_i &gt; A_i\), wire \(S \xrightarrow{D_i - A_i} i\); if \(A_i &gt; D_i\), wire \(i \xrightarrow{A_i - D_i} T\).

Pairwise Arcs: For each \(1 \le i &lt; j \le N\), wire \(i \xleftrightarrow{2 E_{i,j}} j\) (G.add_edge(i, j, 2 * E[i][j], 2 * E[i][j])).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard
Suppose \(N = 2\) with salaries \(A = (5, 6)\) and synergy \(E_{1,2} = E_{2,1} = 4\).

Row sums: \(D_1 = 4, D_2 = 4\); initial \(W_{\text{base}} = 4 + 4 = 8\).
Candidate \(1\): \(4(1 - x_1) + 5x_1 = 4 + 1x_1 \implies\) subtract \(4\), arc \(1 \xrightarrow{1} T\).
Candidate \(2\): \(4(1 - x_2) + 6x_2 = 4 + 2x_2 \implies\) subtract \(4\), arc \(2 \xrightarrow{2} T\).
Pairwise edge: \(1 \xleftrightarrow{8} 2\).
Remaining \(W_{\text{base}} = 8 - 4 - 4 = 0\), and since \(S\) has no outgoing edges, \(\text{MaxFlow} = 0 \implies\) answer \(0\) (hire neither; hiring both gives \(8 - 5 - 6 = -3 &lt; 0\)). If instead \(A = (2, 3)\), then \(S \xrightarrow{2} 1\), \(S \xrightarrow{1} 2\), \(\text{MaxFlow} = 0\), and \(W_{\text{base}} - \min = (8 - 2 - 3) - 0 = 3\) (hire both!).







Note3-Tier Progressive Hints (#078 — Luogu P1791 / BZOJ 2039)






Hint 1: Write the \(2 \times 2\) payoff table for an unordered pair \(\{i, j\}\): \((0,0) \mapsto 0\), \((1,0) \mapsto -E_{ij}\), \((0,1) \mapsto -E_{ij}\), \((1,1) \mapsto +2E_{ij}\).
Hint 2: Verify that \(E_{ij}(x_i + x_j) - 2E_{ij}\big(x_i(1 - x_j) + x_j(1 - x_i)\big)\) matches all four entries of that \(2 \times 2\) table!
Hint 3: Sum \(E_{ij}(x_i + x_j)\) over all pairs so vertex \(i\) gets unary reward \(D_i = \sum_{j=1}^N E_{ij}\) against cost \(A_i\), and each pair \(\{i, j\}\) gets an undirected edge of capacity \(2E_{ij}\).










NoteC++ Model Compilation (#078 — Luogu P1791 / BZOJ 2039: 人员雇佣)





void solve_p1791() {
    int N;
    cin &gt;&gt; N;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; A(N + 1);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; A[i];

    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; E(N + 1, vector&lt;long long&gt;(N + 1));
    vector&lt;long long&gt; D(N + 1, 0);
    long long ans_base = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        for (int j = 1; j &lt;= N; ++j) {
            cin &gt;&gt; E[i][j];
            D[i] += E[i][j];
        }
        ans_base += D[i];
    }

    int S = 0, T = N + 1;
    Dinic&lt;long long&gt; G(T + 1);

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        long long m = min(D[i], A[i]);
        ans_base -= m;
        if (D[i] &gt; A[i]) G.add_edge(S, i, D[i] - A[i], 0);
        else if (A[i] &gt; D[i]) G.add_edge(i, T, A[i] - D[i], 0);
    }

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j &lt;= N; ++j) {
            long long cap = E[i][j] + E[j][i]; // 2 * E_{i,j}
            if (cap &gt; 0) G.add_edge(i, j, cap, cap);
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; ans_base - G.max_flow(S, T) &lt;&lt; "\n";
}







#079 (Mas-1) — Codeforces 311 E: Biologist

Judge URL: https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/311/E
Constraints: \(N \le 10^4\) dogs, \(M \le 2000\) experiments, friend anger penalty \(g \le 10^4\); surgery costs \(v_i \le 10^4\), experiment rewards \(W_k \le 10^4\). Time Limit: 1.5 s.


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal (Recognition)
Each dog \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) starts with binary state \(s_i \in \{0, 1\}\), and flipping \(x_i \neq s_i\) costs \(v_i \ge 0\). Each of \(M\) experiments specifies a target state \(w_k \in \{0, 1\}\), a subset of dogs \(C_k\), a reward \(W_k \ge 0\) if \(\forall i \in C_k, x_i = w_k\), and a boolean flag \(f_k \in \{0, 1\}\): if the experiment fails (\(\exists i \in C_k, x_i \neq w_k\)) and \(f_k = 1\), we not only lose \(W_k\), we must also pay a penalty \(-g\) out of pocket!
This problem tests whether you truly understand the algebraic difference between Optimistic Baseline Pre-Claiming (\(W_{\text{base}}\)) and Regret Arc Capacity (\(c_e\)): when a friend’s experiment fails, the net swing between success (\(+W_k\)) and failure (\(-g\)) is \(W_k - (-g) = W_k + g\)!


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
Let \(\sigma_k(\mathbf{x}) \in \{0, 1\}\) denote whether experiment \(k\) succeeds (\(\sigma_k = \prod_{i \in C_k} x_i\) if \(w_k = 1\), or \(\prod_{i \in C_k} (1 - x_i)\) if \(w_k = 0\)). The net payoff of experiment \(k\) is:
\[W_k \sigma_k(\mathbf{x}) - g \cdot f_k \big(1 - \sigma_k(\mathbf{x})\big) = \underbrace{W_k}_{\text{add to } W_{\text{base}}} - \underbrace{(W_k + g \cdot f_k)}_{\text{hub arc capacity!}}\big(1 - \sigma_k(\mathbf{x})\big).\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Mechanical Graph Compilation)

Baseline: \(W_{\text{base}} = \sum_{k=1}^M W_k\) (pre-claim ONLY \(W_k\), NOT \(W_k + g \cdot f_k\)!).
Dog Unary Arcs: If \(s_i = 1\), wire \(S \xrightarrow{v_i} i\) (cost \(v_i\) if flipped to \(x_i = 0\)); if \(s_i = 0\), wire \(i \xrightarrow{v_i} T\) (cost \(v_i\) if flipped to \(x_i = 1\)).
Experiment Hub Arcs (\(h_k = N + k\)): Let \(C^{\text{regret}}_k = W_k + g \cdot f_k\).

If \(w_k = 1\) (All-in-S): wire \(S \xrightarrow{C^{\text{regret}}_k} h_k\) and \(h_k \xrightarrow{\infty} i\) for all \(i \in C_k\).
If \(w_k = 0\) (All-in-T): wire \(i \xrightarrow{\infty} h_k\) for all \(i \in C_k\) and \(h_k \xrightarrow{C^{\text{regret}}_k} T\).




Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard
Suppose \(N = 1\) dog with \(s_1 = 0, v_1 = 10\), and \(1\) friend experiment (\(f_1 = 1, g = 5\)) requiring \(w_1 = 1\) on \(C_1 = \{1\}\) with reward \(W_1 = 3\).

\(W_{\text{base}} = 3\), and hub capacity is \(W_1 + g = 3 + 5 = 8\).
Path \(S \xrightarrow{8} h_1 \xrightarrow{\infty} 1 \xrightarrow{10} T\) has bottleneck \(\min(8, 10) = 8\).
Net profit: \(W_{\text{base}} - \text{MinCut} = 3 - 8 = -5\) (we leave \(x_1 = 0\) to avoid paying \(v_1 = 10\), failing the friend’s experiment and paying \(-g = -5\)).







Note3-Tier Progressive Hints (#079 — Codeforces 311E)






Hint 1: Write the net contribution of experiment \(k\) in both states: \(+W_k\) when it succeeds, and \(-g \cdot f_k\) when it fails.
Hint 2: Rewrite that two-state payoff as \(W_k - (W_k + g \cdot f_k)(1 - \sigma_k)\).
Hint 3: Add only \(W_k\) to W_base, while setting the hub’s source/sink edge capacity to \(W_k + g \cdot f_k\).










