Hint 1 (Diagnostic): When \(c_i &lt; \infty\), the cut assignment \((x_1, \dots, x_N) \in \{T, S\}^N\) can switch from \(S\) to \(T\) at any pipe \(i \to i+1\) by paying \(c_i\).
Hint 2 (Algebraic): For each segment \([L, R]\), maintain the 4 values dp[u][v] (\(u, v \in \{0, 1\}\)): the minimum cut cost on \([L, R]\) when \(x_L = u\) and \(x_R = v\).
Hint 3 (Compilation): When merging [L, M] (ending in state \(x\)) and [M+1, R] (starting in state \(y\)), add c[M] only when \(x = 1\) (\(S\)) and \(y = 0\) (\(T\))!










NoteComplete C++ Solution for #126 (CF 1919F2 — Wine Factory Hard Version)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 4e18;

struct Mat {
    long long d[2][2];
    Mat() {
        d[0][0] = d[0][1] = d[1][0] = d[1][1] = INF;
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, q;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; q)) return 0;

    vector&lt;long long&gt; a(n + 1), b(n + 1), c(n + 1, 0);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; a[i];
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; b[i];
    for (int i = 1; i &lt; n; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; c[i];

    vector&lt;Mat&gt; tree(4 * n + 4);

    auto merge_nodes = [&](const Mat& L, const Mat& R, long long pipe_c) {
        Mat res;
        for (int u = 0; u &lt; 2; ++u) {
            for (int x = 0; x &lt; 2; ++x) {
                if (L.d[u][x] &gt;= INF) continue;
                for (int y = 0; y &lt; 2; ++y) {
                    long long cross = (x == 1 && y == 0) ? pipe_c : 0;
                    for (int v = 0; v &lt; 2; ++v) {
                        if (R.d[y][v] &gt;= INF) continue;
                        res.d[u][v] = min(res.d[u][v], L.d[u][x] + R.d[y][v] + cross);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return res;
    };

    auto build = [&](auto& self, int u, int l, int r) -&gt; void {
        if (l == r) {
            tree[u] = Mat();
            tree[u].d[0][0] = a[l]; // l in T (sink side)
            tree[u].d[1][1] = b[l]; // l in S (source side)
            return;
        }
        int mid = (l + r) / 2;
        self(self, 2 * u, l, mid);
        self(self, 2 * u + 1, mid + 1, r);
        tree[u] = merge_nodes(tree[2 * u], tree[2 * u + 1], c[mid]);
    };

    auto update = [&](auto& self, int u, int l, int r, int p) -&gt; void {
        if (l == r) {
            tree[u] = Mat();
            tree[u].d[0][0] = a[l];
            tree[u].d[1][1] = b[l];
            return;
        }
        int mid = (l + r) / 2;
        if (p &lt;= mid) self(self, 2 * u, l, mid, p);
        else self(self, 2 * u + 1, mid + 1, r, p);
        tree[u] = merge_nodes(tree[2 * u], tree[2 * u + 1], c[mid]);
    };

    build(build, 1, 1, n);

    while (q--) {
        int p;
        long long x, y, z;
        cin &gt;&gt; p &gt;&gt; x &gt;&gt; y &gt;&gt; z;
        a[p] = x;
        b[p] = y;
        if (p &lt; n) c[p] = z;
        update(update, 1, 1, n, p);

        long long ans = INF;
        for (int u = 0; u &lt; 2; ++u)
            for (int v = 0; v &lt; 2; ++v)
                ans = min(ans, tree[1].d[u][v]);
        cout &lt;&lt; ans &lt;&lt; '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}







#127 (Mas-1) — Codeforces 343E: Pumping Stations

Judge / URL: Codeforces 343E ([EN])
Constraints: Undirected weighted graph with \(N \le 200\) vertices and \(M \le 1000\) edges. Find a permutation \(\pi = (\pi_1, \pi_2, \dots, \pi_N)\) of \(\{1, \dots, N\}\) maximizing \(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1} \operatorname{MinCut}_G(\pi_i, \pi_{i+1})\), and output both the maximum sum and the optimal permutation \(\pi\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal
We want a maximum-weight Hamiltonian path in the complete graph of pairwise minimum cuts \(\lambda_G(u, v)\). While Maximum Travelling Salesman is NP-hard on general metrics, \(\lambda_G(u, v)\) is an ultrametric tree-bottleneck function on the Gomory–Hu tree \(T_{\text{GH}}\)!


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation & Exact Upper Bound Proof
Let \(T_{\text{GH}} = (V, E_T, w_T)\) be the Gomory–Hu tree of \(G\). We prove that for any permutation \(\pi\), its objective is bounded above by the total weight of \(T_{\text{GH}}\), and that this upper bound is always achievable:
\[
\max_{\pi \in S_N} \sum_{i=1}^{N-1} \lambda_G(\pi_i, \pi_{i+1}) = \sum_{e \in E_T} w_T(e).
\]
Proof of Upper Bound & Constructive Equality:

Upper Bound (\(\le\)): Consider the minimum-weight edge \(e_{\min} \in \arg\min_{e \in E_T} w_T(e)\) of \(T_{\text{GH}}\). Removing \(e_{\min}\) splits \(T_{\text{GH}}\) into two subtrees \(T_1\) and \(T_2\). Any permutation \(\pi_1, \dots, \pi_N\) must step between \(V(T_1)\) and \(V(T_2)\) at least once, and that step uses \(e_{\min}\) on its tree path, paying \(\le w_T(e_{\min})\). More generally, mapping each adjacent step \((\pi_i, \pi_{i+1})\) to a bottleneck edge on \(P_T(\pi_i, \pi_{i+1})\) via induction on \(|V|\) shows \(\sum_{i=1}^{N-1} \lambda_G(\pi_i, \pi_{i+1}) \le \sum_{e \in E_T} w_T(e)\).
Constructive Equality (\(=\)): Suppose we order all vertices of \(T_1\) first, followed by all vertices of \(T_2\). Because \(e_{\min}\) is the globally lightest edge in the entire tree \(T_{\text{GH}}\), for any last vertex \(x \in V(T_1)\) and any first vertex \(y \in V(T_2)\), the path \(P_T(x, y)\) crosses \(e_{\min}\) and every other edge on \(P_T(x, y)\) has weight \(\ge w_T(e_{\min})\)! Therefore, the boundary transition \((x, y)\) between \(T_1\) and \(T_2\) achieves bottleneck exactly \(w_T(e_{\min})\)! Applying the exact same rule recursively to \(T_1\) and \(T_2\) achieves \(\sum_{e \in E_T} w_T(e)\)! \(\blacksquare\)



Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Kruskal DSU Concatenation)
Splitting \(T_{\text{GH}}\) top-down at the minimum-weight edge \(e_{\min}\) is bottom-up equivalent to Kruskal’s DSU in descending order of edge weights!

Build \(T_{\text{GH}}\) in \(N - 1\) Dinic calls using Gusfield’s algorithm.
Sort the \(N - 1\) edges of \(T_{\text{GH}}\) in descending order of \(w_T(e)\).
Maintain a DSU where each component \(c\) stores an ordered list order[c] of its vertices (initially order[u] = {u}).
For each tree edge \(e = (u, v)\) from heaviest to lightest: append order[find(v)] to the end of order[find(u)] and unite the two components! At the end, the single list order[find(1)] is the exact optimal permutation \(\pi\)!



Step 4: Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Failure Guard: Splitting at an arbitrary edge (or sorting edges ascending instead of descending in DSU) fails because when two components are concatenated across a heavy edge \(e\), a lighter edge already inside one of the components would become the bottleneck on \(P_T(x, y)\) instead of \(e\)!







Note3-Tier Progressive Hints for #127 (CF 343E)






Hint 1 (Diagnostic): Build the Gomory–Hu tree \(T_{\text{GH}}\). Why can no permutation ever exceed the sum of the \(N - 1\) edge weights of \(T_{\text{GH}}\)?
Hint 2 (Algebraic): Let \(e_{\min}\) be the lightest edge in \(T_{\text{GH}}\), splitting \(T_{\text{GH}}\) into \(T_1\) and \(T_2\). If you visit all of \(T_1\) and then all of \(T_2\), what is the bottleneck between the last node of \(T_1\) and the first node of \(T_2\)? Always \(w_T(e_{\min})\)!
Hint 3 (Compilation): Sort the \(N - 1\) edges of \(T_{\text{GH}}\) in descending order of weight and merge component vertex lists using a Disjoint Set Union (DSU)!










NoteComplete C++ Solution for #127 (CF 343E — Pumping Stations)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const int INF = 1e9;

struct Dinic {
    struct Edge {
        int to, rev, cap, init_cap;
    };
    int n;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; adj;
    vector&lt;int&gt; level, ptr;

    Dinic(int n) : n(n), adj(n + 1), level(n + 1), ptr(n + 1) {}

    void add_undirected_edge(int u, int v, int c) {
        adj[u].push_back({v, (int)adj[v].size(), c, c});
        adj[v].push_back({u, (int)adj[u].size() - 1, c, c});
    }

    void reset() {
        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i)
            for (auto& e : adj[i]) e.cap = e.init_cap;
    }

    bool bfs(int s, int t) {
        fill(level.begin(), level.end(), -1);
        level[s] = 0;
        queue&lt;int&gt; q;
        q.push(s);
        while (!q.empty()) {
            int u = q.front();
            q.pop();
            for (auto& e : adj[u]) {
                if (e.cap &gt; 0 && level[e.to] == -1) {
                    level[e.to] = level[u] + 1;
                    q.push(e.to);
                }
            }
        }
        return level[t] != -1;
    }

    int dfs(int u, int t, int pushed) {
        if (pushed == 0 || u == t) return pushed;
        for (int& cid = ptr[u]; cid &lt; (int)adj[u].size(); ++cid) {
            auto& e = adj[u][cid];
            if (level[u] + 1 != level[e.to] || e.cap == 0) continue;
            int tr = dfs(e.to, t, min(pushed, e.cap));
            if (tr == 0) continue;
            e.cap -= tr;
            adj[e.to][e.rev].cap += tr;
            return tr;
        }
        return 0;
    }

    int max_flow(int s, int t) {
        reset();
        int flow = 0;
        while (bfs(s, t)) {
            fill(ptr.begin(), ptr.end(), 0);
            while (int pushed = dfs(s, t, INF)) flow += pushed;
        }
        return flow;
    }
};

struct TreeEdge {
    int u, v, w;
    bool operator&lt;(const TreeEdge& other) const {
        return w &gt; other.w; // Sort descending!
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; m)) return 0;

    Dinic dinic(n);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; m; ++i) {
        int u, v, w;
        cin &gt;&gt; u &gt;&gt; v &gt;&gt; w;
        dinic.add_undirected_edge(u, v, w);
    }

    vector&lt;int&gt; p(n + 1, 1);
    vector&lt;TreeEdge&gt; tree_edges;
    long long total_weight = 0;

    for (int s = 2; s &lt;= n; ++s) {
        int t = p[s];
        int w = dinic.max_flow(s, t);
        total_weight += w;
        tree_edges.push_back({s, t, w});
        for (int u = s + 1; u &lt;= n; ++u) {
            if (p[u] == t && dinic.level[u] != -1) {
                p[u] = s;
            }
        }
    }

    sort(tree_edges.begin(), tree_edges.end());

    vector&lt;int&gt; dsu(n + 1);
    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; comp_order(n + 1);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        dsu[i] = i;
        comp_order[i] = {i};
    }

    auto find_set = [&](auto& self, int x) -&gt; int {
        return dsu[x] == x ? x : dsu[x] = self(self, dsu[x]);
    };

    for (const auto& e : tree_edges) {
        int ru = find_set(find_set, e.u);
        int rv = find_set(find_set, e.v);
        if (ru != rv) {
            comp_order[ru].insert(comp_order[ru].end(),
                                  comp_order[rv].begin(), comp_order[rv].end());
            comp_order[rv].clear();
            dsu[rv] = ru;
        }
    }

    int root = find_set(find_set, 1);
    cout &lt;&lt; total_weight &lt;&lt; '\n';
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        cout &lt;&lt; comp_order[root][i] &lt;&lt; (i + 1 == n ? '\n' : ' ');
    }
    return 0;
}







#128 (Mas-2) — Codeforces 903G: Yet Another MaxFlow Problem

Judge / URL: Codeforces 903G ([EN])
Constraints: \(N, M, Q \le 2 \cdot 10^5\). Directed left chain \(A_1 \xrightarrow{x_1} A_2 \xrightarrow{x_2} \dots \xrightarrow{x_{N-1}} A_N\), directed right chain \(B_1 \xrightarrow{y_1} B_2 \xrightarrow{y_2} \dots \xrightarrow{y_{N-1}} B_N\), and \(M\) directed cross-edges \(A_u \xrightarrow{z_e} B_v\). Output \(\operatorname{MaxFlow}(A_1, B_N)\) initially and after each of \(Q\) point updates changing a single left-chain capacity \(x_i\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal
We have two coupled forward chains of length \(N = 2 \cdot 10^5\) with \(Q = 2 \cdot 10^5\) updates to \(x_i\). Crucially, notice two facts (Section 2.4):

Because all edges on \(A_1 \to \dots \to A_N\) and \(B_1 \to \dots \to B_N\) point forward from \(s = A_1\) toward \(t = B_N\), any minimal \(A_1\)-\(B_N\) cut puts a contiguous prefix \(\{A_1, \dots, A_i\}\) (\(1 \le i \le N\), paying \(x_i\) with \(x_N = 0\)) and a contiguous prefix \(\{B_1, \dots, B_j\}\) (\(0 \le j \le N - 1\), paying \(y_j\) with \(y_0 = 0\)) on the source side \(S\)!
The \(Q\) online queries only modify \(x_i\)—they never modify \(y_j\) or the cross-edges \(z_e\)!



Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic Representation
For a chosen left cut index \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) (\(A_1 \dots A_i \in S\)) and right cut index \(j \in \{0, \dots, N - 1\}\) (\(B_1 \dots B_j \in S\), \(B_{j+1} \dots B_N \in T\)), a cross-edge \(A_u \to B_v\) of capacity \(z_{uv}\) crosses from \(S\) to \(T\) if and only if \(u \le i\) and \(v &gt; j\) (i.e., \(j \in [0, v - 1]\)):
\[
\operatorname{Cut}(i, j) = x_i + \underbrace{\left( y_j + \sum_{u \le i,\; v &gt; j} z_{uv} \right)}_{f_i(j)}, \qquad \operatorname{MaxFlow}(A_1, B_N) = \min_{1 \le i \le N} \big(x_i + C_i\big),
\]
where \(C_i = \min_{0 \le j \le N - 1} f_i(j)\) is completely independent of \(x\) and can be precomputed once for all \(i = 1, \dots, N\)!


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Sweep-Line Lazy Segment Tree)

Phase 1 (Precomputing \(C_1, \dots, C_N\) in \(O((N + M) \log N)\)):

Build a Lazy Range-Add Range-Min Segment Tree over \(j \in [0, N - 1]\), initialized at leaf \(j\) with \(y_j\) (\(y_0 = 0\)).
Sweep \(i\) from \(1\) to \(N\): for each cross-edge \(A_i \to B_v\) of weight \(z\), add \(+z\) to the prefix range \(j \in [0, v - 1]\) in the Segment Tree!
After processing all cross-edges out of \(A_i\), query the global minimum of the Segment Tree and store it as \(C_i = \text{seg.root\_min}()\).

Phase 2 (Answering \(Q\) Updates to \(x_i\) in \(O(Q \log N)\)):

Maintain the \(N\) values \(x_i + C_i\) in a std::multiset&lt;long long&gt; (or a second point-update Segment Tree), updating \(x_i + C_i\) in \(O(\log N)\) per query!




Step 4: Toy Trace & Failure-Mode Guard

Failure Guard: Do not forget \(j = 0\) (\(y_0 = 0\), placing all \(B_1, \dots, B_N\) on the sink side \(T\)) and \(i = N\) (\(x_N = 0\), placing all \(A_1, \dots, A_N\) on the source side \(S\))! If \(j = 0\) is omitted from the Segment Tree domain \([0, N - 1]\), any cut that leaves \(B_1 \in T\) (which is required whenever \(A_i \to B_1\) is severed!) will be missed.







Note3-Tier Progressive Hints for #128 (CF 903G)






Hint 1 (Diagnostic): Why does an optimal cut never sever more than one edge \(A_i \to A_{i+1}\) on the \(A\)-chain and one edge \(B_j \to B_{j+1}\) on the \(B\)-chain?
Hint 2 (Algebraic): For a fixed \(A\)-prefix \(A_1 \dots A_i \in S\) and \(B\)-prefix \(B_1 \dots B_j \in S\), each cross-edge \(A_u \to B_v\) is cut iff \(u \le i\) and \(j \le v - 1\). Notice that the queries never change \(y_j\) or the cross-edges!
Hint 3 (Compilation): Sweep \(i = 1 \dots N\) and use a Lazy Range-Add Range-Min Segment Tree over \(j \in [0, N - 1]\) to precompute \(C_i = \min_j (y_j + \sum_{u \le i, v &gt; j} z_{uv})\). Then maintain \(\min_i (x_i + C_i)\) under point updates to \(x_i\)!










NoteComplete C++ Solution for #128 (CF 903G — Yet Another MaxFlow Problem)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 4e18;

struct LazySegTree {
    int n;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; mn, lz;

    LazySegTree(int n) : n(n), mn(4 * n + 4, 0), lz(4 * n + 4, 0) {}

    void build(int u, int l, int r, const vector&lt;long long&gt;& y) {
        if (l == r) {
            mn[u] = y[l];
            return;
        }
        int mid = (l + r) / 2;
        build(2 * u, l, mid, y);
        build(2 * u + 1, mid + 1, r, y);
        mn[u] = min(mn[2 * u], mn[2 * u + 1]);
    }

    void apply(int u, long long v) {
        mn[u] += v;
        lz[u] += v;
    }

    void push(int u) {
        if (lz[u] != 0) {
            apply(2 * u, lz[u]);
            apply(2 * u + 1, lz[u]);
            lz[u] = 0;
        }
    }

    void range_add(int u, int l, int r, int ql, int qr, long long v) {
        if (ql &lt;= l && r &lt;= qr) {
            apply(u, v);
            return;
        }
        push(u);
        int mid = (l + r) / 2;
        if (ql &lt;= mid) range_add(2 * u, l, mid, ql, qr, v);
        if (qr &gt; mid) range_add(2 * u + 1, mid + 1, r, ql, qr, v);
        mn[u] = min(mn[2 * u], mn[2 * u + 1]);
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m, q;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; m &gt;&gt; q)) return 0;

    vector&lt;long long&gt; x(n + 1, 0), y(n, 0);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        cin &gt;&gt; x[i] &gt;&gt; y[i];
    }

    vector&lt;vector&lt;pair&lt;int, long long&gt;&gt;&gt; cross(n + 1);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; m; ++i) {
        int u, v;
        long long z;
        cin &gt;&gt; u &gt;&gt; v &gt;&gt; z;
        cross[u].push_back({v, z});
    }

    // Phase 1: Sweep i = 1 .. N to precompute optimal B-cut C[i]
    LazySegTree seg_b(n);
    seg_b.build(1, 0, n - 1, y);

    vector&lt;long long&gt; C(n + 1, 0);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        for (auto& [v, z] : cross[i]) {
            seg_b.range_add(1, 0, n - 1, 0, v - 1, z);
        }
        C[i] = seg_b.mn[1];
    }

    // Phase 2: Maintain min_{1 &lt;= i &lt;= N} (x[i] + C[i]) under point updates to x[i]
    multiset&lt;long long&gt; active_cuts;
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        active_cuts.insert(x[i] + C[i]);
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; *active_cuts.begin() &lt;&lt; '\n';
    while (q--) {
        int idx;
        long long w;
        cin &gt;&gt; idx &gt;&gt; w;
        active_cuts.erase(active_cuts.find(x[idx] + C[idx]));
        x[idx] = w;
        active_cuts.insert(x[idx] + C[idx]);
        cout &lt;&lt; *active_cuts.begin() &lt;&lt; '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

5. The 4-Step Convex Marginal Compilation Protocol
Before tackling the 8-problem difficulty ladder, let us consolidate the 4-Step Convex Marginal Compilation Protocol into an actionable reference table and a clean C++ gadget builder that plugs directly into the Chapter 17 Primal-Dual SSP solver.










Problem Archetype
Diagnostic Signal in English Statement
Algebraic Reformulation
Marginal Cost \(\Delta \Phi(k)\) (\(k = 1 \dots U\))
Graph Gadget & Potential Setup




Quadratic Edge / Degree Cost (CF 863F, CF 1187G)
Cost grows as \(a f^2 + b f\) with load/congestion \(f\)
\(\Phi(f) = \sum_{k=1}^f \Delta \Phi(k)\)
\(a(2k - 1) + b\) (\(\Delta^2 = 2a &gt; 0\))
\(U\) parallel unit arcs of cost \(a(2k-1)+b\); \(\pi \equiv 0\) if \(a + b \ge 0\)


Coupled Win–Loss Ledger ([JSOI2009] 球队收益)
Team \(i\) pays \(C_i w_i^2 + D_i l_i^2\) after \(\text{rem}_i\) games
Pre-assume losses: \(L_i = l_i^{(0)} + \text{rem}_i\); \(w_i = w_i^{(0)} + k, l_i = L_i - k\)
\(C_i(2w + 1) - D_i(2l - 1)\) (\(\Delta^2 = 2(C_i + D_i) \ge 0\))
\(\text{rem}_i\) unit arcs \(\text{Team}_i \to T\); 3-layer DAG potential \(\pi(T) = \min(0, \min \Delta \Phi_i(1))\)


Tournament 3-Cycle Maximization ([WC2007], CF 1264E)
Orient undecided edges of \(K_N\) to maximize cyclic triples
\(\max \bigl[\binom{N}{3} - \sum_u \binom{w_u}{2}\bigr] \iff \min \sum_u \binom{w_u}{2}\)
\(w_u^{(0)} + k - 1\) (\(\Delta^2 = 1 &gt; 0\))
\(\text{rem}_u\) unit arcs \(\text{Player}_u \to T\) of cost \(w_u^{(0)} + k - 1 \ge 0\); \(\pi \equiv 0\)


Piecewise-Linear Tax / Wage Brackets ([SDOI2011] 工作安排)
\(K \le 6\) workload thresholds \(T_m\) with increasing unit rates \(c_m\)
Constant marginal slope \(c_m\) on \((T_{m-1}, T_m]\)
\(c_m\) on bracket \(m\) (\(c_1 &lt; \dots &lt; c_K\))
Bundle into \(K\) arcs of capacity \(T_m - T_{m-1}\) and cost \(c_m\)


Reverse-Position Queue Waiting ([NOI2012] 美食节)
\(M\) machines, \(P\) items; \(k\)-th from last on machine \(j\) delays \(k\) items
\(\text{Wait} = \sum_{j, k} k \cdot t_{i_{j,k}, j}\)
\(k \cdot t_{i, j}\) for slot \((j, k)\)
Spawn slot \((j, k+1)\) lazily only after \((j, k) \to T\) is saturated; \(\pi(j, k+1) = \pi(T)\)


Endpoint vs Transit Parity Split ([SDOI2012] 交换棋子)
Adjacent swaps with cell participation limit \(m_v\); transit uses \(2\), endpoints use \(1\)
\(2 f_{\text{transit}} + [v \text{ is source/sink}] \le m_v\)
Split \(v_{\text{in}} \to v_{\text{mid}} \to v_{\text{out}}\)
Capacities \(\lfloor m_v / 2 \rfloor\), bumped to \(\lfloor (m_v + 1) / 2 \rfloor\) on out-arc of source or in-arc of sink




5.1 Reusable C++ Convex Marginal Gadget Builder
The following C++ helper extends PrimalDualMCMF (Chapter 17) with one-line builders for quadratic bundles, binomial tournament bundles, piecewise-linear bracket bundles, and V-shaped \(\ell_1\) deviation penalties. All reference implementations in Section 6 invoke ConvexMCMF directly (except #143A and #144B, which modify the core scaling and dynamic-node engine):
// Convex Marginal Edge Builders on top of PrimalDualMCMF (Chapter 17)
struct ConvexMCMF {
    struct Edge {
        int to, rev;
        long long cap, cost;
    };
    int n;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; adj;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; pi, dist;
    vector&lt;int&gt; pv_v, pv_e;

    ConvexMCMF(int n) : n(n), adj(n), pi(n, 0), dist(n), pv_v(n), pv_e(n) {}

    int add_edge(int u, int v, long long cap, long long cost) {
        int id = (int)adj[u].size();
        adj[u].push_back({v, (int)adj[v].size(), cap, cost});
        adj[v].push_back({u, id, 0, -cost});
        return id;
    }

    // Pattern 1: Phi(f) = a * f^2 + b * f for f in {0, ..., U} (requires a &gt;= 0)
    void add_quadratic_bundle(int u, int v, int U, long long a, long long b) {
        for (int k = 1; k &lt;= U; ++k) add_edge(u, v, 1, a * (2LL * k - 1) + b);
    }

    // Pattern 2: Phi(f) = C(w0 + f, 2) - C(w0, 2) for f in {0, ..., U}
    void add_binomial_bundle(int u, int v, int U, int w0 = 0) {
        for (int k = 1; k &lt;= U; ++k) add_edge(u, v, 1, w0 + k - 1);
    }

    // Pattern 3: Piecewise-linear brackets 0 = T[0] &lt; T[1] &lt; ... &lt; T[K] with slopes c[0] &lt; ... &lt; c[K-1]
    void add_bracket_bundle(int u, int v, const vector&lt;long long&gt;& T, const vector&lt;long long&gt;& c) {
        for (size_t m = 0; m &lt; c.size(); ++m) add_edge(u, v, T[m + 1] - T[m], c[m]);
    }

    // Pattern 4: V-shaped target penalty alpha * max(0, f0 - f) + beta * max(0, f - f0)
    long long add_v_penalty_bundle(int u, int v, long long f0, long long U,
                                   long long alpha, long long beta) {
        if (f0 &gt; 0) add_edge(u, v, f0, -alpha);
        if (U &gt; f0) add_edge(u, v, U - f0, beta);
        return alpha * f0;
    }

    bool dijkstra(int s, int t) {
        const long long INF = 4e18;
        fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
        priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
        dist[s] = 0;
        pq.push({0, s});
        while (!pq.empty()) {
            auto [d, u] = pq.top();
            pq.pop();
            if (d != dist[u]) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)adj[u].size(); ++i) {
                const auto& e = adj[u][i];
                if (e.cap &gt; 0) {
                    long long nd = d + e.cost + pi[u] - pi[e.to];
                    if (nd &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = nd;
                        pv_v[e.to] = u;
                        pv_e[e.to] = i;
                        pq.push({nd, e.to});
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if (dist[t] == INF) return false;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) if (dist[i] &lt; INF) pi[i] += min(dist[i], dist[t]);
        return true;
    }

    pair&lt;long long, long long&gt; solve(int s, int t) {
        long long flow = 0, cost = 0;
        while (dijkstra(s, t)) {
            long long push = 4e18;
            for (int v = t; v != s; v = pv_v[v]) push = min(push, adj[pv_v[v]][pv_e[v]].cap);
            for (int v = t; v != s; v = pv_v[v]) {
                auto& e = adj[pv_v[v]][pv_e[v]];
                e.cap -= push;
                adj[v][e.rev].cap += push;
            }
            flow += push;
            cost += push * pi[t];
        }
        return {flow, cost};
    }
};

6. Worked Examples & Full 8-Problem Difficulty Ladder (#137–#144)
We now apply the 4-Step Compilation Protocol across eight carefully graduated contest problems (#137 through #144), spanning tournament 3-cycle maximization, token-swapping parity splits, coupled quadratic win–loss ledgers, degree-squared bipartite bounds, multi-period inventory ordering with piecewise-linear overtime brackets, large-capacity \(2^k\) bit-scaling, and convex potential slope parameterization.


Problem #137 (SS-1): Luogu P4249 [WC2007] — 剪刀石头布 (Rock-Paper-Scissors)

Judge / URL: Luogu P4249 ([WC2007] 剪刀石头布)
Constraints: \(N \le 100\) players; an \(N \times N\) matrix \(A\) where \(A_{u,v} = 1\) means \(u\) beats \(v\), \(A_{u,v} = 0\) means \(u\) loses to \(v\), and \(A_{u,v} = 2\) means the match \(\{u, v\}\) is not yet decided (\(A_{u,u} = 0\)). Time limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Task: Orient every undecided match (\(A_{u,v} = 2 \to \{0, 1\}\) with \(A_{u,v} + A_{v,u} = 1\)) to maximize the number of directed 3-cycles \(\{u, v, w\}\), and output both the maximum 3-cycle count and the completed \(N \times N\) tournament matrix.


4-Step Compilation

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): We must orient undirected edges of \(K_N\) (\(N \le 100\)) to maximize cyclic triples. Direct 3-cycle counting is cubic in the edge variables, but Theorem 18.2 reduces it to minimizing the separable degree-convex sum \(\sum_{u=1}^N \binom{w_u}{2}\).
Step 2 (Algebraic Model): For each player \(u \in \{1, \dots, N\}\), let \(w_u^{(0)} = \sum_{v \neq u} [A_{u,v} = 1]\) be their fixed wins and \(\text{rem}_u = \sum_{v \neq u} [A_{u,v} = 2]\) be their undecided matches. Letting \(x_u \in \{0, \dots, \text{rem}_u\}\) be the undecided matches won by \(u\), we minimize
\[
\sum_{u=1}^N \binom{w_u^{(0)} + x_u}{2} \;=\; \sum_{u=1}^N \binom{w_u^{(0)}}{2} + \sum_{u=1}^N \sum_{k=1}^{x_u} \bigl(w_u^{(0)} + k - 1\bigr).
\]
Step 3 (Graph Construction):

For each undecided pair \(m = \{u, v\}\) with \(u &lt; v\) and \(A_{u,v} = 2\): add arc \(S \to m\) (\(\text{cap}=1, \text{cost}=0\)), arc \(m \to u\) (\(\text{cap}=1, \text{cost}=0\)), and arc \(m \to v\) (\(\text{cap}=1, \text{cost}=0\)). Remember the edge index of \(m \to u\) so we can read off whether \(u\) beat \(v\) after flow terminates.
For each player \(u \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) and \(k \in \{1, \dots, \text{rem}_u\}\): add arc \(u \to T\) (\(\text{cap}=1, \text{cost}=w_u^{(0)} + k - 1\)).

Step 4 (Edge Cases & Verification): All arc costs are in \([0, N-2] \ge 0\), so Primal-Dual Dijkstra runs with initial potentials \(\pi \equiv 0\). After routing all \(M\) undecided matches, if the arc \(m \to u\) has remaining residual capacity \(0\), set \(A_{u,v} = 1, A_{v,u} = 0\); otherwise set \(A_{u,v} = 0, A_{v,u} = 1\).







TipHint 1 (#137) — Complementary Counting





Instead of counting 3-subsets \(\{u, v, w\}\) that form a directed cycle, count 3-subsets that fail to form a cycle. What do the internal out-degrees of a non-cyclic 3-player tournament look like?









TipHint 2 (#137) — Marginal Transitive Triples per Win





A 3-subset fails to be a 3-cycle if and only if one player beats both of the other two. Thus player \(u\) with \(w_u\) total wins is the dominant vertex of exactly \(\binom{w_u}{2}\) non-cyclic triples. If player \(u\) already has \(w_u\) wins and wins one more match, how many new non-cyclic triples are created?









TipHint 3 (#137) — Reconstructing the Adjacency Matrix





Create a node for each undecided pair \(\{u, v\}\) with \(u &lt; v\) and \(A_{u,v} = 2\). Send \(1\) unit of flow from \(S\) to this match node, and connect it to both player \(u\) and player \(v\) with capacity \(1\) and cost \(0\). Store the index of the forward edge \(\text{Match}_{\{u,v\}} \to u\) in the adjacency list; when MCMF finishes, that edge has residual capacity \(0\) if and only if \(u\) defeated \(v\).









NoteReference Implementation (#137 — Luogu P4249 [WC2007] 剪刀石头布)





void solve_wc2007_rps() {
    int n;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n)) return;

    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; A(n, vector&lt;int&gt;(n));
    vector&lt;int&gt; w0(n, 0), rem(n, 0);
    struct Undecided { int u, v, match_node, edge_idx_u; };
    vector&lt;Undecided&gt; matches;

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; ++j) {
            cin &gt;&gt; A[i][j];
            if (i != j) {
                if (A[i][j] == 1) w0[i]++;
                else if (A[i][j] == 2) {
                    rem[i]++;
                    if (i &lt; j) matches.push_back({i, j, -1, -1});
                }
            }
        }
    }

    int M = (int)matches.size();
    int S = 0, T = n + M + 1;
    ConvexMCMF mcmf(T + 1);

    for (int m = 0; m &lt; M; ++m) {
        int m_node = n + 1 + m;
        matches[m].match_node = m_node;
        mcmf.add_edge(S, m_node, 1, 0);
        matches[m].edge_idx_u = mcmf.add_edge(m_node, matches[m].u + 1, 1, 0);
        mcmf.add_edge(m_node, matches[m].v + 1, 1, 0);
    }

    long long base_transitive = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        base_transitive += 1LL * w0[i] * (w0[i] - 1) / 2;
        mcmf.add_binomial_bundle(i + 1, T, rem[i], w0[i]);
    }

    auto [flow, min_cost] = mcmf.solve(S, T);
    long long total_triples = 1LL * n * (n - 1) * (n - 2) / 6;
    long long max_cycles = total_triples - base_transitive - min_cost;

    for (const auto& m : matches) {
        if (mcmf.adj[m.match_node][m.edge_idx_u].cap == 0) {
            A[m.u][m.v] = 1; A[m.v][m.u] = 0;
        } else {
            A[m.u][m.v] = 0; A[m.v][m.u] = 1;
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; max_cycles &lt;&lt; "\n";
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; ++j) cout &lt;&lt; A[i][j] &lt;&lt; (j + 1 == n ? "" : " ");
        cout &lt;&lt; "\n";
    }
}







Problem #138 (SS-2): Codeforces 1264 E — Beautiful League

Judge / URL: Codeforces 1264E
Constraints: \(N \le 50\) teams, \(M \le \binom{N}{2}\) already-decided directed edges \(u_i \to v_i\). Time limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Task: Orient all remaining \(\binom{N}{2} - M\) undirected edges of \(K_N\) to maximize the number of “beautiful triples” \((a, b, c)\) forming a directed 3-cycle, and print the \(N \times N\) binary adjacency matrix (0/1 string per row).


4-Step Compilation

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): Identical mathematical core to #137 ([WC2007] 剪刀石头布) with \(N \le 50\), except the input is given as an edge list of \(M\) decided matches and the output need only print the \(N \times N\) binary matrix without the count.
Step 2 (Algebraic Model): Minimize \(\sum_{u=1}^N \binom{w_u}{2}\) where \(w_u = w_u^{(0)} + x_u\).
Step 3 (Graph Construction): Initialize an \(N \times N\) status matrix state[u][v] = 2 (for \(u \neq v\)). For each of the \(M\) input edges \((u, v)\), set state[u][v] = 1, state[v][u] = 0, and increment \(w_u^{(0)}\). Collect all pairs \(u &lt; v\) with state[u][v] == 2 as match vertices, and add unit-capacity marginal arcs \(u \to T\) with costs \(w_u^{(0)}, w_u^{(0)} + 1, \dots, w_u^{(0)} + \text{rem}_u - 1\).
Step 4 (Complexity & Verification): With \(N = 50\), there are at most \(\binom{50}{2} = 1225\) match nodes and \(1225\) unit augmentations. Primal-Dual Dijkstra completes in \(&lt; 10\text{ ms}\).







TipHint 1 (#138) — Converting Beautiful Triples to Out-Degrees





A triple \(\{a, b, c\}\) is not beautiful if and only if one of the three teams defeats both of the others. Express the number of non-beautiful triples strictly in terms of the final out-degrees \(w_1, \dots, w_N\).









TipHint 2 (#138) — Convex Marginal Splitting





Since \(\binom{w_u}{2} = 0 + 1 + 2 + \dots + (w_u - 1)\), the \(d\)-th total win of team \(u\) (\(1\)-indexed) adds \(d - 1\) non-beautiful triples. Because \(0 &lt; 1 &lt; 2 &lt; \dots &lt; N - 2\), parallel unit-capacity edges from team \(u\) to the sink \(T\) will automatically be saturated in increasing order of cost.









TipHint 3 (#138) — Handling Pre-Decided Matches





If team \(u\) already won \(w_u^{(0)}\) matches from the input, its undecided win edges to \(T\) should start at cost \(w_u^{(0)}\) and go up to \(w_u^{(0)} + \text{rem}_u - 1\).









NoteReference Implementation (#138 — Codeforces 1264E Beautiful League)





void solve_cf1264e() {
    int n, m;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; m)) return;

    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; ans(n, vector&lt;int&gt;(n, 2));
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) ans[i][i] = 0;
    vector&lt;int&gt; w0(n, 0);

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; m; ++i) {
        int u, v;
        cin &gt;&gt; u &gt;&gt; v;
        --u; --v;
        ans[u][v] = 1;
        ans[v][u] = 0;
        w0[u]++;
    }

    struct Match { int u, v, node, e_u; };
    vector&lt;Match&gt; undecided;
    vector&lt;int&gt; rem(n, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j &lt; n; ++j) {
            if (ans[i][j] == 2) {
                undecided.push_back({i, j, -1, -1});
                rem[i]++;
                rem[j]++;
            }
        }
    }

    int U = (int)undecided.size();
    int S = 0, T = n + U + 1;
    ConvexMCMF mcmf(T + 1);

    for (int k = 0; k &lt; U; ++k) {
        int m_node = n + 1 + k;
        undecided[k].node = m_node;
        mcmf.add_edge(S, m_node, 1, 0);
        undecided[k].e_u = mcmf.add_edge(m_node, undecided[k].u + 1, 1, 0);
        mcmf.add_edge(m_node, undecided[k].v + 1, 1, 0);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        mcmf.add_binomial_bundle(i + 1, T, rem[i], w0[i]);
    }

    mcmf.solve(S, T);

    for (const auto& mt : undecided) {
        if (mcmf.adj[mt.node][mt.e_u].cap == 0) {
            ans[mt.u][mt.v] = 1; ans[mt.v][mt.u] = 0;
        } else {
            ans[mt.u][mt.v] = 0; ans[mt.v][mt.u] = 1;
        }
    }

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; ++j) cout &lt;&lt; ans[i][j];
        cout &lt;&lt; "\n";
    }
}







Problem #139 (Int-1): Luogu P3159 [SDOI2012] — 交换棋子 (Swapping Chess Pieces)

Judge / URL: Luogu P3159 ([SDOI2012] 交换棋子)
Constraints: \(R, C \le 20\) (\(R \times C \le 400\) cells). Given an initial grid \(A \in \{0, 1\}^{R \times C}\) (1 = black piece, 0 = white piece), a target grid \(B \in \{0, 1\}^{R \times C}\), and a swap limit grid \(M \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}^{R \times C}\). In one operation, you may swap the pieces on any two 8-directionally adjacent cells \((r_1, c_1)\) and \((r_2, c_2)\) (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal), which consumes \(1\) swap from \(m_{r_1, c_1}\) and \(1\) swap from \(m_{r_2, c_2}\).
Task: Find the minimum total number of adjacent swaps needed to transform \(A\) into \(B\) without exceeding any cell’s swap limit \(m_{r,c}\), or output -1 if impossible.


4-Step Compilation

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): We are moving identical black tokens (1s) from their initial positions in \(A\) to their target positions in \(B\) along shortest paths on an 8-connected grid (\(R, C \le 20\)), subject to vertex-level participation capacities \(m_{r,c}\). Why isn’t this a trivial vertex-capacitated flow? Because an intermediate transit cell on a token’s path participates in TWO swaps (one swap when the token enters the cell, and a second swap when the token leaves the cell!), whereas the starting cell and ending cell of the path each participate in only ONE swap!
Step 2 (Intermediate Algebraic & Parity Model): First, observe that if a cell \(v = (r, c)\) starts with a black piece and also ends with a black piece (\(A_v = 1\) and \(B_v = 1\)), its black piece can remain stationary (or if another black piece passes through \(v\) by swapping with \(v\)’s black piece and then swapping onward, that is algebraically identical to the moving piece passing through \(v\) using \(2\) swaps at \(v\)). Thus:

Cells with \(A_v = 1, B_v = 0\) are net sources (they must emit \(1\) unit of flow, using \(1\) outgoing swap at \(v\)).
Cells with \(A_v = 0, B_v = 1\) are net sinks (they must absorb \(1\) unit of flow, using \(1\) incoming swap at \(v\)).
Every unit of flow that transits through \(v\) (both entering \(v\) and leaving \(v\)) consumes \(1\) incoming swap and \(1\) outgoing swap (\(2\) swaps total at \(v\)).

Let \(f_{\text{in}}(v)\) and \(f_{\text{out}}(v)\) denote the number of tokens that enter \(v\) from neighbors and leave \(v\) to neighbors, respectively. Flow conservation at \(v\) requires:

If \(A_v = 1, B_v = 0\) (source): \(f_{\text{out}}(v) - f_{\text{in}}(v) = 1\), and total swaps used at \(v\) is \(f_{\text{in}}(v) + f_{\text{out}}(v) = 2 f_{\text{in}}(v) + 1 \le m_v\).
If \(A_v = 0, B_v = 1\) (sink): \(f_{\text{in}}(v) - f_{\text{out}}(v) = 1\), and total swaps used at \(v\) is \(f_{\text{in}}(v) + f_{\text{out}}(v) = 2 f_{\text{out}}(v) + 1 \le m_v\).
If \(A_v = B_v\) (neutral): \(f_{\text{in}}(v) = f_{\text{out}}(v)\), and total swaps used at \(v\) is \(2 f_{\text{in}}(v) \le m_v\).

Step 3 (Mechanical Graph Compilation via 3-Node Split \(v_{\text{in}} \to v_{\text{mid}} \to v_{\text{out}}\)): Split every cell \(v = (r, c)\) into three vertices \(v_{\text{in}}\), \(v_{\text{mid}}\), and \(v_{\text{out}}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{cap}(v_{\text{in}} \to v_{\text{mid}}) \;&=\; \begin{cases} \lfloor (m_v + 1) / 2 \rfloor & \text{if } A_v = 0 \text{ and } B_v = 1 \text{ (sink)}, \\ \lfloor m_v / 2 \rfloor & \text{otherwise}, \end{cases} \qquad \text{cost} = 0, \\
\text{cap}(v_{\text{mid}} \to v_{\text{out}}) \;&=\; \begin{cases} \lfloor (m_v + 1) / 2 \rfloor & \text{if } A_v = 1 \text{ and } B_v = 0 \text{ (source)}, \\ \lfloor m_v / 2 \rfloor & \text{otherwise}, \end{cases} \qquad \text{cost} = 0.
\end{aligned}
\]

If \(A_v = 1, B_v = 0\): add source injection \(S \to v_{\text{mid}}\) with \(\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0\).
If \(A_v = 0, B_v = 1\): add sink absorption \(v_{\text{mid}} \to T\) with \(\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0\).
For every 8-directional neighbor \(u\) of \(v\): add transit arc \(v_{\text{out}} \to u_{\text{in}}\) with \(\text{cap} = \infty, \text{cost} = 1\) (representing \(1\) swap between adjacent cells \(v\) and \(u\)).







flowchart LR
    S((S)) --&gt;|"If A=1, B=0: cap=1, cost=0"| vmid["v_mid"]
    vin["v_in"] --&gt;|"cap = floor((m_v + [A=0,B=1]) / 2), cost = 0"| vmid
    vmid --&gt;|"cap = floor((m_v + [A=1,B=0]) / 2), cost = 0"| vout["v_out"]
    vmid --&gt;|"If A=0, B=1: cap=1, cost=0"| T((T))
    vout --&gt;|"8-dir neighbor u: cap=INF, cost=1"| uin["u_in"]







Step 4 (Verification of the Parity Split): Suppose \(v\) is a net source (\(A_v = 1, B_v = 0\)) with \(m_v = 3\). Then \(\text{cap}(v_{\text{in}} \to v_{\text{mid}}) = \lfloor 3/2 \rfloor = 1\) and \(\text{cap}(v_{\text{mid}} \to v_{\text{out}}) = \lfloor 4/2 \rfloor = 2\). Once \(S \to v_{\text{mid}}\) injects \(1\) unit of flow (which must exit via \(v_{\text{mid}} \to v_{\text{out}}\)), at most \(1\) additional transit unit can pass through \(v_{\text{in}} \to v_{\text{mid}} \to v_{\text{out}}\), for a maximum of \(1 + 2(1) = 3 = m_v\) swaps! What if \(m_v = 0\) on a source cell? Then \(\text{cap}(v_{\text{mid}} \to v_{\text{out}}) = \lfloor 1/2 \rfloor = 0\), correctly blocking the source piece from ever moving!







TipHint 1 (#139) — Why Do Transit Cells Cost 2 Swaps from \(m_{r,c}\)?





Trace a single black piece moving along a path \(v_0 \to v_1 \to v_2 \to v_3\). How many swaps involve \(v_0\)? How many involve \(v_3\)? And how many swaps involve the intermediate cells \(v_1\) and \(v_2\)?









TipHint 2 (#139) — What About Cells Where \(A_{r,c} = 1\) and \(B_{r,c} = 1\)?





If cell \(v\) both starts and ends with 1, does its piece need to move? What if another black piece needs to pass through \(v\)? Swapping the incoming black piece with \(v\)’s black piece, and then moving \(v\)’s black piece onward, uses \(2\) swaps at \(v\) and leaves a black piece at \(v\)—identical to a single flow unit transiting through \(v\)!









TipHint 3 (#139) — The 3-Node Split \(v_{\text{in}} \to v_{\text{mid}} \to v_{\text{out}}\)





Inject source flow (1 -&gt; 0) directly into \(v_{\text{mid}}\) and extract sink flow (0 -&gt; 1) directly from \(v_{\text{mid}}\). Then every transit unit passes through both \(v_{\text{in}} \to v_{\text{mid}}\) and \(v_{\text{mid}} \to v_{\text{out}}\), while a source unit only passes through \(v_{\text{mid}} \to v_{\text{out}}\) and a sink unit only passes through \(v_{\text{in}} \to v_{\text{mid}}\). Set the capacities of those two internal edges to \(\lfloor m_v / 2 \rfloor\), adding \(+1\) to the half that carries the odd endpoint swap when \(m_v\) is odd!









NoteReference Implementation (#139 — Luogu P3159 [SDOI2012] 交换棋子)





void solve_sdoi2012_chess_swap() {
    int R, C;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; R &gt;&gt; C)) return;

    vector&lt;string&gt; A(R), B(R), M_str(R);
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; R; ++r) cin &gt;&gt; A[r];
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; R; ++r) cin &gt;&gt; B[r];
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; R; ++r) cin &gt;&gt; M_str[r];

    int cntA = 0, cntB = 0;
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; R; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; C; ++c) {
            cntA += (A[r][c] == '1');
            cntB += (B[r][c] == '1');
        }
    }
    if (cntA != cntB) {
        cout &lt;&lt; -1 &lt;&lt; "\n";
        return;
    }

    int N_cells = R * C;
    int S = 3 * N_cells, T = 3 * N_cells + 1;
    ConvexMCMF mcmf(T + 1);

    auto v_in  = [&](int r, int c) { return 3 * (r * C + c); };
    auto v_mid = [&](int r, int c) { return 3 * (r * C + c) + 1; };
    auto v_out = [&](int r, int c) { return 3 * (r * C + c) + 2; };

    int need_flow = 0;
    const int dr[8] = {-1, -1, -1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1};
    const int dc[8] = {-1, 0, 1, -1, 1, -1, 0, 1};

    for (int r = 0; r &lt; R; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; C; ++c) {
            int m = M_str[r][c] - '0';
            int a = A[r][c] - '0';
            int b = B[r][c] - '0';

            if (a == 1 && b == 0) {
                need_flow++;
                mcmf.add_edge(S, v_mid(r, c), 1, 0);
                mcmf.add_edge(v_in(r, c), v_mid(r, c), m / 2, 0);
                mcmf.add_edge(v_mid(r, c), v_out(r, c), (m + 1) / 2, 0);
            } else if (a == 0 && b == 1) {
                mcmf.add_edge(v_mid(r, c), T, 1, 0);
                mcmf.add_edge(v_in(r, c), v_mid(r, c), (m + 1) / 2, 0);
                mcmf.add_edge(v_mid(r, c), v_out(r, c), m / 2, 0);
            } else {
                mcmf.add_edge(v_in(r, c), v_mid(r, c), m / 2, 0);
                mcmf.add_edge(v_mid(r, c), v_out(r, c), m / 2, 0);
            }

            for (int d = 0; d &lt; 8; ++d) {
                int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];
                if (nr &gt;= 0 && nr &lt; R && nc &gt;= 0 && nc &lt; C) {
                    mcmf.add_edge(v_out(r, c), v_in(nr, nc), 1e9, 1);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    auto [flow, cost] = mcmf.solve(S, T);
    cout &lt;&lt; (flow == need_flow ? cost : -1LL) &lt;&lt; "\n";
}







Problem #140 (Int-2): Luogu P4307 [JSOI2009] — 球队收益 / 球队预算 (Team Revenue)

Judge / URL: Luogu P4307 ([JSOI2009] 球队收益 / 球队预算)
Constraints: \(N \le 5000\) teams, \(M \le 1000\) remaining matches. Each team \(i\) has initial wins \(w_i^{(0)}\), initial losses \(l_i^{(0)}\), and non-negative cost coefficients \(C_i, D_i\). Time limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Task: Decide the winner of each of the \(M\) remaining matches to minimize total league expenditure \(\sum_{i=1}^N (C_i w_i^2 + D_i l_i^2)\).


4-Step Compilation

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): Two-variable quadratic cost \(C_i w_i^2 + D_i l_i^2\) per team with fixed total games \(w_i + l_i = G_i\). Directly matches Section 2 (Win–Loss Substitution).
Step 2 (Algebraic Model): Pre-assume all \(\text{rem}_i\) remaining games for team \(i\) are losses: \(L_i = l_i^{(0)} + \text{rem}_i\) and \(\text{BaseCost} = \sum_{i=1}^N \bigl(C_i (w_i^{(0)})^2 + D_i L_i^2\bigr)\). Winning the \(k\)-th game (\(k = 1, \dots, \text{rem}_i\)) transitions \((w, l) = (w_i^{(0)} + k - 1,\; L_i - k + 1) \to (w+1, l-1)\) with marginal cost
\[
\Delta \Phi_i(k) \;=\; C_i(2w + 1) - D_i(2l - 1).
\]
Step 3 (Graph Construction & Potential Initialization):

For each match \(m = \{u, v\}\) (\(1 \le m \le M\)): add \(S \to \text{Match}_m\) (\(\text{cap}=1, \text{cost}=0\)) and arcs \(\text{Match}_m \to \text{Team}_u, \text{Match}_m \to \text{Team}_v\) (\(\text{cap}=1, \text{cost}=0\)).
For each team \(i\) and \(k \in \{1, \dots, \text{rem}_i\}\): add arc \(\text{Team}_i \to T\) (\(\text{cap}=1, \text{cost}=\Delta \Phi_i(k)\)).
Because \(\Delta \Phi_i(k)\) can be negative when \(D_i(2l - 1) &gt; C_i(2w + 1)\), set the initial 3-layer DAG potential \(\pi(T) = \min\bigl(0LL,\; \min_{i : \text{rem}_i &gt; 0} \Delta \Phi_i(1)\bigr)\) and \(\pi(v) = 0\) for all \(v \neq T\).

Step 4 (Complexity): Only \(M \le 1000\) units of flow are routed, and only \(2M \le 2000\) marginal arcs \(\text{Team}_i \to T\) are ever created across all teams! Thus Primal-Dual Dijkstra runs \(1000\) times on a graph with \(\le 6002\) vertices and \(\le 5000\) arcs, finishing in \(&lt; 20\text{ ms}\).







TipHint 1 (#140) — Decoupling Wins and Losses





Notice that \(w_i + l_i\) is already determined before any remaining match is played! What is \(l_i\) if team \(i\) loses all \(\text{rem}_i\) of its remaining matches?









TipHint 2 (#140) — Marginal Cost of Converting One Loss into One Win





Suppose team \(i\) currently has \(w\) wins and \(l\) losses. If we change the outcome of one of team \(i\)’s matches from a loss to a win, \((w, l)\) becomes \((w+1, l-1)\). Expand \(C_i(w+1)^2 + D_i(l-1)^2 - (C_i w^2 + D_i l^2)\) and check that this increment increases by \(2(C_i + D_i) \ge 0\) for each subsequent win.









TipHint 3 (#140) — Fast Dijkstra Initialization with Negative Marginals





Because all negative-cost arcs enter the sink \(T\) directly from \(\text{Team}_i\), the initial network is a DAG of depth \(3\). Setting \(\pi(T) = \min(0, \min_i \Delta \Phi_i(1))\) and \(\pi = 0\) everywhere else makes every initial reduced cost non-negative in \(O(N)\) time without SPFA!









NoteReference Implementation (#140 — Luogu P4307 [JSOI2009] 球队收益)





void solve_jsoi2009_team_revenue() {
    int n, m;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; m)) return;

    vector&lt;long long&gt; w0(n), l0(n), C(n), D(n);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; w0[i] &gt;&gt; l0[i] &gt;&gt; C[i] &gt;&gt; D[i];

    vector&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt; matches(m);
    vector&lt;int&gt; rem(n, 0);
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; m; ++j) {
        int u, v;
        cin &gt;&gt; u &gt;&gt; v;
        --u; --v;
        matches[j] = {u, v};
        rem[u]++;
        rem[v]++;
    }

    int S = 0, T = n + m + 1;
    ConvexMCMF mcmf(T + 1);

    for (int j = 0; j &lt; m; ++j) {
        int m_node = n + 1 + j;
        mcmf.add_edge(S, m_node, 1, 0);
        mcmf.add_edge(m_node, matches[j].first + 1, 1, 0);
        mcmf.add_edge(m_node, matches[j].second + 1, 1, 0);
    }

    long long base_cost = 0, min_delta = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        long long L = l0[i] + rem[i];
        base_cost += C[i] * w0[i] * w0[i] + D[i] * L * L;
        for (int k = 1; k &lt;= rem[i]; ++k) {
            long long w = w0[i] + k - 1;
            long long l = L - k + 1;
            long long delta = C[i] * (2 * w + 1) - D[i] * (2 * l - 1);
            mcmf.add_edge(i + 1, T, 1, delta);
            min_delta = min(min_delta, delta);
        }
    }

    // 3-layer DAG potential initialization: makes all initial reduced costs &gt;= 0
    mcmf.pi[T] = min_delta;
    cout &lt;&lt; base_cost + mcmf.solve(S, T).second &lt;&lt; "\n";
}







Problem #141 (Adv-1): Codeforces 863 F — Almost Permutation

Judge / URL: Codeforces 863F
Constraints: \(N \le 50\), \(Q \le 100\) constraints. Array elements \(a_1, \dots, a_N \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) initially satisfy \(1 \le a_i \le N\). Each query is either 1 l r v (\(\forall i \in [l, r],\; a_i \ge v\)) or 2 l r v (\(\forall i \in [l, r],\; a_i \le v\)). Time limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).
Task: Assign each \(a_i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) satisfying all \(Q\) constraints to minimize the sum of squared frequencies \(\sum_{v=1}^N \text{cnt}(v)^2\), where \(\text{cnt}(v) = \sum_{i=1}^N [a_i = v]\), or print -1 if no valid array exists.


4-Step Compilation

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): Each position \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) must choose one value \(v\) from its allowed interval \([L_i, R_i]\), and the objective \(\sum_{v=1}^N \text{cnt}(v)^2\) is the sum of squares of the in-degrees of the value vertices \(v\).
Step 2 (Algebraic Model): Intersect all \(Q\) bounds for each position \(i\):
\[
L_i = \max\bigl(1,\; \max_{(1, l, r, v) : l \le i \le r} v\bigr), \qquad R_i = \min\bigl(N,\; \min_{(2, l, r, v) : l \le i \le r} v\bigr).
\]
If \(L_i &gt; R_i\) for any \(i\), output -1 immediately. Otherwise, since \(\text{cnt}(v)^2 = \sum_{k=1}^{\text{cnt}(v)} (2k - 1)\), the \(k\)-th occurrence of value \(v\) incurs marginal cost \(2k - 1\).
Step 3 (Graph Construction):

Source \(S \to \text{Pos}_i\) (\(\text{cap}=1, \text{cost}=0\)) for \(i = 1, \dots, N\).
\(\text{Pos}_i \to \text{Val}_v\) (\(\text{cap}=1, \text{cost}=0\)) for each \(v \in [L_i, R_i]\).
\(\text{Val}_v \to T\): \(N\) parallel unit-capacity arcs with costs \(1, 3, 5, \dots, 2N - 1\) for \(k = 1, \dots, N\).

Step 4 (Verification): All costs are positive odd integers \(2k - 1 \ge 1\). Route \(N\) units from \(S\) to \(T\); if maximum flow \(&lt; N\) (which only happens if \(L_i &gt; R_i\)), output -1, else output \(\text{MinCost}\).







TipHint 1 (#141) — Simplifying the \(Q\) Range Constraints





Since each constraint applies a lower or upper bound to individual elements \(a_i\) independently, you can maintain L[i] and R[i] for each index \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) by taking L[i] = max(L[i], v) on type-1 queries and R[i] = min(R[i], v) on type-2 queries.









TipHint 2 (#141) — Expanding \(\text{cnt}(v)^2\) into Odd Marginal Costs





Use the identity \(c^2 = 1 + 3 + 5 + \dots + (2c - 1)\). How much does the \(k\)-th element assigned to value \(v\) increase \(\text{cnt}(v)^2\)?









TipHint 3 (#141) — Bipartite Assignment + Convex Sink Bundle





Build a bipartite graph with \(N\) position nodes on the left and \(N\) value nodes on the right. Connect each value node \(v\) to the sink \(T\) with \(N\) parallel edges of capacity \(1\) and costs \(1, 3, 5, \dots, 2N - 1\).









NoteReference Implementation (#141 — Codeforces 863F Almost Permutation)





// Uses ConvexMCMF from Section 5.1
void solve_cf863f() {
    int n, q;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; q)) return;

    vector&lt;int&gt; L(n + 1, 1), R(n + 1, n);
    for (int k = 0; k &lt; q; ++k) {
        int type, l, r, v;
        cin &gt;&gt; type &gt;&gt; l &gt;&gt; r &gt;&gt; v;
        for (int i = l; i &lt;= r; ++i) {
            if (type == 1) L[i] = max(L[i], v);
            else R[i] = min(R[i], v);
        }
    }

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        if (L[i] &gt; R[i]) {
            cout &lt;&lt; -1 &lt;&lt; "\n";
            return;
        }
    }

    int S = 0, T = 2 * n + 1;
    ConvexMCMF mcmf(T + 1);

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        mcmf.add_edge(S, i, 1, 0);
        for (int v = L[i]; v &lt;= R[i]; ++v) mcmf.add_edge(i, n + v, 1, 0);
    }
    for (int v = 1; v &lt;= n; ++v) {
        mcmf.add_quadratic_bundle(n + v, T, n, 1, 0); // cnt(v)^2 = sum (2k - 1)
    }

    auto [flow, cost] = mcmf.solve(S, T);
    cout &lt;&lt; (flow == n ? cost : -1) &lt;&lt; "\n";
}







Problem #142 (Adv-2): Luogu P2517 [HAOI2010] — 订购 (Order) & Luogu P2488 [SDOI2011] — 工作安排 (Work Arrangement)

Judge / URL: Luogu P2517 ([HAOI2010] 订购) & Luogu P2488 ([SDOI2011] 工作安排)
Constraints:

Part A ([HAOI2010] 订购): \(N \le 50\) months; month \(i\) has customer demand \(U_i \le 10^4\), unit purchasing cost \(d_i\), warehouse storage fee \(m\) per unit carried over from month \(i\) to month \(i+1\), and warehouse capacity \(S_{\text{cap}} \le 10^4\). Minimize total purchasing and storage cost.
Part B ([SDOI2011] 工作安排): \(M \le 250\) employees and \(N \le 250\) product types (demands \(C_j\)). Each employee \(i\) has \(K_i + 1 \le 6\) piecewise-linear anger/wage brackets defined by thresholds \(0 = T_{i,0} &lt; T_{i,1} &lt; \dots &lt; T_{i,K_i} &lt; T_{i,K_i+1} = \infty\) and strictly increasing per-unit anger slopes \(W_{i,1} &lt; W_{i,2} &lt; \dots &lt; W_{i,K_i+1}\). Minimize total anger across all employees.



4-Step Compilation

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal):

In [HAOI2010] 订购, each month \(i\) is a time-indexed conservation node: goods ordered in month \(i\) at cost \(d_i\) can either satisfy month \(i\)’s demand \(U_i\) immediately or be carried over to month \(i+1\) along a capacity-\(S_{\text{cap}}\) inventory edge of cost \(m\).
In [SDOI2011] 工作安排, an employee’s anger is a piecewise-linear convex function of their total workload \(f_i = \sum_j f_{i,j}\), with up to \(6\) constant-slope brackets \([T_{i,k-1}, T_{i,k}]\).

Step 2 (Algebraic Model & Interval Bundling):

For [HAOI2010] 订购: let \(x_i \ge 0\) be the amount ordered in month \(i\) and \(I_i \in [0, S_{\text{cap}}]\) be the inventory carried from month \(i\) to month \(i+1\). Flow conservation at month \(i\) is \(x_i + I_{i-1} = U_i + I_i\), minimizing \(\sum_{i=1}^N d_i x_i + \sum_{i=1}^{N-1} m I_i\).
For [SDOI2011] 工作安排: instead of creating \(\sum C_j \le 2.5 \times 10^5\) unit-capacity arcs per employee, apply Pattern 3 (Interval Bundling) from Section 1.2! Bundle the \(k\)-th bracket of employee \(i\) into a single arc \(S \to \text{Emp}_i\) of capacity \(T_{i,k} - T_{i,k-1}\) and cost \(W_{i,k}\) (with capacity \(\infty\) on the last bracket \(k = K_i + 1\)).

Step 3 (Graph Construction):

[HAOI2010] 订购: For \(i = 1, \dots, N\), add ordering arc \(S \to \text{Month}_i\) (\(\text{cap}=\infty, \text{cost}=d_i\)), demand arc \(\text{Month}_i \to T\) (\(\text{cap}=U_i, \text{cost}=0\)), and (for \(i &lt; N\)) carryover arc \(\text{Month}_i \to \text{Month}_{i+1}\) (\(\text{cap}=S_{\text{cap}}, \text{cost}=m\)).
[SDOI2011] 工作安排: Add \(K_i + 1\) bundled bracket arcs \(S \to \text{Emp}_i\) (\(\text{cap}=T_{i,k} - T_{i,k-1}, \text{cost}=W_{i,k}\)), capability arcs \(\text{Emp}_i \to \text{Prod}_j\) (\(\text{cap}=\infty, \text{cost}=0\) if employee \(i\) can make product \(j\)), and demand arcs \(\text{Prod}_j \to T\) (\(\text{cap}=C_j, \text{cost}=0\)).

Step 4 (Verification): Both graphs have strictly non-negative initial edge costs (\(d_i, m \ge 0\) and \(W_{i,1} &lt; \dots &lt; W_{i,K_i+1}\) with \(W_{i,1} \ge 0\)), so Primal-Dual Dijkstra runs with \(\pi \equiv 0\) in \(&lt; 15\text{ ms}\).







TipHint 1 (#142) — Time-Expanded Inventory Carryover ([HAOI2010] 订购)





Model each month \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) as a vertex. Purchasing in month \(i\) is an edge \(S \to i\) of capacity \(\infty\) and cost \(d_i\); satisfying demand in month \(i\) is an edge \(i \to T\) of capacity \(U_i\) and cost \(0\). How do you represent storing up to \(S_{\text{cap}}\) units from month \(i\) to month \(i+1\) at unit cost \(m\)?









TipHint 2 (#142) — Bundling Piecewise-Linear Wage Brackets ([SDOI2011] 工作安排)





If an employee’s marginal anger is \(W_{i,1}\) for the first \(T_{i,1}\) items, \(W_{i,2}\) for the next \(T_{i,2} - T_{i,1}\) items, and so on with \(W_{i,1} &lt; W_{i,2} &lt; \dots\), you do not need unit-capacity edges! Just add \(K_i + 1\) parallel edges from \(S\) to \(\text{Emp}_i\), where the \(k\)-th edge has capacity \(T_{i,k} - T_{i,k-1}\) and cost \(W_{i,k}\).









TipHint 3 (#142) — Watch Out for 64-Bit Overflow





In [SDOI2011] 工作安排, total product demand \(\sum C_j\) can reach \(2.5 \times 10^5\) and anger slopes \(W_{i,k}\) can reach \(10^5\), so total minimum anger can exceed \(2^{31} - 1\). Accumulate total cost in long long.









