Problem #143 (Mas-1): Piecewise-Linear Large-Capacity Convex Cost Flow (\(2^k\) Bit-Scaling) & AtCoder AGC 034 D — Manhattan Max Matching

Judge / URL: AtCoder AGC 034D (Manhattan Max Matching) & Large-Capacity Piecewise-Linear Convex MCMF (Algorithm 18.1)
Constraints:

Part A (Large-Capacity Piecewise-Linear Convex Cost Flow via \(2^k\) Bit-Scaling): Directed network with \(V \le 400\), \(E \le 1500\) piecewise-linear convex edges with capacities \(U_e \le 10^9\) and costs \(\le 10^9\).
Part B (AtCoder AGC 034D — Manhattan Max Matching): \(N \le 1000\) red points \((R X_i, R Y_i)\) with multiplicity \(R C_i \le 10\) and \(N \le 1000\) blue points \((B X_j, B Y_j)\) with multiplicity \(B C_j \le 10\) (\(\sum R C_i = \sum B C_j \le 10^4\)). Pair all red and blue tokens to maximize the sum of Manhattan distances \(|R X_i - B X_j| + |R Y_i - B Y_j|\).



4-Step Compilation

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal):

In Part A, \(U_e \le 10^9\) makes standard unit-step SSP TLE, requiring Algorithm 18.1 (Edmonds–Karp Binary Capacity Bit-Scaling).
In Part B (AGC 034D), building all \(N^2 = 10^6\) red-to-blue bipartite edges gives a dense graph that is too slow for \(10^4\) flow units. However, the \(\ell_1\) Manhattan distance \(|x_1 - x_2| + |y_1 - y_2|\) is the maximum of four linear sign combinations:
\[
|x_1 - x_2| + |y_1 - y_2| \;=\; \max_{s_x, s_y \in \{-1, +1\}} \Bigl[\bigl(s_x x_1 + s_y y_1\bigr) + \bigl(-s_x x_2 - s_y y_2\bigr)\Bigr].
\]

Step 2 (Algebraic Model & 4-Sign Hub Factorization): Introduce 4 intermediate sign-hub vertices \(H_0, H_1, H_2, H_3\) corresponding to \((s_x, s_y) \in \{(+1, +1), (+1, -1), (-1, +1), (-1, -1)\}\). Every red point \(i\) connects to each hub \(H_b\) with cost \(-(s_x R X_i + s_y R Y_i)\), and each hub \(H_b\) connects to every blue point \(j\) with cost \(-(-s_x B X_j - s_y B Y_j)\)! Because we minimize negated cost (which maximizes the sum), the shortest path from Red \(i\) to Blue \(j\) through \(\{H_0, H_1, H_2, H_3\}\) automatically picks the sign pair \((s_x, s_y)\) achieving the maximum \(= |R X_i - B X_j| + |R Y_i - B Y_j|\)!
Step 3 (Graph Construction):

For Part A (Algorithm 18.1): bundle each piecewise-linear convex edge into linear bracket arcs, add feedback arc \(T \to S\) of capacity \(\sum u_a\) and cost \(-M_\infty\), and run bit-scaling from \(k = \lfloor \log_2 U_{\max} \rfloor\) down to \(0\).
For Part B (AGC 034D): \(2N + 6\) vertices (\(S\), \(T\), \(N\) Red, \(N\) Blue, \(4\) Hubs) and only \(10N \approx 10^4\) edges! Initialize DAG potentials in \(O(N)\) time along the 4-layer DAG \(S \to \text{Red}_i \to H_b \to \text{Blue}_j \to T\) and run Primal-Dual Dijkstra.

Step 4 (Verification): In AGC 034D, since a path could theoretically bounce \(\text{Red}_1 \to H_b \to \text{Red}_2\) in the residual graph after augmentations, the 4-hub graph has \(O(N)\) vertices and \(O(N)\) edges, so \(O(F \cdot N \log N)\) with \(F \le 10^4, N \le 1000\) takes \(\approx 40\text{ ms}\).







TipHint 1 (#143) — Binary Bit-Scaling Invariant (Algorithm 18.1)





When you double all arc capacities and flows (\(f_a \gets 2 f_a, u_a \gets 2 u_a\)), no new residual arcs appear, so \(c_{uv}^\pi \ge 0\) continues to hold everywhere. A violation can only occur when you add \(+1\) to the capacity of a currently saturated arc \((u \to v)\) whose reduced cost \(c_{uv}^\pi\) is negative!









TipHint 2 (#143) — Repairing a Single-Bit Violation via Dijkstra





When unlocking \(+1\) capacity on \((u \to v)\) exposes \(c_{uv}^\pi &lt; 0\), run Dijkstra from \(v\) to \(u\) in the existing residual graph (before adding the new capacity unit), update \(\pi(x) \gets \pi(x) + \text{dist}(x)\), and if \(\text{dist}(u) + c_{uv} + \pi_{\text{old}}(u) - \pi_{\text{old}}(v) &lt; 0\), push \(1\) unit along \(u \to v \leadsto u\).









TipHint 3 (#143) — Expanding \(|x_1 - x_2| + |y_1 - y_2|\) via 4 Hub Nodes (AGC 034D)





Use \(|A| + |B| = \max(A+B, A-B, -A+B, -A-B)\). Create 4 hub vertices in the middle of the bipartite graph; connect every Red node to all 4 hubs and all 4 hubs to every Blue node, slashing the edge count from \(N^2 = 10^6\) to \(8N = 8000\)!









NoteReference Implementation (#143A — 2^k Capacity Bit-Scaling for Large-Capacity Convex MCMF)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 4e18;

// Edmonds-Karp 2^k Binary Capacity Bit-Scaling (Algorithm 18.1)
// Handles arbitrary capacities U &lt;= 10^9 and negative costs/cycles in O(E log U (E + V log V))
struct BitScalingMCMF {
    struct InternalEdge {
        int to, rev;
        long long cap, cost;
    };
    struct InputArc {
        int u, v, idx;
        long long full_cap, cost;
    };

    int n;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;InternalEdge&gt;&gt; adj;
    vector&lt;InputArc&gt; arcs;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; pi, dist;
    vector&lt;int&gt; pv_v, pv_e;

    BitScalingMCMF(int n) : n(n), adj(n), pi(n, 0), dist(n), pv_v(n), pv_e(n) {}

    int add_arc(int u, int v, long long cap, long long cost) {
        int idx = (int)adj[u].size();
        adj[u].push_back({v, (int)adj[v].size(), 0, cost});
        adj[v].push_back({u, idx, 0, -cost});
        arcs.push_back({u, v, idx, cap, cost});
        return (int)arcs.size() - 1;
    }

    void dijkstra(int src) {
        fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
        priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
        dist[src] = 0;
        pq.push({0, src});
        while (!pq.empty()) {
            auto [d, u] = pq.top();
            pq.pop();
            if (d != dist[u]) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)adj[u].size(); ++i) {
                const auto& e = adj[u][i];
                if (e.cap &gt; 0) {
                    long long nd = d + e.cost + pi[u] - pi[e.to];
                    if (nd &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = nd;
                        pv_v[e.to] = u;
                        pv_e[e.to] = i;
                        pq.push({nd, e.to});
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        long long max_d = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) if (dist[i] &lt; INF) max_d = max(max_d, dist[i]);
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
            pi[i] += (dist[i] &lt; INF ? dist[i] : max_d);
        }
    }

    // Adds +1 unit of capacity to adj[u][idx] while maintaining c^pi &gt;= 0
    void unlock_one_unit(int u, int idx) {
        auto& e = adj[u][idx];
        int v = e.to;
        if (e.cap &gt; 0 || e.cost + pi[u] - pi[v] &gt;= 0) {
            e.cap += 1;
            return;
        }
        dijkstra(v);
        if (dist[u] &lt; INF && e.cost + pi[u] - pi[v] &lt; 0) {
            // Augment 1 unit around the negative cycle u -&gt; v ~&gt; u
            adj[v][e.rev].cap += 1;
            for (int cur = u; cur != v; cur = pv_v[cur]) {
                int p = pv_v[cur], ei = pv_e[cur];
                adj[p][ei].cap -= 1;
                adj[cur][adj[p][ei].rev].cap += 1;
            }
        } else {
            e.cap += 1;
        }
    }

    pair&lt;long long, long long&gt; solve_st(int s, int t) {
        long long max_cap = 0, sum_cap = 0, max_cost = 1;
        for (const auto& a : arcs) {
            max_cap = max(max_cap, a.full_cap);
            sum_cap += a.full_cap;
            max_cost = max(max_cost, abs(a.cost));
        }
        if (sum_cap == 0) return {0, 0};
        long long M_inf = 1LL * (n + 1) * max_cost + 1;
        int fb_id = add_arc(t, s, sum_cap, -M_inf);
        max_cap = max(max_cap, sum_cap);

        int B = 63 - __builtin_clzll(max_cap);
        for (int bit = B; bit &gt;= 0; --bit) {
            for (int u = 0; u &lt; n; ++u) {
                for (auto& e : adj[u]) e.cap &lt;&lt;= 1;
            }
            for (const auto& a : arcs) {
                if ((a.full_cap &gt;&gt; bit) & 1LL) {
                    unlock_one_unit(a.u, a.idx);
                }
            }
        }

        const auto& fb = arcs[fb_id];
        long long total_flow = adj[fb.v][adj[fb.u][fb.idx].rev].cap;
        long long total_cost = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)arcs.size() - 1; ++i) {
            const auto& a = arcs[i];
            long long f = adj[a.v][adj[a.u][a.idx].rev].cap;
            total_cost += f * a.cost;
        }
        return {total_flow, total_cost};
    }
};









NoteReference Implementation (#143B — AtCoder AGC 034D Manhattan Max Matching)





// Uses ConvexMCMF from Section 5.1
void solve_agc034d() {
    const long long INF = 1e18;
    int n;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n)) return;

    int S = 0, T = 2 * n + 5;
    int H0 = 2 * n + 1; // 4 Sign-Hubs: H0..H3
    ConvexMCMF mcmf(T + 1);

    const long long sx[4] = {1, 1, -1, -1};
    const long long sy[4] = {1, -1, 1, -1};

    vector&lt;long long&gt; min_hub(4, INF);

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        long long x, y;
        int c;
        cin &gt;&gt; x &gt;&gt; y &gt;&gt; c;
        mcmf.add_edge(S, i, c, 0);
        for (int b = 0; b &lt; 4; ++b) {
            long long w = -(sx[b] * x + sy[b] * y);
            mcmf.add_edge(i, H0 + b, c, w);
            min_hub[b] = min(min_hub[b], w);
        }
    }

    vector&lt;long long&gt; min_blue(n + 1, INF);
    long long min_T = INF;

    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= n; ++j) {
        long long x, y;
        int c;
        cin &gt;&gt; x &gt;&gt; y &gt;&gt; c;
        for (int b = 0; b &lt; 4; ++b) {
            long long w = sx[b] * x + sy[b] * y;
            mcmf.add_edge(H0 + b, n + j, c, w);
            min_blue[j] = min(min_blue[j], min_hub[b] + w);
        }
        mcmf.add_edge(n + j, T, c, 0);
        min_T = min(min_T, min_blue[j]);
    }

    // Exact 4-layer DAG potentials in O(N) without SPFA
    mcmf.pi[S] = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) mcmf.pi[i] = 0;
    for (int b = 0; b &lt; 4; ++b) mcmf.pi[H0 + b] = min_hub[b];
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= n; ++j) mcmf.pi[n + j] = min_blue[j];
    mcmf.pi[T] = min_T;

    cout &lt;&lt; -mcmf.solve(S, T).second &lt;&lt; "\n";
}







Problem #144 (Mas-2): Convex Slope-Function Parameterization (AtCoder Practice2 E — MinCostFlow & Luogu P2050 [NOI2012] 美食节)

Judge / URL: AtCoder Library Practice Contest E (MinCostFlow) & Luogu P2050 ([NOI2012] 美食节)
Constraints:

Part A (AtCoder Practice2 E — MinCostFlow): Given an \(N \times N\) non-negative grid \(A_{r,c} \in [0, 10^9]\) (\(N \le 50\)) and a cap \(K \le N\). Choose any number of cells (not fixed!) such that at most \(K\) cells are chosen in each row and at most \(K\) cells are chosen in each column, maximizing the sum of chosen \(A_{r,c}\), and output both the maximum sum and the \(N \times N\) selection grid.
Part B (Luogu P2050 [NOI2012] 美食节): \(N \le 40\) dishes (demands \(p_i\), \(\sum p_i = P \le 800\)), \(M \le 100\) chefs, cooking time matrix \(t_{i,j} \le 1000\). Minimize total waiting time across all \(P\) dishes via Algorithm 18.2 (动态加边).



4-Step Compilation

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal):

In AtCoder Practice2 E, the total number of chosen cells \(F \in [0, N K]\) is not fixed (if all \(A_{r,c} &gt; 0\), we want to push as much flow as possible, or we can add a zero-reward bypass edge \(\text{Row}_r \to T\) of capacity \(K\) and cost \(0\) so the total flow is fixed at \(N K\)!). Furthermore, by Theorem 17.5 (Convexity of the Minimum-Cost Flow Value Function \(C(F)\)), as flow \(F\) increases from \(0\) to \(N K\), the per-unit path cost \(\pi(T) - \pi(S)\) is monotonically non-decreasing (ACL’s mcf_graph::slope(s, t) returns the complete piecewise-linear convex hull of \((F, C(F))\)!).
In [NOI2012] 美食节, \(N \times M \times P = 3.2 \times 10^6\) edges is too dense to pre-allocate, requiring Algorithm 18.2 (Dynamic Lazy Edge Insertion).

Step 2 (Algebraic Model & Fixed-Hop Cost Shift for Practice2 E): Let \(M_{\max} = \max_{r,c} A_{r,c} = 10^9\). Every chosen cell \((r, c)\) traverses \(S \to \text{Row}_r \to \text{Col}_c \to T\) at cost \(M_{\max} - A_{r,c} \ge 0\), while an unchosen slot on \(\text{Row}_r\) traverses the bypass arc \(\text{Row}_r \to T\) at cost \(M_{\max} \ge 0\). Then every unit of the \(N K\) flow incurs a \(+M_{\max}\) baseline shift, so \(\text{MaxSum} = N K \cdot M_{\max} - \text{MinCost}(N K)\), and all edge costs are \(\ge 0\)!
Step 3 (Graph Construction for [NOI2012] 美食节): Start with only slot \((j, 1)\) for each chef \(j \in \{1, \dots, M\}\). After each unit augmentation \(S \to \text{Dish}_i \to (j_\star, k_\star) \to T\), immediately allocate vertex \((j_\star, k_\star + 1)\) with potential \(\pi(j_\star, k_\star + 1) = \pi(T)\), arc \((j_\star, k_\star + 1) \to T\) (\(\text{cap}=1, \text{cost}=0\)), and \(N\) arcs \(\text{Dish}_i \to (j_\star, k_\star + 1)\) (\(\text{cap}=1, \text{cost}=(k_\star + 1) t_{i, j_\star}\)).
Step 4 (Verification): Both runs maintain exact non-negative reduced costs at every step and finish in \(&lt; 30\text{ ms}\).







TipHint 1 (#144) — Turning Variable-Size Selection into Fixed-Flow Non-Negative MCMF (Practice2 E)





Instead of stopping when the shortest augmenting path cost becomes positive, shift every cell reward \(-A_{r,c}\) by \(+M_{\max}\) (\(M_{\max} = 10^9\)) to make all cell edges have cost \(M_{\max} - A_{r,c} \ge 0\), and add a direct bypass edge \(\text{Row}_r \to T\) of capacity \(K\) and cost \(M_{\max}\). Now you always push exactly \(N \cdot K\) units of flow!









TipHint 2 (#144) — Lazy Chef Slot Spawning ([NOI2012] 美食节)





When you augment \(1\) unit of flow from \(S\) to \(T\), look at pv_v[T]—that is the exact chef slot node \(u\) that was just used! Maintain chef_id[u] and tier_k[u] for each slot node so you can immediately spawn (chef_id[u], tier_k[u] + 1).









TipHint 3 (#144) — Keeping Johnson Potentials Valid When Spawning a New Vertex





When you dynamically add vertex \(v_{\text{new}} = (j_\star, k_\star + 1)\) during Primal-Dual Dijkstra, set pi[v_new] = pi[T]. Why does every new edge \(\text{Dish}_i \to v_{\text{new}}\) automatically have non-negative reduced cost? Because \(\pi(\text{Dish}_i) + k_\star t_{i, j_\star} \ge \pi(j_\star, k_\star) \ge \pi(T)\) held before the augmentation!









NoteReference Implementation (#144A — AtCoder Practice2 E MinCostFlow)





// Uses ConvexMCMF from Section 5.1
void solve_acl_practice2_e() {
    const long long BIG = 1e9;
    int n, K;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; K)) return;

    int S = 0, T = 2 * n + 1;
    ConvexMCMF mcmf(T + 1);

    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; edge_id(n, vector&lt;int&gt;(n));
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; n; ++r) {
        mcmf.add_edge(S, r + 1, K, 0);
        mcmf.add_edge(r + 1, T, K, BIG); // Bypass: leave slot unused at cost BIG
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; n; ++c) {
            long long a;
            cin &gt;&gt; a;
            edge_id[r][c] = mcmf.add_edge(r + 1, n + 1 + c, 1, BIG - a);
        }
    }
    for (int c = 0; c &lt; n; ++c) mcmf.add_edge(n + 1 + c, T, K, 0);

    long long min_cost = mcmf.solve(S, T).second;
    cout &lt;&lt; 1LL * n * K * BIG - min_cost &lt;&lt; "\n";

    for (int r = 0; r &lt; n; ++r) {
        string row(n, '.');
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; n; ++c) {
            if (mcmf.adj[r + 1][edge_id[r][c]].cap == 0) row[c] = 'X';
        }
        cout &lt;&lt; row &lt;&lt; "\n";
    }
}









NoteReference Implementation (#144B — Luogu P2050 [NOI2012] 美食节 via Dynamic Lazy Edge Insertion)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

const long long INF = 1e18;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; m)) return 0;

    vector&lt;int&gt; p(n + 1);
    int P = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        cin &gt;&gt; p[i];
        P += p[i];
    }

    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; t_cook(n + 1, vector&lt;long long&gt;(m + 1));
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        for (int j = 1; j &lt;= m; ++j) {
            cin &gt;&gt; t_cook[i][j];
        }
    }

    struct Edge {
        int to, rev, cap;
        long long cost;
    };

    int max_nodes = n + m + P + 5;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; adj(max_nodes);
    vector&lt;long long&gt; pi(max_nodes, 0), dist(max_nodes);
    vector&lt;int&gt; pv_v(max_nodes), pv_e(max_nodes);
    vector&lt;int&gt; chef_of(max_nodes, 0), tier_of(max_nodes, 0);

    auto add_edge = [&](int u, int v, int cap, long long cost) {
        adj[u].push_back({v, (int)adj[v].size(), cap, cost});
        adj[v].push_back({u, (int)adj[u].size() - 1, 0, -cost});
    };

    int S = 0, T = 1;
    int cur_nodes = 2 + n; // 2..n+1 are Dish nodes

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
        add_edge(S, 1 + i, p[i], 0);
    }

    auto spawn_slot = [&](int j, int k) {
        int u = cur_nodes++;
        chef_of[u] = j;
        tier_of[u] = k;
        pi[u] = pi[T]; // Preserves c^pi &gt;= 0 on all new arcs!
        add_edge(u, T, 1, 0);
        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
            add_edge(1 + i, u, 1, 1LL * k * t_cook[i][j]);
        }
    };

    // Initially spawn ONLY tier k = 1 for each chef j = 1..m
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= m; ++j) {
        spawn_slot(j, 1);
    }

    long long total_wait = 0;
    for (int step = 0; step &lt; P; ++step) {
        fill(dist.begin(), dist.begin() + cur_nodes, INF);
        priority_queue&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;, vector&lt;pair&lt;long long, int&gt;&gt;, greater&lt;&gt;&gt; pq;
        dist[S] = 0;
        pq.push({0, S});

        while (!pq.empty()) {
            auto [d, u] = pq.top();
            pq.pop();
            if (d != dist[u]) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)adj[u].size(); ++i) {
                const auto& e = adj[u][i];
                if (e.cap &gt; 0) {
                    long long nd = d + e.cost + pi[u] - pi[e.to];
                    if (nd &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = nd;
                        pv_v[e.to] = u;
                        pv_e[e.to] = i;
                        pq.push({nd, e.to});
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i &lt; cur_nodes; ++i) {
            if (dist[i] &lt; INF) pi[i] += dist[i];
        }

        for (int v = T; v != S; v = pv_v[v]) {
            auto& e = adj[pv_v[v]][pv_e[v]];
            e.cap -= 1;
            adj[v][e.rev].cap += 1;
        }
        total_wait += pi[T];

        // The vertex immediately preceding T on the augmenting path is the newly saturated chef slot!
        int used_slot = pv_v[T];
        int j = chef_of[used_slot];
        int k = tier_of[used_slot];
        if (k &lt; P) {
            spawn_slot(j, k + 1);
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; total_wait &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}

6. Worked Examples & Progressive 8-Problem Difficulty Ladder (#153–#160)
We now apply our five structural modules across the eight canonical contest problems of Chapter 20, progressing from Stepping Stones to Grandmaster Mastery.

6.1 Problem #153 (SS-1) — [Luogu P3171 / TJOI2013] 循环格 (Cyclic Torus Grid)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P3171
Bounds: \(R, C \le 15\) (\(N = R \times C \le 225\)).


Problem Statement
Given an \(R \times C\) grid on a torus (wrapping top-to-bottom and left-to-right) where each cell \((r, c)\) contains a direction arrow \(d_{r,c} \in \{\text{'U'}, \text{'D'}, \text{'L'}, \text{'R'}\}\), find the minimum number of cells whose arrows must be changed so that every cell in the grid has in-degree \(1\) and out-degree \(1\) (forming a vertex-disjoint directed cycle cover of the torus).


4-Step Compilation Summary

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): Each cell has out-degree \(1\), and we require every cell to also have in-degree \(1\) — i.e., the transition map \((r, c) \mapsto \text{next}(r, c)\) must be a bijection on the \(R \times C\) cells. Because \(R, C \le 15\), \(V = 2RC + 2 \le 452\) is tiny, and we want a minimum-cost permutation of grid cells where edges to the 4 torus neighbors cost \(0\) (if matching \(d_{r,c}\)) or \(1\) (if modified).
Step 2 (Algebraic Formulation): Let \(x_{u, v} \in \{0, 1\}\) for adjacent torus cells \(u, v\). Minimize \(\sum_{u} \sum_{v \in N(u)} \mathbf{1}[v \neq \text{dir}(u)] x_{u, v}\) subject to \(\sum_v x_{u, v} = 1\) (out-degree \(1\)) and \(\sum_u x_{u, v} = 1\) (in-degree \(1\)).
Step 3 (Graph Compilation): Split each cell \(u\) into \(u_{\text{out}}\) (left) and \(u_{\text{in}}\) (right).

\(S \to u_{\text{out}}\) with \((\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0)\) and \(u_{\text{in}} \to T\) with \((\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0)\).
For each of the 4 torus neighbors \(v\) of \(u\), add \(u_{\text{out}} \to v_{\text{in}}\) with \(\text{cap} = 1\) and \(\text{cost} = 0\) if \(v\) lies in direction \(d_u\), or \(\text{cost} = 1\) otherwise.

Step 4 (Bonus Efficiency Insight): Because all edge costs are in \(\{0, 1\}\) and all capacities are \(1\), any shortest augmenting path step in the residual network has edge weights in \(\{-1, 0, +1\}\), and with Johnson potentials all reduced costs are non-negative integers with path length \(\le RC\). Standard SSP runs in \(&lt; 5\text{ ms}\) for \(RC \le 225\).







TipHint 1 — Structural Recognition (Problem #153)





What does “every cell is visited in a cycle cover, and each cell steps to the next cell along its arrow” mean for the in-degree and out-degree of every cell \((r, c)\)?









TipHint 2 — Bipartite Split Formulation (Problem #153)





Every cell always has out-degree \(1\) (it points somewhere). For the arrows to partition the grid into disjoint cycles, every cell must also have in-degree \(1\). How do we model \(\text{out-deg}(u) = 1\) and \(\text{in-deg}(v) = 1\) simultaneously as a bipartite assignment?









TipHint 3 — Full Network Specification (Problem #153)





Split each cell \(u = (r, c)\) into \(u_{\text{out}}\) and \(u_{\text{in}}\). Connect \(S \to u_{\text{out}}\) \((1, 0)\) and \(u_{\text{in}} \to T\) \((1, 0)\). For each of the 4 torus neighbors \(v\) of \(u\), add edge \(u_{\text{out}} \to v_{\text{in}}\) with capacity \(1\) and cost \(0\) if \(v\) matches \(u\)’s original arrow, or cost \(1\) otherwise. The min-cost max-flow of value \(RC\) is the answer.









NoteComplete C++ Solution — Problem #153 ([TJOI2013] 循环格)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

struct MCMF {
    struct Edge { int to, rev, cap, cost; };
    int n;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; g;
    vector&lt;int&gt; dist, pv, pe;
    vector&lt;bool&gt; inq;

    MCMF(int n) : n(n), g(n), dist(n), pv(n), pe(n), inq(n) {}

    void add_edge(int u, int v, int cap, int cost) {
        g[u].push_back({v, (int)g[v].size(), cap, cost});
        g[v].push_back({u, (int)g[u].size() - 1, 0, -cost});
    }

    int min_cost_max_flow(int s, int t) {
        int total_cost = 0;
        const int INF = 1e9;
        while (true) {
            fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
            fill(inq.begin(), inq.end(), false);
            queue&lt;int&gt; q;
            dist[s] = 0;
            q.push(s);
            inq[s] = true;
            while (!q.empty()) {
                int u = q.front();
                q.pop();
                inq[u] = false;
                for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)g[u].size(); ++i) {
                    auto &e = g[u][i];
                    if (e.cap &gt; 0 && dist[u] + e.cost &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = dist[u] + e.cost;
                        pv[e.to] = u;
                        pe[e.to] = i;
                        if (!inq[e.to]) {
                            inq[e.to] = true;
                            q.push(e.to);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            if (dist[t] == INF) break;
            int push = INF;
            for (int v = t; v != s; v = pv[v])
                push = min(push, g[pv[v]][pe[v]].cap);
            for (int v = t; v != s; v = pv[v]) {
                auto &e = g[pv[v]][pe[v]];
                e.cap -= push;
                g[v][e.rev].cap += push;
            }
            total_cost += push * dist[t];
        }
        return total_cost;
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int R, C;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; R &gt;&gt; C)) return 0;
    vector&lt;string&gt; grid(R);
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; R; ++r) cin &gt;&gt; grid[r];

    int N = R * C;
    int S = 2 * N, T = 2 * N + 1;
    MCMF mcmf(2 * N + 2);

    const int dr[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
    const int dc[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};
    const char dir_ch[4] = {'U', 'D', 'L', 'R'};

    for (int r = 0; r &lt; R; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; C; ++c) {
            int u = r * C + c;
            mcmf.add_edge(S, u, 1, 0);
            mcmf.add_edge(N + u, T, 1, 0);
            for (int d = 0; d &lt; 4; ++d) {
                int nr = (r + dr[d] + R) % R;
                int nc = (c + dc[d] + C) % C;
                int v = nr * C + nc;
                int cost = (grid[r][c] == dir_ch[d]) ? 0 : 1;
                mcmf.add_edge(u, N + v, 1, cost);
            }
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; mcmf.min_cost_max_flow(S, T) &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.2 Problem #154 (SS-2) — [Luogu P2053 / SCOI2007] 修车 (Car Repair)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P2053
Bounds: \(M \le 9\) technicians, \(N \le 60\) cars, repair times \(t_{i, j} \le 1000\).


Problem Statement
\(N\) customers bring their cars to a repair shop with \(M\) technicians. Technician \(j\) takes \(t_{i, j}\) minutes to repair car \(i\). Each technician repairs their assigned cars sequentially one at a time. Minimize the average waiting + repair completion time across all \(N\) customers (accurate to \(2\) decimal places).


4-Step Compilation Summary

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): Minimizing \(\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N C_i\) is equivalent to minimizing total completion time \(\sum_{i=1}^N C_i\). A forward queue index \(q\) depends on the durations of the \(q - 1\) cars before it (coupled state!), whereas a reverse queue index \(r \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) (counting from the last car repaired by technician \(j\)) delays exactly \(r\) cars (itself plus the \(r - 1\) cars after it).
Step 2 (Algebraic Formulation): By Equation (2), placing car \(i\) into reverse slot \((j, r)\) contributes exactly \(r \cdot t_{i, j}\) to \(\sum C_i\). Let \(x_{i, j, r} \in \{0, 1\}\). Minimize \(\sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{j=1}^M \sum_{r=1}^N (r \cdot t_{i, j}) x_{i, j, r}\) subject to \(\sum_{j, r} x_{i, j, r} = 1\) for each car \(i\), and \(\sum_i x_{i, j, r} \le 1\) for each slot \((j, r)\).
Step 3 (Graph Compilation):

Left nodes: \(N\) cars \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\), with \(S \to i\) \((\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0)\).
Right nodes: \(M \cdot N\) reverse slots \((j, r)\), with \((j, r) \to T\) \((\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = 0)\).
Bipartite edges: \(i \to (j, r)\) with \(\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = r \cdot t_{i, j}\).

Step 4 (Scale Verification): With \(N \le 60, M \le 9\), \(M \cdot N \le 540\) slots and \(N \cdot M \cdot N \le 32{,}400\) edges fit easily in static memory without needing lazy node spawning (which we will deploy in #156).







TipHint 1 — Breaking Forward-Queue Coupling (Problem #154)





If car \(i\) is the \(q\)-th car repaired by technician \(j\), its completion time depends on the repair times of the \(q - 1\) cars before it. What if instead you count its position \(r \in \{1, \dots, k_j\}\) from the back of technician \(j\)’s queue (\(r = 1\) is the last car repaired)?









TipHint 2 — Contribution Counting (Problem #154)





If car \(i\) is the \(r\)-th from last car repaired by technician \(j\), how many cars in technician \(j\)’s queue have to wait for car \(i\)’s repair to finish? Exactly \(r\) cars (car \(i\) itself, plus the \(r - 1\) cars behind it)! So car \(i\)’s standalone contribution to the total waiting time is \(r \cdot t_{i, j}\).









TipHint 3 — Bipartite Assignment Graph (Problem #154)





Create \(N\) car vertices on the left and \(M \times N\) slot vertices \((j, r)\) on the right (\(1 \le j \le M, 1 \le r \le N\)). Connect car \(i\) to slot \((j, r)\) with capacity \(1\) and cost \(r \cdot t_{i, j}\). Run MCMF and divide the minimum cost by \(N\).









NoteComplete C++ Solution — Problem #154 ([SCOI2007] 修车)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

struct MCMF {
    struct Edge { int to, rev, cap; long long cost; };
    int n;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; g;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist;
    vector&lt;int&gt; pv, pe;
    vector&lt;bool&gt; inq;

    MCMF(int n) : n(n), g(n), dist(n), pv(n), pe(n), inq(n) {}

    void add_edge(int u, int v, int cap, long long cost) {
        g[u].push_back({v, (int)g[v].size(), cap, cost});
        g[v].push_back({u, (int)g[u].size() - 1, 0, -cost});
    }

    long long min_cost_max_flow(int s, int t) {
        long long total_cost = 0;
        const long long INF = 1e18;
        while (true) {
            fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
            fill(inq.begin(), inq.end(), false);
            queue&lt;int&gt; q;
            dist[s] = 0;
            q.push(s);
            inq[s] = true;
            while (!q.empty()) {
                int u = q.front();
                q.pop();
                inq[u] = false;
                for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)g[u].size(); ++i) {
                    auto &e = g[u][i];
                    if (e.cap &gt; 0 && dist[u] + e.cost &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = dist[u] + e.cost;
                        pv[e.to] = u;
                        pe[e.to] = i;
                        if (!inq[e.to]) {
                            inq[e.to] = true;
                            q.push(e.to);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            if (dist[t] == INF) break;
            int push = 1e9;
            for (int v = t; v != s; v = pv[v])
                push = min(push, g[pv[v]][pe[v]].cap);
            for (int v = t; v != s; v = pv[v]) {
                auto &e = g[pv[v]][pe[v]];
                e.cap -= push;
                g[v][e.rev].cap += push;
            }
            total_cost += 1LL * push * dist[t];
        }
        return total_cost;
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int M, N;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; M &gt;&gt; N)) return 0;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; t(N, vector&lt;int&gt;(M));
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; ++i)
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; M; ++j)
            cin &gt;&gt; t[i][j];

    int S = N + M * N, T = S + 1;
    MCMF mcmf(T + 1);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; ++i) {
        mcmf.add_edge(S, i, 1, 0);
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; M; ++j) {
            for (int r = 1; r &lt;= N; ++r) {
                int slot = N + j * N + (r - 1);
                mcmf.add_edge(i, slot, 1, 1LL * r * t[i][j]);
            }
        }
    }
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; M; ++j)
        for (int r = 1; r &lt;= N; ++r)
            mcmf.add_edge(N + j * N + (r - 1), T, 1, 0);

    long long min_cost = mcmf.min_cost_max_flow(S, T);
    cout &lt;&lt; fixed &lt;&lt; setprecision(2) &lt;&lt; (double)min_cost / N &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.3 Problem #155 (Int-1) — [Luogu P1251 / 网络流24题] 餐巾计划问题 (Napkin Plan)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P1251 (and Large-Scale Direction B: Luogu P4480 [BJWC2018] 餐巾计划问题)
Bounds: \(N \le 2000\) days, \(r_t \le 10^7\), costs \(\le 10^4\).


Problem Statement
A restaurant requires \(r_t\) clean napkins on day \(t \in \{1, \dots, N\}\). Each day, it can buy new napkins at cost \(p\) each; after day \(t\)’s meal, the \(r_t\) used (dirty) napkins can either be held in dirty storage for the next day, sent to a fast laundry taking \(m_1\) days at cost \(c_1\) each, or sent to a slow laundry taking \(m_2\) days at cost \(c_2\) each. Find the minimum total cost to meet all \(N\) days of demand.


4-Step Compilation Summary

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): Used napkins are not consumed — they transition from clean to dirty, wait in storage or laundry, and re-emerge as clean napkins \(m_1\) or \(m_2\) days later. As proved in FM-20a, a single node per day cannot simultaneously enforce exact consumption \(r_t\) and re-inject those \(r_t\) dirty napkins into the timeline without destroying clean-napkin inventory.
Step 2 (Algebraic Formulation): Decouple dirty supply \(X_t\) (injected from \(S\), cap \(r_t\)) and clean demand \(Y_t\) (drained to \(T\), cap \(r_t\)):

Dirty conservation at \(X_t\): \(r_t + h_{t-1} = h_t + f_t + s_t\).
Clean conservation at \(Y_t\): \(b_t + f_{t-m_1} + s_{t-m_2} = r_t\).

Step 3 (Graph Compilation):

\(S \to X_t\) \((r_t, 0)\) and \(Y_t \to T\) \((r_t, 0)\) for \(1 \le t \le N\).
Buy new: \(S \to Y_t\) \((\infty, p)\).
Dirty holdover: \(X_t \to X_{t+1}\) \((\infty, 0)\) for \(t &lt; N\).
Fast laundry: \(X_t \to Y_{t+m_1}\) \((\infty, c_1)\) for \(t + m_1 \le N\).
Slow laundry: \(X_t \to Y_{t+m_2}\) \((\infty, c_2)\) for \(t + m_2 \le N\).








NoteBridge to Chapter 22 (Direction B): \([BJWC2018]\) 餐巾计划问题 (\(N \le 3 \times 10^5\)) via Ternary Search + Monotone Deque



When \(N \le 3 \times 10^5\) (Luogu P4480), explicit MCMF is too slow, so we simulate the flow directly: 1. Ensure \(p &gt; c_1 &gt; c_2\) and \(m_1 &lt; m_2\) (removing dominated options). 2. As a function of the total number of newly purchased napkins \(K \in [\max_{T \le m_1} \sum_{t=1}^T r_t,\; \sum_{t=1}^N r_t]\), the minimum laundry cost \(g(K)\) is discrete convex (since it is the parametric min-cost flow cost as the capacity on the \(S \to Y_1\) purchase edge varies). 3. For a fixed \(K\) (bought on day \(1\) since clean holdover is free), we simulate day by day \(t = 1 \dots N\) using a doubly-ended queue of dirty napkins indexed by creation day: - First use any remaining initial stock from \(K\). - Next, greedily launder the oldest available dirty napkins from days \(\le t - m_2\) via slow laundry at cost \(c_2\) (FIFO from the front of the queue, saving newer dirty napkins that are not yet old enough for slow laundry!). - Finally, if demand remains, launder the newest available dirty napkins from days \(\le t - m_1\) via fast laundry at cost \(c_1\) (LIFO from the back of the eligible window, leaving older dirty napkins to mature into slow-laundry eligibility on future days!). Ternary search over \(K\) with this \(O(N)\) deque check solves \(N = 3 \times 10^5\) in \(O(N \log(\sum r_t))\) time!








TipHint 1 — Why Single-Node Per Day Fails (Problem #155)





If you represent day \(t\) by a single vertex and drain \(r_t\) units to the sink \(T\), those \(r_t\) units disappear from the network and cannot be sent to laundry for days \(t + m_1\) or \(t + m_2\). How do we separate “clean napkins consumed on day \(t\)” from “dirty napkins produced on day \(t\)”?









TipHint 2 — Dirty Supply vs. Clean Demand Split (Problem #155)





Since we know every day \(t\)’s demand of \(r_t\) clean napkins will be met in a valid max-flow, we know beforehand that exactly \(r_t\) dirty napkins are generated at the end of day \(t\). Create a dirty supply node \(X_t\) receiving \(r_t\) units from \(S\), and a clean demand node \(Y_t\) sending \(r_t\) units to \(T\).









TipHint 3 — Edge Catalogue & long long Capacities (Problem #155)





Connect \(S \to X_t\) \((r_t, 0)\), \(Y_t \to T\) \((r_t, 0)\), \(S \to Y_t\) \((\infty, p)\), \(X_t \to X_{t+1}\) \((\infty, 0)\), \(X_t \to Y_{t+m_1}\) \((\infty, c_1)\), and \(X_t \to Y_{t+m_2}\) \((\infty, c_2)\). Watch out: \(r_t \le 10^7\) and \(N \le 2000\), so both flow capacities and total cost must use long long!









NoteComplete C++ Solution — Problem #155 ([网络流24题] 餐巾计划问题)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

struct MCMF {
    struct Edge { int to, rev; long long cap, cost; };
    int n;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; g;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist;
    vector&lt;int&gt; pv, pe;
    vector&lt;bool&gt; inq;

    MCMF(int n) : n(n), g(n), dist(n), pv(n), pe(n), inq(n) {}

    void add_edge(int u, int v, long long cap, long long cost) {
        g[u].push_back({v, (int)g[v].size(), cap, cost});
        g[v].push_back({u, (int)g[u].size() - 1, 0, -cost});
    }

    long long min_cost_max_flow(int s, int t) {
        long long total_cost = 0;
        const long long INF = 4e18;
        while (true) {
            fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
            fill(inq.begin(), inq.end(), false);
            queue&lt;int&gt; q;
            dist[s] = 0;
            q.push(s);
            inq[s] = true;
            while (!q.empty()) {
                int u = q.front();
                q.pop();
                inq[u] = false;
                for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)g[u].size(); ++i) {
                    auto &e = g[u][i];
                    if (e.cap &gt; 0 && dist[u] + e.cost &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = dist[u] + e.cost;
                        pv[e.to] = u;
                        pe[e.to] = i;
                        if (!inq[e.to]) {
                            inq[e.to] = true;
                            q.push(e.to);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            if (dist[t] == INF) break;
            long long push = INF;
            for (int v = t; v != s; v = pv[v])
                push = min(push, g[pv[v]][pe[v]].cap);
            for (int v = t; v != s; v = pv[v]) {
                auto &e = g[pv[v]][pe[v]];
                e.cap -= push;
                g[v][e.rev].cap += push;
            }
            total_cost += push * dist[t];
        }
        return total_cost;
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int N;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; N)) return 0;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; r(N + 1);
    for (int t = 1; t &lt;= N; ++t) cin &gt;&gt; r[t];
    long long p, m1, c1, m2, c2;
    cin &gt;&gt; p &gt;&gt; m1 &gt;&gt; c1 &gt;&gt; m2 &gt;&gt; c2;

    int S = 0, T = 2 * N + 1;
    const long long INF = 1e16;
    MCMF mcmf(T + 1);

    for (int t = 1; t &lt;= N; ++t) {
        int X_t = t;         // Dirty supply node on day t
        int Y_t = N + t;     // Clean demand node on day t
        mcmf.add_edge(S, X_t, r[t], 0);
        mcmf.add_edge(Y_t, T, r[t], 0);
        mcmf.add_edge(S, Y_t, INF, p);
        if (t + 1 &lt;= N) mcmf.add_edge(X_t, t + 1, INF, 0);
        if (t + m1 &lt;= N) mcmf.add_edge(X_t, N + t + m1, INF, c1);
        if (t + m2 &lt;= N) mcmf.add_edge(X_t, N + t + m2, INF, c2);
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; mcmf.min_cost_max_flow(S, T) &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.4 Problem #156 (Int-2) — [Luogu P2050 / NOI2012] 美食节 (Food Festival)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P2050
Bounds: \(n \le 40\) dish types, \(m \le 100\) chefs, total dishes \(P = \sum_{i=1}^n p_i \le 800\), \(t_{i, j} \le 1000\).


Problem Statement
At a food festival, \(n\) types of dishes are ordered, with \(p_i\) orders of dish type \(i\) (total orders \(P = \sum_{i=1}^n p_i \le 800\)). There are \(m\) chefs; chef \(j\) takes \(t_{i, j}\) time to cook one dish of type \(i\). Each chef cooks their assigned dishes one by one. Minimize the sum of waiting times of all \(P\) dish orders.


4-Step Compilation Summary

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): Identical objective to #154 ([SCOI2007] 修车), except dishes of type \(i\) are bundled with multiplicity \(p_i\) (\(S \to \text{Dish}_i\) has capacity \(p_i\)) and total dishes \(P \le 800\) with \(m \le 100\) makes the static graph (\(n \cdot m \cdot P = 3.2 \times 10^6\) edges) 40x too large for 800 SSP augmentations.
Step 2 (Algebraic Formulation): By Theorem 4.1 (Frontier Slot Dominance), for each chef \(j\), the first unallocated reverse slot \((j, r_j)\) strictly dominates all higher slots \((j, r)\) with \(r &gt; r_j\).
Step 3 (Dynamic Lazy Compilation):

Initially create only \(r = 1\) slots \((j, 1)\) for each chef \(j \in \{1, \dots, m\}\).
Run strictly 1-unit SSP augmentation per step (push = 1).
Identify the chef slot \(u_{\text{slot}} = \text{pv}[T]\) that just became saturated on the edge \(u_{\text{slot}} \to T\). If it belongs to chef \(j^*\) at rank \(r^* &lt; P\), allocate a new vertex for \((j^*, r^* + 1)\), add \((j^*, r^* + 1) \to T\) \((1, 0)\), and add \(\text{Dish}_i \to (j^*, r^* + 1)\) \((1, (r^* + 1) t_{i, j^*})\) for \(i = 1 \dots n\).

Step 4 (Complexity Check): Total vertices spawned \(= n + m + P + 2 \le 942\); total edges ever added \(= (m + P)n + m + P \le 36{,}900\). Runs in \(&lt; 80\text{ ms}\)!







TipHint 1 — Why Static Graph Construction TLEs (Problem #156)





With \(n = 40, m = 100, P = 800\), how many edges would the static Car Repair graph from #154 have? \(40 \times 100 \times 800 = 3.2 \times 10^6\) edges! Over \(P = 800\) augmentations, scanning \(3.2 \times 10^6\) edges per SPFA pass takes \(\sim 2.5 \times 10^9\) operations.









TipHint 2 — Frontier Slot Dominance (Problem #156)





Notice that for any chef \(j\) and any dish \(i\), slot \((j, r+1)\) costs \((r+1) t_{i, j} &gt; r \cdot t_{i, j}\). Could a shortest augmenting path ever use slot \((j, r+1)\) while slot \((j, r)\) is still empty? Never! And since each augmentation saturates at most one sink slot, how many slots across all \(m\) chefs can ever be used after \(P\) augmentations?









TipHint 3 — On-Demand Spawning Protocol (Problem #156)





Start the graph with only slot \((j, 1)\) for each of the \(m\) chefs. In each step of SSP, push \(1\) unit of flow to \(T\). Check which slot vertex \(u = \text{pv}[T]\) was just used to reach \(T\): if \(u\) belongs to chef \(j^*\) at rank \(r^*\), dynamically append a new vertex for \((j^*, r^* + 1)\) and its \(n + 1\) incident edges!









NoteComplete C++ Solution — Problem #156 ([NOI2012] 美食节)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

struct Edge {
    int to, rev, cap;
    long long cost;
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, m;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; m)) return 0;
    vector&lt;int&gt; p(n);
    int P = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        cin &gt;&gt; p[i];
        P += p[i];
    }
    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; t(n, vector&lt;int&gt;(m));
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i)
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; m; ++j)
            cin &gt;&gt; t[i][j];

    int max_nodes = n + m + P + 5;
    int S = 0, T = 1;
    int next_node = 2 + n;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; g(max_nodes);
    vector&lt;int&gt; slot_chef(max_nodes, -1), slot_rank(max_nodes, 0);

    auto add_edge = [&](int u, int v, int cap, long long cost) {
        g[u].push_back({v, (int)g[v].size(), cap, cost});
        g[v].push_back({u, (int)g[u].size() - 1, 0, -cost});
    };

    auto spawn_slot = [&](int j, int r) {
        int u = next_node++;
        slot_chef[u] = j;
        slot_rank[u] = r;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
            add_edge(2 + i, u, 1, 1LL * r * t[i][j]);
        }
        add_edge(u, T, 1, 0);
    };

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        add_edge(S, 2 + i, p[i], 0);
    }
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; m; ++j) {
        spawn_slot(j, 1);
    }

    const long long INF = 4e18;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist(max_nodes);
    vector&lt;int&gt; pv(max_nodes), pe(max_nodes);
    vector&lt;bool&gt; inq(max_nodes);
    long long total_cost = 0;

    for (int step = 0; step &lt; P; ++step) {
        fill(dist.begin(), dist.begin() + next_node, INF);
        fill(inq.begin(), inq.begin() + next_node, false);
        queue&lt;int&gt; q;
        dist[S] = 0;
        q.push(S);
        inq[S] = true;
        while (!q.empty()) {
            int u = q.front();
            q.pop();
            inq[u] = false;
            for (int idx = 0; idx &lt; (int)g[u].size(); ++idx) {
                auto &e = g[u][idx];
                if (e.cap &gt; 0 && dist[u] + e.cost &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                    dist[e.to] = dist[u] + e.cost;
                    pv[e.to] = u;
                    pe[e.to] = idx;
                    if (!inq[e.to]) {
                        inq[e.to] = true;
                        q.push(e.to);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        // Push strictly 1 unit along the shortest augmenting path
        for (int v = T; v != S; v = pv[v]) {
            auto &e = g[pv[v]][pe[v]];
            e.cap -= 1;
            g[v][e.rev].cap += 1;
        }
        total_cost += dist[T];

        // Identify the chef slot that just saturated its edge to T
        int used_slot = pv[T];
        int j = slot_chef[used_slot];
        int r = slot_rank[used_slot];
        if (r + 1 &lt;= P) {
            spawn_slot(j, r + 1);
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; total_cost &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.5 Problem #157 (Adv-1) — [Luogu P4542 / ZJOI2011] 营救皮卡丘 (Rescuing Pikachu)

Judge URL: https://www.luogu.com.cn/problem/P4542 (and Unbounded-Fleet Direct-Jump Sibling: Luogu P2469 [SDOI2010] 星际竞速)
Bounds: \(N \le 150\) bases (indexed \(0, 1, \dots, N\)), \(M \le 20{,}000\) undirected weighted roads, \(K \le 10\) rescue teams.


Problem Statement
You start at base \(0\) with \(K\) rescue teams and must unlock bases \(1, 2, \dots, N\) in order. A team can travel along roads to unlock base \(j\) only after every intermediate base \(v\) along its road path with \(v &lt; j\) has already been unlocked; in particular, when moving from a previously unlocked base \(i &lt; j\) to unlock \(j\), a team may only pass through intermediate vertices \(k \le \max(i, j) = j\). Minimize the total road distance traveled by all \(K\) teams to unlock all bases \(1, \dots, N\).


4-Step Compilation Summary

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): Every base \(j \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) is unlocked by some team arriving from an already-unlocked base \(i \in \{0, 1, \dots, j-1\}\). The restriction “all bases \(&lt; j\) are already unlocked when unlocking \(j\)” means the shortest valid road path from \(i\) to \(j\) can use any intermediate vertex \(k \in \{0, 1, \dots, j\}\). Once those pairwise shortest distances \(D[i][j]\) (\(0 \le i &lt; j \le N\)) are precomputed, the problem reduces to covering DAG vertices \(\{1, \dots, N\}\) with at most \(K\) directed paths starting at \(0\).
Step 2 (Two-Stage Decomposition):

Stage 1 (Restricted Floyd–Warshall): Run Floyd–Warshall with outer pivot \(k = 0, 1, \dots, N\). Record \(D[i][j]\) (\(i &lt; j\)) immediately after pivot \(k = j\) completes (so only intermediate nodes \(\le j\) are used!).
Stage 3 (Bipartite Split for \(K\)-Team Path Cover): Split \(u \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) into \(u_{\text{out}}\) and \(u_{\text{in}}\), plus base \(0_{\text{out}}\) of supply \(K\): \[\min \sum_{0 \le i &lt; j \le N} D[i][j] x_{i, j} \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \sum_{j=1}^N x_{0, j} \le K, \quad \sum_{i=0}^{j-1} x_{i, j} = 1 \;\; (\forall j \ge 1), \quad \sum_{j=i+1}^N x_{i, j} \le 1 \;\; (\forall i \ge 1).\]

Step 3 (Graph Compilation):

\(S \to 0_{\text{out}}\) \((K, 0)\); for \(1 \le i \le N\), \(S \to i_{\text{out}}\) \((1, 0)\) and \(i_{\text{in}} \to T\) \((1, 0)\).
For \(0 \le i &lt; j \le N\) with \(D[i][j] &lt; \infty\), add \(i_{\text{out}} \to j_{\text{in}}\) \((1, D[i][j])\).
Find the minimum-cost flow that saturates all \(N\) sink edges \(j_{\text{in}} \to T\) (total flow \(= N\); notice \(S \to 0_{\text{out}} \cup \{i_{\text{out}}\}\) has total capacity \(K + N\), and any unused capacity simply stays at \(S\), which corresponds to paths terminating or \(&lt; K\) teams being used!).








TipHint 1 — Decoupling the Road Graph from the Team Routing (Problem #157)





When a team travels from an already-unlocked base \(i &lt; j\) to unlock base \(j\), which bases are already unlocked at that moment? Bases \(0, 1, \dots, j-1\)! So the team can pass through any intermediate vertex \(k \le j\). How can you compute the shortest path \(D[i][j]\) between all pairs \(0 \le i &lt; j \le N\) using only intermediate vertices \(\le j\)?









TipHint 2 — Restricted Floyd–Warshall + Path Cover (Problem #157)





In standard Floyd–Warshall, the outer loop iterates over \(k = 0, 1, \dots, N\). At the exact moment when outer loop iteration \(k\) finishes, dist[i][j] is the shortest path using only intermediate vertices in \(\{0, \dots, k\}\). Thus for \(i &lt; j\), \(D[i][j]\) is simply dist[i][j] after outer step \(k = j\)! Once \(D[i][j]\) is known, how do we cover vertices \(\{1, \dots, N\}\) with \(\le K\) paths starting at \(0\)?









TipHint 3 — Bipartite Supply–Demand Network (Problem #157)





Create \(0_{\text{out}}\) with edge \(S \to 0_{\text{out}}\) \((\text{cap} = K, \text{cost} = 0)\). For each \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\), create \(i_{\text{out}}\) with \(S \to i_{\text{out}}\) \((1, 0)\) and \(i_{\text{in}}\) with \(i_{\text{in}} \to T\) \((1, 0)\). For all \(0 \le i &lt; j \le N\), add \(i_{\text{out}} \to j_{\text{in}}\) \((1, D[i][j])\). The min-cost max-flow (of flow value \(N\)) is the answer!









NoteComplete C++ Solution — Problem #157 ([ZJOI2011] 营救皮卡丘)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

struct MCMF {
    struct Edge { int to, rev, cap; long long cost; };
    int n;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; g;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist;
    vector&lt;int&gt; pv, pe;
    vector&lt;bool&gt; inq;

    MCMF(int n) : n(n), g(n), dist(n), pv(n), pe(n), inq(n) {}

    void add_edge(int u, int v, int cap, long long cost) {
        g[u].push_back({v, (int)g[v].size(), cap, cost});
        g[v].push_back({u, (int)g[u].size() - 1, 0, -cost});
    }

    long long min_cost_max_flow(int s, int t) {
        long long total_cost = 0;
        const long long INF = 4e18;
        while (true) {
            fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
            fill(inq.begin(), inq.end(), false);
            queue&lt;int&gt; q;
            dist[s] = 0;
            q.push(s);
            inq[s] = true;
            while (!q.empty()) {
                int u = q.front();
                q.pop();
                inq[u] = false;
                for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)g[u].size(); ++i) {
                    auto &e = g[u][i];
                    if (e.cap &gt; 0 && dist[u] + e.cost &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = dist[u] + e.cost;
                        pv[e.to] = u;
                        pe[e.to] = i;
                        if (!inq[e.to]) {
                            inq[e.to] = true;
                            q.push(e.to);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            if (dist[t] == INF) break;
            int push = 1e9;
            for (int v = t; v != s; v = pv[v])
                push = min(push, g[pv[v]][pe[v]].cap);
            for (int v = t; v != s; v = pv[v]) {
                auto &e = g[pv[v]][pe[v]];
                e.cap -= push;
                g[v][e.rev].cap += push;
            }
            total_cost += 1LL * push * dist[t];
        }
        return total_cost;
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int N, M, K;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; N &gt;&gt; M &gt;&gt; K)) return 0;

    const long long INF = 1e15;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; fw(N + 1, vector&lt;long long&gt;(N + 1, INF));
    vector&lt;vector&lt;long long&gt;&gt; D(N + 1, vector&lt;long long&gt;(N + 1, INF));
    for (int i = 0; i &lt;= N; ++i) fw[i][i] = 0;

    for (int e = 0; e &lt; M; ++e) {
        int u, v;
        long long w;
        cin &gt;&gt; u &gt;&gt; v &gt;&gt; w;
        fw[u][v] = min(fw[u][v], w);
        fw[v][u] = min(fw[v][u], w);
    }

    // Restricted Floyd-Warshall: record D[i][j] (i &lt; j) after pivot k &lt;= j
    for (int k = 0; k &lt;= N; ++k) {
        for (int i = 0; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
            for (int j = 0; j &lt;= N; ++j) {
                fw[i][j] = min(fw[i][j], fw[i][k] + fw[k][j]);
                if (k &gt;= max(i, j)) {
                    D[i][j] = min(D[i][j], fw[i][j]);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // Nodes: 0..N are i_out, N+1..2N are j_in, S = 2N+1, T = 2N+2
    int S = 2 * N + 1, T = 2 * N + 2;
    MCMF mcmf(T + 1);
    mcmf.add_edge(S, 0, K, 0);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        mcmf.add_edge(S, i, 1, 0);
        mcmf.add_edge(N + i, T, 1, 0);
    }
    for (int i = 0; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j &lt;= N; ++j) {
            if (D[i][j] &lt; INF) {
                mcmf.add_edge(i, N + j, 1, D[i][j]);
            }
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; mcmf.min_cost_max_flow(S, T) &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.6 Problem #158 (Adv-2) — [Codeforces 802 C] Heidi and Library (hard)

Judge URL: https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/802/C
Bounds: \(n \le 80\) requests, cache capacity \(k \le 80\), book costs \(c_i \le 10^6\).


Problem Statement
On day \(t \in \{1, \dots, n\}\), book \(a_t\) is requested. The library holds at most \(k\) books at a time. If \(a_t\) is not currently in the library, Heidi must buy it for cost \(c_{a_t}\) (first discarding an existing book if the library already has \(k\) books). Minimize the total purchase cost over all \(n\) days.


4-Step Compilation Summary

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): Offline Bélády caching fails when book purchase costs \(c_b\) are heterogeneous and we must jointly choose which \(\le k\) books occupy the cache across time. Because \(n, k \le 80\), we can compile the cache into an \(O(n)\)-vertex flow network using either Compilation A (Split-Request Token Routing) or Compilation B (Timeline Saving Chords) from Section 3.2.
Step 2 (Algebraic Formulation — Split-Request Token Routing): Split day \(t\) into \(t_{\text{in}}\) and \(t_{\text{out}}\) with required internal edge \(t_{\text{in}} \to t_{\text{out}}\) (implemented by supply/demand splitting or a large negative bonus \(-M\)). A token leaving \(t_{\text{out}}\) can either be preserved as the same book until its next request \(\text{next}(t)\) at cost \(0\), or overwritten by a newly purchased book on day \(t + 1\) at cost \(c_{a_{t+1}}\).
Step 3 (Clean Supply–Demand Split Implementation): Rather than using a large \(-M\) bonus, we can also use our exact Bipartite Supply–Demand Split directly:

Every day \(t \in \{1, \dots, n\}\) must be satisfied (supply \(S \to t_{\text{out}}\) \((1, 0)\), demand \(t_{\text{in}} \to T\) \((1, 0)\)).
How is request \(t_{\text{in}}\) satisfied? Either by buying book \(a_t\) on day \(t\) (\(S \to t_{\text{in}}\) or via the idle pool at cost \(c_{a_t}\)), OR by carrying book \(a_t\) directly from its previous occurrence \(p = \text{prev}(t)\) at cost \(0\) — provided the cache capacity \(k\) is respected!
Even simpler: in the Timeline Saving Chord model (Section 3.2 Compilation B), start with base cost \(\sum_{t=1}^n c_{a_t}\), run a backbone \(1 \to 2 \to \dots \to n\) of capacity \(k - 1\) (since \(1\) slot is occupied by today’s requested book \(a_t\)), and for each consecutive occurrence pair \((p, t)\) of book \(a_t\):

If \(t = p + 1\), subtract \(c_{a_t}\) immediately (occupies \(0\) extra cache slots between \(p\) and \(p+1\)).
If \(t &gt; p + 1\), add chord \((p + 1) \to t\) with \(\text{cap} = 1, \text{cost} = -c_{a_t}\) (occupying \(1\) of the \(k - 1\) background cache slots during days \(p + 1, \dots, t - 1\) to save \(c_{a_t}\)!).









TipHint 1 — Base Cost minus Cache Savings (Problem #158)





Suppose you bought a fresh copy of \(a_t\) on every single day \(t\): you would pay \(\sum_{t=1}^n c_{a_t}\). When can you save the purchase cost \(c_{a_t}\) on day \(t\)? Precisely when you kept book \(a_t\) in the cache from its previous request day \(p = \text{prev}(t)\) all the way to day \(t\)!









TipHint 2 — Counting Background Cache Slots (Problem #158)





On any day \(d\), today’s requested book \(a_d\) must be in the library, leaving \(k - 1\) background slots for other books being held across day \(d\). If you keep book \(a_t\) from day \(p\) to day \(t\), which intermediate days \(d\) require a background slot for \(a_t\)? Exactly days \(d \in \{p + 1, \dots, t - 1\}\)!









TipHint 3 — Interval Packing Network (Problem #158)





Build a timeline \(1 \to 2 \to \dots \to n\) where edge \(d \to d+1\) has capacity \(k - 1\) and cost \(0\). Connect \(S \to 1\) \((k - 1, 0)\) and \(n \to T\) \((k - 1, 0)\). For each request \(t\) with previous occurrence \(p = \text{prev}(t)\): if \(t = p + 1\), subtract \(c_{a_t}\) from the base cost directly; if \(t &gt; p + 1\), add edge \((p + 1) \to t\) with capacity \(1\) and cost \(-c_{a_t}\). Run MCMF and add the (negative) min-cost to the base cost!









NoteComplete C++ Solution — Problem #158 (CF 802 C: Heidi and Library hard)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

struct MCMF {
    struct Edge { int to, rev, cap; long long cost; };
    int n;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; g;
    vector&lt;long long&gt; dist;
    vector&lt;int&gt; pv, pe;
    vector&lt;bool&gt; inq;

    MCMF(int n) : n(n), g(n), dist(n), pv(n), pe(n), inq(n) {}

    void add_edge(int u, int v, int cap, long long cost) {
        g[u].push_back({v, (int)g[v].size(), cap, cost});
        g[v].push_back({u, (int)g[u].size() - 1, 0, -cost});
    }

    long long min_cost_max_flow(int s, int t) {
        long long total_cost = 0;
        const long long INF = 4e18;
        while (true) {
            fill(dist.begin(), dist.end(), INF);
            fill(inq.begin(), inq.end(), false);
            queue&lt;int&gt; q;
            dist[s] = 0;
            q.push(s);
            inq[s] = true;
            while (!q.empty()) {
                int u = q.front();
                q.pop();
                inq[u] = false;
                for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)g[u].size(); ++i) {
                    auto &e = g[u][i];
                    if (e.cap &gt; 0 && dist[u] + e.cost &lt; dist[e.to]) {
                        dist[e.to] = dist[u] + e.cost;
                        pv[e.to] = u;
                        pe[e.to] = i;
                        if (!inq[e.to]) {
                            inq[e.to] = true;
                            q.push(e.to);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            if (dist[t] &gt;= 0) break; // Stop as soon as no negative-cost saving path remains
            int push = 1e9;
            for (int v = t; v != s; v = pv[v])
                push = min(push, g[pv[v]][pe[v]].cap);
            for (int v = t; v != s; v = pv[v]) {
                auto &e = g[pv[v]][pe[v]];
                e.cap -= push;
                g[v][e.rev].cap += push;
            }
            total_cost += 1LL * push * dist[t];
        }
        return total_cost;
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, k;
    if (!(cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; k)) return 0;
    vector&lt;int&gt; a(n + 1);
    for (int t = 1; t &lt;= n; ++t) cin &gt;&gt; a[t];
    vector&lt;long long&gt; c(n + 1);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) cin &gt;&gt; c[i];

    int S = 0, T = n + 1;
    MCMF mcmf(T + 1);
    mcmf.add_edge(S, 1, k - 1, 0);
    for (int t = 1; t &lt; n; ++t) {
        mcmf.add_edge(t, t + 1, k - 1, 0);
    }
    mcmf.add_edge(n, T, k - 1, 0);

    long long base_cost = 0;
    vector&lt;int&gt; last_pos(n + 1, 0);
    for (int t = 1; t &lt;= n; ++t) {
        int book = a[t];
        base_cost += c[book];
        int p = last_pos[book];
        if (p != 0) {
            if (p + 1 == t) {
                base_cost -= c[book];
            } else {
                mcmf.add_edge(p + 1, t, 1, -c[book]);
            }
        }
        last_pos[book] = t;
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; base_cost + mcmf.min_cost_max_flow(S, T) &lt;&lt; "\n";
    return 0;
}







6.7 Problem #159 (Mas-1) — [AtCoder AGC 031 E] Snuke the Phantom Thief

Judge URL: https://atcoder.jp/contests/agc031/tasks/agc031_e
Bounds: \(N \le 80\) jewels, \(M \le 320\) conditions, coordinates \(x_i, y_i \le 100\), values \(v_i \le 10^{15}\).


Problem Statement
There are \(N\) jewels in 2D at \((x_i, y_i)\) with value \(v_i\). You are given \(M\) constraints of four types (L, R, D, U): - L \(a_j, b_j\): at most \(b_j\) stolen jewels have \(x \le a_j\). - R \(a_j, b_j\): at most \(b_j\) stolen jewels have \(x \ge a_j\). - D \(a_j, b_j\): at most \(b_j\) stolen jewels have \(y \le a_j\). - U \(a_j, b_j\): at most \(b_j\) stolen jewels have \(y \ge a_j\).
Find the maximum total value of a valid subset of jewels.


4-Step Compilation Summary

Step 1 (Diagnostic Signal): Prefix/suffix count bounds are non-local over subsets, but if we fix the total number of stolen jewels \(K \in \{1, \dots, N\}\), every L/R/D/U constraint becomes a point bound on the \(k\)-th smallest \(x\)-coordinate \(x_{(k)}\) or \(y\)-coordinate \(y_{(k)}\)!
Step 2 (Interval Monotonization): For fixed \(K\):

Initialize \(L_k^x = 1, R_k^x = 100, L_k^y = 1, R_k^y = 100\) for \(k = 1 \dots K\).
L \((a, b)\): if \(b + 1 \le K\), \(L_{b+1}^x = \max(L_{b+1}^x, a + 1)\).
R \((a, b)\): if \(K - b \ge 1\), \(R_{K-b}^x = \min(R_{K-b}^x, a - 1)\).
Propagate \(L_k^x = \max(L_k^x, L_{k-1}^x)\) and \(R_k^x = \min(R_k^x, R_{k+1}^x)\) (and identically for \(y\)).

Step 3 (3-Layer Hub Network):

\(S \to X_k\) \((1, 0)\) for \(1 \le k \le K\).
\(X_k \to J_i^{\text{in}}\) \((1, 0)\) iff \(L_k^x \le x_i \le R_k^x\).
\(J_i^{\text{in}} \to J_i^{\text{out}}\) \((1, -v_i)\) for \(1 \le i \le N\).
\(J_i^{\text{out}} \to Y_r\) \((1, 0)\) iff \(L_r^y \le y_i \le R_r^y\).
\(Y_r \to T\) \((1, 0)\) for \(1 \le r \le K\).

Step 4 (Uncrossing Guarantee): By Theorem 5.1, any minimum-cost flow of value \(K\) can be uncrossed inside the jewel layer so that \(X_k\) matches to the \(k\)-th smallest \(x\)-coordinate and \(Y_r\) matches to the \(r\)-th smallest \(y\)-coordinate. Maximize over \(K = 1 \dots N\).







TipHint 1 — Fixing the Subset Size \(K\) (Problem #159)





Since \(N \le 80\), we can afford to iterate over the exact number of stolen jewels \(K \in \{1, \dots, N\}\). Once \(K\) is fixed, what does “at most \(b\) stolen jewels have \(x \le a\)” say about the \((b + 1)\)-th smallest \(x\)-coordinate among the \(K\) stolen jewels?









TipHint 2 — Translating Count Bounds to Coordinate Intervals (Problem #159)





If at most \(b\) stolen jewels have \(x \le a\), then the \((b+1)\)-th smallest stolen \(x\)-coordinate must satisfy \(x_{(b+1)} \ge a + 1\). Similarly, if at most \(b\) stolen jewels have \(x \ge a\), then the \((K - b)\)-th smallest stolen \(x\)-coordinate must satisfy \(x_{(K - b)} \le a - 1\). Propagate these bounds so each rank \(k \in \{1, \dots, K\}\) has a monotone window \([L_k^x, R_k^x]\) and \([L_k^y, R_k^y]\).









TipHint 3 — Three-Layer Rank-Jewel-Rank MCMF (Problem #159)





For a fixed \(K\), build a flow network with \(K\) \(x\)-rank nodes \(X_1 \dots X_K\), \(N\) split jewel nodes \(J_i^{\text{in}} \to J_i^{\text{out}}\) of capacity \(1\) and cost \(-v_i\), and \(K\) \(y\)-rank nodes \(Y_1 \dots Y_K\). Connect \(X_k \to J_i^{\text{in}}\) if \(x_i \in [L_k^x, R_k^x]\) and \(J_i^{\text{out}} \to Y_r\) if \(y_i \in [L_r^y, R_r^y]\). If the max flow from \(S\) to \(T\) reaches \(K\), update the answer with \(-\text{min\_cost}\).









NoteComplete C++ Solution — Problem #159 (AtCoder AGC 031 E: Snuke the Phantom Thief)





#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;

struct MCMF {
    struct Edge { int to, rev, cap; long long cost; };
