Problem #169 (SS-1): General Graph Maximum Matching & Gallai–Edmonds Walk Game ([UOJ #79 / Luogu P6113], QOJ #5437, CF 1089B)

Judge Links: UOJ #79 / Luogu P6113 / CF 1089B Constraints: \(N \le 500\) (up to \(1000\)), \(M \le \binom{N}{2}\), Time Limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal
We must compute the maximum size \(\nu(G)\) (and explicit edge pairing) of a set of vertex-disjoint edges in an arbitrary undirected graph \(G = (V, E)\) that may contain odd cycles, or determine for every starting vertex \(s \in V\) whether the first player wins the non-repeating vertex walk game (QOJ #5437), or handle vertices of capacity \(2\) that can also self-pair (CF 1089B). Bipartite Dinic / Hopcroft–Karp fails because odd cycles break 2-coloring (Broken Gadget 22C).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Logical Representation
We seek \(x_e \in \{0, 1\}\) maximizing \(\sum_{e \in E} x_e\) subject to \(\sum_{e \in \delta(v)} x_e \le 1\) and the odd-set constraints \(\sum_{e \in E(B)} x_e \le \frac{|B|-1}{2}\) for every odd subset \(B \subseteq V\). For the walk game variant, vertex \(s\) is a first-player winning start iff \(s\) is covered by every maximum matching of \(G\), i.e., \(s \notin D(G)\) in the Gallai–Edmonds decomposition \(V = D(G) \sqcup A(G) \sqcup C(G)\). For CF 1089B (each of \(N\) guests has 2 hands and can either hold hands with 2 chambers or clasp their own 2 hands together as 1 pair), each guest \(i\) has the 2-Vertex Twin reward \(\text{matches}(i) = c_i + [c_i = 0]\).


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Blossom Contraction & Twin Gadget)

For CF 1089B: Split each guest \(i\) into \(i_1, i_2\) with internal edge \((i_1, i_2)\) and connect both \(i_1, i_2\) to all preferred chambers \(j\). Then \(\nu(G') - N\) equals the exact maximum number of accommodated guests!
For UOJ #79 & Walk Game: Iterate \(i = 1 \dots N\); if match[i] == 0, run bfs(i) to grow an alternating tree rooted at \(i\). When an outer-to-outer edge \((u, v)\) (vis[u] == 1 && vis[v] == 1) with orig[u] != orig[v] is scanned, compute \(b = \operatorname{LCA}(u, v)\) and call contract(u, v, b) and contract(v, u, b) to flip all inner nodes on the cycle to vis = 1 and push them into the queue.
Gallai–Edmonds Extraction: After finding a maximum matching, initialize an empty BFS queue, push all unmatched vertices \(r\) (match[r] == 0) with vis[r] = 1, orig[r] = r simultaneously, and run the exact same Blossom BFS (which will find no augmenting path). Then \(D(G) = \{v : \text{vis}[v] == 1\}\), \(A(G) = \{v : \text{vis}[v] == 2\}\), and \(C(G) = \{v : \text{vis}[v] == 0\}\).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Broken Gadget Counterexample
Trace \(G = K_3\) on \(\{1, 2, 3\}\) with initial matching \(M = \{(2, 3)\}\) and unmatched root \(1\):

bfs(1) sets vis[1] = 1. Scanning edge \((1, 2)\) sees match[2] = 3: set pre[2] = 1, vis[2] = 2, vis[3] = 1 and push \(3\).
Next, outer node \(3\) (vis[3] = 1) scans edge \((3, 1)\): both vis[3] == 1 and vis[1] == 1! We found the triangle blossom \(B = \{1, 2, 3\}\) with base \(b = \operatorname{LCA}(3, 1) = 1\).
contract(3, 1, 1) sets pre[3] = 1, contract(1, 3, 1) sets pre[2] = 3, flips vis[2] = 1 (pushing \(2\) into the queue!), and sets orig[1] = orig[2] = orig[3] = 1.
Now if vertex \(2\) had an external edge \((2, 4)\) to an unmatched vertex \(4\), 2 (now outer, vis[2] = 1!) would immediately augment along \(4 \gets 2 \gets_{\text{pre}} 3 \Longleftrightarrow_{\text{match}} 2 \dots\) wait: augment(4) sets match[4] = 2, jumps to x = pre[2] = 3 (which contract rewired across the bridge \((3, 1)\)!), and pairs \((3, 1)\), yielding the 2-edge matching \(\{(4, 2), (3, 1)\}\)!
What Breaks If You Omit pre[x] = y in contract? Without pre[2] = 3, pre[2] remains 1, so augmenting from \((2, 4)\) leaves vertex \(3\) completely orphaned (match[3] = 0) and fails to match \((3, 1)\)!







NoteProgressive Hints for Problem #169






Hint 1: In lca(u, v), always jump via u = orig[pre[match[u]]] after first setting u = orig[u], because intermediate blossoms may have already been contracted along the path to the root.
Hint 2: In contract(u, v, b), start with int x = u, y = v; and loop while (orig[x] != b): inside the loop, set pre[x] = y; y = match[x]; if (vis[y] == 2) { vis[y] = 1; q.push(y); } orig[x] = orig[y] = b; x = pre[y];.
Hint 3: In augment(v), walk while (v): save int pv = pre[v], nv = match[pv]; match[v] = pv; match[pv] = v; v = nv;. Notice how pre[x] = y inside contract makes augment(v) work without ever expanding blossoms!










TipComplete C++ Implementation Contract — Problem #169 (Edmonds’ Blossom & Gallai–Edmonds)





struct BlossomSolver {
    int n, timer = 0;
    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; adj;
    vector&lt;int&gt; match, pre, vis, orig, tim;
    queue&lt;int&gt; q;

    BlossomSolver(int n) : n(n), adj(n + 1), match(n + 1, 0),
        pre(n + 1, 0), vis(n + 1, 0), orig(n + 1, 0), tim(n + 1, 0) {}

    void add_edge(int u, int v) {
        adj[u].push_back(v);
        adj[v].push_back(u);
    }

    int find_orig(int x) {
        return orig[x] == x ? x : orig[x] = find_orig(orig[x]);
    }

    int lca(int u, int v) {
        ++timer;
        u = find_orig(u); v = find_orig(v);
        while (tim[u] != timer) {
            tim[u] = timer;
            u = find_orig(pre[match[u]]);
            if (v) swap(u, v);
        }
        return u;
    }

    void contract(int u, int v, int b) {
        int x = u, y = v;
        while (find_orig(x) != b) {
            pre[x] = y;
            y = match[x];
            if (vis[y] == 2) { vis[y] = 1; q.push(y); }
            orig[x] = orig[y] = b;
            x = pre[y];
        }
    }

    void augment(int v) {
        while (v) {
            int pv = pre[v], nv = match[pv];
            match[v] = pv; match[pv] = v;
            v = nv;
        }
    }

    bool bfs(int root) {
        fill(vis.begin(), vis.end(), 0);
        fill(pre.begin(), pre.end(), 0);
        iota(orig.begin(), orig.end(), 0);
        while (!q.empty()) q.pop();
        q.push(root); vis[root] = 1;
        while (!q.empty()) {
            int u = q.front(); q.pop();
            for (int v : adj[u]) {
                if (find_orig(u) == find_orig(v) || vis[v] == 2) continue;
                if (!vis[v]) {
                    vis[v] = 2; pre[v] = u;
                    if (!match[v]) { augment(v); return true; }
                    vis[match[v]] = 1; q.push(match[v]);
                } else {
                    int b = lca(u, v);
                    contract(u, v, b); contract(v, u, b);
                }
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    int solve() {
        int ans = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i)
            if (!match[i] && bfs(i)) ++ans;
        return ans;
    }
};







Problem #170 (SS-2): [BZOJ 3218 / Luogu P3218] a + b Problem

Judge Links: Luogu P3218 / BZOJ 3218 Constraints: \(N \le 5000\), \(0 \le a_i, l_i, r_i \le 10^9\), \(0 \le b_i, w_i, p_i \le 10^5\), Time Limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal
Each cell \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) is painted either Black (reward \(b_i\)) or White (reward \(w_i\)). However, if cell \(i\) is painted Black and there exists at least one earlier cell \(j &lt; i\) painted White whose value \(a_j\) lies in \([l_i, r_i]\), cell \(i\) becomes “strange” and incurs a single bundled penalty \(p_i\) (paid at most once regardless of how many such \(j\) exist). Binary choices with a bundled implication penalty scream Chapter 09 Project Selection Min-Cut, and the 2D prefix-range predicate \(\{j &lt; i : l_i \le a_j \le r_i\}\) requires a Prefix Persistent Segment Tree (主席树).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Logical Representation
Let \(x_i \in \{0, 1\}\) be \(1\) if cell \(i\) is painted Black (\(i \in S\)) and \(0\) if White (\(i \in T\)). Starting from the ideal reward \(\sum_{i=1}^N (b_i + w_i)\), we minimize the lost reward plus penalties by introducing a binary penalty indicator \(z_i \in \{0, 1\}\) for each cell \(i\):
\[\min_{x, z \in \{0, 1\}^N} \sum_{i=1}^N \Big[ w_i (1 - x_i) + b_i x_i + p_i x_i (1 - z_i) + \sum_{\substack{1 \le j &lt; i \\ l_i \le a_j \le r_i}} \infty \cdot z_i (1 - x_j) \Big]\]
Check the logic: if cell \(i\) is Black (\(x_i = 1\)) and sets \(z_i = 1\) to avoid paying \(p_i\), then the \(\infty\)-penalty forces \(x_j = 1\) (Black) for every \(j &lt; i\) with \(a_j \in [l_i, r_i]\). If any such \(j\) is White (\(x_j = 0\)), we are forced to set \(z_i = 0\) and pay \(p_i\)!


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Persistent SegTree Compilation)

For each cell \(i\), connect \(S \xrightarrow{b_i} i\) and \(i \xrightarrow{w_i} T\), and create an auxiliary penalty hub \(u_i^{\text{pen}}\) wired \(i \xrightarrow{p_i} u_i^{\text{pen}}\).
Coordinate-compress all \(a_1, \dots, a_N\) into \([1, U]\) (\(U \le N\)). Maintain a Persistent Out-Segment Tree where version \(\text{root}_{i-1}\) contains leaves for cells \(1 \dots i-1\).
For cell \(i\), query \(\text{root}_{i-1}\) on the compressed range \([l_i, r_i]\) and wire \(u_i^{\text{pen}} \xrightarrow{\infty} h\) for each of the \(O(\log N)\) returned persistent hubs \(h\).
Insert cell \(i\) at \(\text{pos}(a_i)\) into \(\text{root}_{i-1}\) to create \(\text{root}_i\): at the new leaf \(v_{\text{new}}\), wire \(v_{\text{new}} \xrightarrow{\infty} i\) AND if an old leaf \(v_{\text{old}}\) already existed at \(\text{pos}(a_i)\), wire \(v_{\text{new}} \xrightarrow{\infty} v_{\text{old}}\) (Duplicate-Key Leaf Chaining Fix)!



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Broken Gadget Counterexample
Suppose \(N = 3\) with \(a_1 = 5, a_2 = 5, a_3 = 10\), and cell \(3\) queries \([l_3, r_3] = [5, 5]\).

Version \(1\) creates leaf \(L_1 \xrightarrow{\infty} 1\) at key \(5\).
Version \(2\) inserts cell \(2\) at the same key \(5\), creating leaf \(L_2 \xrightarrow{\infty} 2\) and chaining \(L_2 \xrightarrow{\infty} L_1\).
When cell \(3\) queries key \(5\) in \(\text{root}_2\), it connects \(u_3^{\text{pen}} \xrightarrow{\infty} L_2\), which reaches both \(2\) (directly) and \(1\) (via \(L_2 \to L_1\)).
What Breaks Without \(L_2 \to L_1\)? If \(L_2\) overwrote \(L_1\) without chaining, cell \(1\) could be painted White (\(1 \in T\)) while cell \(2\) is Black (\(2 \in S\)) and cell \(3\) is Black (\(3 \in S\)) without severing \(i \xrightarrow{p_3} u_3^{\text{pen}}\), dodging the mandatory penalty \(p_3\)!







NoteProgressive Hints for Problem #170






Hint 1: What is the base Project Selection graph before data-structure optimization? \(S \xrightarrow{b_i} i \xrightarrow{w_i} T\), plus \(i \xrightarrow{p_i} u_i^{\text{pen}} \xrightarrow{\infty} j\) for all \(j &lt; i\) with \(a_j \in [l_i, r_i]\).
Hint 2: Only compress the values \(a_1, \dots, a_N\) (size \(U \le N\)); for each query \([l_i, r_i]\), use lower_bound and upper_bound on the sorted compressed array to find the index interval \([L, R]\).
Hint 3: In insert(prev_root, 1, U, pos, i), allocate a new node u = ++tot, copy children from prev_root, connect u -&gt; i if L == R, and always if prev_root &gt; 0, add add_edge(u, prev_root, INF)!










TipComplete C++ Implementation Contract — Problem #170 (Persistent SegTree + Min-Cut)





// Inside Persistent SegTree builder coupled with Dinic:
int lc[MAX_NODE], rc[MAX_NODE], root[MAXN], tot_nodes;

void insert_pst(int &u, int prev, int l, int r, int pos, int cell_id, Dinic &dinic) {
    u = ++tot_nodes;
    lc[u] = lc[prev]; rc[u] = rc[prev];
    if (prev) dinic.add_edge(u, prev, INF); // Mandatory duplicate-key / version chain!
    if (l == r) {
        dinic.add_edge(u, cell_id, INF);
        return;
    }
    int mid = (l + r) &gt;&gt; 1;
    if (pos &lt;= mid) {
        insert_pst(lc[u], lc[prev], l, mid, pos, cell_id, dinic);
        dinic.add_edge(u, lc[u], INF);
    } else {
        insert_pst(rc[u], rc[prev], mid + 1, r, pos, cell_id, dinic);
        dinic.add_edge(u, rc[u], INF);
    }
}

void query_pst(int u, int l, int r, int ql, int qr, int pen_node, Dinic &dinic) {
    if (!u || ql &gt; qr) return;
    if (ql &lt;= l && r &lt;= qr) {
        dinic.add_edge(pen_node, u, INF);
        return;
    }
    int mid = (l + r) &gt;&gt; 1;
    if (ql &lt;= mid) query_pst(lc[u], l, mid, ql, qr, pen_node, dinic);
    if (qr &gt; mid)  query_pst(rc[u], mid + 1, r, ql, qr, pen_node, dinic);
}







Problem #171 (Int-1): [CF 793G] Oleg and chess

Judge Link: Codeforces 793G Constraints: \(N \le 10^4\), \(Q \le 10^4\) disjoint forbidden rectangles \([x_1, x_2] \times [y_1, y_2]\), Time Limit \(2.5\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal
We want to place the maximum number of non-attacking rooks (or equivalently cover all valid cells with the minimum number of rows and columns) on an \(N \times N\) chessboard after removing \(Q \le 10^4\) forbidden rectangles. By König’s Theorem (Chapter 07), this is Maximum Bipartite Matching between \(N\) rows and \(N\) columns where edge \((x, y)\) exists iff cell \((x, y)\) is not inside any forbidden rectangle. Materializing \(N^2 = 10^8\) edges TLEs/MLEs, so we need a Sweep-Line + Persistent Out-Tree Subtree Splicing architecture (Architecture 3).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Logical Representation
Let \(x_r \in \{0, 1\}\) and \(y_c \in \{0, 1\}\) indicate whether row \(r\) and column \(c\) are chosen in the Minimum Vertex Cover:
\[\min \sum_{r=1}^N x_r + \sum_{c=1}^N y_c \quad \text{s.t.} \quad x_r + y_c \ge 1 \quad \forall (r, c) \notin \bigcup_{k=1}^Q [x_{1,k}, x_{2,k}] \times [y_{1,k}, y_{2,k}]\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Persistent Subtree Splicing)

Build an initial complete Out-Segment Tree \(\text{root}_0\) over rows \([1, N]\), where each row leaf \(r\) connects \(r \xrightarrow{\text{cap } 1} T\) and internal nodes connect \(p \xrightarrow{\infty} lc, rc\).
Sweep columns \(c = 1 \dots N\):

When a forbidden rectangle \([x_1, x_2] \times [y_1, y_2]\) starts at column \(c = y_1\), update \(\text{root}_c\) on \([x_1, x_2]\) by setting the pointers of the covered canonical segment-tree nodes to 0 (creating \(O(\log N)\) cloned ancestor nodes along the boundary).
When that rectangle ends at column \(c = y_2 + 1\), update \(\text{root}_c\) on \([x_1, x_2]\) by copying the node pointers directly from \(\text{root}_0\) at the covered canonical intervals! Because the rectangles are disjoint, every cell in \([x_1, x_2]\) becomes completely free at column \(y_2 + 1\).

At each column \(c\), after applying all events at \(c\), connect \(S \xrightarrow{\text{cap } 1} \text{root}_c\).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Broken Gadget Counterexample
On \(N = 4\) with one forbidden rectangle \([2, 3] \times [2, 2]\):

At \(c = 1\): \(S \xrightarrow{1} \text{root}_0\) reaches all rows \(\{1, 2, 3, 4\}\).
At \(c = 2\): \([2, 3]\) is nulled out in \(\text{root}_2\), so \(S \xrightarrow{1} \text{root}_2\) reaches only leaves \(1\) and \(4\).
At \(c = 3\): \([2, 3]\) is spliced back from \(\text{root}_0\) into \(\text{root}_3\), restoring reachability to \(\{1, 2, 3, 4\}\) in \(O(\log N)\) nodes!







NoteProgressive Hints for Problem #171






Hint 1: Why don’t we need a 2D rectangle decomposition into \(O(Q)\) disjoint free rectangles? Because persistent subtree splicing from \(\text{root}_0\) automatically maintains the exact set of free rows at column \(c\) in \(O(\log N)\) time per rectangle boundary!
Hint 2: In modify(u_prev, u_full, l, r, ql, qr, type), u_full is the corresponding node in \(\text{root}_0\). If ql &lt;= l && r &lt;= qr, return type == REMOVE ? 0 : u_full without allocating any new node!
Hint 3: Only allocate a new persistent node when ql &lt;= l && r &lt;= qr is false (partial overlap), and only add Dinic edges u -&gt; lc[u] and u -&gt; rc[u] if the child pointer is non-zero.










TipComplete C++ Implementation Contract — Problem #171 (Persistent Subtree Splicing)





int build_full(int l, int r, Dinic &dinic, int T) {
    int u = ++tot_nodes;
    if (l == r) {
        dinic.add_edge(u, T, 1);
        return u;
    }
    int mid = (l + r) &gt;&gt; 1;
    lc[u] = build_full(l, mid, dinic, T);
    rc[u] = build_full(mid + 1, r, dinic, T);
    dinic.add_edge(u, lc[u], INF);
    dinic.add_edge(u, rc[u], INF);
    return u;
}

int modify_pst(int prev, int full_u, int l, int r, int ql, int qr, bool enable, Dinic &dinic) {
    if (ql &lt;= l && r &lt;= qr) return enable ? full_u : 0;
    int u = ++tot_nodes;
    int mid = (l + r) &gt;&gt; 1;
    lc[u] = (ql &lt;= mid) ? modify_pst(lc[prev], lc[full_u], l, mid, ql, qr, enable, dinic) : lc[prev];
    rc[u] = (qr &gt; mid)  ? modify_pst(rc[prev], rc[full_u], mid + 1, r, ql, qr, enable, dinic) : rc[prev];
    if (lc[u]) dinic.add_edge(u, lc[u], INF);
    if (rc[u]) dinic.add_edge(u, rc[u], INF);
    return u;
}







Problem #172 (Int-2): [LOJ #3093 / Luogu P5331 / SDOI2019] 通信 (Communication)

Judge Links: LOJ #3093 / Luogu P5331 Constraints: \(N \le 1000\), \(W \le 10^9\), \(0 \le a_i \le 10^9\), Time Limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal
Each station \(i \in \{1, \dots, N\}\) must either connect directly to the control center at cost \(W\), or connect to a strictly earlier station \(j &lt; i\) at cost \(|a_i - a_j|\), where each earlier station \(j\) can serve as the target for at most one later station \(i\). This is the exact DAG Minimum-Cost Path Cover (星际竞速, #155) model on \(N\) vertices, except that the \(O(N^2)\) bipartite edges \((i_{\text{out}}, j_{\text{in}})\) (\(j &lt; i\)) carry cost \(|a_i - a_j|\). On \(N = 1000\), \(O(N^2) = 5 \times 10^5\) edges in MCMF will TLE; we must compress the graph to \(O(N \log N)\) edges using CDQ Divide-and-Conquer + 1D Bi-Directional Sorted Value Chain (Architecture 4).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Logical Representation
Using the standard vertex-splitting path cover formulation (\(S \xrightarrow{1, 0} i_{\text{out}}\) and \(j_{\text{in}} \xrightarrow{1, 0} T\)), every station \(i\) must send \(1\) unit of flow from \(i_{\text{out}}\) either directly to \(T\) at cost \(W\) (\(i_{\text{out}} \xrightarrow{1, W} T\)) or to some \(j_{\text{in}}\) (\(j &lt; i\)) at cost \(|a_i - a_j|\):
\[\min \sum_{i=1}^N W \cdot f_{i \to T} + \sum_{1 \le j &lt; i \le N} |a_i - a_j| \cdot f_{i \to j} \quad \text{s.t.} \quad f_{i \to T} + \sum_{j &lt; i} f_{i \to j} = 1, \quad \sum_{i &gt; j} f_{i \to j} \le 1\]


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (CDQ + 1D Value Chain)
Call cdq(l, r) recursively on \([1, N]\):

Let \(mid = (l + r) / 2\), and recursively call cdq(l, mid) and cdq(mid + 1, r).
Collect and sort all unique values \(v_1 &lt; v_2 &lt; \dots &lt; v_k\) appearing in \(\{a_t : l \le t \le r\}\).
Create \(k\) chain hubs \(h_1, \dots, h_k\) with bidirectional edges \(h_m \xleftrightarrow{\text{cap } \infty,\text{ cost } v_{m+1} - v_m} h_{m+1}\).
For each right-half station \(i \in [mid + 1, r]\), connect \(i_{\text{out}} \xrightarrow{\infty, 0} h_{\text{pos}(a_i)}\).
For each left-half station \(j \in [l, mid]\), connect \(h_{\text{pos}(a_j)} \xrightarrow{\infty, 0} j_{\text{in}}\).



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Broken Gadget Counterexample
For \(N = 3\) with \(a = [10, 50, 20]\) and \(W = 100\):

In cdq(1, 3) with \(mid = 2\), the sorted values are \(v = [10, 20, 50]\) with chain hubs \(h_{10} \xleftrightarrow{\infty, 10} h_{20} \xleftrightarrow{\infty, 30} h_{50}\).
Right-half \(i = 3\) (\(a_3 = 20\)) enters \(3_{\text{out}} \xrightarrow{\infty, 0} h_{20}\).
Left-half \(j = 1\) (\(a_1 = 10\)) and \(j = 2\) (\(a_2 = 50\)) exit \(h_{10} \xrightarrow{\infty, 0} 1_{\text{in}}\) and \(h_{50} \xrightarrow{\infty, 0} 2_{\text{in}}\).
Thus \(3_{\text{out}}\) can reach \(1_{\text{in}}\) at cost \(10\) or \(2_{\text{in}}\) at cost \(30\), and cannot reach \(3_{\text{in}}\)!







NoteProgressive Hints for Problem #172






Hint 1: Why must right-half nodes \([mid+1, r]\) ONLY have edges INTO the value chain, and left-half nodes \([l, mid]\) ONLY have edges OUT OF the value chain? To enforce \(j \le mid &lt; i\) (\(j &lt; i\)) without ever allowing \(i \le j\)!
Hint 2: What is the total number of chain hubs across all CDQ calls? At depth \(d\), the sizes sum to \(\le N\), so \(\le N \lceil\log_2 N\rceil \approx 10^4\) vertices and \(\approx 4 \times 10^4\) edges!
Hint 3: Since all edge costs (\(W\) and \(v_{m+1} - v_m\)) are strictly non-negative, you can run Dijkstra-with-Johnson-potentials or SPFA-Dinic MCMF directly in \(O(N^2 \log N)\).










TipComplete C++ Implementation Contract — Problem #172 (CDQ Value-Chain Builder)





void cdq_build(int l, int r, const vector&lt;long long&gt; &a, MCMF &mcmf, int &tot_nodes) {
    if (l &gt;= r) return;
    int mid = (l + r) &gt;&gt; 1;
    cdq_build(l, mid, a, mcmf, tot_nodes);
    cdq_build(mid + 1, r, a, mcmf, tot_nodes);

    vector&lt;long long&gt; vals;
    vals.reserve(r - l + 1);
    for (int i = l; i &lt;= r; ++i) vals.push_back(a[i]);
    sort(vals.begin(), vals.end());
    vals.erase(unique(vals.begin(), vals.end()), vals.end());

    int k = vals.size();
    int base = tot_nodes;
    tot_nodes += k;
    for (int m = 0; m + 1 &lt; k; ++m) {
        long long diff = vals[m + 1] - vals[m];
        mcmf.add_edge(base + m + 1, base + m + 2, INF, diff);
        mcmf.add_edge(base + m + 2, base + m + 1, INF, diff);
    }
    for (int i = l; i &lt;= r; ++i) {
        int pos = lower_bound(vals.begin(), vals.end(), a[i]) - vals.begin() + 1;
        if (i &lt;= mid) {
            mcmf.add_edge(base + pos, in_node(i), 1, 0);   // Left half exits chain
        } else {
            mcmf.add_edge(out_node(i), base + pos, 1, 0);  // Right half enters chain
        }
    }
}







Problem #173 (Adv-1): [WC2016 / Luogu P4258 / UOJ #171] 挑战 NPC (Challenging NPC)

Judge Links: UOJ #171 / Luogu P4258 Constraints: \(T \le 30\) test cases, \(N \le 3M\), \(M \le 100\) (\(N\) balls, \(M\) baskets, so \(N + 3M \le 600\) vertices), Time Limit \(1.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal
We must place \(N\) balls into \(M\) baskets (each basket \(j\) holds at most \(3\) balls) so that every ball is placed, and among all valid placements we maximize the number of “half-empty” baskets that contain \(\le 1\) ball. Why does Bipartite Flow / MCMF fail? In an MCMF network, giving basket \(j\) a \(+1\) reward for having \(0\) or \(1\) ball and \(0\) reward for having \(2\) or \(3\) balls corresponds to marginal step rewards \((+1, 0, -1, 0)\) for the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd ball — which is neither convex nor concave (the 2nd ball costs \(-1\) while the 3rd ball costs \(0\))! Whenever a degree reward has a parity or pair-breaking jump at capacity \(2\) vs. \(3\) on \(V \le 600\), reach for the 3-Vertex Triangle Blossom Gadget (Section 4.2).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Logical Representation
Let \(c_j \in \{0, 1, 2, 3\}\) be the number of balls placed in basket \(j\), with \(\sum_{j=1}^M c_j = N\). We want to maximize:
\[\sum_{j=1}^M c_j + \sum_{j=1}^M [c_j \le 1] = \sum_{j=1}^M \big(c_j + [c_j \le 1]\big)\]
subject to all \(N\) balls being matched (\(\sum_{j=1}^M c_j = N\)). Look at the table of \(f(c_j) = c_j + [c_j \le 1]\):










Balls in Basket \(j\) (\(c_j\))
\(c_j = 0\)
\(c_j = 1\)
\(c_j = 2\)
\(c_j = 3\)




Target Gadget Matches \(f(c_j)\)
\(0 + 1 = \mathbf{1}\)
\(1 + 1 = \mathbf{2}\)
\(2 + 0 = \mathbf{2}\)
\(3 + 0 = \mathbf{3}\)





Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (3-Vertex Triangle Blossom Gadget & Two-Phase Order)

Split each basket \(j \in \{1, \dots, M\}\) into three vertices \(b_{j,1}, b_{j,2}, b_{j,3}\).
For each ball \(i\) that can go into basket \(j\), add undirected edges from \(i\) to all three vertices \(b_{j,1}, b_{j,2}, b_{j,3}\).
Add a single internal edge \((b_{j,2}, b_{j,3})\) inside basket \(j\).
Why This Realizes \(f(c_j)\): If \(c_j \in \{0, 1\}\), the ball (if any) matches \(b_{j,1}\) while \((b_{j,2}, b_{j,3})\) remains intact (\(+1\) bonus match). If \(c_j \in \{2, 3\}\), the second ball must match \(b_{j,2}\) or \(b_{j,3}\), breaking \((b_{j,2}, b_{j,3})\) (\(0\) bonus match)!
Mandatory Two-Phase Blossom Execution:

Phase 1: Loop over all ball vertices \(i = 1, \dots, N\) and call bfs(i). If any ball \(i\) fails to match, output 0 (or impossible) immediately!
Phase 2: Loop over all basket vertices \(b_{j,2}, b_{j,3}\) (\(j = 1, \dots, M\)) and call bfs(v). Because augmenting paths in Blossom never unmatch an already-matched vertex, all \(N\) balls remain matched, and the total matching size \(\nu(G')\) satisfies \(\text{Ans} = \nu(G') - N\)!




Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Broken Gadget Counterexample
Suppose \(N = 2\) balls \(\{u_1, u_2\}\) and \(M = 2\) baskets \(\{B_1, B_2\}\) where both balls can go into either basket:

After Phase 1 and Phase 2, \(u_1\) matches \(b_{1,1}\) (with \((b_{1,2}, b_{1,3})\) matched internally) and \(u_2\) matches \(b_{2,1}\) (with \((b_{2,2}, b_{2,3})\) matched internally). Total matches \(= 4\), so \(\text{Ans} = 4 - 2 = 2\) baskets with \(\le 1\) ball.
What Breaks If You Connect a Full Triangle \((b_{j,1}, b_{j,2}), (b_{j,2}, b_{j,3}), (b_{j,3}, b_{j,1})\)? Even with a full triangle inside \(\{b_{j,1}, b_{j,2}, b_{j,3}\}\), at most \(1\) internal edge can be matched when \(c_j \in \{0, 1\}\), so a full triangle also works — however, if you run bfs on basket vertices before ball vertices without checking that all \(N\) balls are matched, an isolated ball \(u_1\) with no edges would leave \(\nu(G') &gt; 0\) even though no valid placement of all \(N\) balls exists!







NoteProgressive Hints for Problem #173






Hint 1: Write down the total number of matched edges inside basket \(j\) (external ball edges + internal basket edges) when \(0, 1, 2, 3\) balls enter basket \(j\).
Hint 2: Split basket \(j\) into \(b_{j,1}, b_{j,2}, b_{j,3}\) and add \((b_{j,2}, b_{j,3})\). Verify that \(c_j\) balls can achieve \(c_j + [c_j \le 1]\) matched edges!
Hint 3: Always augment all \(N\) ball vertices first (for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) bfs(i)), then augment the remaining basket vertices, and output total_matches - n along with belong[i] = (match[i] - n - 1) / 3 + 1.










TipComplete C++ Implementation Contract — Problem #173 (Two-Phase Blossom Gadget)





void solve_case() {
    int n, m, e;
    cin &gt;&gt; n &gt;&gt; m &gt;&gt; e;
    // Vertices 1..n: balls; n + 3*(j-1) + 1..3: basket j slots
    BlossomSolver solver(n + 3 * m);
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= m; ++j) {
        int b1 = n + 3 * (j - 1) + 1;
        int b2 = b1 + 1, b3 = b1 + 2;
        solver.add_edge(b2, b3); // Internal bonus edge!
    }
    for (int k = 0; k &lt; e; ++k) {
        int u, j;
        cin &gt;&gt; u &gt;&gt; j;
        int b1 = n + 3 * (j - 1) + 1;
        solver.add_edge(u, b1);
        solver.add_edge(u, b1 + 1);
        solver.add_edge(u, b1 + 2);
    }
    int matched = 0;
    // Phase 1: Match all N balls first (mandatory!)
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i)
        if (!solver.match[i]) matched += solver.bfs(i);
    // Phase 2: Match remaining internal basket bonus edges
    for (int v = n + 1; v &lt;= n + 3 * m; ++v)
        if (!solver.match[v]) matched += solver.bfs(v);

    cout &lt;&lt; (matched - n) &lt;&lt; "\n";
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i)
        cout &lt;&lt; (solver.match[i] - n - 1) / 3 + 1 &lt;&lt; (i == n ? '\n' : ' ');
}







Problem #174 (Adv-2): [CF 1045A] Last Chance

Judge Link: Codeforces 1045A Constraints: \(N \le 5000\) weapons, \(M \le 5000\) spaceships, Time Limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal
We have \(N\) weapons of three types targeting \(M\) spaceships:

Type 0: Fires at \(1\) spaceship from an explicit list \(K_i\).
Type 1: Fires at \(1\) spaceship in a contiguous interval \([l_i, r_i]\) (requires 1D Segment Tree Out-Tree).
Type 2: Fires at either \(0\) or \(2\) spaceships from a given triple \(\{a_i, b_i, c_i\}\), where the triples of all Type 2 weapons are pairwise disjoint!



Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Logical Representation
In standard Max-Flow, an edge \(S \xrightarrow{2} w_i\) allows Type 2 weapon \(w_i\) to destroy \(0, 1,\) or \(2\) spaceships in \(\{a_i, b_i, c_i\}\). Why can we relax \(\{0, 2\}\) to \(\{0, 1, 2\}\) during Dinic and fix any degree-\(1\) violation in \(O(1)\) time afterward?
Suppose in the maximum flow, a Type 2 weapon \(w_i\) pushes only \(1\) unit of flow (say to \(a_i\)), while neither \(b_i\) nor \(c_i\) is matched to \(w_i\). Could \(b_i\) be completely unmatched in the maximum flow? No! If \(b_i\) were unmatched, \(S \to w_i \to b_i \to T\) would be a valid augmenting path, contradicting maximality of the flow! Therefore, both \(b_i\) and \(c_i\) must already be matched by some Type 0 or Type 1 weapon (since Type 2 triples are pairwise disjoint!). We can simply steal \(b_i\) from that Type 0/1 weapon and give \(b_i\) to \(w_i\) (so \(w_i\) now destroys \(2\) ships \(\{a_i, b_i\}\), while the Type 0/1 weapon drops from \(1\) ship to \(0\) ships), preserving the total number of destroyed ships!


Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Out-Tree + Post-Flow Leaf Theft)

Build an Out-Segment Tree over spaceships \([1, M]\) with leaves wired \(\text{leaf}_s \xrightarrow{1} T\) and internal edges \(p \xrightarrow{\infty} lc, rc\).
Connect Type 0 weapons \(S \xrightarrow{1} w_i \xrightarrow{1} \text{leaf}_s\) (\(s \in K_i\)), Type 1 weapons \(S \xrightarrow{1} w_i \xrightarrow{1} h\) for canonical Out-Tree hubs \(h \subseteq [l_i, r_i]\), and Type 2 weapons \(S \xrightarrow{2} w_i \xrightarrow{1} \text{leaf}_{a_i}, \text{leaf}_{b_i}, \text{leaf}_{c_i}\).
Run Dinic. Then decompose the flow on the Out-Tree DAG to find which weapon \(w_i\) actually hit each matched spaceship \(s\). Finally, for any Type 2 weapon \(w_i\) that only hit \(1\) ship \(a_i\), reassign \(b_i\) from its current Type 0/1 owner to \(w_i\)!



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Broken Gadget Counterexample
Suppose Type 1 weapon \(w_1\) covers \([1, 2]\) and Type 2 weapon \(w_2\) covers \(\{1, 2, 3\}\). If Dinic routes \(w_1 \to 1, w_2 \to 2\) (and \(3\) is also hit by \(w_3 \to 3\)), \(w_2\) has degree \(1\). Post-processing steals ship \(1\) from \(w_1\) and assigns \(\{1, 2\}\) to \(w_2\), leaving \(w_1\) idle and \(w_2\) with \(\{1, 2\}\) (size \(2\))!






NoteProgressive Hints for Problem #174






Hint 1: Why can’t a Type 2 weapon with \(1\) unit of flow have an unmatched neighbor \(b_i\)? Because \(S \to w_i\) still has residual capacity \(2 - 1 = 1\), so \(S \to w_i \to b_i \to T\) would augment the flow!
Hint 2: How do you reconstruct the explicit matching pairs \((w_i, \text{ship})\) through the Segment Tree Out-Tree? Run a simple DFS/path-peeling from each Type 1 weapon \(w_i\) with \(f(S \to w_i) = 1\) down the Out-Tree edges with \(f(u \to v) &gt; 0\), decrementing \(f(u \to v)\) by \(1\) along the path to the leaf!
Hint 3: After recovering owner[ship] for all ships, loop over Type 2 weapons; if assigned[w].size() == 1, pick one of its other two targets x, erase x from owner[x], and assign owner[x] = w.










TipComplete C++ Implementation Contract — Problem #174 (Path Peeling & Type-2 Theft)





// After Dinic max-flow on Out-Tree:
int find_leaf_and_decrement(int u, Dinic &dinic) {
    if (is_ship_leaf[u]) return ship_id[u];
    for (auto &e : dinic.adj[u]) {
        if (e.to != parent_or_rev && e.flow &gt; 0) {
            e.flow--;
            return find_leaf_and_decrement(e.to, dinic);
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
// Post-processing for Type 2 weapons with exactly 1 assigned ship:
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i) {
    if (type[i] == 2 && hits[i].size() == 1) {
        int taken = hits[i][0];
        int steal = (triple[i][0] != taken) ? triple[i][0] : triple[i][1];
        int old_w = owner[steal];
        hits[old_w].clear();
        owner[steal] = i;
        hits[i].push_back(steal);
    }
}







Problem #175 (Mas-1): All-Edges Matching Classification (可行边 & 必须边) & Matching Matroid Basis (CF Gym 102155 J / Tutte Pfaffian Oracle)

Judge Link: CF Gym 102155 Problem J (Choosing Subsets) Constraints: \(N \le 500\), \(M \le \binom{N}{2}\), Vertex weights \(w_v \le 10^9\), Time Limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal
Given a general non-bipartite graph \(G = (V, E)\) with \(N \le 500\), we must either:

Task A (All-Edges Classification): Determine for every edge \(e = (u, v) \in E\) whether \(e\) belongs to at least one maximum matching (可行边) and whether \(e\) belongs to every maximum matching (必须边).
Task B (CF Gym 102155 J, Matching Matroid Basis): Find a maximum-matching-saturable subset of vertices \(S \subseteq V\) of maximum total vertex weight \(\sum_{v \in S} w_v\).

Both tasks require algebraic reasoning on the Skew-Symmetric Tutte Matrix \(T(G)\) (Theorem 22.4) in \(O(N^3)\) time over \(\mathbb{F}_{998244353}\).


Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Logical Representation
Let \(T(G)\) be the \(N \times N\) skew-symmetric Tutte matrix with random \(x_{u,v} \in \mathbb{F}_p^\times\).

For Task B (Matching Matroid): By Tutte’s Theorem, a subset \(S \subseteq V\) can be covered by some matching of \(G\) iff the row space of \(T(G)[S, V]\) has full rank \(|S|\) — that is, the rows of \(T(G)\) form a linear matroid over \(\mathbb{F}_p\) that represents the Matching Matroid! Therefore, we simply sort the vertices \(v_1, \dots, v_N\) in descending order of weight \(w_v\) and maintain a Linear Basis (Gaussian Elimination) over the rows of \(T(G)\), greedily inserting row \(T_{v_i, *}\) if it is linearly independent of previously chosen rows!
For Task A (Feasible & Essential Edges): Let \(r = \operatorname{rank}(T(G)) = 2\nu(G)\). Pad \(T(G)\) with \(d = N - r\) universal dummy vertices \(w_1, \dots, w_d\) connected to all \(v \in V\) with random weights to form an invertible \((N+d) \times (N+d)\) skew-symmetric matrix \(\hat{T}\), and compute \(B = \hat{T}^{-1} \pmod p\). Then:

\((u, v)\) is Feasible (可行边) \(\iff B_{u,v} \not\equiv 0 \pmod p\);
\((u, v)\) is Essential (必须边) \(\iff T_{u,v} \cdot B_{v,u} \equiv 1 \pmod p\).




Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (Gaussian Elimination & Matrix Inversion)

Generate \(T_{u,v} = \text{rng}() \bmod (p - 1) + 1\) and \(T_{v,u} = (p - T_{u,v}) \bmod p\) for each \((u, v) \in E\).
For CF Gym 102155 J: Sort vertices by \(w_v\) descending; insert each row into an \(N\)-dimensional Gaussian basis basis[col]. Every vertex whose row successfully inserts into basis is chosen!
For All-Edges Classification: Find \(r = \operatorname{rank}(T)\), append \(d = N - r\) universal dummy rows/columns to build \(\hat{T}\) of size \(K = N + d\), invert \(\hat{T}\) via Gauss–Jordan elimination to get \(B = \hat{T}^{-1}\), and read off \(B_{u,v} \neq 0\) and \(T_{u,v} B_{v,u} \equiv 1 \pmod p\) in \(O(1)\) per edge!



Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Broken Gadget Counterexample
On \(G = K_3 = \{1, 2, 3\}\), \(\nu(K_3) = 1\), so \(r = 2\) and we add \(d = 3 - 2 = 1\) universal dummy vertex \(4\). Every pair in \(\{1, 2, 3\}\) can match with the remaining vertex paired to \(4\), so \(B_{1,2}, B_{2,3}, B_{3,1}\) are all non-zero (all 3 edges are feasible), while none equals \(T_{u,v}^{-1}\) (0 edges are essential).

What Breaks If You Forget the \(d = N - 2\nu(G)\) Dummy Vertices? On \(K_3\), \(N = 3\) is odd, so \(\det T(K_3) = 0\) identically (T is singular and cannot be inverted at all)! Even if \(N\) is even, whenever \(\nu(G) &lt; N/2\), \(T(G)\) is singular and must be padded with \(d = N - 2\nu(G)\) universal dummies before inversion.







NoteProgressive Hints for Problem #175






Hint 1: Why do the rows of \(T(G)\) represent the Matching Matroid? Because if \(S\) is covered by a matching \(M\), then \(V(M)\) is an even-sized superset of \(S\) where \(\det T[V(M), V(M)] \neq 0\), meaning the rows of \(S\) are linearly independent in \(T(G)\)!
Hint 2: How do you find \(d = N - 2\nu(G)\) before building \(\hat{T}\)? Run one Gaussian elimination on a copy of \(T(G)\) to find its rank \(r = 2\nu(G)\), then set \(d = N - r\).
Hint 3: Once \(B = \hat{T}^{-1}\) is computed in \(O((N+d)^3)\), feasible(u, v) is B[u][v] != 0 and essential(u, v) is 1LL * T[u][v] * B[v][u] % MOD == 1.










TipComplete C++ Implementation Contract — Problem #175 (Tutte Inverse & Matching Matroid)





const int MOD = 998244353;
long long qpow(long long a, long long b = MOD - 2) {
    long long r = 1;
    for (; b; b &gt;&gt;= 1, a = a * a % MOD) if (b & 1) r = r * a % MOD;
    return r;
}

// Invert K x K matrix A mod 998244353; returns rank and writes A^{-1} into invA
int invert_matrix(vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; A, vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; &invA) {
    int K = A.size();
    invA.assign(K, vector&lt;int&gt;(K, 0));
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; K; ++i) invA[i][i] = 1;
    int rank = 0;
    for (int col = 0; col &lt; K; ++col) {
        int piv = -1;
        for (int row = rank; row &lt; K; ++row)
            if (A[row][col]) { piv = row; break; }
        if (piv == -1) continue;
        swap(A[rank], A[piv]); swap(invA[rank], invA[piv]);
        long long inv_p = qpow(A[rank][col]);
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; K; ++j) {
            A[rank][j] = A[rank][j] * inv_p % MOD;
            invA[rank][j] = invA[rank][j] * inv_p % MOD;
        }
        for (int row = 0; row &lt; K; ++row) if (row != rank && A[row][col]) {
            long long factor = A[row][col];
            for (int j = 0; j &lt; K; ++j) {
                A[row][j] = (A[row][j] - factor * A[rank][j]) % MOD;
                if (A[row][j] &lt; 0) A[row][j] += MOD;
                invA[row][j] = (invA[row][j] - factor * invA[rank][j]) % MOD;
                if (invA[row][j] &lt; 0) invA[row][j] += MOD;
            }
        }
        ++rank;
    }
    return rank;
}







Problem #176 (Mas-2): [NOI2019 / Luogu P5471 / LOJ #3159] 弹跳 (Bounce) & 2D KD-Tree / Manhattan Hub Synthesis (AGC 034D)

Judge Links: LOJ #3159 / Luogu P5471 / AtCoder AGC 034D Constraints: \(N \le 7 \times 10^4\) cities, \(M \le 1.5 \times 10^5\) 2D rectangle jump devices, Memory Limit \(128\text{ MB}\), Time Limit \(2.0\text{ s}\).


Step 1: Non-Graph Diagnostic Signal
In [NOI2019] 弹跳, each jump device at city \(p_k\) can reach all cities inside a 2D axis-aligned rectangle \([L_k, R_k] \times [D_k, U_k]\) at cost \(t_k\), under a strict \(128\text{ MB}\) memory limit. In its companion [AGC 034D] Manhattan Max Matching, we must pair \(N\) red points \((RX_i, RY_i, RC_i)\) with \(N\) blue points \((BX_j, BY_j, BC_j)\) (\(\sum RC_i \le 10^4\)) to maximize total Manhattan distance \(\sum (|RX_i - BX_j| + |RY_i - BY_j|)\).

In NOI2019 弹跳, building a 2D Segment Tree of \(O(N \log^2 N)\) nodes plus \(O(M \log^2 N)\) explicit graph edges requires \(&gt; 500\text{ MB}\) and immediately MLEs! We must replace it with an \(O(N)\)-node 2D KD-Tree with implicit bounding-box pruning!
In AGC 034D, building \(N^2 = 10^6\) bipartite MCMF edges TLEs; we factor the 2D Manhattan norm through 4 Sign Hubs (Architecture 6).



Step 2: Intermediate Algebraic / Logical Representation

2D KD-Tree Bounding-Box Decomposition: Partition the \(N\) points recursively by alternating nth_element on \(x\) (even depth) and \(y\) (odd depth). Each KD-Tree node \(u\) stores its exact bounding box \([xl_u, xr_u] \times [yl_u, yr_u]\) and uses strictly \(N\) nodes in total! In a Min-Cut or Flow problem on 2D rectangles, \(u \xrightarrow{\infty, 0} lc_u, rc_u\) forms an \(O(N)\)-node Out-Tree where any rectangle query attaches to \(O(\sqrt{N})\) canonical KD-Tree hubs. Under NOI2019 弹跳’s 128 MB limit, we don’t even materialize the query-to-hub edges: the priority queue pushes (dist, rect_id) and lazily relaxes the KD-Tree subtrees, skipping any node \(u\) whose min_val[u] &lt;= d!
4-Sign Manhattan Factorization (AGC 034D): Rewrite the Manhattan distance between red point \(i\) and blue point \(j\) as:
\[|RX_i - BX_j| + |RY_i - BY_j| = \max_{s_x, s_y \in \{+1, -1\}} \Big[ (s_x RX_i + s_y RY_i) + (-s_x BX_j - s_y BY_j) \Big]\]
Since we are maximizing total cost in Max-Cost Max-Flow, the \(\max\) over the \(4\) sign pairs \((s_x, s_y) \in \{+1, -1\}^2\) is chosen automatically by the maximum-cost flow!



Step 3: Reverse-Engineering Scratchpad (KD-Tree & 4-Sign Hubs)

For AGC 034D: Create just \(4\) virtual hubs \(H_0, H_1, H_2, H_3\) corresponding to \((s_x, s_y) \in \{(+1, +1), (+1, -1), (-1, +1), (-1, -1)\}\).

For each red group \(i\), connect \(S \xrightarrow{RC_i, 0} R_i\), and for each \(k \in \{0, 1, 2, 3\}\) connect \(R_i \xrightarrow{\infty,\; s_x RX_i + s_y RY_i} H_k\).
For each blue group \(j\), connect \(B_j \xrightarrow{BC_j, 0} T\), and for each \(k \in \{0, 1, 2, 3\}\) connect \(H_k \xrightarrow{\infty,\; -s_x BX_j - s_y BY_j} B_j\).
Total vertices \(= 2N + 6\), total edges \(= 10N\)!

For NOI2019 弹跳: Build the \(N\)-node KD-Tree over the \(N\) cities in \(O(N \log N)\) time. When Dijkstra pops a rectangle jump [L, R, D, U] with time \(d\), recursively traverse the KD-Tree:

If d &gt;= tag[u] or [L, R] x [D, U] is disjoint from [xl[u], xr[u]] x [yl[u], yr[u]], prune immediately!
If [xl[u], xr[u]] x [yl[u], yr[u]] is completely inside [L, R] x [D, U], update tag[u] = d, relax the point at u, and stop!




Step 4: Hand-Checkable Toy Trace & Broken Gadget Counterexample
In AGC 034D with \(R_1 = (1, 2)\) and \(B_1 = (4, 0)\):

The 4 paths \(R_1 \to H_k \to B_1\) have weights:

\((+1, +1)\): \((1 + 2) + (-4 - 0) = -1\)
\((+1, -1)\): \((1 - 2) + (-4 + 0) = -5\)
\((-1, +1)\): \((-1 + 2) + (4 - 0) = \mathbf{+5} = |1 - 4| + |2 - 0|\)
\((-1, -1)\): \((-1 - 2) + (4 + 0) = +1\)

Max-Cost Flow automatically routes through \(H_{(-1, +1)}\) to pick the exact maximum \(+5\)!
What Breaks If You Use 4-Sign Hubs for MINIMUM Manhattan Matching? If you want to minimize \(\sum (|RX_i - BX_j| + |RY_i - BY_j|)\), Min-Cost Flow picks the minimum over the 4 hubs (\(-5\) instead of \(+5\)), which equals \(-|RX_i - BX_j| - |RY_i - BY_j|\)! The 4-Sign Hub trick works only when maximizing Manhattan distance (or cutting in a dual formulation); for minimum Manhattan distance in 1D, you must use the CDQ Bi-Directional Sorted Value Chain (Architecture 4, Problem #172)!







NoteProgressive Hints for Problem #176






Hint 1: Why does NOI2019 弹跳 give a \(128\text{ MB}\) memory limit? Specifically to fail \(O(M \log^2 N)\)-edge 2D Segment Tree graphs! An \(N\)-node 2D KD-Tree uses only \(O(N)\) memory.
Hint 2: In the KD-Tree traversal apply_rect(u, L, R, D, U, d), always check if (d &gt;= sub_tag[u]) return; at the very top of the function so each KD-Tree node is tagged at most once in increasing order of Dijkstra distance d!
Hint 3: For AGC 034D, remember to negate the costs (-cost) and add Johnson potentials (or Bellman-Ford initial potentials) before running Dijkstra-SSP for Max-Cost Max-Flow!










TipComplete C++ Implementation Contract — Problem #176 (2D KD-Tree & 4-Sign Manhattan Hubs)





// Part A: 4-Sign Manhattan Hub Compilation (AGC 034D)
void build_manhattan_hubs(int N, MCMF &mcmf, int S, int T) {
    int H[4] = {2 * N + 1, 2 * N + 2, 2 * N + 3, 2 * N + 4};
    int sx[4] = {1, 1, -1, -1}, sy[4] = {1, -1, 1, -1};
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= N; ++i) {
        mcmf.add_edge(S, i, RC[i], 0);
        for (int k = 0; k &lt; 4; ++k)
            mcmf.add_edge(i, H[k], INF, -(sx[k] * RX[i] + sy[k] * RY[i]));
    }
    for (int j = 1; j &lt;= N; ++j) {
        mcmf.add_edge(N + j, T, BC[j], 0);
        for (int k = 0; k &lt; 4; ++k)
            mcmf.add_edge(H[k], N + j, INF, -(-sx[k] * BX[j] - sy[k] * BY[j]));
    }
}

// Part B: 2D KD-Tree Bounding-Box Relaxation (NOI2019 Bounce, 128 MB)
void relax_kdt(int u, int L, int R, int D, int U, long long d) {
    if (!u || d &gt;= sub_tag[u] || xr[u] &lt; L || xl[u] &gt; R || yr[u] &lt; D || yl[u] &gt; U)
        return;
    if (L &lt;= xl[u] && xr[u] &lt;= R && D &lt;= yl[u] && yr[u] &lt;= U) {
        sub_tag[u] = d;
        if (d &lt; pt_dist[u]) {
            pt_dist[u] = d;
            pq.push({d, -u}); // -u denotes city u
        }
        return;
    }
    if (L &lt;= px[u] && px[u] &lt;= R && D &lt;= py[u] && py[u] &lt;= U && d &lt; pt_dist[u]) {
        pt_dist[u] = d;
        pq.push({d, -u});
    }
    relax_kdt(lc[u], L, R, D, U, d);
    relax_kdt(rc[u], L, R, D, U, d);
}

